BEGIN;
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An analog-to-digital converter is a mixed-signal integrated circuit that transforms a continuous-time, continuous-amplitude analog input into a discrete-time sequence of digitally coded words, by periodically sampling the input at a defined rate and quantizing each sample to the nearest of a finite set of levels fixed by its resolution in bits. Its transfer characteristic is anchored to a reference voltage that establishes full scale, and its quality is described by static metrics such as differential and integral nonlinearity and by dynamic metrics such as signal-to-noise-and-distortion ratio and effective number of bits. Common architectures include successive-approximation, delta-sigma, pipelined, and flash converters, each trading resolution against conversion rate.' WHERE id='e8e42bfb-94a5-4cd6-9927-30b9685c4dd4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An audio operational amplifier is an operational amplifier optimized for amplifying audio-band signals, exhibiting low input-referred voltage and current noise, low total harmonic distortion, a wide gain-bandwidth product, high slew rate, and sufficient output current to drive typical audio loads across the roughly 20 Hz to 20 kHz band and beyond. It is applied in preamplifiers, active filters, summing and mixing stages, and line drivers, where its large open-loop gain combined with negative feedback linearizes the closed-loop response. Representative parts are the bipolar dual NE5532 and various FET-input audio devices.' WHERE id='716b1bf6-32ff-4794-bf3d-0b7052204f9f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A bias transistor is a transistor used not as a signal-gain element but to establish and stabilize the quiescent operating point of another stage, typically by generating a controlled reference voltage or current. In an audio power-amplifier output stage it most often appears as the Vbe-multiplier (or rubber-diode) device, whose collector-emitter voltage sets the standing bias across the output pair''s base-emitter junctions. When thermally coupled to the output devices or heatsink it tracks the temperature dependence of their turn-on voltage, counteracting the drift in quiescent current that would otherwise lead to thermal runaway.' WHERE id='b3ed9a8a-555c-4e9b-ba49-354677c85d47' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A bipolar junction transistor is a three-terminal semiconductor device comprising two back-to-back p-n junctions that define emitter, base, and collector regions in either NPN or PNP polarity, and in which conduction involves both electrons and holes. In the forward-active region the base-emitter junction is forward biased while the base-collector junction is reverse biased, so a small base current controls a much larger collector current, giving current gain (beta). The collector current follows an exponential dependence on base-emitter voltage as described by the transistor''s transport model, and the device provides both current and voltage gain in amplifier and switching circuits.' WHERE id='dfb9647f-9901-46df-9808-9d457d850db4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A bipolar-input operational amplifier is an op amp whose input differential stage is built from bipolar junction transistors rather than field-effect devices. This construction yields low input-referred voltage noise, high input-stage transconductance, and good DC matching and offset performance, but it draws non-negligible input bias and offset currents into the transistor bases, which must be accommodated by the surrounding source and feedback impedances. It is favoured for low-noise signal conditioning driven from low-to-moderate source resistances.' WHERE id='3a36bea0-402e-4487-aa8c-dd4547b65e65' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='BJT is the standard abbreviation for the bipolar junction transistor, a three-terminal active device with emitter, base, and collector terminals formed by two p-n junctions in an NPN or PNP arrangement, whose operation relies on both majority- and minority-carrier conduction. In the forward-active region a small base current, equivalently an exponential function of base-emitter voltage, controls a much larger collector current, providing the current and voltage gain used throughout analog amplifier and digital switching circuits.' WHERE id='d15f8351-c62b-42c2-ac3f-50d68da44403' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A cascode stage is a two-transistor amplifier configuration in which a common-emitter (or common-source) input transistor is stacked directly beneath a common-base (or common-gate) transistor that conveys the signal current to the output. By holding the input device''s collector or drain voltage nearly constant, the cascode suppresses the Miller multiplication of the input capacitance and raises the output resistance seen at the load node, thereby extending bandwidth and increasing achievable voltage gain. It is a fundamental building block of wideband and high-gain integrated amplifiers.' WHERE id='f77bd91f-ecbb-453c-995d-94e260ef2922' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Class-A stage is an amplifier gain or output stage biased so that its active device (or devices) conducts current throughout the entire signal cycle, giving a conduction angle of 360 degrees, with a quiescent current at least equal to the peak load current. This fully linear mode produces the lowest crossover and switching distortion but has poor power efficiency, with a theoretical maximum near 25 percent for resistive coupling and 50 percent for transformer or current-source loading, the remaining supplied power being dissipated as heat.' WHERE id='4fa41e9e-2848-4698-bf8b-3d88b722b079' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Class-AB stage is a push-pull output stage biased with a small standing current so that each device conducts for somewhat more than half of the signal cycle, giving a conduction angle between 180 and 360 degrees, with both devices simultaneously active around the zero crossing. This intermediate bias captures much of Class-B''s efficiency while greatly reducing the crossover distortion of pure Class-B, and it is the dominant topology for audio power output stages.' WHERE id='8f35441f-7d76-4175-bbd7-88d9186ac5fa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Class-B stage is a push-pull output stage in which each of two complementary devices conducts for only one half of the signal cycle, a conduction angle of 180 degrees, handing conduction to the other device at the zero crossing and ideally carrying no quiescent current. It offers high efficiency, up to a theoretical 78.5 percent for a sinusoidal signal, but the abrupt handover where neither device fully conducts produces crossover distortion, which is why practical designs usually add bias to operate in Class-AB.' WHERE id='c0ac2fa6-3af3-4aaf-a235-add1d7a27c93' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The collector is one of the three terminals of a bipolar junction transistor, connected to the collector region that gathers the charge carriers injected from the emitter across the base. In normal forward-active operation the collector-base junction is reverse biased, and the collector carries almost all of the emitter current, the fraction being set by the common-base current gain alpha, while sustaining the largest terminal voltage of the device. The collector current and collector-emitter voltage together define the transistor''s output characteristics and its power dissipation.' WHERE id='0dbd0bec-3050-4e76-910d-f530a7879dcf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A common-base stage is a single-transistor BJT amplifier configuration in which the base is held at signal ground while the input is applied to the emitter and the output taken from the collector. It presents a low input resistance and a high output resistance, has a current gain slightly less than unity but substantial voltage gain, and, because it is free of the Miller effect, offers excellent high-frequency response. It is commonly used as the upper transistor of a cascode and in wideband and radio-frequency stages.' WHERE id='9cb75232-4855-4f0d-9a47-57ddc5a83dce' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A common-emitter stage is the most widely used single-transistor BJT amplifier configuration, in which the emitter is the terminal common to input and output, the signal is applied to the base, and the output is taken from the collector. It provides substantial voltage and current gain, and therefore high power gain, with a 180-degree phase inversion, together with a moderate input resistance and a high output resistance; its high-frequency bandwidth is limited by Miller multiplication of the collector-base capacitance.' WHERE id='7b91870b-b39f-4a09-a5db-9385bd0dbfa3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A common-source stage is the field-effect counterpart of the common-emitter amplifier, in which the source is common to input and output, the signal is applied to the gate, and the output is taken from the drain. It provides a voltage gain equal to the product of the device transconductance and the effective load resistance with an inverting, 180-degree phase relationship, a very high input resistance set by the insulated or reverse-biased gate, and a bandwidth limited by the Miller-multiplied gate-drain capacitance.' WHERE id='065b1790-ef49-4fbe-80b5-33197545a021' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A comparator is an analog integrated circuit that compares two analog input voltages and produces a two-state, logic-level output indicating which input is larger, functioning as an open-loop high-gain differential amplifier optimized for fast large-signal switching rather than linear operation. Dedicated comparators offer short propagation delay and outputs compatible with logic families, and they often incorporate hysteresis, whether internal or added by positive feedback, to prevent output chatter on slowly varying or noisy inputs. Unlike an operational amplifier, a comparator is designed to be driven into saturation.' WHERE id='d107990b-1d85-41df-b177-7cfb3e3bbf5b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A complementary pair is a matched pair of transistors of opposite polarity, an NPN and a PNP bipolar transistor or an n-channel and a p-channel MOSFET, having closely similar but mirror-image characteristics. Such pairs form the basis of push-pull output stages, in which one device sources current to the load on positive half-cycles and its complement sinks current on negative half-cycles, and of complementary (for example CMOS) circuits, providing symmetrical drive with low standing dissipation.' WHERE id='203d593e-1428-430b-8d20-74db326c782b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A constant-current source is an active circuit, typically built around one or more transistors, that delivers a fixed output current substantially independent of the voltage across its terminals, presenting a very high dynamic output resistance over its compliance range. In discrete and integrated amplifier design it biases differential pairs and gain stages, serves as a high-impedance active load that raises voltage gain, and defines the tail current of long-tailed pairs.' WHERE id='3d4773a8-613f-481b-a819-d16f4836ac1f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A current mirror is a transistor circuit that replicates a reference current set in one device into one or more output devices, ideally producing an output current equal to, or a fixed multiple of, the reference and largely independent of the output voltage. Relying on matched devices operating at equal base-emitter (or gate-source) voltages, it is a fundamental building block for biasing and for active loads in integrated amplifiers, where it also performs differential-to-single-ended conversion; refinements such as the cascode and Wilson mirrors improve output resistance and copying accuracy.' WHERE id='01725dba-ba61-49e0-861e-e4b815918064' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A digital-to-analog converter is a mixed-signal integrated circuit that converts a digital input code into a corresponding analog output voltage or current proportional to the code and a reference, reconstructing a discrete-amplitude signal from binary words at a defined update rate. Its accuracy is described by resolution in bits and by static errors such as differential and integral nonlinearity, offset and gain error, together with dynamic measures such as spurious-free dynamic range; common architectures include binary-weighted, R-2R ladder, and delta-sigma converters.' WHERE id='a0d9716a-dc2f-47b9-b697-8ddc199e547f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Darlington pair is a compound connection of two bipolar transistors in which the emitter of the first drives the base of the second and their collectors are joined, so that the combination behaves as a single transistor with a current gain approximately equal to the product of the two individual betas. It offers very high input resistance and current gain suitable for output drivers and buffers, at the expense of a combined base-emitter turn-on voltage of about two junction drops, slower switching, and a higher collector-emitter saturation voltage.' WHERE id='da341b5c-94ae-42e8-9041-dd976756f966' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A demultiplexer is a combinational digital device, or an analog-switch array, that routes a single input to one of several outputs, the destination being chosen by a set of binary select lines, thereby performing the inverse function of a multiplexer. In its 1-to-2^n digital form it is functionally a decoder driven by an enable or data input, while in analog form it is realized with bilateral CMOS switches for bidirectional signal routing.' WHERE id='508b6b48-cdc1-4c26-a856-bbe8be84b841' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A difference amplifier is an amplifier, most commonly a single operational amplifier surrounded by four matched resistors, whose output is proportional to the difference between two input voltages while rejecting the component common to both. Its common-mode rejection depends critically on the matching ratio of the resistor network, and it presents finite and generally unequal input resistances at its two inputs; it is the basic subtractor stage and forms the output stage of the classic three-op-amp instrumentation amplifier.' WHERE id='80177ab6-5654-4490-85ef-590af22d83d1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A differential amplifier is an amplifier that responds to the difference between two input signals and ideally rejects any common-mode component present on both, characterized by its differential gain, common-mode gain, and their ratio, the common-mode rejection ratio. Realized in integrated form as an emitter- or source-coupled pair fed by a shared current source, it is the input stage of virtually all operational amplifiers and provides balanced, drift-cancelling amplification.' WHERE id='0d3c2168-a215-4b72-a7ac-184115b0e001' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A differential pair is the pair of matched transistors, emitter-coupled in bipolar form or source-coupled in field-effect form, whose common node is supplied by a constant tail current and which together constitute the core of a differential amplifier. The tail current is steered between the two devices according to the differential input voltage, giving a transconductance characteristic that is linear for small signals and saturates as the current is fully steered for large ones; matching of the two devices sets the input offset voltage and its drift.' WHERE id='9ab6312e-e5b7-4ef4-a5c3-12c9962e2aa4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A digital audio receiver is an integrated circuit that recovers audio data and clock from a serial digital audio interface such as AES3/EBU or S/PDIF, decoding the biphase-mark-coded, self-clocking bitstream into separated left and right PCM audio samples together with channel-status and user data and a recovered clock. It typically embeds a clock-recovery phase-locked loop for jitter attenuation and delivers audio on a standard synchronous serial (I2S) bus to a downstream converter; representative devices are the AES3/S/PDIF receivers produced by Cirrus Logic and Texas Instruments.' WHERE id='ba1d7a03-83c6-4c31-b6c6-2ea9bc2ce631' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A digital audio transmitter is an integrated circuit that encodes PCM audio samples together with channel-status and user data into the biphase-mark-coded serial format defined for the AES3/EBU and S/PDIF interfaces and drives the balanced, coaxial, or optical line. Accepting audio over a synchronous serial (I2S) input with a reference clock, it generates the subframe and block framing and the preambles specified by the standard; representative devices are the digital audio transmitters produced by Cirrus Logic and Texas Instruments.' WHERE id='398d0c57-8ce8-41f1-8360-b840812abe2f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The drain is one of the three terminals of a field-effect transistor, being the terminal through which majority carriers leave the conducting channel and toward which channel current flows from the source under the control of the gate voltage. In an n-channel device the drain is biased positive with respect to the source; the drain-source voltage relative to the gate overdrive determines whether the device operates in the triode (ohmic) or the saturation region, and the drain current constitutes the transistor''s controlled output current.' WHERE id='f3d9de40-fca6-44aa-9c07-8fb451154507' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A driver transistor is a transistor stage placed ahead of a power output device to supply the base or gate current and voltage swing that device requires, buffering the preceding high-impedance stage from the low and variable input impedance of the output devices. In an audio power-amplifier output stage the drivers form the first tier of an emitter-follower chain feeding the output power transistors, as in complementary Darlington or triple configurations, providing current gain and isolating the voltage-amplifier stage from the output devices.' WHERE id='58b55782-cd1c-45a9-886f-f771566255df' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dual op amp is an integrated circuit containing two independent operational amplifiers in a single package, sharing common power-supply pins but otherwise fully separate. The configuration saves board area and cost and promotes close thermal and parametric matching between the two amplifiers, making it convenient for stereo audio channels and multi-stage active filters; the industry-standard dual audio device is the NE5532.' WHERE id='6e6838a2-5a8f-4ac0-bc31-3cd50cc87151' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dual transistor is a component comprising two individual transistors fabricated on a common die, or otherwise closely integrated within a single package, to achieve tight parametric matching and thermal tracking between them. Such matched dual devices are used where offset, drift, and gain matching are critical, for example the input differential pair of low-drift amplifiers and the mirror devices of precision current mirrors, minimizing input offset voltage and its variation with temperature.' WHERE id='b7697b1a-8aaf-49af-bee0-934a2faac163' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dual-gate FET is a field-effect transistor, usually a depletion-mode MOSFET, provided with two gates in series along a single channel, so that it is functionally equivalent to a cascode of two devices in one package. The two independent gate voltages jointly control the drain current, allowing one gate to carry the signal while the other applies gain control or a second mixing or oscillator input; this arrangement gives low feedback capacitance and good reverse isolation, making dual-gate MOSFETs popular in radio-frequency amplifiers, mixers, and automatic-gain-control stages.' WHERE id='158347d4-05ba-4255-89f8-d0625b003cf3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An EEPROM, or electrically erasable programmable read-only memory, is a non-volatile semiconductor memory that stores data in floating-gate transistor cells and can be electrically written and erased in-circuit, characteristically at the byte level, retaining its contents without power. Programming and erasure rely on Fowler-Nordheim tunnelling of charge onto and off the floating gate; the technology offers high per-cell write and erase endurance but lower density than flash, and is commonly used for configuration and calibration data.' WHERE id='b43b7c19-db37-457e-aa8d-c41a1b73d5ef' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An emitter follower is the common-collector BJT configuration, in which the signal is applied to the base and taken from the emitter while the collector is held at signal ground, so that the emitter voltage follows the base voltage less one base-emitter drop. It provides a voltage gain slightly less than unity with no phase inversion, a high input resistance, a low output resistance, and substantial current gain, making it the standard buffer and output or driver stage for impedance transformation.' WHERE id='4e9e4422-b155-4b8d-bf33-dc6f8c9fd9ec' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An Ethernet controller is an integrated circuit that implements the media-access-control layer, and often the physical-layer transceiver, of an IEEE 802.3 Ethernet interface, handling frame assembly and disassembly, address filtering, CRC generation and checking, and the carrier-sense multiple-access or full-duplex access protocol. It buffers packets and exchanges them with a host processor over an interface such as MII/RMII, SPI, or a parallel host bus; an example is the SPI-interfaced device that integrates both MAC and PHY for embedded networking.' WHERE id='28c01d3e-3055-4fc2-8e89-8fde005cc778' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A FET-input operational amplifier is an op amp whose input differential stage uses junction or MOS field-effect transistors, giving extremely high input resistance and very low input bias and offset currents, typically in the picoampere range, at the cost of somewhat higher input voltage noise and offset voltage than a comparable bipolar-input design. Such amplifiers are preferred for high-source-impedance signals, integrators, sample-and-hold circuits, and photodiode or charge amplifiers, where minimal input current is essential.' WHERE id='e599b31f-2616-42b4-9ee4-f2ed4d1ed09b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field-effect transistor is a three-terminal unipolar semiconductor device, with gate, source, and drain terminals, in which the current flowing through a conducting channel between source and drain is modulated by the transverse electric field produced by the voltage applied to the gate. Conduction involves only majority carriers, and the gate is either insulated by an oxide (MOSFET) or reverse-biased across a junction (JFET), so the device is essentially voltage-controlled with very high input resistance, its drain current governed by the gate-source voltage relative to a threshold or pinch-off voltage.' WHERE id='af847e3b-e64c-40e4-9fc8-ff69893856cb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Flash memory is a high-density non-volatile floating-gate semiconductor memory that, unlike byte-erasable EEPROM, is erased in large blocks or sectors rather than individual bytes, trading fine-grained erase for greater density and lower cost per bit. Its cells are programmed and erased by charge tunnelling to and from the floating gate; the two dominant architectures are NOR flash, which offers random read access suited to in-place code execution, and NAND flash, which is optimized for high-density sequential mass storage.' WHERE id='95055b93-1251-40ae-a4d0-3bd5e6bd2c9a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A flip-flop is a clocked bistable digital storage element that holds one bit of state and, unlike a level-sensitive latch, changes its stored value only in response to an active clock edge, that is, it is edge-triggered. Common types include the D (data), JK, T (toggle), and SR flip-flops, distinguished by how their inputs determine the next state; they are the fundamental storage elements of registers, counters, and synchronous sequential logic.' WHERE id='15bf31b7-a70e-4284-a214-c74e420931cb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Gain rank refers to the classification of transistors, and the corresponding suffix or package marking, into groups according to their measured DC current gain (hFE or beta), a sorting the manufacturer performs because process spread makes gain vary widely between nominally identical devices. A rank letter or code, such as the O, Y, GR, and BL groups used on some small-signal transistors, narrows the guaranteed beta range for a given part number, helping designers who need predictable biasing or reasonably matched devices.' WHERE id='638a3fb8-e880-49a2-956f-774194b26f03' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An instrumentation amplifier is a precision differential-input amplifier, most commonly constructed from three operational amplifiers, that provides an accurately defined differential gain, very high and balanced input resistance at both inputs, and high common-mode rejection, with the gain typically set by a single external resistor. It is designed to extract small differential signals riding on large common-mode voltages from transducers such as bridges and thermocouples; unlike a single-op-amp difference amplifier, its common-mode rejection does not depend on external resistor matching.' WHERE id='c722a679-506f-4265-ad1f-73ac0b7260c1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated circuit is an electronic circuit in which many interconnected active and passive components, such as transistors, diodes, resistors, and capacitors, are fabricated together on and within a single monolithic semiconductor substrate, usually silicon, by photolithographic patterning and selective doping. Integration provides greatly reduced size, cost, and power together with improved speed, reliability, and close device matching; integrated circuits are classified by function as analog, digital, or mixed-signal and by their scale of integration.' WHERE id='6b0f6df3-fa99-4c90-b32c-bbbfbd30f2ee' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An inverting amplifier is the operational-amplifier configuration in which the input signal is applied through a series resistor to the inverting input while the non-inverting input is grounded and feedback is returned from the output to the inverting input, creating a virtual ground at that node. Its closed-loop gain is set by the ratio of the feedback resistor to the input resistor and is negative, giving a 180-degree phase inversion, and its input resistance equals the input series resistor.' WHERE id='58c07e57-d14d-4a6c-83b5-214299d1ecbd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A junction field-effect transistor is a unipolar FET in which the conducting channel between source and drain is controlled by the reverse-biased p-n junction of the gate; increasing the reverse gate-source voltage widens the depletion region and narrows the channel, reducing drain current until pinch-off. Being a depletion-mode device that conducts at zero gate voltage and draws only tiny gate leakage current, the JFET offers very high input resistance and low noise, and is widely used in low-noise and high-impedance input stages.' WHERE id='0b91d03f-58ae-4acf-942d-c91ce1be0a59' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A latch is a level-sensitive bistable digital storage element that holds one bit and is transparent while its enable input is active, passing the input through to the output, and holds the last value when the enable is deasserted, in contrast to the edge-triggered flip-flop. The basic SR latch is formed from cross-coupled gates, while the gated D latch adds an enable to control transparency and serves as the storage primitive from which edge-triggered flip-flops are constructed.' WHERE id='760d1109-ecf8-40b1-a961-5b380175a45a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A lateral MOSFET is a MOSFET in which the source, gate, and drain are arranged side by side at the surface of the die so that drain current flows laterally through the channel, in contrast to the vertical conduction path of most power (DMOS or trench) devices. In audio power amplifiers, lateral power MOSFETs are valued for the gentle negative temperature coefficient of drain current at typical bias, which resists thermal runaway and simplifies biasing, and for their freedom from the secondary-breakdown limitation of bipolar power transistors.' WHERE id='10f2d2e4-ff5d-4492-a970-dbcd28c7c462' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A logarithmic amplifier is a non-linear amplifier whose output is proportional to the logarithm of its input, realized by placing a device with an exponential current-voltage relationship, typically a diode or the base-emitter junction of a bipolar transistor, in the feedback path of an operational amplifier. Exploiting the exponential dependence of collector current on base-emitter voltage, it compresses a wide input dynamic range into a smaller output span and is used for signal compression, analog computation, and wide-range power measurement, though it requires temperature compensation for accuracy.' WHERE id='370df13d-b39c-429a-9351-dc7f5b533ffb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A logic IC is a digital integrated circuit that implements Boolean logic functions, operating on signals restricted to two valid voltage ranges representing logic 0 and logic 1, and ranging in complexity from simple gates and flip-flops to complex functional blocks. Such devices are grouped into logic families, for example TTL and CMOS such as the 74HC series, defined by their fabrication and by characteristic parameters including supply range, logic-threshold and noise-margin levels, propagation delay, fan-out, and power dissipation.' WHERE id='5c785137-580e-49b6-b260-107eddbf5c59' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A long-tailed pair is the classical name for an emitter-coupled, or source-coupled, differential amplifier in which two matched devices share a common emitter or source connection returned to a supply rail through a high-impedance current source, or large resistor, forming the long tail that sets the total bias current. The long tail forces the two devices to share the tail current according to the differential input voltage, giving high common-mode rejection, and it forms the input stage of operational amplifiers and power-amplifier front ends.' WHERE id='ef0661f4-f719-4fbf-85a0-e49a80534a3a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A low-distortion operational amplifier is an op amp designed to add minimal total harmonic and intermodulation distortion to signals, achieved through high open-loop gain and linearity, output stages that minimize crossover distortion, and a wide gain-bandwidth product so that ample feedback remains available at high audio frequencies. Characterized by specified total-harmonic-distortion-plus-noise figures at given loads and frequencies, such devices are used in high-fidelity audio and precision signal paths.' WHERE id='7ff33c81-ace6-4e09-b3f6-b32425086789' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A low-noise operational amplifier is an op amp optimized for minimal input-referred noise, exhibiting low equivalent input voltage-noise density, and in bipolar designs correspondingly higher current noise, so as to preserve signal-to-noise ratio when amplifying small signals from low-impedance sources. Its noise is specified as spot noise density in nanovolts per root hertz and as broadband noise, with a characteristic low-frequency flicker (1/f) corner; such parts are used in microphone and moving-coil preamplifiers and sensor front ends.' WHERE id='68cbf7ba-4954-4e42-a566-3a79c10144a7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A low-noise transistor is a transistor selected or designed for minimal contribution of internally generated noise, characterized by a low noise figure over a specified frequency range and range of source impedances. In bipolar low-noise devices this is achieved through low base spreading resistance and geometry and bias chosen to minimize shot and flicker noise, so that the transistor adds little noise in sensitive first stages such as microphone preamplifiers and radio-frequency front ends.' WHERE id='27bae05a-b11a-4766-9ec0-bc8b7447fe49' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A matched transistor pair is two transistors deliberately selected or co-fabricated to have very closely equal parameters, particularly base-emitter voltage (or threshold voltage) and current gain, and to track together with temperature. Such matching minimizes input offset voltage and drift in circuits that depend on device symmetry, notably differential input pairs and current mirrors, and is best obtained from a monolithic dual device whose two transistors share a common die and thermal environment.' WHERE id='3e7b720f-dd4f-48e4-8ad9-41d7af8666b2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microcontroller is a single integrated circuit that combines a processor core with program and data memory, typically flash and SRAM, and a set of on-chip peripherals such as timers, general-purpose I/O, serial communication interfaces, and analog-to-digital converters, forming a self-contained computer for embedded control. Optimized for low cost, low power, and deterministic real-time operation rather than raw throughput, it executes a fixed stored program to sense inputs and drive outputs in a dedicated application.' WHERE id='97d5f9fa-7594-40a1-bc16-400d9a6542c0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A MOSFET, or metal-oxide-semiconductor field-effect transistor, is a field-effect transistor whose gate is capacitively coupled to the channel through a thin insulating oxide layer, so that the gate voltage induces or depletes an inversion channel between source and drain while drawing essentially zero steady gate current. Made in n- and p-channel and in enhancement- and depletion-mode variants and characterized by a threshold voltage, it is the dominant device of digital VLSI in CMOS form and a major power-switching and amplifying element.' WHERE id='f69a034c-37b9-4172-bfcb-805261988cbf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A multiplexer is a device that selects one of several input signals and routes it to a single output under the control of binary select lines, effectively a many-to-one switch; a digital 2^n-to-1 multiplexer uses n select inputs, while an analog multiplexer uses bilateral CMOS switches to pass signals in either direction. Multiplexers are used for data selection and routing, for time-division sharing of a common resource, and for implementing combinational logic functions.' WHERE id='921e413f-9d11-4f48-9b2a-537f54321528' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An n-channel MOSFET is a MOSFET whose source and drain are n-type regions in a p-type body, conducting through an electron inversion channel formed when the gate-source voltage exceeds the threshold voltage in an enhancement device, or through an existing n-channel narrowed by the gate in a depletion device. With the drain positive relative to the source, drain current flows by electron transport; because electron mobility exceeds hole mobility, n-channel devices offer higher transconductance and lower on-resistance than comparable p-channel devices and form the pull-down and high-current elements of CMOS and power circuits.' WHERE id='0d272ed5-aeb4-405d-b41d-3f0dafdd1d56' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A non-inverting amplifier is the operational-amplifier configuration in which the input signal is applied directly to the non-inverting input and feedback is returned from the output through a resistive divider to the inverting input. Its closed-loop gain equals one plus the ratio of the feedback resistor to the grounded resistor and is positive, with no phase inversion, and it presents a very high input resistance set by the amplifier''s own input; when the divider is reduced to a direct connection it becomes the unity-gain voltage follower.' WHERE id='f23f7e19-d43b-44a0-a38b-5a3ca6a13bc8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An NPN transistor is a bipolar junction transistor formed by a thin p-type base sandwiched between n-type emitter and collector regions, in which electrons injected from the emitter are the principal charge carriers. In forward-active operation the base-emitter junction is forward biased, requiring the base and collector to be positive with respect to the emitter, and a small base current controls a much larger electron current from collector to emitter; being electron-conduction devices, NPN transistors are generally faster than their PNP complements and are the more commonly used polarity.' WHERE id='fc4e15ae-83dd-444c-9c0b-9fa0b76cab02' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An operational amplifier is a high-gain, direct-coupled, differential-input and typically single-ended-output voltage amplifier intended for use with external negative feedback that sets its precise closed-loop behaviour. The ideal model assumes infinite open-loop gain, infinite input resistance, zero output resistance, and infinite bandwidth, leading to the virtual-short approximation between its inputs; real devices are characterized by finite gain-bandwidth product, input offset voltage and bias current, slew rate, and common-mode rejection, and they implement functions from amplification and buffering to filtering and analog computation.' WHERE id='8ea3f733-42ac-4ec9-8af6-b7da01ca812f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An operational transconductance amplifier is a differential-input amplifier whose output is a current proportional to the differential input voltage, its transconductance being set, and made electronically variable, by an external bias current. Unlike a conventional operational amplifier it is intended to drive a defined load and presents a high output impedance, behaving as a current-source output; this makes it useful for voltage-controlled amplifiers and filters, analog multipliers, and other tunable functions, classic examples being the CA3080 and the LM13700.' WHERE id='7bea54aa-a952-49b6-b4df-7f13ba78e341' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An operational transconductance amplifier is a differential-input active device whose output is a current proportional to the differential input voltage, characterized by a transconductance gm that is typically set by an external bias current. Unlike a conventional voltage-feedback operational amplifier, its output behaves as a high-impedance current source rather than a low-impedance voltage source, so the effective gain and bandwidth can be tuned electronically through the bias input. This voltage-controlled behavior makes the OTA a building block for variable-gain amplifiers, analog multipliers, continuous-time filters, and voltage-controlled amplifiers.' WHERE id='ac3f06cb-fc0c-4198-80f5-8ccc00a1b65e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An output transistor is the power device in the final stage of an amplifier that delivers load current to the loudspeaker or other load, operating at the highest current and dissipation of any device in the signal chain. It is most often configured as one half of a complementary emitter-follower or source-follower pair, providing current gain at close to unity voltage gain, and must be selected for adequate collector or drain current, breakdown voltage, and safe-operating-area margin. Both bipolar power transistors and power MOSFETs are used, mounted on a heatsink to remove the dissipated heat.' WHERE id='625ca561-26cc-4a7d-ac51-a3b61b4c92ed' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A p-channel MOSFET is a field-effect transistor in which conduction between source and drain occurs through a channel of holes formed in an n-type body when a sufficiently negative gate-to-source voltage is applied. In the common enhancement mode the device is off at zero gate bias and turns on when the gate is driven below the source by more than the magnitude of the threshold voltage, which is negative. P-channel devices complement n-channel MOSFETs in CMOS logic and in complementary output and switching stages, though for a given die area they show higher on-resistance because hole mobility is lower than electron mobility.' WHERE id='86b2c3bb-12e0-4697-830e-def925bd8a5d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A PNP transistor is a bipolar junction transistor formed by a thin n-type base between p-type emitter and collector regions, in which the majority carriers injected across the forward-biased emitter-base junction are holes. It is turned on by pulling the base negative with respect to the emitter, and in normal active operation the emitter is held at the highest potential while conventional current flows from emitter to collector under the control of a much smaller base current. The PNP is the polarity complement of the NPN transistor and is used for high-side switching, current sourcing, and in complementary push-pull stages.' WHERE id='be5c3842-4cbc-4af3-8954-d6af9f271037' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A power MOSFET is a metal-oxide-semiconductor field-effect transistor structured for high current and voltage handling, most commonly built as a vertical double-diffused device so that current flows through the silicon to a drain contact on the underside of the die. It is a majority-carrier, voltage-controlled switch whose conducting state is characterized by an on-resistance RDS(on), and it exhibits a positive temperature coefficient of that resistance, which promotes current sharing among paralleled devices and resists thermal runaway. Power MOSFETs switch rapidly and are widely used in switch-mode power supplies, class-D amplifier output stages, and motor drives.' WHERE id='45194d02-8067-44d3-b28f-0a050b8d691e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A power transistor is any transistor designed to handle large currents, voltages, and power dissipation, supplied in a low-thermal-resistance package intended for mounting to a heatsink. The term covers both bipolar junction power transistors and power field-effect devices used in amplifier output stages, linear voltage regulators, and switching converters. Compared with small-signal types, power transistors accept lower current gain or transconductance and lower speed in exchange for the ability to dissipate watts to hundreds of watts within their rated safe operating area.' WHERE id='257ce66f-84e3-4eee-bc49-f822832e9ba4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A pre-driver transistor is a low- to medium-power stage placed ahead of the driver and output devices in a multi-stage amplifier output stage, supplying the base or gate current those later devices need while presenting only a light load to the preceding voltage-amplifier stage. In a triple emitter-follower output it forms the first of three cascaded current-gain stages, multiplying current gain so that the high-current output devices can be driven from a high-impedance node without loading it. It dissipates far less power than the output transistors and may share their thermal-bias network.' WHERE id='d90b6959-4818-4b90-bd5d-9628e38635b8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A push-pull stage is an output or driver configuration using two active devices that conduct on opposite half-cycles of the signal, one sourcing current into the load while the other sinks it. This symmetrical arrangement cancels even-order harmonic distortion and the standing DC component, and yields far greater output power and efficiency than a single-ended stage built from the same devices. Push-pull stages may be biased in class A, class B, or the near-universal class AB, and are realized with complementary or quasi-complementary transistor pairs or with a phase-splitting transformer.' WHERE id='47636f5a-e65d-424c-9585-7a69952b2f6c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A PWM controller is an integrated circuit that regulates a switch-mode power converter by varying the duty cycle of a pulse train that drives the power switch, comparing a feedback sample of the output against a reference and adjusting the on-time each cycle to hold the output constant. It typically contains an oscillator, an error amplifier, a PWM comparator, and a gate-drive output, and commonly adds current limiting, soft-start, and undervoltage lockout. Voltage-mode and current-mode variants differ in whether the ramp compared with the error signal is the oscillator ramp or a sensed inductor-current signal.' WHERE id='90fc9df6-f7c3-43b7-83db-a6c34ceae5d7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A quad op amp is an integrated circuit that contains four independent operational amplifiers sharing common power-supply pins in a single package. Each amplifier has its own inverting input, non-inverting input, and output, allowing four gain, filter, or buffer functions to be built compactly with well-matched characteristics and a reduced parts count. Common examples are supplied in a 14-pin package, and because the supply pins are shared all four amplifiers operate from the same rails.' WHERE id='98ae85b2-e806-44da-9949-db53157d602b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A rail-to-rail op amp is an operational amplifier whose input common-mode range, output voltage swing, or both extend to within a few millivolts of the positive and negative supply rails. Rail-to-rail output stages use a complementary common-source topology instead of emitter followers so the output can approach each rail, while rail-to-rail inputs typically combine complementary differential pairs to keep the amplifier operating across the full supply range. The feature is valued in low-voltage, single-supply systems where preserving usable signal range is critical.' WHERE id='e979fe76-9f8e-40c0-8605-409ed5a58846' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The safe operating area is the region on a transistor''s voltage-versus-current plane, usually plotted on logarithmic axes, within which the device can operate without damage. Its boundaries are set by the maximum current, the maximum voltage, the maximum power dissipation, and, for bipolar devices, a second-breakdown limit that further restricts allowable current at high collector-emitter voltage; the area contracts as the conduction time lengthens toward continuous DC. Designers must keep the instantaneous operating point inside this region under all load and fault conditions, particularly with reactive loads that force high voltage and current simultaneously.' WHERE id='57a4d568-1a2e-4852-b017-fa8f0a873b9f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A sample rate converter is a device or integrated circuit that converts a digital audio stream from one sampling frequency to another while preserving the signal, computing output samples at the new rate by interpolation and filtering of the input samples. An asynchronous sample rate converter accommodates source and destination clocks that are unrelated in frequency and phase by continuously estimating the instantaneous ratio between them and updating a time-varying interpolation filter, which additionally attenuates input clock jitter. Such converters interface equipment running on independent clocks and translate between standard rates such as 44.1 and 48 kHz.' WHERE id='b678ba2c-4ed1-49ee-a371-8dac0e85848d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Schmitt trigger is a comparator circuit with positive feedback that exhibits hysteresis, switching its output to one state when the input rises above an upper threshold and back only when the input falls below a distinct, lower threshold. The gap between the two thresholds prevents multiple transitions and false triggering when a slowly varying or noisy input crosses the switching point, cleanly squaring such signals into well-defined logic levels. It is implemented with op amps or comparators plus a feedback resistor network, or as a dedicated logic gate with built-in hysteresis.' WHERE id='c0132350-801b-4eb6-9323-96f44425d477' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Secondary breakdown is a destructive failure mode of bipolar power transistors in which localized current concentration creates hot spots that thermally run away, causing an abrupt collapse of collector-emitter voltage and device destruction at power levels below the simple product of the voltage, current, and thermal ratings. Because current crowds into the hottest region and local current density rises with temperature, the effect imposes an additional boundary on the safe operating area that is most restrictive at high collector-emitter voltage. Power MOSFETs are largely immune, since the positive temperature coefficient of their on-resistance forces current to spread rather than concentrate.' WHERE id='48e3309f-be41-4146-a79d-a89cf9126771' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A shift register is a sequential digital circuit of cascaded flip-flops in which the stored bits are moved one position along the chain on each clock edge, so that data entered at one end appears at successive stages on successive clocks. Configurations include serial-in serial-out, serial-in parallel-out, parallel-in serial-out, and bidirectional or universal types, providing functions such as serial-to-parallel and parallel-to-serial conversion, temporary storage, and digital delay. Shift registers are fundamental building blocks in data communication and in expanding the input/output capacity of a controller.' WHERE id='534d0516-0208-4609-9f1a-66ecdb007d90' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A single op amp is an integrated-circuit package containing one operational amplifier, typically brought out with its two inputs, its output, and the two supply pins, and sometimes an offset-null pair. It contrasts with dual and quad packages that place two or four amplifiers in one body, and is chosen when only one amplifier is required or when a specialized, higher-performance part is offered only in single form. An 8-pin package is the traditional form for a single op amp.' WHERE id='d0849f83-926d-47cf-9a22-2dba27fa5fdb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A single-ended stage is an amplifier stage in which one active device, or a group acting as one, handles the entire signal waveform, conducting continuously and referenced to one supply rail through a load such as a resistor, a current source, or a transformer. Because the single device carries a standing quiescent current for the whole cycle it operates in class A, giving low distortion at the expense of poor efficiency and the generation of even-order harmonics that a push-pull stage would cancel. The term distinguishes such stages from push-pull configurations that divide the waveform between two devices.' WHERE id='e415a9f5-7723-46aa-bd89-17ea159b8492' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small-signal transistor is a transistor intended to amplify or switch low-power signals, rated for currents typically up to a few hundred milliamps and dissipation of a fraction of a watt, and optimized for gain, low noise, or high frequency rather than for power handling. Housed in small packages with relatively high thermal resistance, such devices populate the input and voltage-gain stages of amplifiers where signal levels are low. They are analyzed with small-signal models that linearize the device about its quiescent operating point.' WHERE id='7065742d-5636-419a-841e-bdd3698da1ec' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='SOA, the safe operating area, is the bounded region of simultaneous voltage and current within which a transistor can operate without damage, conventionally plotted on logarithmic current-versus-voltage axes. Its edges are formed by the maximum current, the maximum voltage, the constant-power dissipation limit, and, for bipolar devices, a second-breakdown limit, with the whole area shrinking as the conduction time lengthens toward DC. Keeping the operating point inside the SOA under worst-case load, especially reactive loads, is essential to device survival.' WHERE id='fe881f85-c7bf-4cc4-a6bd-103e2958144b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The source is one of the three principal terminals of a field-effect transistor: the electrode from which the majority carriers that form the channel current enter the device, electrons in an n-channel device and holes in a p-channel device. The gate-to-source voltage is the controlling variable that sets the channel''s conductivity, and in a common-source stage the source is usually the terminal referenced to the supply return. It is the field-effect counterpart of the emitter of a bipolar transistor.' WHERE id='23b05211-7396-4b74-ac89-eaf9e4e89acb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A summing amplifier is an operational-amplifier circuit that produces an output proportional to the weighted sum of several input voltages, formed by connecting each input through its own resistor to the inverting input, which feedback holds at a virtual ground. Because that node is a virtual earth, the input currents add without interacting and the output equals the negative sum of each input scaled by the ratio of the feedback resistor to that input''s resistor. It underlies audio mixing consoles, offset addition, and digital-to-analog conversion by weighted resistors.' WHERE id='cd5c156f-683e-4cf8-9588-9b5adb789aeb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A thermal tracking transistor is a transistor, often the sensing device of a bias-generating network such as a VBE multiplier, that is mounted in intimate thermal contact with the output devices or their heatsink so that its junction temperature follows theirs. As the output transistors heat and their base-emitter voltages fall, the sensing transistor''s own base-emitter voltage falls in step, reducing the bias spreading voltage to hold the output-stage quiescent current stable and prevent thermal runaway. Accurate compensation depends on close thermal coupling and reasonably matched thermal time constants between the sensor and the devices it protects.' WHERE id='4394fe57-5d4f-4b1e-bfe6-eb66a9c59a08' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transconductance amplifier is an amplifier whose output current is proportional to its input voltage, its gain expressed as a transconductance in siemens rather than as a dimensionless ratio. Ideally it presents infinite input impedance and infinite output impedance, so the output current is set only by the input voltage and is independent of the load, making it a voltage-controlled current source. It is one of the four canonical amplifier types and is embodied in devices such as the operational transconductance amplifier.' WHERE id='9e2d242a-1f76-4609-8100-e93ab70b9294' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transistor is a three-terminal semiconductor device in which a voltage or current applied to a control terminal modulates the current flowing between the other two terminals, providing amplification or switching. In the bipolar junction transistor a small base current controls a much larger collector current through two coupled p-n junctions, while in the field-effect transistor the voltage on an insulated or junction gate controls the conductivity of a channel between source and drain. As the fundamental active device of modern electronics, the transistor underlies both analog amplification and digital logic.' WHERE id='371f9b03-af0f-45cb-8adf-426fb7595988' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transistor junction is a metallurgical p-n boundary between oppositely doped regions within a bipolar transistor, of which there are two: the emitter-base junction and the collector-base junction. In normal active operation the emitter-base junction is forward biased to inject carriers into the base while the collector-base junction is reverse biased to collect them, and the exponential current-voltage law of the forward-biased junction sets the roughly 0.6 to 0.7 volt base-emitter drop of a silicon device. Junction properties such as built-in potential, depletion capacitance, and reverse leakage govern the transistor''s biasing, speed, and temperature behavior.' WHERE id='f4e768c1-d545-42b1-95d5-03930ae3689a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Transistor substitution is the practice, in servicing and design, of replacing a transistor with a different part number that matches the original''s critical parameters closely enough to function correctly in circuit. The substitute must at minimum share the same polarity and device type and meet or exceed the breakdown voltages, current and power ratings, and, where relevant, the current gain, transition frequency, and package or pinout; cross-reference guides and generic replacement lines help identify candidates. Careless substitution that overlooks a parameter such as safe operating area or gain can degrade performance or cause failure.' WHERE id='827baec9-0287-4cfc-9335-adc4060edffe' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A tube equalizer is an audio equalizer whose gain and make-up stages are built around vacuum tubes, shaping a signal''s frequency balance through passive or active filter networks with valve amplification restoring the resulting loss. In classic passive designs a reactive filter section sits between valve stages, so the tubes provide gain and buffering while the tone shaping is performed by the surrounding inductors, capacitors, and resistors, imparting the harmonic character associated with valve circuitry. Such units are valued in recording and mastering for their sonic signature as much as for their corrective function.' WHERE id='5576a65b-e630-4fea-8bc8-b5e0f1e7fb49' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A tube limiter is a dynamics processor that constrains a signal''s peak level using vacuum-tube gain stages together with a valve- or optically-controlled gain element, so that increases in input above a threshold produce little or no further increase in output. According to the design, the gain reduction is achieved by varying the bias of a remote-cutoff pentode, by a variable-mu principle, or by an optical attenuator followed by tube make-up gain, and the valve circuitry contributes a characteristic saturation and harmonic coloration. Such limiters are used to control peaks and add warmth in recording, broadcast, and mastering.' WHERE id='0ea1fc01-fbf6-495f-98e9-ee9c456e39a5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A USB audio controller is an integrated circuit that implements the interface between a USB host and audio converters, enumerating as a USB Audio Class device and transporting digital audio streams isochronously across the bus in both directions. It manages USB protocol handling, endpoint buffering, clock recovery or generation for the audio sample clock, and typically connects to codecs or ADC and DAC devices over a serial audio interface such as I2S. Class-compliant controllers operate with the host''s built-in drivers, allowing an interface to work without custom software.' WHERE id='037e4164-5e1a-49d7-a59b-5f39670b244c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The voltage-amplifier stage, or VAS, is the second gain stage of the conventional three-stage power-amplifier topology, sitting between the input differential pair and the output stage and supplying almost all of the amplifier''s open-loop voltage gain. It is usually built as a common-emitter transistor with a current-source or bootstrap collector load, often enhanced by cascoding or a beta-boosting buffer, and it develops a large voltage swing across a high-impedance node where the dominant-pole compensation capacitor connects. Its linearity and the compensation applied there largely set the amplifier''s high-frequency distortion and stability.' WHERE id='a9ca936a-bfb1-45f0-a09a-f9f027137b02' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A VBE multiplier is a transistor bias circuit that generates an adjustable, temperature-dependent voltage equal to a multiple of a transistor''s base-emitter voltage, used to set the quiescent bias spreading voltage across the bases of a push-pull output stage. A resistor divider from collector to base to emitter forces the transistor to conduct just enough to hold its collector-emitter voltage at the base-emitter voltage times the ratio of the total divider resistance to the emitter-side resistance, and because that reference scales with the device''s own base-emitter voltage it falls as temperature rises. Mounting the transistor on the output-stage heatsink lets the falling reference track the output devices and stabilize their bias current against thermal runaway.' WHERE id='5c75abc1-defe-4bb9-bd9f-c3509c830f9d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A voltage follower is a unity-gain amplifier whose output voltage tracks its input, providing no voltage gain but substantial current gain to buffer a high-impedance source from a low-impedance load. Implemented as an op amp with its output fed directly back to the inverting input, or as a single-transistor emitter or source follower, it presents very high input impedance and very low output impedance so that connecting a load does not disturb the source. It is used to isolate stages and prevent loading errors.' WHERE id='48d7ddee-1ede-4c2b-b39b-c0e507c0be65' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A voltage-amplifier stage is an amplifier stage whose purpose is to produce voltage gain, developing a large output voltage swing from a small input signal, in contrast to a current-gain output stage that swings little voltage but delivers current. In the classic three-stage power amplifier it is the middle stage between the input transconductance pair and the output followers, realized as a high-gain common-emitter or common-source stage with a high-impedance load, and it carries the dominant compensation capacitor that sets the closed-loop bandwidth. Because it supplies most of the open-loop gain, its own linearity strongly influences overall distortion.' WHERE id='e3a32ea3-0cd8-4978-b603-600e4ed8b6dd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A cloud-based, machine-learning speech-enhancement service (offered through Adobe Podcast and integrated into Premiere Pro and Audition) that reprocesses recorded dialogue to attenuate background noise and reverberation while re-synthesising the voice so it approximates a clean, close-miked studio recording. It behaves as a generative restoration model rather than a purely subtractive filter, reconstructing masked or degraded speech components, and is applied chiefly to podcast, interview, and video-dialogue material captured in acoustically poor conditions.' WHERE id='ad2bc716-2609-4940-afb6-d94f66e0afea' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In machine-learning source separation and restoration, the reduction of processing-induced artefacts—signal components absent from the clean target that are introduced by the algorithm itself, such as musical noise, spectral holes, transient smearing, or warbling tonal ''birdies''. Objective evaluation isolates this quantity through the artefacts-related metric SAR (Sources-to-Artifacts Ratio) within the BSS Eval / SI-SDR framework, and suppression strategies include perceptually weighted masking, over-suppression penalties in the training loss, and post-filtering that trades residual interference against added artefacts.' WHERE id='c1f55b78-f760-40f9-ad46-fe9cec31f176' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A machine-learning task that reconstructs a high-sampling-rate, wide-bandwidth signal from a band-limited, low-sampling-rate input by predicting the missing high-frequency content, directly analogous to image super-resolution. Approaches range from time-domain convolutional networks that upsample the waveform to spectral and diffusion-based generative models; performance is reported with measures such as log-spectral distance and signal-to-noise ratio against the full-band reference.' WHERE id='66ca77c6-1ee1-453c-a7fc-28e2ed5f0b53' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The use of learned models to increase the effective sampling rate of a signal by generating plausible high-frequency detail, as opposed to classical polyphase or interpolation upsampling, which only inserts a band-limited, zero-stuffed spectrum. Because linear upsampling cannot create energy above the original Nyquist limit, ML upsampling is functionally a form of audio super-resolution or bandwidth extension in which a neural network infers the missing upper spectrum consistent with the observed low-band content.' WHERE id='d92a038c-a54b-415b-a04c-289bc1e1d72e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The recovery or synthesis of frequency content outside the band present in a degraded signal—most commonly regenerating the high band lost to low-rate capture, codec band-limiting (as in narrowband telephony), or lowpass filtering. Classical parametric methods such as Spectral Band Replication transpose and shape existing low-band energy, whereas modern machine-learning approaches train networks to predict the missing band from the observed spectrum; the term also covers reconstruction of absent low-frequency content.' WHERE id='6ea5d9a8-a670-4d4a-89ce-6ee04bb06c70' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The isolation of the bass stem—the low-frequency instrumental line, typically electric or upright bass—from a mixed music recording using source-separation models. In the standard four-stem task (vocals, drums, bass, other) established by the MUSDB18 benchmark, a dedicated bass target is estimated by architectures such as Open-Unmix, Spleeter, and Demucs, with quality reported as signal-to-distortion ratio (SDR) against the isolated reference stem.' WHERE id='01aac910-dd0a-463e-a093-315e7214fbca' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In audio restoration and separation workflows, the evaluative comparison of a signal''s characteristics before and after processing, used to judge the benefit of a treatment and to detect newly introduced artefacts. It combines gain-matched perceptual A/B (bypass) auditioning and spectrogram comparison with objective improvement metrics—most commonly the increase in SDR or SI-SDR between the degraded input and the processed output relative to a reference, reported as SDR improvement (SDRi).' WHERE id='ed351ec9-e09b-4e6e-b710-1d1debf8059d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The attenuation of noise spread continuously across the spectrum—tape hiss, ventilation rumble, preamp and thermal hiss, or wind—rather than energy concentrated at discrete frequencies. Distinguished from narrowband techniques such as de-hum that target tonal components, broadband denoisers estimate a time-frequency noise profile or, in machine-learning systems, a learned per-bin spectral gain; classical spectral-subtraction variants of this kind are prone to musical noise, which learned suppressors are designed to avoid.' WHERE id='c04f23a6-11e4-4720-8164-b3320ec9ee95' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A real-time, neural-network-based dereverberation plug-in in the Waves Clarity Vx family that separates a speech or vocal signal from its reverberant field and provides continuous control over the amount of reverberation removed. Built on a deep-learning model trained to distinguish direct-path voice from early and late reflections, it is used in dialogue post-production, broadcast, and vocal production to reduce room reverberation with fewer artefacts than classical inverse-filtering de-reverb.' WHERE id='39c6450f-e57b-41b9-bbde-3b1fdd7a6bff' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The repair of clipped audio, in which samples that exceeded the recording chain''s or converter''s headroom were flattened at a fixed maximum level, destroying waveform peaks and generating harmonic distortion. Restoration detects clipped regions (runs of samples pinned at or near full scale) and reconstructs the missing peak trajectories by constrained interpolation or, in machine-learning systems, by a generative model that infers plausible unclipped waveforms consistent with the surrounding signal.' WHERE id='f084295a-7e47-454f-bac9-1b68815fec36' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The signal-reconstruction problem of estimating the original, unclipped waveform from a hard- or soft-clipped observation in which amplitude information above the clipping threshold has been lost. It is formulated as a constrained inverse or inpainting problem—the reconstruction must exceed the clip level within clipped regions while matching the observed samples elsewhere—and is solved with sparsity-based optimisation, autoregressive interpolation, or trained neural networks.' WHERE id='77dac7cd-6899-4d0e-b554-58aa9bb66aab' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Restoration processing that detects and removes short impulsive defects—clicks, ticks, and crackle from vinyl surface damage, digital dropouts, or edit discontinuities—which appear as brief broadband spikes. Detection typically flags samples poorly predicted by a local autoregressive model, after which the corrupted samples are excised and replaced by interpolation from the surrounding waveform.' WHERE id='d0f6afa6-a5c7-48d1-bf3c-6c4c05b7a83a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Removal of steady tonal interference at the AC mains fundamental (50 or 60 Hz) and its harmonics, together with related buzz, arising from ground loops and electromagnetic coupling. It is implemented with tuned notch or comb filtering locked to the fundamental and its harmonic series, or with adaptive and learned models that track a slowly varying hum profile, distinguishing it from broadband denoising by its narrowband, harmonic target.' WHERE id='8c41d595-79d1-4c07-88f1-9752697bf7a7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Processing that reduces the reverberation—early reflections and the diffuse late-reverberation tail—in a recording so as to raise the direct-to-reverberant ratio and increase perceived proximity and intelligibility. Modern implementations use deep neural networks trained to separate the direct-path signal from its convolved room response, superseding classical spectral-enhancement and inverse-filtering approaches based on the room impulse response.' WHERE id='bea17624-4a61-457f-b34b-ef890d8765b7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A class of deep-learning speech-enhancement models that suppress non-stationary background noise from a noisy speech signal in real time, and the name of the recurring INTERSPEECH/ICASSP DNS Challenge that standardised training and test data and evaluation for the task. Typical systems predict a time-frequency mask or complex spectral gain with recurrent or convolutional networks, and are assessed with intrusive and perceptual metrics such as PESQ, STOI, and DNSMOS.' WHERE id='5ad597d4-e17a-40c1-8339-b5012802ee3a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The application of trained neural networks to remove additive noise from speech, learning a mapping from noisy to clean speech (or a masking function) from large paired corpora rather than relying on hand-tuned statistical estimators. Common formulations regress an ideal ratio mask or complex mask in the STFT domain or operate directly on the waveform; performance is measured with SI-SDR/SNR improvement and perceptual metrics such as PESQ and STOI.' WHERE id='70bb3b38-75fa-452b-8ae1-2e68b880c6f3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The signal-processing task of removing or suppressing the effect of room acoustics from a signal captured at distance, recovering an estimate of the anechoic or direct source. Formally it seeks to invert or attenuate the convolution of the source with the room impulse response; established methods include multichannel linear prediction (Weighted Prediction Error, WPE) and, more recently, supervised deep-learning models, with results reported using measures such as cepstral distance, SRMR, and PESQ.' WHERE id='592ed3d3-7d93-4ffb-880f-1d89caf4dd5a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Source separation applied to production and location sound to extract intelligible speech from a mixture also containing background noise, music, ambience, and effects. Realised with deep-learning separation and enhancement networks trained on speech-plus-noise mixtures, it is a core film and broadcast post-production operation, related to the speech-versus-non-speech targets used in cinematic source-separation research.' WHERE id='5e711da4-5a94-49e2-808c-306d692dc616' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Restoration of signals degraded by nonlinear distortion—clipping, overloaded preamps or converters, or saturated media—in which the amplitude transfer has become nonlinear, adding spurious harmonics and intermodulation products. Repair estimates and inverts the distortion characteristic or reconstructs the undistorted waveform through inpainting/declipping or learned models, as distinct from removing additive noise.' WHERE id='d999370b-69c5-4df6-8c65-6ce4831f297d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Isolation of the drums or percussion stem from a musical mixture using source separation, one of the four canonical targets (vocals, drums, bass, other) in the MUSDB18 benchmark. Percussive sources are estimated by architectures such as Spleeter, Open-Unmix, and Demucs; the broad percussive/harmonic split is also addressed by harmonic-percussive source separation, with quality reported as SDR against the isolated drum reference.' WHERE id='2e88d94f-30da-49db-a70c-c2260e2783d9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Correction of flutter, the rapid (roughly above 4 Hz) cyclic pitch and time modulation imparted by mechanical instability in tape and disc transports, such as capstan or roller eccentricity and speed irregularity. Correction estimates the instantaneous speed variation—often from a recorded reference tone or from pitch tracking of stable spectral components—and resamples the signal onto a corrected time base to cancel the modulation; it is distinguished from wow by its higher modulation rate.' WHERE id='0f6614ef-f569-43cd-bfdb-152a456cf4b3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Restoration carried out in the frequency (spectral) domain, in which localized defects are addressed on a time-frequency representation—attenuating, notching, or reconstructing energy in specific frequency regions—rather than on the time-domain waveform. It underlies spectral-editing and spectral-repair tools that interpolate or resynthesise damaged spectrogram areas from surrounding content, and is complementary to time-domain repair of short impulsive defects.' WHERE id='30c3c769-7740-4cbd-8de2-2835bd7696d5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The isolation of a specific instrument''s contribution from a polyphonic mixture via source separation. It generalises the fixed four-stem music-separation task to arbitrary or user-specified targets, addressed by query- or label-conditioned separation models and by dedicated per-instrument networks; separation quality is reported as SDR/SI-SDR against the isolated instrument reference.' WHERE id='bceafe07-eab8-4643-b506-d2615d6ce94f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The hands-on correction of audio by directly manipulating its spectrogram: an operator selects a time-frequency region and attenuates, gains, replaces, or interpolates the energy within it to remove a discrete sound event—a cough, phone ring, or squeak—with minimal effect on overlapping wanted signal. It is the interactive counterpart to automatic restoration and is the core function of spectral-editing tools such as iZotope RX''s Spectral Repair.' WHERE id='b8620719-bef9-4c5d-808f-f0ed4cbd2472' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Analysis or synthesis that represents a signal at several time-frequency resolutions simultaneously, trading time localisation against frequency localisation to suit transient versus tonal content. In machine-learning audio separation and restoration it appears as multi-resolution STFT losses (summing spectral error over several window sizes) and as multi-band, wavelet, or filterbank architectures, improving both transient fidelity and low-frequency accuracy.' WHERE id='ef450d44-08ac-41c8-a7fe-42e8fb8e5942' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Source separation that extracts the musical component from a mixture—for example separating a background score from speech and effects in a soundtrack—or, conversely, removing music to leave speech. In cinematic audio it is one target of speech/music/effects separation, addressed with deep-learning separators and evaluated as SDR/SI-SDR against reference stems.' WHERE id='cc2f3ff0-1f14-4d33-8c92-01414e423b67' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Mitigation of musical noise, the characteristic artefact of spectral-subtraction-type denoisers in which randomly surviving isolated time-frequency peaks appear and disappear, producing warbling tonal ''birdie'' sounds. Suppression techniques include over-subtraction with spectral flooring, decision-directed a-priori SNR estimation, smoothing across time and frequency, and learned masks that inherently avoid the isolated-peak pattern.' WHERE id='d078ac76-3265-4bd8-8827-1d9d4124210e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In the AI-audio context, the software removal of unwanted background sound from a recorded or streamed signal using trained neural networks that estimate and mask or subtract the noise component, leaving the target voice or music. It is distinct from acoustic active noise cancellation, which cancels sound physically with an anti-phase signal; AI ''noise cancellation'' operates on the captured signal in the digital domain, as in real-time communication denoisers.' WHERE id='82c9834c-37d8-4561-ac01-c7d6f7244fbd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The attenuation of the noise component of a noisy signal to improve intelligibility or quality, typically formulated as estimating a time-varying gain (real mask) or complex mask applied per time-frequency bin so that noise-dominated bins are suppressed and speech-dominated bins preserved. It spans classical statistical estimators (Wiener, MMSE-STSA) and modern deep-learning suppressors, and is the umbrella task behind real-time communication denoisers.' WHERE id='2964305a-fc20-4d20-92f3-3264da922e0d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Isolation of the piano part from a musical mixture using source separation. Because pianos fall outside the standard vocals/drums/bass stems, they are handled through the ''other'' stem or by target- and label-conditioned separation and instrument-specific models; datasets such as Slakh provide piano references, with quality reported as SDR against the isolated piano stem.' WHERE id='8f083dc8-5405-4f8f-8175-f7e83df9b124' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Correction of unwanted pitch instability in a recording—slow drift (wow), rapid modulation (flutter), or overall speed error—by estimating the time-varying pitch or speed deviation and resampling the signal onto a stable time base. In music restoration it locks sustained tones or a reference pitch to remove analogue-transport modulation, and is the pitch-domain complement to speed correction.' WHERE id='e4ff00e7-385a-4e41-8abe-d7a38fbdfd33' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Noise reduction performed on a time-frequency (spectrogram) representation using machine learning: a network estimates a per-bin suppression gain or mask from the noisy magnitude or complex spectrum and applies it before inverse transformation. Compared with fixed spectral subtraction it adapts to non-stationary noise and is trained to avoid musical-noise artefacts.' WHERE id='a0a99e49-f57b-49c4-9c9e-6fde1acdd84b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A restoration operation that repairs a localized damaged region of the spectrogram by synthesising replacement content from the surrounding time-frequency neighbourhood, analogous to a ''healing brush'' in image editing. It is a form of audio inpainting: missing or corrupted spectral areas are filled by interpolation or model-based prediction so the reconstruction blends seamlessly with adjacent material.' WHERE id='503d44c6-f9ea-43de-85c5-fb6310c4b200' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A spectral-editing capability that reconstructs a selected time-frequency region of a recording by attenuating it, replacing it with surrounding content, or interpolating across it, used to remove discrete unwanted events (coughs, clicks, transient noises) that overlap wanted signal. It formalises audio inpainting in the STFT domain and is the defining function of iZotope RX''s Spectral Repair module, which offers attenuate, replace, and interpolate modes.' WHERE id='3f0fbca0-32c3-47ec-af85-92d1104c933c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The broad field concerned with improving the quality and/or intelligibility of a speech signal degraded by additive noise, reverberation, or other distortion, by estimating the clean speech from the observation. It encompasses statistical estimators (spectral subtraction, Wiener filtering, MMSE-STSA) and modern deep-learning methods, evaluated with intrusive metrics such as PESQ and STOI and, for separation-style tasks, SI-SDR.' WHERE id='de26c3d5-382f-4339-ae41-382c6b031c42' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Correction of an incorrect or drifting playback speed in transferred analogue recordings, where a wrong capstan speed or transport instability shifts pitch and tempo together. It is performed by resampling to a target reference—such as a known mains-frequency bias tone, tuning pitch, or nominal transport speed—restoring correct pitch and duration; a sustained speed error is a constant ratio, whereas wow and flutter are its time-varying forms.' WHERE id='b7dde34c-33d4-4c69-8eab-b06ac8437ea3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Restoration operations carried out directly on the sampled waveform rather than on a spectral representation—detecting and reconstructing corrupted samples through autoregressive interpolation, declipping, or click concealment. It is well suited to short impulsive defects (clicks, dropouts, clipped peaks) whose extent is compact in time, complementing frequency-domain (spectral) repair for tonal or spectrally localized problems.' WHERE id='22dbd27e-c20a-4a47-81fa-5f5646d163c1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Isolation of the lead-vocal stem from a musical mixture via source separation, the vocals target of the standard four-stem (vocals/drums/bass/other) MUSDB18 task. It is estimated by architectures such as Open-Unmix, Spleeter, and Demucs and their MDX-Net successors, with separation quality reported as SDR/SI-SDR against the isolated vocal reference; it is widely used for remixing, karaoke, and sampling.' WHERE id='caedf131-d438-435e-a055-3dc1bc761f0f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The separation of vocals from the accompanying instrumental mix so the voice can be soloed, or inversely removed to yield an instrumental. Functionally equivalent to vocal extraction, it is achieved with deep-learning music-source-separation models that estimate a vocal mask or waveform, and underpins karaoke generation, a cappella extraction, and rebalancing tools.' WHERE id='2ec5c6c3-28c0-48cb-9d27-a66496ede06c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Correction of wow, the slow (roughly below 4 Hz) cyclic pitch and speed modulation caused by transport irregularities such as an off-centre disc hole or capstan and turntable speed variation. It estimates the low-frequency speed-deviation curve—from a steady reference tone or from pitch tracking of stable harmonics—and resamples onto a corrected time base to cancel the wandering pitch; it is distinguished from flutter by its lower modulation rate.' WHERE id='8460c7a9-c467-4210-a721-334a382ed81e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diffusion-based generative model for versatile audio super-resolution that predicts missing high-frequency content to upsample band-limited audio; it accepts inputs band-limited anywhere from roughly 2 kHz to 16 kHz and reconstructs full-band audio to a 24 kHz bandwidth at a 48 kHz sampling rate. Unlike earlier bandwidth-extension models restricted to a single content type, it robustly handles speech, music, and general sound effects and can act as a plug-and-play enhancement stage for other audio generators.' WHERE id='fdacb737-0a2f-403c-bb8f-043566aec68c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A web-based automated audio post-production service that applies a chain of processing—loudness normalisation to broadcast targets, adaptive levelling, filtering, noise and reverb reduction, and encoding—to speech-centric content such as podcasts. Its loudness processing conforms to the ITU-R BS.1770 / EBU R128 measurement standards, and its noise and reverb reduction stages use machine-learning models; it also generates metadata such as automatic transcripts and chapter marks.' WHERE id='9abd83a6-b660-4500-89df-e893ebcfa761' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A machine-learning restoration module (as in iZotope RX) that reduces reverberation specifically from spoken dialogue, separating the direct-path voice from room reflections and the late-reverberation tail to improve intelligibility and match perspective in film and broadcast post-production. It applies deep-learning dereverberation tuned for speech rather than a general inverse-filtering approach.' WHERE id='f83b4437-13b4-44d8-b64b-696811daf46b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A machine-learning module (as in iZotope RX) that separates intelligible dialogue from background noise and ambience in a single operation, using a neural network trained on speech-plus-noise mixtures to output an isolated-speech stem with an adjustable separation and noise balance. It is used in film and broadcast post-production to salvage location dialogue recorded in noisy environments.' WHERE id='e998accb-07d3-44d1-842e-dbe7b22a23f6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Accusonus ERA (Enhancement and Repair of Audio) bundle, a suite of simplified, largely single-control audio-repair plug-ins—including De-Noise, De-Reverb, De-Clip, De-Ess, De-Bleed, and Voice Leveler—that used machine-learning and source-separation algorithms to make common restoration tasks accessible to non-specialists. Accusonus was acquired by Meta in 2022, after which the ERA products were discontinued.' WHERE id='98c2db8d-87e6-4902-b62c-7dc0fe20ef40' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A digital guitar amplifier and effects processor built around Kemper''s Profiling technology, a proprietary system-identification technique that captures the full tonal and dynamic response of a reference tube amplifier—together with its cabinet and microphone—by exciting it with test signals and analysing the result, producing a ''Profile'' that reproduces the rig''s sound and feel. The captured Profile remains editable for gain, EQ, and cabinet, and each factory Profile is validated by A/B comparison against the original amplifier.' WHERE id='814c8a98-2370-426f-b38d-3447ade7a567' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A machine-learning module (in iZotope RX) that decomposes a finished stereo music mix into separated components—vocals, bass, drums/percussion, and other instruments—and lets the user independently raise or lower each, enabling remixing, level correction, or extraction and removal of a part (for example soloing or muting vocals) from a mix whose multitrack is unavailable. It is built on source-separation networks.' WHERE id='3b10a6ba-ffa9-4976-81c0-f01d8e5c1515' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Neural DSP''s proprietary technology (in the Quad Cortex) that uses a neural-network algorithm to learn and replicate the sonic and dynamic behaviour of a physical amplifier, overdrive, or cabinet from a captured reference, producing a model that responds to playing dynamics like the original device. It is the machine-learning counterpart to profiling-based amp modelling and runs on the Quad Cortex''s multi-core processing platform.' WHERE id='7c39f90e-37cd-4e90-abf3-64d8c16615f9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An NVIDIA application for content creators that applies real-time AI effects to microphone and camera streams on GeForce RTX GPUs, running deep-learning models on the GPU''s tensor cores. Its audio features include AI noise and echo/room removal that suppress background sound from the microphone (and can clean incoming audio); it succeeds the earlier RTX Voice utility and also provides video effects such as virtual background and auto-frame.' WHERE id='4b961009-0e7d-4dbd-9124-190047c4fb02' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A multi-core, floor-based guitar amp modeller and multi-effects processor from Neural DSP featuring a touchscreen and Neural Capture, its machine-learning system for cloning physical amplifiers, drives, and cabinets. Built on a multi-core DSP architecture, it runs amp and effect models together with user or shared captures simultaneously and hosts a large library of amps, effects, and impulse responses.' WHERE id='0b9c3c88-ae93-4a7b-8b30-49934e2ae52c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An assistive machine-learning module (in iZotope RX) that analyses a problem recording, automatically detects defects—noise, hum, clipping, clicks, and reverberation—and proposes a corrective processing chain with adjustable intensity settings, letting the user preview and apply multi-stage restoration without manually configuring each tool. It functions as an intelligent front end to RX''s individual repair modules.' WHERE id='050c5b48-78b7-4891-adf2-0f1bd052cd7b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A compact, real-time noise-suppression algorithm by Jean-Marc Valin that combines classical DSP with a small recurrent neural network: rather than operating on raw spectral bins, it computes suppression gains over 22 Bark-scale frequency bands using three GRU layers together with feature-based pitch analysis, retaining the conventional signal-processing structure while letting the network learn the noise-versus-speech decision. Designed for VoIP, it is efficient enough for real-time use on modest hardware.' WHERE id='92d132e6-6372-405c-9045-b8d5cb7d6087' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An earlier NVIDIA plug-in that removes background noise from microphone input, and optionally from incoming audio, in real time using a deep-learning model accelerated by the tensor cores on RTX GPUs. It exposed a virtual audio device that could be inserted into any communication or streaming application, and its functionality was later folded into the NVIDIA Broadcast app.' WHERE id='6391dae2-e2d9-4dea-bd1f-107ee428e1ea' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A machine-learning module (introduced in iZotope RX 10) that performs bandwidth extension on band-limited speech—such as audio captured over telephone or web-conference codecs that cut off around 4 to 8 kHz—by synthesising plausible high-frequency content up to roughly 20 kHz, restoring intelligibility and fullness to dialogue. It reconstructs the missing upper spectrum from the low-band content rather than merely boosting existing high frequencies.' WHERE id='e67efe41-10bc-4a47-b94a-beeae5ad6b1e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A denoising operation targeted at spoken-voice recordings that suppresses background noise—hiss, hum, ambience, and room tone—while preserving the intelligibility and timbre of the voice, typically implemented with a machine-learning speech-enhancement model that estimates a per-band suppression gain. It is the voice-specific case of noise suppression, tuned to protect speech formants and transients.' WHERE id='120948ef-345a-400c-8321-1254cc7b19db' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An instantaneous recording disc consisting of a rigid substrate, usually aluminium (glass during wartime metal shortages), surface-coated with a soft nitrocellulose lacquer plasticised with castor oil, into which an audio-modulated groove is engraved directly by a cutting stylus so the disc can be played back at once without further processing. Despite the colloquial name, the recording surface is cellulose nitrate lacquer rather than cellulose acetate; the soft coating permits real-time groove cutting but wears rapidly with repeated play and deteriorates chemically with age. Such discs were used for reference dubs, radio transcriptions, and mastering test cuts.' WHERE id='9f05f6c0-afb5-4e07-8579-b9644d046bba' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The pre-electric method of sound recording (roughly 1877 to 1925) in which acoustic energy radiated by performers is gathered by a collecting horn and coupled mechanically to a diaphragm whose vibration drives a cutting stylus, engraving a groove into a wax or foil medium with no electronic amplification. Because the entire signal chain is purely mechanical, sensitivity and usable bandwidth are severely restricted (approximately 250 Hz to 2.5 kHz) and performers had to crowd close to the horn. It was superseded by electrical recording using microphones and amplifiers.' WHERE id='bcdea3bf-4570-4d6f-9ca7-69863c25df0b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The preparation and transfer of a completed audio programme to the Compact Cassette format, including optimisation of record level, bias current and playback equalisation time constants (nominally 120 microseconds for ferric Type I and 70 microseconds for chrome and metal Types II and IV), any noise-reduction encoding such as Dolby B or C, and creation of a duplication master for high-speed slave copying. The goal is to fit the programme within the cassette''s limited dynamic range and high-frequency headroom while preserving tonal balance across the duplicated copies.' WHERE id='37f76cde-0561-4515-9615-c3f22e307d47' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The process of preparing final audio for the Compact Disc Digital Audio (Red Book) format: assembling the programme as 16-bit, 44.1 kHz linear PCM, defining track and index points and the subcode (PQ) data, applying final level control, limiting and dither, and generating a physical or file-based master (originally a photoresist glass master, later a Pre-Master CD or DDP image) from which stampers and replicated discs are produced.' WHERE id='91f33fef-6dad-43f6-b9e8-b882c71a3481' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The power amplifier that drives the electromagnetic cutting head of a disc-mastering lathe, delivering the current required to move the cutting stylus in proportion to the audio signal. It incorporates the recording (RIAA) pre-emphasis characteristic and the motional-feedback loop that stabilises the head''s motion, protecting the drive coils and controlling groove velocity and amplitude so the cut stays within safe mechanical and playback limits.' WHERE id='c1972817-9cac-489d-8a1d-cfedead98289' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The cutting tool mounted in the disc cutter head that engraves the modulated groove into a lacquer master, typically a sapphire or ruby (occasionally diamond) ground to a chisel or V-shaped tip. Its included tip angle and burnishing facets define the groove-wall angle (nominally 90 degrees) and surface finish, and electrical heating of the tip (the hot-stylus technique) reduces surface noise by producing a cleaner cut.' WHERE id='2808a061-d554-4c3c-ac5f-a5b35ab6417e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A stylus tipped with shaped diamond, the hardest practical gem material, ground to a defined tip radius and profile (spherical, elliptical, line-contact, and similar) for tracing the record groove with minimal wear and distortion. Its extreme hardness gives service life far exceeding that of a sapphire tip, making it the standard for quality playback styli.' WHERE id='832e5cb6-a676-4b1b-9193-8553117f4b87' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The preparation and transfer of programme material to the Digital Audio Tape (R-DAT) format, a rotary-head digital cassette that records 16-bit linear PCM at sampling rates of 48, 44.1 or 32 kHz and was widely used as a mastering and delivery medium. It includes level setting, insertion of start-ID and program-number subcodes, and observance of the sampling-rate and Serial Copy Management System conventions defined for the format.' WHERE id='affe5a28-192b-434d-9d99-dd51efc0a4dc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The era, beginning in the late 1970s, in which audio is captured, stored and processed as discrete numerical samples (initially linear PCM, later other coded formats) rather than as continuous analogue magnetisation or groove modulation. It encompasses digital multitrack recorders, the Compact Disc, and ultimately computer- and disk-based recording, characterised by editing without generation loss and error-corrected data storage.' WHERE id='8858971b-1316-4fd3-a969-61559c34f8f2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A one-bit, highly oversampled coding scheme developed by Sony and Philips for the Super Audio CD, in which the waveform is represented as a stream of single-bit pulse-density-modulated samples produced by a sigma-delta modulator running at 2.8224 MHz (64 times the CD sampling rate), rather than as multi-bit PCM words. The high sampling rate pushes quantisation noise well above the audio band, where it is removed by noise shaping and low-pass filtering.' WHERE id='c86e06a7-8cbd-4b6a-91fe-2b2dc4b634a5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A disc-mastering technique in which the live performance is mixed in real time and cut straight onto the lacquer master with no intervening tape stage. Eliminating the tape recorder preserves full dynamic range and avoids tape generation losses and noise, but it requires a flawless performance for an entire disc side, since the cut cannot be edited or re-taken piecemeal.' WHERE id='f747aa4e-949c-4754-8260-aea581491c62' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The electromechanical transducer on a mastering lathe that converts the audio electrical signal into mechanical motion of the cutting stylus to engrave the groove. Modern stereophonic heads are moving-coil, motional-feedback devices with two independent drive systems oriented at plus and minus 45 degrees, cutting the two channels into the two groove walls of the 45/45 stereo system.' WHERE id='fcbe0ef8-5235-400e-baea-c4a83d3528eb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Sound recording using a microphone, electronic amplifier and electromagnetically driven cutting head, introduced commercially around 1925. By replacing the purely mechanical horn-and-diaphragm chain of acoustic recording, it greatly extended frequency range, dynamic range and sensitivity, and allowed microphones to be placed for musical balance independently of the cutting lathe.' WHERE id='dde0792a-5fb0-4a55-9d27-88312724ee29' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The act of engraving the modulated spiral groove into a lacquer or wax master with a cutting stylus driven by the cutter head. It requires simultaneous control of groove depth, the unmodulated land between adjacent turns, pitch (grooves per inch) and the lateral or vertical wall modulation that encodes the audio, keeping the cut within the geometric and level limits that allow reliable playback.' WHERE id='7c94d0c1-6568-47d5-a7e5-df6a3ed4e622' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The set of dimensional parameters describing a record groove, including the included wall angle (nominally 90 degrees), groove width and depth, bottom radius, and, for stereo, the 45/45 modulation geometry in which each wall carries one channel. These dimensions govern stylus fit and tracing distortion and must be matched between the cutting and playback processes for correct reproduction.' WHERE id='8ce3c60f-2724-4cf6-a907-98a5dd2f5c6b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The capture of digital audio directly to a computer hard-disk drive as sample data held in files, rather than to a linear medium such as tape. Because any point in the recording can be addressed immediately, hard-disk recording supports nonlinear, non-destructive editing and random-access playback, and forms the storage basis of the digital audio workstation.' WHERE id='d55f3dcc-0f90-48c0-a32f-f8f4690913a7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The blank disc used to cut an original master: a metal (or occasionally glass) substrate coated with a soft nitrocellulose lacquer, on which the mastering lathe engraves the first groove. This original is then silvered and electroplated to form the metal master, mother and stamper parts used to press production records; the terms lacquer disc and acetate are used interchangeably in practice.' WHERE id='b90e48e3-e231-425c-83c9-9a069821877c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The disc-mastering workflow of transferring a programme to a lacquer master on a cutting lathe. It involves applying the RIAA recording characteristic, controlling cutting level, and regulating groove pitch and depth, commonly through automatic variable-pitch and variable-depth systems fed by a preview (advance) head, so as to maximise playing time and level while keeping the cut playable; the resulting master is the source for plating and pressing.' WHERE id='618d0b92-59e5-4c4c-ab6a-89dba9b7b1e1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The era, from the 1930s and 1940s onward, in which audio is stored as patterns of remanent magnetisation on a moving medium, first steel wire and then coated magnetic tape. Tape''s ability to be edited by splicing, erased and re-recorded, and configured for overdubbing and multitrack work transformed recording practice and enabled modern studio production methods.' WHERE id='af7752e7-6600-4017-8111-ad50eb627d76' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A rewritable storage method, used by the MiniDisc and by magneto-optical data discs, in which a focused laser heats a magnetic recording layer above its Curie temperature while an external bias field sets the orientation of the heated domain, thereby writing data. Read-back is optical: the polarisation of reflected polarised light is rotated one way or the other by the magnetic domains through the polar Kerr effect, and this rotation is detected as the signal.' WHERE id='8886d910-d379-4fbc-8acd-a638266f64fc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The original lacquer disc cut on the mastering lathe from which the metal parts, the master (father), mothers and stampers, are electroformed for record pressing. It is the first physical carrier of the released programme''s groove and defines the cut that every pressed copy reproduces.' WHERE id='244283cc-494e-4b47-bd10-4171939c4b9d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Recording to Sony''s MiniDisc, a 64 mm disc housed in a cartridge that stores audio compressed with the ATRAC perceptual coder at a reduced bit rate. Recordable MiniDiscs use magneto-optical writing (laser heating with a magnetic bias field) and Kerr-effect read-back, while prerecorded discs are optical like a CD; a memory buffer provides shock resistance during playback.' WHERE id='35fb127e-a375-4ce4-9e27-3190e2264f63' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Recording sound onto motion-picture film as a photographic image running alongside the picture, in which the audio modulates either the width of an exposed area (variable-area) or the density of a uniformly wide track (variable-density). On playback the developed track is scanned by a light beam whose transmitted intensity, varying with the recorded modulation, is converted back to an electrical signal by a photocell.' WHERE id='837a6e23-f658-43aa-912a-cc549e283697' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Digital recording in which a band-limited analogue waveform is sampled at a fixed rate and each sample is quantised to a binary code word of fixed length, producing linear pulse-code modulation. PCM is the fundamental encoding underlying the Compact Disc, DAT and most professional digital audio, with sampling rate setting the bandwidth and word length setting the quantisation-noise floor and dynamic range.' WHERE id='198cb418-177d-4c56-a815-3f4f8018c356' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The flared acoustic collector used in acoustic-era recording to gather sound radiated by performers and concentrate it onto the diaphragm that drives the cutting stylus. Acting as an acoustic transformer between air and the mechanical cutting system, its length, mouth size and taper largely determined the sensitivity and the restricted frequency response of horn recording.' WHERE id='ad411885-5a27-472c-acc9-e74f679b7351' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A stylus tipped with synthetic sapphire (single-crystal corundum), used both as a heated cutting stylus for lacquer mastering and, historically, as a playback tip. Sapphire is far harder than steel and holds a precise cutting edge, but as a playback tip it wears faster and has a shorter service life than diamond.' WHERE id='6e710a6f-a679-46a6-974d-15bd2cc2a070' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The period of analogue audio production dominated by vacuum-tube (thermionic-valve) electronics in microphones, preamplifiers, mixing consoles, tape machines and disc-cutting equipment, before the general transition to solid-state devices. It is associated with characteristic transformer-coupled gain-stage topologies and the soft harmonic-distortion behaviour of tube circuits under overload.' WHERE id='9b0415a4-26ed-4064-8bfe-5a8f3c0db323' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A type of photographic film soundtrack in which the audio modulates the width of a transparent area whose boundary traces the waveform, so that the total area passing light varies with signal amplitude. On playback a fixed light beam scans the track and the transmitted intensity reproduces the signal; the variable-area format became the dominant optical soundtrack type.' WHERE id='bda55214-f27d-48e6-a69c-7411e407382a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A disc groove in which the signal is engraved as variation in groove depth, that is, vertical or hill-and-dale modulation, as used in cylinder recording and certain early disc systems, in contrast with lateral cutting where depth is nominally constant and modulation is side to side. In the 45/45 stereophonic disc a vertical (depth-varying) component is inherent, and depth must be controlled so the playback stylus remains properly engaged.' WHERE id='dcc7995b-e87f-49fc-bee9-3720924de1e8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A lathe technique in which the groove pitch, the spacing between adjacent turns, is varied automatically according to programme level and low-frequency content: spacing is widened for loud or bass-heavy passages to prevent adjacent grooves from overcutting into one another (echo or over-cut), and tightened during quiet passages to conserve space. It is driven by a preview or advance head that reads the signal ahead of the cut, maximising playing time while protecting the cut.' WHERE id='b96e1a65-d3e9-4c6d-93b0-69939f9f9198' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An early magnetic recording method that stores audio as longitudinal patterns of magnetisation along a thin, fast-moving steel or stainless-steel wire drawn past a ring recording head. Predating coated magnetic tape, it offered portability and re-recordability but limited fidelity and dynamic range, and was awkward to edit because splices had to be knotted or welded and the wire was prone to tangling.' WHERE id='e1172f1c-1f12-4ca3-9423-e9adaddc7ffa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An accessible mixing position is a front-of-house control location in an assembly or performance space designed so that a person with a mobility disability, including someone using a wheelchair, can reach and operate the sound and related controls. It is served by an accessible route and provides a level, adequately sized area with the clear floor space and reach ranges needed to work at the console, so that the mix position does not itself become a barrier to participation. The concept is applied under accessibility design requirements for assembly areas, which treat the operation of event equipment as a function that must be usable by disabled staff and volunteers.' WHERE id='65a05a37-a81c-46a4-9c84-0374ab9b7a3e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An accessible route is a continuous, unobstructed path connecting all the accessible elements and spaces of a building or site, so that a person with a disability can travel between them without encountering a barrier. Its components—walking surfaces, ramps, curb ramps, doorways, and elevators or platform lifts—must meet defined requirements for clear width, running and cross slope, surface firmness, changes in level, and passing and turning space. In venues it is the design basis that links entrances, seating, assistive-listening provision, and control positions, and it is a foundational requirement of accessibility standards.' WHERE id='6ef1e05b-0edf-46a4-8b19-3f393526c9e4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A hearing-loop system, or audio-frequency induction loop, delivers sound directly to hearing aids and cochlear-implant processors by driving a loop of wire installed around a listening area with a current representing the programme audio, which creates a modulated magnetic field within the looped space. A hearing aid switched to its telecoil setting picks up this field and reproduces the signal, giving the listener the wanted audio free of distance, reverberation, and background noise and without a separate receiver. Performance is standardized in terms of the magnetic field strength and frequency response the loop must produce and the telecoil sensitivity of the hearing aid, so that any compliant loop and aid interoperate.' WHERE id='5b945cf4-7652-477d-ab30-1008926e2bb7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Infrared assistive listening distributes sound to listeners by modulating the audio onto invisible infrared light radiated from emitter panels throughout a room and received by worn units that demodulate it and feed a headphone, neck loop, or hearing aid. Because infrared does not pass through opaque walls, the signal is naturally confined to the room, which both prevents interference between adjacent spaces and provides confidentiality, making it well suited to courtrooms, cinemas, and other settings where privacy or channel separation matters. Its dependence on line-of-sight light means coverage must account for shadowing by bodies and obstacles and for interference from strong ambient or sunlight.' WHERE id='46afa7d7-5356-4731-b76a-7046a5078c02' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Radio-frequency assistive listening distributes sound by modulating the programme audio onto a radio carrier broadcast from a stationary transmitter and received by portable body-worn units that feed a headphone, neck loop, or hearing aid; systems using the FM band are also called FM assistive listening. Radio waves pass through walls and around obstructions, giving wide, non-line-of-sight coverage suited to large or irregular venues and to outdoor use, at the cost that the signal can spill into neighbouring areas unless channels or reception are managed. Dedicated portions of the radio spectrum are reserved for auditory assistance so that these systems can operate with low interference.' WHERE id='0d313cd5-f404-41dd-b9f5-14fc2521805e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Audio archival restoration is the corrective processing applied to recordings held for their cultural or historical value, aimed at reducing the audible defects introduced by the original medium, its degradation, and the transfer process—clicks, crackle, hiss, hum, wow and flutter, and distortion—so as to improve intelligibility and fidelity while remaining faithful to the source. In the archival context it is subordinate to preservation ethics: the unaltered transfer of the carrier is kept as the preservation master, and any restoration is carried out on derivative copies, documented, and kept reversible so that no information present in the original is destroyed. It is distinguished from remastering by its goal of recovering the recording as it was rather than reinterpreting it for contemporary release.' WHERE id='3bb567ec-d732-4bf7-90c0-19fb4ea420cc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Audio preservation is the coordinated set of policies and actions that secure the long-term accessibility and integrity of sound recordings, encompassing the stabilization and storage of original carriers, their transfer to sustainable digital formats before they become unplayable, and the ongoing management of the resulting files. It centres on the preservation master—a complete, unmodified, best-possible copy of the source—together with checksums, technical and descriptive metadata, and controlled storage with regular integrity checking and migration to guard against media obsolescence and bit loss. Preservation is distinguished from restoration in that its objective is to safeguard the content and its context intact, not to improve how it sounds.' WHERE id='caaac413-b196-4ddb-88a4-a2d3e45532a5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Azimuth alignment is the adjustment of the angular relationship between the magnetic head gap and the direction of tape travel so that the gap is perpendicular to the tape edges, or, when replaying an archival tape, so that the reproduce-head azimuth matches the azimuth at which the tape was recorded. A mismatch rotates the effective gap relative to the recorded flux transitions, progressively delaying one part of the track relative to another across the head width and cancelling high frequencies, which produces loss of treble and, on stereo or multitrack material, phase and image errors. In archival transfer the reproduce azimuth is optimized per tape—commonly by maximizing high-frequency output and interchannel phase coherence on the actual recording—because historic tapes were often recorded on imperfectly aligned machines.' WHERE id='6c6a2750-6722-4e68-9d44-608e10058b97' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Broadcast archive restoration is the transfer and corrective treatment of legacy programme material held by broadcasters—magnetic tape, transcription and instantaneous discs, optical film tracks, and early digital carriers—undertaken to recover content that is at risk from media decay or format obsolescence. The work combines carrier stabilization and optimized replay with reduction of medium-specific defects such as dropouts, print-through, wow and flutter, mains hum, and disc surface noise, while conforming to preservation practice by retaining an unrestored transfer as the archival master. Because broadcast holdings are large and heterogeneous, the process typically follows documented, standardized workflows so that transfers are consistent, well described, and suitable for reuse.' WHERE id='dedbe9dd-0217-46a7-bda7-97a80692d286' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In archival audio transfer the capture chain is the complete signal path used to move the content of an original carrier into the digital domain, running from the playback machine and its transducer through any preamplification, equalization, and level control to the analogue-to-digital converter and the recording system. Each element is selected, aligned, and calibrated to add as little coloration, noise, and distortion as possible so that the digitized file faithfully represents the carrier, and the configuration—machine, stylus or head, replay curve, converter, and sample rate and bit depth—is documented as part of the transfer''s technical metadata. A correctly specified capture chain is a precondition for a valid preservation master, since defects introduced here cannot later be removed without loss.' WHERE id='b5682a8c-d90e-4e44-9624-01f98361a22d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Crackle removal is the suppression of the dense, near-continuous field of very short, low-level impulsive noises—crackle—that is characteristic of shellac and vinyl disc replay and of some optical and cylinder sources, as distinct from the sparser, louder events treated as clicks or pops. Restoration systems detect these many small transient events statistically and reconstruct the underlying waveform across each by interpolation from the surrounding samples, so that the impulses are replaced rather than merely attenuated. Because crackle overlaps the wanted signal in both time and frequency, effective processing balances thoroughness of detection against the risk of dulling transients or introducing artefacts.' WHERE id='184fcfba-54cc-40da-9a1e-fcc6c38fc6d7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='De-rustle processing is the reduction of the broadband, transient friction noise produced when a lavalier or body-worn microphone is disturbed by clothing movement, a defect common in field, broadcast, and film dialogue recordings. Because the rustle is a rapid succession of short, noise-like bursts that share the spectrum of speech, the processing detects these transient events and attenuates or reconstructs the affected time–frequency regions rather than applying a static broadband filter, so that the underlying dialogue is preserved. It is applied during dialogue cleanup and archival treatment where re-recording is impossible and the disturbance would otherwise remain within the wanted speech.' WHERE id='ccf9d532-c6ee-4614-bfde-11aebd14a2ad' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Harmonic reconstruction is a restoration technique that regenerates spectral content missing from a degraded recording by synthesizing harmonically related components derived from the material that survives, most often to restore high-frequency energy lost to band-limited media, aggressive noise reduction, or transmission and codec limitations. Rather than simply boosting an empty region with equalization, the process analyses the existing partials and generates new harmonics at the appropriate frequencies and relative levels so that timbre and brightness are perceptually restored while remaining consistent with the source. It is used cautiously in archival and dialogue work because fabricated content must plausibly extend, not contradict, the original signal.' WHERE id='a480e19a-20cb-4b22-9b34-301c7b8df99b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Master-tape restoration is the stabilization and corrective treatment of original analogue master tapes so that they can be played back safely and transferred with maximum fidelity. It addresses the physical failure modes of aged tape—most notably binder hydrolysis, or sticky-shed syndrome, which is commonly reversed temporarily by controlled low-temperature incubation, or baking, before transfer—along with cleaning, repair of splices and pack problems, and correction of replay speed and azimuth to match the original recording. The aim is a single, well-documented, best-possible transfer, since repeated playback of a fragile master risks irreversible shedding and loss.' WHERE id='453f76a1-2064-49d9-a810-6873ac5edbec' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Mouth-click removal is the elimination of the short, sharp transient sounds produced inside a speaker''s mouth—saliva clicks, lip smacks, and tongue noises—that are captured on close vocal and dialogue recordings. Detection targets these brief, isolated impulses by their transient character and their position between phonated speech, and each is repaired by interpolating or resynthesizing the waveform across the click so that the surrounding voice is unaffected. The technique is standard in dialogue editing, voiceover, and audiobook cleanup, where such noises are distracting yet cannot be removed by broadband filtering without dulling the voice.' WHERE id='772a5c49-620a-410e-9ac0-231210bcf245' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Tape oxide loss is the physical shedding or detachment of the magnetic pigment layer that carries the recorded signal from a tape''s backing, resulting in permanent dropouts, reduced output, and increased noise where coating is missing. It arises chiefly from deterioration of the polymer binder that holds the oxide to the base film—typically hydrolytic breakdown that weakens adhesion and cohesion—so that oxide is scraped away or flakes off during handling and playback. Because the loss removes recorded information outright, it cannot be restored by signal processing, and prevention through proper storage and careful, sometimes stabilized, replay is the only safeguard.' WHERE id='ae96a241-203c-41bd-8c19-1e4d21867025' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Tape restoration is the physical treatment of degraded magnetic tape to render it playable for transfer, distinct from the later cleaning of the recovered audio signal. It encompasses diagnosis and remediation of medium failures such as sticky-shed syndrome, addressed by controlled baking; cleaning of debris and contamination; repair of broken or failing splices; correction of poor tape pack and deformation; and careful rewinding, all so that the tape survives at least one high-quality pass through an aligned replay machine. The guiding principle is minimal, reversible intervention that stabilizes the carrier without destroying the recorded information.' WHERE id='748b4a61-f7b3-427f-9648-1bd717c1b33d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Vinyl restoration is the reduction of the characteristic defects of disc-record replay—clicks and pops from scratches and damage, continuous crackle and surface noise, low-frequency rumble, and worn-groove or end-of-side distortion—applied when transferring lacquer, shellac, and vinyl discs. Effective work begins with optimized playback, using the correct stylus profile and size, tracking, and replay equalization, and with cleaning of the disc, after which impulsive noises are detected and interpolated and broadband surface noise is attenuated with spectral techniques, in an order that avoids one stage aggravating another. Restoration follows preservation practice by retaining a flat, unrestored transfer and working on copies.' WHERE id='d862f8fd-53b5-4063-8231-66d8edef4959' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Wax-cylinder restoration is the recovery of audio from phonograph cylinders of wax and related materials, combining specialized replay with treatment of the extracted signal. Playback demands cylinder-specific equipment and an appropriately profiled stylus, correct rotational speed, and gentle handling of a soft, brittle, and often mould-affected or cracked medium; optical or other non-contact scanning is sometimes used where grooves are too fragile to track mechanically. The captured signal, which carries heavy surface noise, rumble, and a restricted, uneven frequency response, is then treated by de-noising and equalization, always retaining an unrestored preservation transfer because each play risks further wear of the original.' WHERE id='f574ade0-2ed5-4ede-b4cc-197d561acd98' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A silent disco is a dance event in which each attendee wears a wireless headphone receiver rather than the room being filled by a loudspeaker PA. One or more DJs transmit their mixes over radio-frequency links to the headphone receivers, and where several channels are provided each listener can switch between simultaneous DJs, so people at the same event may be dancing to different programmes at once. Because no acoustic sound is radiated into the room, the format sidesteps venue noise limits and neighbourhood curfews and lets multiple sound sources coexist without acoustic interference; typical implementations use multi-channel UHF or 2.4 GHz transmitters feeding colour-coded, channel-switchable receiver headsets.' WHERE id='68c4faa9-93bf-479e-a7aa-2366f332ca46' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In radio production, the station imaging voice is the recorded voice talent used to deliver a station''s imaging elements—the branded identifiers, promos, sweepers, liners, and legal identifications that establish the station''s on-air personality and are woven between programme segments and music. A single, consistent voice is chosen and used across these produced elements so that the station projects a recognizable sonic identity distinct from its presenters, and the recordings are typically finished with signature processing and music beds in the station''s imaging style. The role is a branding function rather than a programme-hosting one, and the same voice may be contracted to supply a station''s imaging remotely on an ongoing basis.' WHERE id='81dff517-dc50-48ee-abff-ad2733756fdf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A method of connecting two circuit nodes or amplifier stages through a series element, typically a capacitor (occasionally a transformer), that transmits the time-varying signal component while blocking the direct-current component. The series coupling capacitor together with the following stage''s input resistance forms a first-order high-pass filter whose -3 dB corner sets the low-frequency limit of transmission, and the arrangement allows adjacent stages to sit at different DC operating potentials without disturbing each other''s bias.' WHERE id='fbc21245-fb8e-418e-9f81-920cec9334e9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A network of bilateral analog switches, usually CMOS transmission gates, that connects one of several continuous-time input channels to a common output (or routes one input to one of several outputs) under digital address control. Because the switches are bidirectional the analog signal passes in either direction subject to a finite on-resistance and its variation with signal level, channel charge injection, and off-channel leakage and crosstalk; it is commonly used for channel selection ahead of a single analog-to-digital converter or for general signal routing.' WHERE id='0de0502f-3047-4a06-8d79-86c2bd99edd6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The complete chain of continuous-time analog blocks a signal traverses from input to output, such as an input buffer, one or more gain stages, filters, and an output driver, before any conversion to the digital domain. The cumulative gain structure, bandwidth, noise contributions, and distortion of the stages in this path collectively determine the end-to-end analog performance and available headroom.' WHERE id='f5b7eac5-b0b7-4cfa-91ff-739276bf87b0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated circuit implementing one or more voltage-controlled bilateral switches, typically CMOS transmission gates, that make or break an analog signal connection under logic control. Its defining parameters are on-resistance and its flatness versus signal voltage, off-isolation, charge injection, switching time, and signal bandwidth, and it serves as the building block for analog routing, muxing, and gain-switching functions.' WHERE id='66585b6a-6c59-4344-b5a2-6aaba1097720' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An electronic circuit designed to process signals within the audio band, nominally 20 Hz to 20 kHz, performing functions such as amplification, filtering, mixing, and buffering. To qualify as audio-grade it must meet requirements for low noise, low harmonic and intermodulation distortion, adequate headroom above the nominal operating level, and a controlled frequency response across the band.' WHERE id='71c53710-a398-437c-9361-8e064b1f4e83' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated circuit that provides the first stage of voltage amplification for a low-level audio source such as a microphone, phono cartridge, or instrument pickup, raising it toward line level. It is characterized by high available gain, very low input-referred noise, an input impedance appropriate to the source, and frequently a gain that is set or varied by an external resistor or a control input.' WHERE id='1f967128-e3f5-4a32-9440-f3e45e291ae3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The route an audio signal follows through the successive processing blocks of a system, from its input through amplification, equalization, mixing, and output stages. The term denotes the ordered sequence of stages and their interconnections, whose combined gain, noise, and distortion define how faithfully the audio is conveyed from source to output.' WHERE id='fb5358f3-c538-4226-b64d-ec83666d399a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A single functional amplifying or processing block within an audio circuit, such as an input stage, gain stage, or output stage, comprising one active device or amplifier together with its biasing and any local feedback. Each stage has its own gain, input and output impedance, and its individual contribution to the overall noise and distortion budget of the circuit.' WHERE id='b32863d7-8d35-4c6d-8a3d-92774582e8f6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A differential-input circuit at the receiving end of a balanced interconnection, typically built as a difference amplifier or instrumentation-amplifier topology, that amplifies the wanted differential signal while rejecting interference common to both conductors such as ground noise and induced electromagnetic pickup. Its common-mode rejection depends on close matching of the surrounding resistor ratios, and it converts the balanced signal to a single-ended output.' WHERE id='252542bb-79ef-4f96-922c-cec012cdca92' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An analog multiplying circuit that forms the product of a carrier and a modulating signal in such a way that both original inputs are suppressed at the output, leaving only the sum and difference (sideband) frequency components. It is realized either as a doubly-balanced diode ring or as a Gilbert-cell four-quadrant transconductance multiplier, and it produces double-sideband suppressed-carrier signals used in modulation and, in audio, in ring-modulation effects.' WHERE id='1e0732cb-105a-4735-9352-5a84692d285b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Abbreviation for bucket-brigade device, an analog sampled-data delay line formed by a cascade of storage capacitors interconnected by MOS switches driven from a two-phase clock. On each clock transition the charge packet representing a signal sample is passed from one stage to the next, so the total delay equals the number of stages divided by twice the clock frequency; because the output is a stepped, sampled waveform it requires anti-alias input filtering and reconstruction output filtering, and it is used for analog delay, chorus, and flanging.' WHERE id='b62975dc-c6ae-4dcb-911c-bd06e8be7480' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The DC operating point of an active device or amplifier stage established by its biasing network in the absence of any input signal, defined by the quiescent terminal currents and voltages such as collector current and collector-emitter voltage. This point fixes the device''s small-signal parameters and sets the symmetrical output swing available before clipping, and it is chosen for the required class of operation, linearity, and headroom.' WHERE id='da451497-8514-4464-a1d1-7db66a079d1a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An analog sampled-data delay line consisting of a chain of storage capacitors linked by MOS transfer switches operated from a two-phase clock, in which discrete charge packets representing successive signal samples are shifted from stage to stage on each clock edge. The delay is set by the number of stages and the clock rate, equalling the stage count divided by twice the clock frequency, and the stepped output must be band-limited on input and smoothed on output; it provides analog time delay for effects such as chorus, flanging, and echo.' WHERE id='8183c099-75b7-4692-bd22-085a0c55ab5e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An amplifier stage of unity or near-unity voltage gain presenting a high input impedance and a low output impedance, such as an emitter or source follower or an op-amp voltage follower, inserted to isolate a source from the loading of the following stage. It provides current gain and impedance transformation so that a high-impedance signal can drive a lower-impedance load without loss of level or added distortion.' WHERE id='84bf9e06-605a-4600-96fd-9f67f35bef88' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A stable, self-contained tone generator built into equipment that produces a signal of known frequency and known amplitude for the purpose of setting or verifying gain, level metering, and alignment. By injecting a reference tone at a defined level it allows a signal chain to be lined up so that indicated and actual levels agree, and its usefulness depends on the amplitude stability and low distortion of the oscillator itself.' WHERE id='70140f00-46dc-4356-aa26-c70cccb8c5ab' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The gain of a feedback amplifier measured with its feedback network connected, given by A/(1 + A*beta) where A is the open-loop gain and beta the feedback fraction. When the loop gain is large it approaches 1/beta and is therefore fixed very largely by the passive feedback network rather than the imprecise open-loop gain, yielding a lower but far more accurate and stable gain with correspondingly extended bandwidth and reduced distortion.' WHERE id='7be04380-845c-4b99-96d5-8a7ed3bc9f7a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Common-mode rejection ratio, the ratio of a differential amplifier''s differential-mode gain to its common-mode gain, normally expressed in decibels as 20*log10(Ad/Acm). It measures how effectively the amplifier rejects a signal applied equally to both inputs, and it is limited by mismatch in the input devices and surrounding resistors and by the finite common-mode gain; a high value is essential for rejecting interference on balanced lines.' WHERE id='2a1704f5-2c13-474f-b2b8-c97e625b1074' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor deliberately added to a feedback amplifier, most often connected for Miller compensation around the voltage-amplifier stage, to shape the open-loop frequency response by creating a dominant pole. It rolls the open-loop gain down so that the loop gain falls below unity before the accumulated phase shift reaches the point of instability, thereby securing adequate phase and gain margin at the expense of some bandwidth and slew rate.' WHERE id='2ff346c9-f34b-45ca-8057-cd7549b32f01' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The means by which a signal is transferred from one stage or circuit to the next, which may be direct (DC), capacitive (AC), transformer, or optical, each transmitting the signal while defining the DC and impedance relationship between the connected stages. The term also covers unintended transfer of energy through parasitic capacitive, inductive, or common-impedance paths, which conveys interference rather than the wanted signal.' WHERE id='9acf8311-7ba3-4c6f-8256-324a312ebc43' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A balanced output stage built from two driver amplifiers whose feedback is cross-connected so that each leg''s output depends on the other, causing the pair to behave as a floating differential source that mimics a transformer. This arrangement holds the differential output level constant whether the line is driven fully balanced or with one leg grounded or shorted, providing the level constancy of a transformer output without the transformer.' WHERE id='744fd45c-7f75-4c4b-8edd-b94bac1bae04' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A direct connection between circuit nodes or stages that conducts both the direct-current and alternating-current components, using no series capacitor or transformer, so the frequency response extends down to zero hertz. Because the DC operating potentials of the coupled stages are shared, any offset is transmitted and amplified along with the signal, so DC coupling requires careful bias and offset management such as a DC servo.' WHERE id='9a4c3df3-f532-4a5f-a7b6-4b023aad0090' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrator-based feedback loop wrapped around a DC-coupled amplifier that senses the low-frequency average, or DC offset, of the output and returns a correcting signal to an earlier point in the circuit to null it. This holds the output DC near zero without placing a coupling capacitor in the signal path, and the integrator time constant sets a very low high-pass corner so that only near-DC content is controlled while the audio band passes unaffected.' WHERE id='860b781d-36f8-452b-9530-3dc3807df0f4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated circuit that emulates a mechanical potentiometer or rheostat using a resistor string or ladder tapped by an array of analog switches, the wiper position being selected by a digitally stored code loaded through a serial interface such as SPI or I2C. It provides programmable, repeatable resistance or division in discrete steps and is used for electronic gain, level, and trim adjustment.' WHERE id='bde1d09e-ddb3-4f53-b985-aabaf68d7dca' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The portion of a system''s signal chain in which the signal exists as a stream of sampled, quantized numerical values, extending from the analog-to-digital converter output through digital processing such as filtering, mixing, and effects to the digital-to-analog converter input. Its fidelity is governed by sample rate, word length, and numerical precision rather than by analog thermal noise and device distortion.' WHERE id='a7b349af-4fbd-4be6-ad4b-56e456d49c2f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The intermediate amplifier stage that precedes and drives the output stage, supplying the voltage swing and, in particular, the base or gate current required to control the output devices. In a power amplifier it drives the output transistors and commonly incorporates level shifting and the bias-setting element, isolating the high-gain voltage-amplifier stage from the heavy, varying current demand of the output.' WHERE id='ebc11950-6470-4024-9575-6bb911534add' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated circuit that sets audio gain or attenuation by electronic means, using a digitally controlled resistor network, a switched R-2R or ladder attenuator, or a variable-gain amplifier core, in place of a mechanical potentiometer. It offers programmable, repeatable, and closely channel-matched level control, typically with click-free zero-crossing or ramped updates and low added noise and distortion.' WHERE id='9d887894-2909-4002-b452-d64d17bb3a90' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In the analog-synthesizer sense, an integrated circuit that generates a time-varying control voltage contour, classically the attack-decay-sustain-release shape, in response to a gate or trigger input. It produces the contour by charging and discharging a timing capacitor through controlled resistances, and the resulting envelope is used to modulate amplitude, filter cutoff, or other parameters over the duration of a note.' WHERE id='378e1a60-3b1d-44dd-b575-e48405f57854' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The closed signal path in which a defined fraction of an amplifier''s output is returned and combined with its input. In the negative-feedback case the returned signal opposes the input, trading raw open-loop gain for a precisely defined closed-loop gain, reduced distortion, and modified input and output impedances; the loop gain, the product of forward gain and feedback fraction, governs the size of these benefits while the loop''s phase response governs stability.' WHERE id='6095940c-ed1c-4527-b3e1-f85a62c310f8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An amplifier stage whose primary purpose is to provide voltage gain, built around one or more active devices in a configuration such as common-emitter or common-source so that the output amplitude exceeds the input. The gain is determined by the device transconductance acting into its load, or is set by local negative feedback, and the stage contributes to the overall noise and distortion of the circuit.' WHERE id='4b1d024b-ec32-405a-9a97-2f3806289946' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='For an amplifier with a single dominant-pole response, the constant product of closed-loop gain and the resulting -3 dB bandwidth, numerically equal to the unity-gain frequency at which the open-loop gain has fallen to one. Because the product is fixed, increasing the closed-loop gain reduces the available bandwidth in inverse proportion, so it directly characterizes the gain-versus-speed trade-off of the device.' WHERE id='bcdc95df-c59c-4687-b17f-861b32c4aad3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated output amplifier designed to drive the low impedance of headphones, typically in the range of about 16 to 600 ohms, to audio listening levels. It supplies the necessary output current at low output impedance and distortion, and often incorporates a ground-referenced or capacitorless output arrangement, such as a charge-pump split supply, together with click and pop suppression during power sequencing.' WHERE id='a2cd98bc-926a-42e5-a85e-788b1e8a4206' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A single-ended output made compatible with a balanced input by driving the signal on the hot conductor while terminating the cold conductor to ground through an impedance equal to the hot conductor''s output impedance. Both conductors then present the same source impedance to the line, so a balanced receiver''s common-mode rejection cancels interference picked up equally on the pair, even though only one conductor actually carries signal.' WHERE id='ad861e8d-5c87-4068-a40d-a5b68e03cdc4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The DC current that must flow into or out of each input terminal of an amplifier to establish the correct operating condition of its input devices, being the base current of a bipolar input pair or the gate leakage current of a FET input pair. Flowing through the external source resistances it develops an offset voltage and associated drift, its average value being specified as the input bias current and the difference between the two inputs as the input offset current.' WHERE id='1fe4586e-6341-4126-ad75-9a02900e200e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The range of common-mode voltage, applied simultaneously to both inputs, over which an amplifier''s input stage continues to operate linearly and correctly, with its input transistors held in their active region. Outside this range the input devices saturate or cut off, causing gain and common-mode rejection to collapse; a rail-to-rail input stage is one whose common-mode range extends to or beyond both supply rails.' WHERE id='4dbba03a-723b-453a-ae27-d8a60598f620' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The small differential DC voltage that must be applied between an amplifier''s two inputs to force its output to the ideal null value, arising from unavoidable mismatch in the input differential pair such as base-emitter voltage or threshold, load, and device geometry differences. Specified together with its temperature drift, it sets a fundamental limit on the DC accuracy achievable with the amplifier.' WHERE id='b9d17eae-517b-47fa-b2db-11a2444fdc0d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The first amplifying stage of an amplifier or operational amplifier, usually a differential pair, which performs the differencing needed for feedback and provides the initial gain. It largely determines the input impedance, common-mode range, input-referred noise, and offset of the whole amplifier, so its design dominates the circuit''s DC precision and noise performance.' WHERE id='deea9ede-550b-4705-8978-4a6404da26a6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The total intrinsic noise of an amplifier expressed as an equivalent source, comprising a noise voltage and a noise current, placed at the input, obtained by dividing the measured output noise by the amplifier gain. Referring the noise to the input makes it independent of the gain and, combined with the source impedance, allows the achievable signal-to-noise ratio and noise figure to be predicted.' WHERE id='1bcc58f1-19c7-4894-9390-30435c0528e4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The manner in which one amplifier stage is connected to the next, being direct or DC coupling, resistance-capacitance or capacitive coupling, or transformer coupling. The chosen method transfers the signal between stages while defining their DC relationship and setting the low-frequency response of the interconnection.' WHERE id='4ac8af37-ad31-4fbc-a64f-db73e8d7b200' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An amplifier stage whose output is in antiphase with its input, that is, it applies a 180-degree phase shift or sign inversion. Examples are the common-emitter and common-source stages and the operational amplifier in its inverting configuration, where the input sees a virtual earth and the gain equals the negative ratio of the feedback resistor to the input resistor.' WHERE id='ea9903c7-730c-468c-a710-e027688b17f2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated output amplifier designed to drive the relatively low impedance and cable capacitance of interconnecting audio lines at line level, sourcing sufficient current at low output impedance and low distortion. It is frequently arranged as a balanced or differential output, sometimes cross-coupled, so as to present a robust, interference-tolerant signal to the receiving equipment.' WHERE id='2561cb37-d0c3-49f7-b630-18127e75e869' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated differential-input amplifier that receives a balanced line-level signal and converts it to single-ended form while rejecting interference common to both conductors. Its high common-mode rejection relies on precise on-chip matching of the internal resistor network, which is often laser-trimmed, so that ground and induced noise are cancelled at the output.' WHERE id='e0eb84ee-b467-475c-bd53-655cb0c35dd9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated low-noise preamplifier that supplies the high, usually variable, gain required to raise a microphone''s low-level output to line level. It features very low input-referred noise, quoted as equivalent input noise, a balanced differential input suited to microphone sources, and commonly a current-feedback topology in which a single external resistor sets the gain.' WHERE id='2322ee59-a936-4937-bbca-1b73401f249f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A circuit or integrated circuit that combines analog and digital functions within the same design or on the same substrate, such as a data converter, codec, or digitally controlled analog block. Its design demands careful grounding, supply partitioning, and layout isolation so that the fast switching currents of the digital section do not couple into and corrupt the sensitive analog section.' WHERE id='d55aff64-22fd-4948-8dcb-79914137e798' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An amplifier stage whose output is in phase with its input, applying no sign inversion. Examples are the emitter or source follower and the operational amplifier in its non-inverting configuration, which presents a high input impedance and a gain equal to one plus the ratio of the feedback resistor to the grounded resistor.' WHERE id='9178f585-9237-49ed-8e5f-a87f049dbd83' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The provision for nulling an amplifier''s input offset voltage so that its output DC is brought to zero, implemented either as an external trimmer connected to a dedicated null pin or as an internal laser-trimmed or digitally programmable adjustment in the input stage''s load. Trimming removes the static offset but does not eliminate its subsequent drift with temperature and time.' WHERE id='dbcaa83f-e82f-4264-978a-fdae4fcd52c8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The gain of an amplifier with no feedback applied, that is, the raw forward voltage gain from input to output. In an operational amplifier it is very large at DC, often of the order of 100,000 to 1,000,000, and is neither precise nor stable, rolling off with frequency; feedback then divides this large, imprecise gain down to the accurate closed-loop value.' WHERE id='6141b400-9674-4248-8791-d47dd10799c0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The final amplifier stage, which delivers the required current and power to the load at low output impedance, commonly built as a push-pull emitter-follower or complementary pair operating in class A or class AB. It is designed for the necessary voltage swing and current capability into the rated load while maintaining low distortion, and it dominates the amplifier''s large-signal and crossover behaviour.' WHERE id='f28d7981-8edb-4b85-b30d-e9e664a70a7f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The maximum peak-to-peak voltage an amplifier output can produce before clipping, limited by the supply rails less the saturation or dropout voltage of the output devices, or approaching the rails in a rail-to-rail output. Because supplying output current increases the device drop, the achievable swing is load-dependent and shrinks as the load impedance falls.' WHERE id='255055d2-4ac0-4541-9140-a6b668927b98' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The maximum current an amplifier''s output stage can source into and sink from a load while remaining in linear operation, before current limiting or excessive distortion and voltage drop set in. It determines the minimum load impedance the amplifier can drive to full output swing and hence its ability to drive low-impedance or reactive loads.' WHERE id='b3d6d429-2f57-45e7-a938-c03232bf312a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An unwanted, generally high-frequency self-sustained oscillation in an amplifier caused by unintended feedback through parasitic reactances such as lead and wiring inductance, stray and junction capacitance, and shared supply impedance, which together satisfy the conditions for oscillation. It is suppressed by careful layout, supply decoupling, base or gate stopper resistors, and adequate frequency compensation.' WHERE id='0b435af2-3bcf-4a46-9022-435329b7c1a5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Abbreviation for programmable-gain amplifier, an amplifier whose closed-loop gain is selected through a digital interface by switching feedback or attenuator resistors, often a resistor ladder, around an amplifier core. It provides discrete, accurately matched, and repeatable gain steps, and is commonly placed ahead of an analog-to-digital converter to scale the input signal to the converter''s full-scale range.' WHERE id='53ef8b29-82ba-488d-9e87-c6f016d2224f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A stability metric for a feedback amplifier, equal to the amount by which the phase shift of the loop gain at the frequency where the loop gain magnitude is unity falls short of 180 degrees. A large phase margin gives a well-damped response, a small margin produces gain peaking and transient ringing, and zero margin means the loop oscillates.' WHERE id='1965aa4f-c89a-4a59-b813-a40a1a3a9804' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Feedback returned to the input in phase with it, so that the returned signal reinforces rather than opposes the input and increases the loop gain toward regeneration. It is used deliberately to create oscillators, to give comparators hysteresis as in the Schmitt trigger, and to bootstrap impedances, but when unintended it leads to instability and oscillation.' WHERE id='ab8e7f14-e23a-4ad7-a510-bdcc0dd924b6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated circuit containing a complete power-amplifier chain, comprising voltage-gain stages and a current-delivering output stage, able to drive a loudspeaker load directly to watt-level output. It typically includes on-chip protection such as thermal shutdown and safe-operating-area or overcurrent limiting to guard the output devices under fault and overload conditions.' WHERE id='461e560c-d829-49b3-9de0-3ff5daefcb3a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The ratio, usually expressed in decibels, of a change in an amplifier''s supply voltage to the resulting change in its output referred to the input, quantifying how effectively the circuit rejects ripple and noise on its supply rails. It is generally high at DC and degrades with increasing frequency, and it is specified separately for the positive and negative supplies.' WHERE id='124330e2-bad5-42a8-af5b-d609455eed7d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An amplifier whose closed-loop gain is selected through a digital control interface by switching feedback or attenuator resistors around an amplifier core, giving a set of discrete, accurately matched, and repeatable gain values. It is used to scale a signal to fit the input range of a following stage, most often the full-scale range of an analog-to-digital converter, under program control.' WHERE id='33a3187b-4017-4756-a08d-297d6bb08349' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Abbreviation for power-supply rejection ratio, the ratio in decibels of a change in an amplifier''s supply voltage to the resulting change in its output referred to the input. It measures the circuit''s immunity to ripple and noise on its supply rails, is highest at DC, and falls with increasing frequency.' WHERE id='19f7138a-f19e-4be2-a62e-9002ebd92101' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The DC operating point of an active device or amplifier stage under no-signal conditions, defined by the quiescent current and voltage set by the bias network, about which the signal subsequently swings. It establishes the device''s small-signal parameters, the available headroom, and the class of operation of the stage.' WHERE id='5c33a018-51f4-4bda-88b4-fcacbc41bbc3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A stable oscillator that provides a precise, known reference frequency, most often crystal controlled, from which other frequencies and clocks in a system are derived or against which they are compared. It commonly serves as the master timing reference for sample clocks and phase-locked loops, and its accuracy and stability set the frequency accuracy of everything locked to it.' WHERE id='e4ef9a0a-b50a-445b-ade7-2a5e0ee2f5f9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The noise remaining at a circuit''s output under defined quiescent conditions, with no input signal and the input suitably terminated, representing the intrinsic noise floor from thermal, shot, and flicker sources together with any residual hum and interference that cannot be removed. It is measured over a specified bandwidth and weighting to characterize the ultimate noise floor of the circuit.' WHERE id='6552ea51-ba06-40cb-a4a6-6db3c9bd410f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A doubly-balanced modulator, classically built from a ring of four diodes fed by an input and a carrier transformer, that multiplies its two input signals so that the output contains only the sum and difference frequencies of their spectral components while both original inputs are suppressed. In audio use this produces characteristic inharmonic, metallic sidebands, and the same function is realized electronically with a Gilbert-cell analog multiplier.' WHERE id='08e42e4c-8965-4ad3-8ef0-204b1328bace' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated circuit that captures the instantaneous value of an analog input at a commanded instant and holds it steady on a hold capacitor while a following stage, typically an analog-to-digital converter, operates on it. Its performance is characterized by acquisition time, aperture time and jitter, hold-mode droop rate, and the pedestal or hold step caused by switch charge injection.' WHERE id='7a6ed68f-e0f7-4300-97bd-7b25e0f898de' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A balanced output stage in which a feedback, or servo, loop senses both output legs and forces their sum toward zero, so the differential output level is held constant and the output emulates a floating, transformer-like source. This keeps the delivered level unchanged whether the line is connected balanced or with one leg grounded, giving much of the behaviour of a transformer output without the transformer.' WHERE id='1117175c-4b1e-4ccb-9af0-6be8bafd3cd7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The analog processing applied to a signal to prepare it for the requirements of the following stage, particularly a data converter, encompassing amplification or attenuation to fit the input range, filtering for band-limiting or anti-aliasing, level shifting, buffering, and impedance matching. The conditioned signal is thereby brought into the amplitude, bandwidth, and impedance window that the next block requires.' WHERE id='837f9b11-ec99-4ec4-85c6-d28628ae691d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The reversal of a signal''s polarity, that is, a sign change equivalent to a 180-degree phase shift, so that positive excursions become negative and negative become positive. It is produced by an inverting amplifier stage, by a reversal of transformer or wiring connections, or by a unity-gain inverter, and it changes absolute polarity without altering the signal''s magnitude.' WHERE id='b82fe43c-520a-4023-b18d-7276b9ec6407' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The nonlinear condition in which an amplifier''s output cannot change fast enough to follow its input because the current available to charge the internal compensation capacitor is limited, so the rate of change of output voltage saturates at the slew rate. It produces slew-induced or transient intermodulation distortion and imposes a full-power bandwidth ceiling above which large signals can no longer be reproduced undistorted.' WHERE id='e5d3a296-fcf5-49cf-846a-ad1f00d1f8b2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A high-quality, low-distortion audio signal generator, typically a Wien-bridge or state-variable sine-wave source, used in studios and test benches to produce accurate reference tones. Its stable amplitude and very low harmonic distortion make it suitable for lining up levels, calibrating equipment, and measuring gain, frequency response, and distortion.' WHERE id='0eff6a58-233c-4367-bbc0-b780c4ba60ed' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The circuit node, most commonly the inverting input or virtual earth of an operational-amplifier mixer, at which several input currents, each defined by its own input resistor, combine according to Kirchhoff''s current law and are converted to an output voltage by the feedback resistor. Because feedback holds the node at virtual ground, the input channels do not interact and their signals add without mutual loading.' WHERE id='39361ce0-0604-45c3-a9e7-329923cb6d47' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Distortion arising from periodic self-heating of devices at the signal rate, in which dissipation-driven changes in temperature modulate device parameters such as base-emitter voltage or resistance and hence the gain or offset of the stage. Because the thermal effect lags the signal it produces predominantly low-order, low-frequency distortion, sometimes termed memory distortion, that is not revealed by conventional steady-state harmonic measurement.' WHERE id='3ceb751e-7e0c-4663-9e5a-98dcc8e1e0bb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Distortion that appears on rapidly changing, transient signals rather than in the steady state, of which the principal form is transient intermodulation distortion, also called slew-induced distortion. It occurs when a fast input transient momentarily demands a rate of change beyond the amplifier''s slew capability, driving the input stage into nonlinearity before the feedback loop can correct it.' WHERE id='f9c8da1a-0a67-4c54-b709-017dcc0caf74' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Describing an amplifier that remains stable, with adequate phase margin, even under the worst-case feedback condition of unity closed-loop gain, that is, with 100 percent feedback. Its internal compensation places the dominant pole so that the loop gain falls below unity before the phase shift reaches 180 degrees; by contrast a decompensated part is only stable at some higher minimum gain.' WHERE id='ef2022b1-a211-446e-9154-e1eb300a5312' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An amplifier whose gain can be varied continuously by a control input, either a control voltage or current, rather than in fixed digital steps. It is used for automatic gain control, level riding, and fades, and is distinguished from a programmable-gain amplifier, which offers only discrete digitally selected gain values, by its smooth continuous control.' WHERE id='aa9698fb-8334-4f76-b077-c512a9949832' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Abbreviation for variable-gain amplifier, an amplifier whose gain is set continuously by a control voltage or current input. It is applied where the gain must be adjusted smoothly and dynamically, as in automatic gain control, level riding, and fades, in contrast to the discrete steps of a programmable-gain amplifier.' WHERE id='226aa570-b8d8-4a0d-9856-cd737da82a10' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone preamplifier built to, or reproducing, classic discrete or valve designs, characterized in circuit terms by input and output transformers and discrete bipolar or vacuum-tube gain stages rather than modern monolithic amplifiers. These features impart the coloration associated with older console and outboard preamps, including added harmonic content, transformer saturation at high levels, and a deliberately limited bandwidth.' WHERE id='4f37399f-53bb-4387-b04c-aa528f431df7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A circuit node held at, or very close to, ground potential by feedback rather than by a physical connection to ground, the classic example being the inverting input of an operational amplifier with negative feedback, where the high open-loop gain forces the differential input voltage toward zero so the node tracks the grounded non-inverting input. The term is also applied to a mid-supply reference rail that serves as the signal ground in single-supply circuits.' WHERE id='ad580f88-6281-4e1a-8549-fa69d236b748' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An integrated circuit that produces a precise, stable, temperature-compensated fixed DC output voltage that is largely independent of supply voltage, load current, and temperature, realized typically as a bandgap or buried-Zener reference. It is used to define converter full-scale values, set bias currents, and provide calibration levels throughout a system.' WHERE id='e5883b70-f791-4275-b6b8-6da1c7843baf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An amplifier whose gain is set by an applied control voltage, most often implemented with a translinear log-antilog core so that the gain in decibels is a linear function of the control voltage. It is the fundamental gain-control element in analog synthesizers, dynamics processors such as compressors, and automated mixing consoles where it provides fader automation.' WHERE id='e9a95f33-92dd-4b42-a189-615f73ce5a08' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In popular-song form, the bridge is a contrasting section that departs from the verse and chorus in melody, harmony, and often lyric perspective, providing relief from their repetition before a return to the familiar material. It typically appears once and later in the song, classically as the B of an AABA form or after the second chorus in verse-chorus form, and frequently introduces new chord movement or a shift of tonal centre while building back toward a final chorus. In the mix it is a common point at which arrangement and processing are varied to mark the contrast.' WHERE id='6ddab021-6777-4c87-81b0-1fa5cf4badcf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A build-up is a transitional section, characteristic of electronic dance music, that progressively increases tension and energy to prepare the arrival of a new section, most often the drop or chorus. It is created by cumulatively adding layers and raising intensity through devices such as rising pitch or filter sweeps, snare or percussion rolls that accelerate, swelling noise risers, and the momentary removal of the bass just before the release. Its function is purely anticipatory, resolving into the high-energy section that follows.' WHERE id='5b155890-38ea-427f-9e24-da1fefa5007e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A cold ending, also called a hard or button ending, is a song ending in which the music stops abruptly on a final chord or beat at full level rather than fading out. It provides a decisive, definite conclusion, often a held final chord or a single articulated hit, and contrasts with the fade ending in which level is gradually reduced to silence. Cold endings are used where a strong, performed sense of closure is wanted and are handled in mastering by trimming and fading the tail cleanly down to the noise floor.' WHERE id='e8d0d27c-0fce-47db-858a-404266d7872f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A fade ending, or fade-out, is a song ending in which the overall level is gradually reduced to silence over the final seconds, creating the impression that the music continues beyond audibility. Executed as a mix or mastering automation move on the stereo output, it is used to close repetitive outro or chorus vamps that have no composed final cadence, and its length and contour, whether linear or curved, are chosen musically. It contrasts with the cold ending, which stops abruptly at full level.' WHERE id='3fa57fc8-b46d-4aa2-9785-13932b7b0690' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An interlude is a self-contained instrumental or textural passage inserted between two main vocal sections of a song, providing contrast or transition without the principal lyric material. Shorter or more incidental than a full solo section and distinct from the intro and outro that frame the song, it may reuse existing material or introduce new content, and it gives the arrangement breathing space. In the mix it is often an opportunity for altered instrumentation, effects, or dynamics.' WHERE id='d2cff328-73ae-4e0c-b470-6d3b17700daa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The intro, or introduction, is the opening section of a song that precedes the first verse or chorus, establishing key, tempo, groove, and mood and drawing the listener in. It ranges from a single held chord or drum count to a fully developed instrumental passage, and it frequently previews motivic or harmonic material that returns later. In arrangement and mixing it sets the initial sonic scene and commonly builds toward the entry of the lead vocal.' WHERE id='e56a19d6-7e39-494b-9e9f-96902af1bac3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A musical arrangement is the specific realisation of a composition in terms of which instruments and voices play which parts, in what register, texture, and order, across the course of the piece. It encompasses the choice and disposition of parts, the sectional structure and its dynamics, and the interplay of rhythmic, harmonic, and melodic layers, thereby shaping how the underlying song is presented. Because the arrangement determines what elements compete for space at any given moment, it is a primary determinant of how readily a track can be mixed.' WHERE id='a4b48c93-a0dc-4554-b50d-e994c11627e7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The outro, also called the coda or ending, is the closing section of a song that follows the final chorus or verse and brings the piece to its conclusion. It may take the form of a repeated vamp faded out, a newly composed closing passage, a return of intro material, or a decisive final cadence, and its purpose is to release the accumulated energy and provide closure. In mixing and mastering it is where the fade or cold ending is executed and the tail is resolved to silence.' WHERE id='1420ec38-1c13-49a7-a0da-270311206495' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A post-chorus is a section that immediately follows the chorus, extending its energy and hook while presenting distinct material, often a catchy instrumental figure or a short repeated vocal phrase, before the song returns to a verse or repeats. Recognised in contemporary pop analysis, it functions as an appended second climax that reinforces the chorus without being the chorus itself, and it is distinguished from a simple refrain tag by having its own separate melodic and textural identity. In the mix it is typically a high-energy, wide, and dense passage.' WHERE id='4da2ce72-185f-4bac-bc3c-bab4ec181998' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A pre-chorus, also called a lift, climb, or build, is a section placed between the verse and the chorus that heightens momentum and expectation to prepare the chorus''s arrival. It typically uses its own repeated harmonic and melodic material, often a rising line or a harmonic move that creates tension resolving into the chorus, and it recurs before each chorus, helping to differentiate verse from chorus. In arrangement and mixing it commonly features a controlled increase in density and energy leading into the hook.' WHERE id='60310347-a938-42f5-ab99-1b2939793cff' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A refrain is a recurring line or short phrase, usually carrying the song''s title or central lyric, that returns with the same words and melody at a fixed point in each verse or at the end of sections. In verse-refrain forms it functions as the memorable return without constituting a separate full chorus; the term is sometimes used loosely as a synonym for the chorus, but strictly it denotes a brief repeated tag rather than an independent section. It provides unity and recognisability across the song.' WHERE id='9e80873a-da7d-49af-86db-bbfdff39d305' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A register change is a shift of the musical material into a different pitch region, higher or lower, than it previously occupied, whether by a vocalist moving between chest and head voice, an instrument transposing by an octave, or the arrangement relocating parts to a new range. Used structurally, it marks contrast between sections, for example lifting a final chorus up an octave to raise intensity, and it alters timbre and perceived energy. In mixing, register changes affect frequency masking and the tonal balance between sections.' WHERE id='83ff8014-a457-482c-a824-07929b256c97' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A solo section is a passage in which a single instrument, or occasionally a voice, is featured as the melodic focus, typically improvising or playing a written lead over the accompanying arrangement, often across the chord progression of a verse or chorus. It provides contrast and virtuosic interest, is commonly placed after the second chorus or as an extended instrumental break, and foregrounds the soloist while the rhythm section supports. In mixing, the featured instrument is raised in level and often treated to enhance its prominence.' WHERE id='9d14e275-46bf-4a07-99a8-2c2e69139b00' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Song form is the overall structural organisation of a song, the ordering and repetition of its constituent sections, such as intro, verse, pre-chorus, chorus, bridge, and outro, into a coherent whole. Common schemes include strophic (AAA), verse-chorus, and AABA forms, and the form governs how contrast, repetition, and climax are distributed across time. Understanding a song''s form is fundamental to arranging and to mixing, because processing and dynamic decisions are usually made section by section in service of the form.' WHERE id='55946938-d183-45c8-8b32-315f04e7a56d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The verse is the section of a song that carries the narrative or developing lyric, using a recurring melody and harmonic progression but different words on each appearance, and typically leading toward the chorus. It is generally lower in energy and density than the chorus, establishing context and advancing the story, and it recurs two or more times across the song. In mixing it is commonly balanced to sit below the chorus in loudness and width so that the chorus provides contrast and lift.' WHERE id='e80c1154-73a7-4c93-aa4d-87cc97a1f577' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Archive migration is the periodic process of transferring archived digital assets from one storage medium, carrier, or file format to another in order to counter media degradation and technological obsolescence, so the content stays readable and usable over the long term. In audio and media archiving it typically involves copying data onto fresh media, such as refreshing successive generations of LTO tape, or transcoding to a currently supported format, with integrity verification performed before and after to confirm that nothing was lost or altered. It is a core preservation activity that keeps an archive from becoming inaccessible as hardware, software, and carriers age.' WHERE id='738fd8ed-0a3f-4dd5-9ea4-b77c50e1d683' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An archive server is a dedicated system, distinct from active production storage, that manages the long-term storage and controlled retrieval of completed projects and media assets, often front-ending nearline or offline tiers such as LTO tape libraries or high-capacity disk. It maintains a catalog of stored content and handles ingest, indexing, and restore requests, commonly applying integrity checks and keeping multiple copies to protect against loss. In a facility workflow it offloads finished material from fast working storage, freeing capacity while keeping assets recoverable on demand.' WHERE id='cf4d643a-b2f8-4370-b30f-ef803a3ea50b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Central storage is a shared, high-bandwidth storage system that houses a facility''s media in one managed location so multiple workstations can access the same projects and assets over a network rather than keeping separate local copies. It supports collaborative editing and mixing by providing concurrent access, consistent file locations, and centralized management of capacity, permissions, and backup. Bandwidth and contention are managed so that several simultaneous streams of audio or video can be served without dropouts.' WHERE id='1bebff36-ec19-434b-a33e-c4ca5818fc10' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Checksum validation is the verification of a file''s integrity by computing a checksum or hash value over its contents and comparing the result against a previously stored reference value; a match indicates the data is unchanged, while a mismatch reveals corruption or alteration introduced during transfer or storage. It is widely used when copying or archiving media to confirm that a duplicate is identical to its source. The reliability of the check depends on the algorithm used, ranging from simple cyclic-redundancy checksums to cryptographic hash functions.' WHERE id='15e87264-2ba0-44d6-a435-a6e4d93e8f89' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Data integrity is the property that data remains complete, accurate, and unaltered, other than by authorized processes, throughout its lifecycle of creation, transfer, storage, and retrieval. Maintaining it means protecting against both accidental corruption, from media faults, transmission errors, or hardware failure, and unauthorized modification, commonly using checksums, hashing, error correction, redundancy, and access controls to detect or prevent change. In media and archiving contexts it is what assures that a stored file still faithfully represents the original recording.' WHERE id='6e9a49bb-d16d-4180-b2b3-c4cfdbc5e570' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='File hashing is the application of a hash function to a file''s contents to produce a fixed-length digest, or hash value, that serves as a compact and effectively unique fingerprint of that data; any change to the file yields a different digest. Cryptographic hash algorithms such as those in the SHA family are used so the value can verify integrity and identity, confirming for example that a copied or archived file matches its original, and can reveal tampering or corruption. Because the same input always produces the same hash, the values allow reliable comparison across time and across systems.' WHERE id='910b8fdd-6b89-43a9-8ed3-a6484921e5d0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Long-term preservation is the set of managed strategies and actions that keep digital content authentic, intact, and accessible over extended periods, well beyond the lifespan of any single storage medium or file format, despite media decay and technological obsolescence. It combines redundant storage, regular integrity checking, and periodic migration or format normalization, typically guided by reference models for trustworthy digital repositories. Its goal is the continued usability of the content, not merely the retention of the underlying bits.' WHERE id='9ac039e5-5661-4cfb-9449-c5468e1eaf52' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Network storage is data storage accessed by client systems over a network rather than as direct-attached local disks, allowing capacity to be shared among multiple users and workstations. It is commonly implemented as network-attached storage (NAS), which serves files over protocols such as SMB or NFS, or as a storage area network (SAN), which presents block-level devices over a dedicated high-speed fabric. In production environments it underpins collaborative access to shared media as well as centralized backup and management.' WHERE id='3de5b54e-c6bb-4955-a633-c6ead4985cca' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Proxy media are lower-resolution or lower-bitrate substitute copies of high-resolution source files, used during editing so a system can play back and edit smoothly with reduced processing and storage bandwidth. Each proxy is linked to its full-quality original, and at finishing or export the project is relinked, or conformed, so the final render uses the high-resolution masters rather than the proxies. This proxy-and-conform workflow is common where the original media is very large or the workstation cannot handle the native files in real time.' WHERE id='b5a84ea2-0333-409c-b30a-220d8a4ac116' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Shared storage is a storage system that multiple users or workstations can access simultaneously, enabling collaborative workflows in which several editors or mixers work from the same pool of media and projects. It provides concurrent multi-client access with management of permissions, file locking, and bandwidth so that simultaneous read and write streams neither conflict nor starve one another. Implemented over network or SAN infrastructure, it contrasts with direct-attached storage, which serves only one machine at a time.' WHERE id='56af8506-9e06-4181-bfd9-9570e463318f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In Ableton Live''s Arrangement View, the Arrangement Loop is a region, marked by the loop brace in the beat-time ruler and toggled from the Control Bar, that makes the transport play back repeatedly between its start and end points. Its boundaries can be dragged, nudged by the current grid with the arrow keys, or lengthened and shortened, and when no material is selected the loop spans the whole Arrangement. Besides repeating a section during playback, it also delimits the region used by punch-in and punch-out recording.' WHERE id='e4111914-f77d-4646-87bd-5071bd2d101b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Arrangement Overdub is a MIDI recording mode in which newly recorded notes are merged into the MIDI already present in the underlying Arrangement clip instead of replacing it. With it engaged, each recording pass layers the incoming performance onto the existing material on that track, so a part such as a drum pattern can be built up incrementally over several passes. It applies to MIDI tracks only.' WHERE id='707dcd22-159c-4fed-953d-93ea2f835075' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Arrangement recording is the capture of audio or MIDI onto the linear timeline of Live''s Arrangement View, started with the Control Bar''s Arrangement Record button on armed tracks. Whether recording begins at once or waits for the transport to start is governed by the Start Playback with Record preference, and the incoming signal is written as new clips along the timeline. It can be confined to a region by the punch-in and punch-out points together with the Arrangement Loop.' WHERE id='6a5dbfd6-37d8-449c-817f-3caa6abaa7dc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Back to Arrangement button, in the upper-right of the Arrangement View, lights up whenever launching Session View clips has made what is heard differ from the recorded Arrangement, because a launched Session clip temporarily overrides Arrangement playback on its track. Clicking the button stops those overriding Session clips and returns the affected tracks to playing their Arrangement content. It thus serves both as an indicator that playback diverges from the Arrangement and as the control that restores it.' WHERE id='94f70674-8096-4a84-8def-808c35cf7c9a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Beats is a Warp Mode optimized for rhythmic material such as drum loops and other audio with a strong transient-driven pulse. It uses granular processing that keeps the segments between chosen cut points intact while repeating or skipping audio at those boundaries to achieve time-stretching, and its Preserve control sets whether the audio is cut at detected transients or at a fixed grid division. This retains the attack and groove of percussive sounds when the tempo is changed.' WHERE id='e125b815-37c1-4bc9-8f38-57dacb689431' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Capture MIDI is a retroactive recording function: Live continuously buffers MIDI input on armed or input-monitored tracks even when the transport is not recording, so pressing the Capture MIDI button retrieves the material just played and places it into a clip. Live analyses the captured performance to set an appropriate clip loop length and tempo, letting an improvised idea be recovered without having armed recording beforehand.' WHERE id='d84b3f44-c0f2-427a-bc34-9dddfc032b0e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In an audio clip, Detune is the fine-pitch field beside the semitone Transpose control that shifts the clip''s playback pitch in cents, where one hundred cents equal one semitone. It provides sub-semitone tuning, for example to correct a slightly out-of-tune sample or to beat two layers against one another, and works non-destructively on playback without altering the underlying sample.' WHERE id='244b2400-af80-468e-b5d5-50739d93f9ab' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Clip End marker sets the position in an audio or MIDI clip at which playback stops. It can be repositioned by dragging in the Sample Editor or MIDI Note Editor, or moved with the arrow keys, and together with the Start marker it delimits the portion of the underlying material the clip plays; when looping is off, playback runs from the Start marker to the End marker.' WHERE id='af1f41db-38c6-406c-895e-bd6da36310e9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Clip envelopes are per-clip breakpoint curves that shape a chosen parameter over the length of a clip independently of the clip''s own notes or audio. They can drive mixer settings such as volume, pan and sends, device parameters on the track''s chain, MIDI controller values in MIDI clips, and clip-specific properties such as transposition or sample offset in audio clips, all computed in real time so the sample on disk is never changed. Because they are independent of the clip''s content, many clips built from one sample can each sound different.' WHERE id='d706cc46-beaf-47b4-957d-fb3463a80f0b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Clip launch is the triggering of a Session View clip so that it begins playing, initiated by clicking its triangular clip-launch button, pressing a mapped key, or using an assigned controller. The clip''s Launch Mode - Trigger, Gate, Toggle or Repeat - determines how pressing and releasing the control start and stop it, and the actual moment of starting is aligned to the prevailing launch quantization.' WHERE id='51972bcb-ca8f-4b5f-85a6-e9e60f202058' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The per-clip Launch Quantization setting fixes the metric grid to which a Session clip''s launch is aligned, delaying its actual start to the next chosen subdivision - such as a bar or a beat - after it is triggered. It can be set to a specific value, to None for immediate launch, or to Global so the clip follows the Set''s global quantization; any value other than None also quantizes the clip''s Follow Actions.' WHERE id='a834938e-4cde-4549-9e31-aee01c978744' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Clip Loop is the switch that makes a clip repeat: when enabled, playback cycles continuously over the region shown by the loop brace rather than stopping at the end marker. For audio clips looping requires warping to be active, and the loop region drawn by the brace in the clip editor can be moved or resized.' WHERE id='ef109dbe-7ad6-4c89-87ae-58c80cafa160' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Clip Loop End is the point at which a clip''s loop region terminates before playback jumps back to the loop start. It is set through the loop Position and Length fields or by dragging the right edge of the loop brace, and together with the loop start it defines the boundaries of the section that repeats while the clip''s loop switch is on.' WHERE id='0451926a-7ea4-4648-921a-dfde48132205' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Clip Loop Start is the point at which a clip''s loop region begins and to which playback returns each time it reaches the loop end. It is set through the loop Position and Length fields or by dragging the left edge of the loop brace, defining, with the loop end, the repeating portion of the clip when looping is enabled.' WHERE id='d5ca3e26-f6a8-4d73-96bb-b21ade682694' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Clip Start marker sets the position at which a clip begins playing when it is launched. It can be dragged in the Sample Editor or MIDI Note Editor, or moved with the arrow keys, and is distinct from the loop start; when a clip is looped it plays from the Start marker on the first pass and thereafter cycles the loop region.' WHERE id='4fb96dd0-46f5-4e10-90c7-5abbadfd72a4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In a clip, Transpose - the Pitch control - shifts the clip''s pitch in semitones. For audio clips the audible result depends on the active Warp Mode and is unavailable in Re-Pitch mode, where transposition instead follows changes in playback speed; for MIDI clips it offsets the pitches of the contained notes. A neighbouring Detune field adds cent-level fine tuning, and the adjustment is applied non-destructively during playback.' WHERE id='c6104316-2a87-4630-ac0d-658341b06170' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Complex Pro is a Warp Mode, a higher-quality variant of Complex mode, built on an advanced spectral time-stretching algorithm that works especially well on polyphonic textures and complete songs. It adds parameters governing how the signal''s formant and envelope characteristics are preserved under stretching, generally yielding cleaner results than Complex mode at the cost of greater CPU use.' WHERE id='17d29e02-4d2c-4945-b4e7-3ba06fec8468' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Complex is a Warp Mode intended for heterogeneous material that combines beats, melodies and textures, such as entire songs or fully mixed excerpts, where no single specialized mode is ideal. It applies a spectral time-stretching algorithm that processes the whole signal together, trading some CPU efficiency for the ability to stretch such mixtures reasonably well.' WHERE id='751e8a5d-f720-4346-a917-a107d19837fc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Global quantization is the Set-wide launch grid, set in the Control Bar, that governs when clips and scenes actually start for anything following the global value. It corrects launch timing to the next chosen subdivision - from bars down to fractional-beat values, or None for immediate launch - can be changed quickly with keyboard shortcuts, and any setting other than None also quantizes Follow Actions.' WHERE id='8ccb1819-ba3a-40d9-9aee-0a347a6e50fe' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Within the Beats Warp Mode, the Preserve control sets the points at which the audio may be cut during granular time-stretching so that transients are kept intact. It can be set to preserve detected Transients, or to a fixed note division such as a sixteenth note that cuts the audio on that grid regardless of transient content, or to None; using a division keeps rhythmic attacks aligned and avoids smearing when the tempo is changed.' WHERE id='68d4758f-cb27-4f6f-9dbe-083a06f1bddd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Punch-In point is the position, matching the Arrangement Loop''s start, at and after which recording is permitted once the Punch-In switch is enabled, so that material before it is protected from being overwritten. Used together with the Punch-Out point, it lets recording engage precisely at the loop-start boundary and confines new recording to a defined region of the Arrangement.' WHERE id='55acbe25-9199-442a-a834-65f097720c3d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Punch-Out point is the position, matching the Arrangement Loop''s end, at which recording stops once the Punch-Out switch is enabled, so that material after it is protected from being overwritten. Used together with the Punch-In point, it confines Arrangement recording to the region between the two points.' WHERE id='8a6ab786-3e0d-4930-a100-64a4c7bbba95' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Re-Pitch is a Warp Mode that time-stretches by changing the sample''s playback rate, so tempo and pitch shift together as with varispeed or turntable pitch control rather than being processed independently. Because pitch is tied to speed, the clip''s transposition controls are deactivated in this mode: slowing the clip lowers its pitch and speeding it up raises it.' WHERE id='13b927ca-f837-448d-b7e9-5f859d57a835' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Record Quantization automatically aligns recorded MIDI notes to a chosen metric grid as they are captured, so note starts snap to the selected subdivision without a separate post-recording step. When recording into the Arrangement it is applied as its own entry in Live''s undo history, so the quantization can be undone independently of the recording itself.' WHERE id='01bfa0bb-23fb-460d-a85a-7a21ebc00340' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A scene is a row of Session View clip slots spanning all tracks, and launching it with its Scene Launch button simultaneously triggers every clip contained in that row across the tracks. By default the scene below the one launched is automatically selected as the next to launch, enabling top-to-bottom playback of a Set, and the launch is aligned to the prevailing quantization.' WHERE id='59c62e84-4505-44da-a3d3-d112e06425cb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Session Overdub is a MIDI recording mode in Session View in which a looping clip accumulates newly played notes on each pass, layering them onto what is already in the clip rather than replacing it. Repeatedly pressing the Session Record button toggles between overdubbing and plain playback, so a pattern can be built up gradually while the clip loops.' WHERE id='13220074-78ef-4e05-bc9a-ae0b98f40c20' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Texture is a Warp Mode suited to sounds without a clear pitch or rhythmic structure, such as ambient pads, noise, drones, and dense polyphonic or orchestral material. It relies on granular resynthesis with adjustable grain characteristics, favouring smooth, sonically consistent stretching of unpitched or evolving material over transient precision.' WHERE id='0e47c025-cb08-458c-821f-87eb67320a64' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Tones is a Warp Mode designed for material with a clear pitch, such as vocals, monophonic instruments and basslines. Its granular processing is tuned to follow the pitched content of the signal, and it exposes a grain-size control governing the size of the segments used, giving good results on melodic single-voice sources.' WHERE id='e4b2010c-81d3-406e-8573-fbe88eeea37f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Track Delay is a per-track control that shifts a track''s output earlier or later in time, in milliseconds, to compensate for timing discrepancies from human performance, acoustics, hardware latency and other real-world delays. It accepts positive delay and negative pre-delay values; Ableton advises against changing it during live performance because doing so can cause clicks or pops in the audio.' WHERE id='b5e1268b-fc69-43fc-9727-6baf72641853' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Warp Mode is the algorithm Live uses to time-stretch a warped audio clip so its playback follows the Set''s tempo independently of pitch. Live offers several modes - Beats, Tones, Texture, Re-Pitch, Complex and Complex Pro - each using different granular or spectral techniques, and the best choice depends on the character of the source, whether rhythmic, monophonic-pitched, textural, or a full mix. The chosen mode and its parameters determine the stretching quality and the artefacts introduced.' WHERE id='7a7a2750-f315-467f-a2f0-f6a8235fa8fd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A load-balancing mechanism in Cubase/Nuendo that shifts as much processing as possible off the time-critical ASIO real-time path onto a separate, buffered ASIO-Guard path. Channels, tracks, and virtual instruments that are not currently being monitored or record-enabled are pre-calculated ahead of the playback position, which lowers the strain on the real-time audio thread and permits smaller buffer sizes, more simultaneous plug-ins, and fewer dropouts. Because the pre-processed path introduces its own latency, higher ASIO-Guard levels increase the delay before parameter changes such as fader moves are heard, and any channel switched to input monitoring is automatically returned to real-time processing. Real-time-dependent signals such as external hardware effects and instruments cannot be handled by it.' WHERE id='8465c571-b063-4e45-a761-7754ef0a6ae4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording safeguard governed by the Audio Pre-Record Seconds preference, under which Cubase continuously captures incoming audio into a memory buffer while the transport is playing or stopped. When a take is started, the buffered material from the moments preceding the record command can be included, so a performance that began before recording was engaged is not lost. The buffer length is defined by the user in seconds, and the retained audio is placed on the record-enabled track in correct time relation to the project.' WHERE id='71d6c6cc-8355-44aa-a3c2-ae6c48bd39d1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The set of real-time time-stretching and timing-manipulation features in the Cubase/Nuendo Sample Editor used to alter the rhythmic placement of audio without changing its pitch. Working from warp markers anchored to musical positions, it lets the user apply swing, match audio to the project tempo, and manually drag beats onto grid positions. It underlies functions such as Musical Mode and Free Warp and operates non-destructively, leaving the original audio clip intact.' WHERE id='c96b0ed6-537e-4d29-9dcd-b61efe7a241a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An Audio-menu command that renders one or more selected audio events into a single new audio file, reproducing them as they sound including any gaps and any applied fade-in, fade-out, and event volume. The user can either replace the selected events with the resulting file or create a new file while leaving the originals intact. It is commonly used to flatten edited or comped material and to commit clip-based gain and fade edits to a continuous file.' WHERE id='041cc66f-b5b2-4ac6-8cdd-05f78900b447' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dedicated editing tool used when assembling a composite take from material recorded across multiple lanes. Unlike the Object Selection and Range Selection tools, which affect a single take on an individual lane, it acts on all takes across all lanes at once: clicking and dragging a range splits every take at the range boundaries and brings the selected lane''s audio to the front, so the strongest sections of several passes can be joined into one continuous performance. It works best when the takes share the same start and end positions.' WHERE id='eb3abaa9-eb68-49c5-a9b3-825feb2b710d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The operation of dividing an audio event into separate slices at its detected hitpoints, carried out from the Hitpoints section of the Sample Editor. Once sliced, the segments can follow tempo and timing changes without altering pitch or sound quality, allowing a loop to be conformed to a new project tempo, quantized, or have individual hits replaced or extracted. Slicing is most effective on rhythmic or percussive material where hitpoints coincide with note onsets.' WHERE id='32cea144-df0c-4bde-9777-aaf6c74e503d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The setting that determines what happens to recorded material when the transport loops repeatedly between the left and right locators during cycle recording. The available modes differ for audio and MIDI: audio offers modes such as Keep History and Keep Last, while MIDI adds Mix, Overwrite, Keep Last, and the Stacked modes that place each lap on its own lane as a separate part. The chosen mode decides whether successive laps are merged, overwritten, retained as separate takes, or reduced to only the last complete pass.' WHERE id='4aeb51a7-82f8-4dd7-ac70-ebf8d5fa18c2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The function that renders the audio lying between the left and right locators, or within cycle-marker ranges, to one or more audio files. Any combination of output busses, group channels, and audio channels can be chosen as sources, and the mixdown can be produced in real time or offline in a selected file format and resolution. An option allows the exported file to be re-imported into the project pool and timeline automatically.' WHERE id='59ba29b9-f53b-413a-8618-e6ddd537c401' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Sample Editor tool for manually correcting the timing of individual events within an audio clip. Placing the pointer on a beat and dragging inserts a warp marker and moves that point to a new time position, compressing or expanding the surrounding audio while leaving pitch unchanged. It is typically used to tighten performance timing against the grid or other tracks and operates non-destructively on the audio clip.' WHERE id='02c93b4f-ce28-47dc-825e-56f7b5349be4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Project window toolbar tool that joins separate events or parts into a single part. Clicking an event with it glues that event to the following event on the same track, and the combined material becomes one part that can be handled as a unit while the underlying source events are retained inside it. It is the counterpart to splitting and is used to consolidate fragmented recordings or edits.' WHERE id='e1675ecd-153f-4147-84ef-97e61d7f9fa8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A folder-track mode that temporarily links events and parts on the contained tracks so they are edited as a coherent unit. When active, all events sharing the same start and end positions and the same playback priority are treated together, so cuts, moves, and timing edits applied to one track are applied identically to every grouped track. This preserves the phase relationships between simultaneously recorded channels, making it essential for editing multi-microphone recordings such as a drum kit.' WHERE id='3380c9b9-ed53-48b4-be5d-08774da5411f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The automatic analysis by which Cubase locates musically significant positions in an audio file and marks them with hitpoints. The algorithm examines the material for transient onsets and melodic changes, placing hitpoints at these points when audio is recorded or imported. Detection is most reliable on drums, percussion, and other rhythmic loops, and the resulting hitpoints form the basis for slicing, quantizing, groove extraction, and warp-marker creation.' WHERE id='f6b4d4fa-8ffd-41eb-8c24-0fc7024be905' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A filter control in the Sample Editor''s Hitpoints section that hides or reveals detected hitpoints according to their peak level. Raising it discards hitpoints belonging to quieter events, such as low-level bleed or crosstalk from adjacent instruments, leaving only the stronger transients, while lowering it exposes more of the softer onsets. It is used together with the other hitpoint filters to isolate the intended beats before slicing or quantizing.' WHERE id='43c511d7-ab9a-46f8-bb15-390156121513' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Activating input monitoring on a track through its Monitor button, so the incoming signal is routed through the channel and heard in the Cubase mix, with any insert effects, while preparing to record or during recording. Its automatic behaviour is governed by the Auto Monitoring preference, which offers Manual, While Record Enabled, While Record Running, and Tapemachine Style options that determine when monitoring switches on by itself. When monitoring is engaged the affected channel is processed in real time rather than by ASIO-Guard.' WHERE id='3960b8fd-9dc0-4199-856a-568fdac6bbf8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A per-clip setting that makes an audio event follow the project tempo by applying real-time time-stretching, so the audio behaves like tempo-based MIDI and adapts automatically to tempo changes. It draws on the clip''s defined tempo and length, and Cubase enables it automatically for ACID-format files that carry embedded tempo information. It can be switched on from the Sample Editor''s AudioWarp and Definition sections, the toolbar, or the Pool.' WHERE id='7ebcce9e-284f-4d4e-bed1-477eb6a4bc09' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Project window toolbar tool that silences individual events or parts without deleting them. Clicking an event with it toggles that event''s mute state, and muted events are excluded from playback while remaining in place for later reactivation. It provides event-level muting distinct from muting an entire track.' WHERE id='49cf568a-5b85-4a69-972e-9b78077f1c60' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The default Project window pointer tool, used to select, move, copy, resize, and otherwise arrange events and parts on the timeline. It is the primary tool for general arranging, and its resizing behaviour can be switched between normal sizing, sizing that moves the contents, and sizing that applies time stretch. Most standard editing gestures in the Project window are performed with it.' WHERE id='b9d14cca-6f34-4b27-ae22-59636fb18b48' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Project window toolbar tool that selects arbitrary time ranges across one or more tracks rather than whole events. The highlighted range can be cut, copied, deleted, moved, or duplicated independently of event boundaries, and it can also be used to create and edit fades. It complements the Object Selection tool by enabling edits that cut across event divisions.' WHERE id='e8d099ce-0e1a-4ffe-8421-9ce28d453897' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The recording behaviours that determine how newly recorded material interacts with existing events. The Common Record Modes govern punch and re-record behaviour, for example whether recording stops or restarts and from which position, while the Audio and MIDI Record Modes, such as Keep History and Replace for audio and New Parts, Merge, and Replace for MIDI, determine whether overlapped existing material is kept or overwritten. Together they let the user tailor whether takes accumulate, replace, or punch into prior recordings.' WHERE id='7c76016b-8f78-41c3-8ee4-d912db9a1e20' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capture safeguard in which Cubase continuously buffers incoming MIDI—and, with the equivalent setting enabled, incoming audio—even when the transport is not recording, so a performance played in stop mode or during playback can be recovered afterward. Selecting Retrospective Record from the Transport menu converts the buffered data into a part on the record-enabled track, placed in correct time relation to the project. The buffer size is configured in the preferences, and the feature acts as a net for ideas played before recording was engaged.' WHERE id='2d390bda-bef6-46dd-a5fc-6532057db166' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The time-stretching capability used to change the length and tempo of an audio selection without altering its pitch. It is available as an offline audio process, in which the target length or tempo is entered numerically and an algorithm renders the result, and interactively by resizing an event with the Object Selection tool set to Sizing Applies Time Stretch. It is used to fit audio to a required duration or tempo while preserving pitch and character.' WHERE id='ad1d1b06-4741-4a8a-add5-312df1da1514' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Project window toolbar tool that edits the project''s tempo map by dragging musical positions in the material to specific time positions. Rather than moving events, it stretches or compresses the tempo grid so that bar-and-beat positions line up with the timing of freely recorded or imported material. It is used to align the metric grid to performances that were not played to a click.' WHERE id='6825379b-d562-45e8-a25c-b9dbd8e773ed' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The integrated pitch- and time-editing system in the Cubase/Nuendo Sample Editor for monophonic audio, principally vocals. It analyses the recording and divides it into pitch segments that represent individual notes, each of which can be moved in pitch, quantized to a scale, tuned, and re-timed, and its formants shifted, to correct intonation and timing non-destructively. It is optimised for single-voice material, and the quality of the result depends on the clarity of the recording.' WHERE id='dd77db98-3720-455c-93aa-ec4c311016b7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A VariAudio edit that raises or lowers the formants of a pitch segment independently of its fundamental pitch and timing. Because formants define the spectral character and perceived timbre of a voice, shifting them changes tonal quality, making a vocal sound larger or smaller, darker or brighter, without transposing the note; it is often used to keep pitch-corrected notes sounding natural.' WHERE id='1b1c7c46-f9f7-4b45-824b-2c92d9bb82f4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The unit into which VariAudio divides analysed monophonic audio, each segment being the graphic representation of a single detected note. Segments carry the note''s pitch and timing and are the objects the user manipulates, moving them vertically to change pitch, quantizing them to semitones, tuning them, or splitting and gluing them to correct the analysis. Accurate segmentation is the basis for all subsequent VariAudio pitch and timing editing.' WHERE id='9dd637a8-e1a6-4804-9754-c50fde1025b4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A VariAudio function that flattens the natural micro-pitch fluctuation within a segment by reducing the deviation of its pitch curve from the segment''s average pitch. Applied in adjustable amounts, it smooths vibrato and drift toward a steadier tone while leaving the segment''s overall pitch and position unchanged. It is used to tighten intonation without fully quantizing the note.' WHERE id='5896244a-fb02-41ec-b3b0-c5c1fd05d4d3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In Logic Pro, Autopunch is a recording mode that automatically starts (punches in) and stops (punches out) recording at predefined locator positions, so that only the material within a set range is overwritten while audio before and after it is left untouched. It is engaged with the Autopunch button in the control bar, which displays a coloured stripe in the ruler whose left and right edges are dragged to set the punch-in and punch-out points. Because the boundaries are fixed in advance, the performer can concentrate on playing rather than on manually triggering recording, and the punch points can be placed with high precision. It is typically used with Auto Input Monitoring so the source is heard only within the punch region.' WHERE id='074ba0eb-50c2-4b6d-a596-fd142a2848b9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An auxiliary (aux) channel strip in Logic Pro is a signal-routing channel that carries no recorded track content of its own but instead receives audio from a bus, from other channel strips'' sends, or from a multi-output instrument. Aux channels are most commonly used as effect return paths, where one aux hosts a shared effect such as reverb or delay fed by the sends of several source channels, and as subgroups that combine multiple channels under a single set of level, pan, insert, and send controls. Each aux provides standard insert slots, sends, pan, and a volume fader, allowing the summed signal to be processed and routed onward before it reaches an output channel strip.' WHERE id='e8d6f6ce-42da-4b08-aef4-1a9ae447baad' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A send slot on a Logic Pro channel strip is a control, located below the insert slots, that taps the channel''s signal and routes a portion of it to a bus feeding an auxiliary channel strip. Each audio, instrument, or aux channel strip provides up to eight send slots; choosing a bus in a slot connects to (or creates) the corresponding aux, and an associated Send level knob sets how much signal is routed. The tap point relative to the fader and pan is selectable as Pre Fader, Post Fader, or Post Pan. Sends let several channels share a single effect or feed a common submix without duplicating processing on each channel.' WHERE id='63999b10-a0ab-4121-b8a6-0fe2587ca12a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Capture Recording (Flashback Capture) in Logic Pro retroactively creates a region from a performance that was played but not actively being recorded, so a spontaneous take is not lost. Because Logic continuously buffers incoming MIDI and, while the project is playing, incoming audio, clicking the Capture Recording button in the control bar converts the most recent buffered performance into a region on the selected track. For audio the project must be playing for the signal to be captured, since nothing is buffered while playback is stopped, and captured audio is limited to a short buffer; MIDI can be captured even from a stopped state. The result is functionally equivalent to having had the track record-enabled during the performance.' WHERE id='34482d64-a81c-4481-aa75-861e40debec7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The count-in setting in Logic Pro determines how many bars the metronome plays before recording actually begins, giving the performer a rhythmic lead-in to lock to the project tempo. It is toggled with the Count-in button in the control bar and configured either from the Record menu or in the Recording project settings, where the count-in length is chosen from a pop-up menu. When enabled, pressing Record starts the audible count before recording commences at the target position. The count-in affects only recording, not ordinary playback.' WHERE id='911e8205-e6ac-48df-b9fa-e8e7aa0a70bc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Cycle recording in Logic Pro is a mode in which the Cycle (loop) area repeats continuously during recording, letting the performer lay down successive passes over the same section without stopping. How the repeated passes are handled is governed by the recording settings: for MIDI, options include creating a take folder, merging passes into a single region, keeping only the current merged recording, or creating a new (optionally muted) track per repetition; for audio, cycle passes are collected into a take folder. This supports the iterative capture of multiple attempts that can later be comped or merged.' WHERE id='080bb64e-c36d-414f-86e6-5221cc20efbf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Export All Tracks as Audio Files is a Logic Pro command, reached through the File menu''s Export submenu, that renders every track in the project (audio, software instrument, and Drummer tracks) to its own individual audio file, with each file spanning from the project start so the results are time-aligned as stems. A dialog sets the file format, bit depth, and options such as bypassing effect plug-ins, including volume and pan automation, adding an audio tail, and normalization. Because each exported file reflects that track''s inserts and automation as chosen, the command is the standard way to produce stems for mixing or delivery in another application.' WHERE id='498ea9d2-f2d9-43d4-876b-e794b64014b9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Export Region as Audio File in Logic Pro writes a single selected region to a standalone audio file on disk, as distinct from bouncing the whole project or exporting complete tracks. The export dialog lets the user choose the destination, file format, and bit depth, and whether to add the resulting file to the Project Audio Browser. It is used to hand off, reuse, or archive an individual region as an independent audio file.' WHERE id='4b852bfb-b7d9-40ff-8595-de4f996fbc2f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Flatten and Merge is a Logic Pro take-folder command that first flattens the current comp, replacing the take folder with separate regions representing the selected comp sections and discarding the unused take material, and then merges those regions into a single continuous audio file. The result is one consolidated region reflecting the comped performance, in contrast to the plain Flatten command, which leaves the comp as multiple adjacent regions. It is used to commit a finished comp to a single audio file.' WHERE id='e7a3a415-5655-4fac-9d67-e345be62f7c3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An input channel strip in Logic Pro represents a physical audio input of the connected audio interface and lets that input signal be monitored and processed through inserts and sends directly in the mixer, independently of any track. Retained mainly for compatibility with earlier Logic workflows and accessed through the Environment, input channels can route hardware inputs to buses or to record-enabled tracks and were historically used to apply processing to a live input during recording. In current versions this monitoring role is largely handled by audio (track) channel strips together with input monitoring.' WHERE id='c419bcfc-ae55-4d04-9f0d-95e2d443bd55' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Input Monitoring button in Logic Pro, marked with an ''I'' in the track header and on the channel strip, routes a track''s live input signal through its inserts and sends to the output so it can be heard even when the track is not record-enabled. This lets a performer or engineer audition the incoming source and its processing before or between takes without arming the track for recording. It works alongside Auto Input Monitoring, which governs whether the input or the previously recorded material is heard during punch recording.' WHERE id='2242888e-c3dc-4639-9411-ae4511a2a946' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Join command in Logic Pro combines several selected regions into one. Joining MIDI regions produces a single region, normalizing differing region parameters such as transposition, velocity, and dynamics beforehand, while joining audio regions renders the selection nondestructively to a new mixdown audio file that replaces the originals on the track and is added to the Project Audio Browser. A related Join per Tracks option joins the selected regions separately on each track rather than into one region across tracks. It is the standard way to merge edited segments so they behave as a single unit.' WHERE id='0a8100f1-37e7-4f9f-93b5-358be77c6076' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Left input channel format in Logic Pro is a channel-strip input setting, chosen from the Channel Mode (format) button, in which only the left side of a stereo input or stereo audio file is played back and processed, with the level meter shown as a single column and indicated by two circles with the left one filled. It lets a channel take just the left channel of a stereo source, which is useful when the wanted signal is present only on the left of a stereo interface input or file. Its counterpart, the Right input format, uses the right channel instead.' WHERE id='eeb96fb5-32f0-4de9-8d17-191c1e081071' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Low Latency Mode in Logic Pro reduces monitoring latency during recording by routing the signal around latency-inducing plug-ins so that the total delay across the signal path stays below a user-set limit, defined by the Limit slider in the General Audio preferences. It is intended for real-time playing or recording of software instruments and audio after latency-heavy plug-ins have been inserted, keeping the performance in time with the project. Because affected plug-ins are effectively bypassed to meet the limit, the monitored sound may change while the mode is engaged.' WHERE id='b37acdd1-7dd6-417c-975e-b803a18ac1d4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Master channel strip in Logic Pro is a global level control that adjusts the gain of all output channel strips together while preserving the relative level balance between them, effectively acting as an overall volume for the project''s summed outputs. Unlike an output channel strip it does not itself connect to a physical interface output, and it appears as a single strip in the mixer; it is commonly used to trim total output level or to fade the entire mix. It operates on the combined output stage rather than on an individual channel''s signal.' WHERE id='3736d6aa-c1b3-4db4-8d74-6b772b26b04d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Mono input format is a Logic Pro channel-strip setting, selected with the Channel Mode (format) button, in which the channel processes a single audio channel and displays a single-column level meter, indicated by one circle. A mono-format channel records and plays one channel of audio and uses mono panning and metering, as opposed to the stereo format''s two-channel signal path. It is chosen when the source is monaural, such as a single microphone or a DI instrument.' WHERE id='550b81ff-bf23-4712-8d61-60215010b465' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An output channel strip in Logic Pro represents a physical output (or output pair) of the audio interface and carries the summed signal of all channels routed to it, providing the final inserts, level, and metering before the audio leaves the application. By default a project has one output channel strip that receives all tracks, with additional output channels available on interfaces that have more outputs. Because it is a summing and output stage it commonly hosts master-bus or mastering-type inserts but does not offer send slots; signals reach it directly or by way of buses and aux channels.' WHERE id='98a62ac9-0c98-43cb-91d8-b2eefc6ec67b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Post Fader send option in Logic Pro taps a channel''s send signal after the volume fader but before the pan control, so the amount fed to the bus follows the channel''s fader level while remaining unaffected by the pan position. It is the usual choice for effect sends such as reverb and delay, because the balance between the dry channel and its wet return then stays consistent as the channel''s level is adjusted. Logic indicates a post-fader send with a blue ring around the Send level knob.' WHERE id='da0b3e7d-b887-4d8e-93f9-4d36c99e3a7d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Post Pan send option in Logic Pro taps the send signal after both the volume fader and the pan (or balance) control, so both the amount and the stereo position sent to the bus follow the channel''s fader and pan settings. This preserves the channel''s panning in the send path, which is useful when the effect return should sit in the same stereo location as the source. Logic marks a post-pan send with a green ring around the Send level knob.' WHERE id='d16d5175-caf2-4128-bfe5-c488b6dd07b3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Pre Fader send option in Logic Pro taps a channel''s send signal before the volume fader and pan controls, so the level fed to the bus is independent of the channel''s fader and pan positions. This lets the send amount stay constant even when the channel is faded down, including to silence, which is useful for headphone or cue mixes and for certain parallel-processing setups. Logic indicates a pre-fader send with a blue ring on the Send level knob offset to the left of the send''s menu.' WHERE id='e90077eb-b181-4942-9b56-c966a010a1a6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A project bounce in Logic Pro renders the signal at an output channel strip, typically the main Stereo Out, to an audio file, capturing all unmuted tracks together with their inserts, sends, and automation as a finished mixdown. The Bounce dialog offers destinations including uncompressed PCM (such as AIFF, WAVE, or CAF), MP3, and M4A/AAC, and a processing mode of Offline, which is faster and limited to internal sources, or Realtime, which is required for external MIDI or live audio inputs, along with a bounce range and normalization options. It is the standard way to produce a stereo master or deliverable from a project.' WHERE id='a1381747-1ce6-43bd-8250-75f7478e41ea' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Punch on the fly in Logic Pro, enabled by the Allow Quick Punch-In setting, lets the user switch into and out of recording manually during playback, in real time, without setting punch locators in advance. Because recording runs continuously in the background from the moment playback starts, toggling the Record (Record Toggle) command drops in and out seamlessly at the chosen moments, overwriting only the section recorded while engaged. It contrasts with Autopunch, where the punch points are fixed beforehand; quick punch-in requires roughly twice the channel resources of the enabled tracks to keep the background recording running.' WHERE id='832ef238-3fc6-4d61-a617-3f19fe4189a0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Record Enable button, the ''R'' control in a Logic Pro track header, arms a track so it can receive and record its assigned audio or MIDI input, analogous to arming a track on a hardware multitrack recorder. When armed the button blinks red and turns solid red during actual recording; multiple tracks can be record-enabled at once, and Logic advises that simultaneously recorded audio tracks should each use a different interface input. For an audio track the button is unavailable if the channel''s Input is set to No Input.' WHERE id='2296c603-fd5e-43c5-ad9c-60535fac795c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Replace mode in Logic Pro causes newly recorded material to overwrite existing content in the recording area rather than being layered over or merged with it. Enabled by the Replace button in the control bar, it offers selectable behaviors, such as erasing the whole region section versus only the parts where new data occurs, and for MIDI erasing the region as a whole or only its note content, so the extent of the overwrite can be controlled. It is used to re-record a passage cleanly in place of the previous take.' WHERE id='2d98b8cf-0c3f-4919-b722-f466b19aad30' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Send level knob on a Logic Pro channel strip''s send slot sets how much of the channel''s signal is routed through the assigned bus to the destination aux channel strip, thereby controlling the amount of effect applied or the channel''s contribution to a submix. It works together with the send''s tap position (Pre Fader, Post Fader, or Post Pan), whose colour is reflected in the knob''s ring, and can be adjusted independently for each send. Turning it up raises the level sent to the aux without altering the channel''s own direct (dry) output.' WHERE id='dbd8f77d-587a-4811-b21f-bcd2461e2838' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Stereo input format is a Logic Pro channel-strip setting, selected with the Channel Mode (format) button, in which the channel handles a two-channel left/right signal and displays a two-column level meter, indicated by two interlocked circles. A stereo-format channel records and plays a stereo pair and provides balance or stereo panning appropriate to two channels, as opposed to the single-channel mono format. It is chosen when the source is a stereo pair, such as a stereo synthesizer, keyboard, or a stereo microphone setup.' WHERE id='061efc1a-8946-463b-8205-e8b9e4b27f98' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Stereo Out is the default main output channel strip of a Logic Pro project, the stereo output stage normally routed to the audio interface''s outputs 1-2, which sums the signals of all channels routed to it and sets the overall level heard on playback and captured in a bounce. As an output channel strip it hosts inserts, often used for master-bus or mastering processing, and provides the final fader and metering, but it does not offer send slots. All tracks and aux returns feed it directly or by way of buses unless they are routed to a different output.' WHERE id='18bc8ff2-96d4-4a27-9434-9ceda939aa9b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A take folder in Logic Pro is a container region that holds multiple recorded takes of the same section on a track, created automatically when several passes are recorded (for example during cycle recording) with Create Take Folder selected, so the alternatives are stacked and managed as a single unit. From the take folder the user auditions and selects individual takes and, using Quick Swipe Comping, assembles a composite (comp) from the best portions of different takes. Quick Swipe Comping is available for audio take folders, and the folder can later be flattened to commit the comp to ordinary regions.' WHERE id='97f44e13-6948-4d67-bb84-fa492d4f4eed' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Flattening a take folder in Logic Pro replaces the folder with individual regions that represent the current comp''s selected sections, discarding the take material not used in the comp and leaving the chosen segments as ordinary adjacent regions on the track. The related Flatten and Merge command goes a step further, additionally merging those regions into a single continuous audio file. Flattening is used to commit a comped performance so it can be edited and handled like normal regions.' WHERE id='b10a35d9-866d-4a12-8059-e25e725afbbc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A REAPER option that arms a track for recording automatically when it becomes the selected track, removing the need to toggle the record-arm button by hand. Enabled from the track''s record-arm context menu, it suits workflows in which recording readiness should follow track selection, so that moving the selection focus arms the newly selected track. It is documented in the user guide''s coverage of auto-arming tracks.' WHERE id='9360c0fb-8b69-4392-879f-b57620e5bc0c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A function, reached through the Consolidate/Export Tracks dialog, that renders selected tracks or a time selection into continuous audio files, one per track, matching item positions along the timeline. Unlike a master-mix render it processes tracks individually and can optionally omit silent sections so that each region of audio becomes its own file. It is used to tidy fragmented edits, prepare stems, or commit takes to single files.' WHERE id='2ddb1c4f-517e-46fd-b7df-1ee66e464f26' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A CPU-saving operation that renders a track''s output, its media items together with its processing chain, to a temporary audio file and then disables the track''s effects while retaining their settings, so the track can later be unfrozen and edited again. Freezing lets a project carry more plug-in-heavy tracks than the processor could sustain in real time, trading live editability of the frozen elements for reduced load. The original items and effect settings are preserved for restoration on unfreeze.' WHERE id='bd49236e-abf5-41ac-af67-765c1d583d93' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An editing command that combines two or more selected media items on a track into a single new item backed by one rendered audio file. Gluing bakes any splits, trims, fades, crossfades, and item-level adjustments into the new file, producing a continuous item that is easier to move and manage. It is commonly used to consolidate a comped or heavily edited passage into one item.' WHERE id='d871fe7a-a9b2-45da-82af-3d62209fd596' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The curve profile of a media item''s fade-in or fade-out, and of crossfades between overlapping items, chosen from several shapes ranging from linear to convex, concave, and S-curves. The shape controls how gain changes across the fade''s duration, affecting the smoothness of the transition and the perceived level through it. REAPER applies default crossfades to overlapping items, and each fade''s length and shape can be edited per item.' WHERE id='88400b44-940b-4529-90bf-923c2ce46dbb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A feature that links selected media items into a group so that selecting or moving one selects or moves them all together, preserving their relative arrangement. Items are added to a group with the group-items command and can then be moved, copied, or otherwise edited as a single unit until they are ungrouped. It is used to keep related items, such as the pieces of a comp or multitrack-aligned parts, locked together during editing.' WHERE id='28502b34-e2eb-4957-8716-5b336379dd53' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A per-item property that scales the speed at which the item''s source media is played back, set in the item properties. Values above one shorten and speed up the item while values below one lengthen and slow it; by default this also transposes the audio, unless the item''s preserve-pitch option is enabled so that only the tempo changes. It is used to conform an item to a different tempo or to create deliberate speed and pitch effects.' WHERE id='adad8807-e8d8-4ac8-90ef-52f80effad92' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A per-item, or per-track, setting that governs how a media item responds to project tempo changes, overriding the project timebase where set. The options are Time, under which the item stays fixed to absolute time; Beats (position, length, rate), under which the item''s start, length, and playback rate follow the tempo; and Beats (position only), under which only the item''s start position moves with tempo while its length is unchanged. It determines whether audio stretches, merely shifts, or is unaffected when the tempo map changes.' WHERE id='7259d334-5701-49ad-a200-e3837f22ac09' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A per-item gain control that raises or lowers the playback level of an individual media item independently of its track fader, adjustable from the item''s properties or by dragging its volume handle. Because it acts at the item level it lets different items on the same track sit at different levels and is a common step in balancing comped or assembled material. It is distinct from, and combines with, take volume and track volume.' WHERE id='c273f31b-2754-4b68-a266-d19a5d9d6412' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The project-wide setting that controls how media items, envelopes, and project markers behave when the project tempo changes. It can be set to Time, so content stays anchored to absolute time, or to Beats variants, so content''s position, and optionally its length and rate, follows the tempo. Individual tracks or items can override the project timebase with their own timebase setting.' WHERE id='66d41daa-3c26-43de-9f5a-2b2bdfbeb3a9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The signal source assigned to a track for recording, chosen from the track''s record-arm and input controls: an audio input channel, mono or stereo, from the audio device, or MIDI input from a chosen device and channel. The selected input determines what is captured when the armed track records and is also what is heard when input monitoring is active. Inputs are assigned per track and can be changed before recording.' WHERE id='a1694db8-0031-47ea-b0b5-022277386788' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='REAPER''s automatic alignment of newly recorded material to correct for the round-trip input and output latency of the audio hardware and any plugin delay, so that recorded audio lands where it was actually played rather than shifted late. The compensation is applied during recording, keeping overdubs in time with existing material, and a related option preserves plugin-delay-compensated monitoring timing in the recorded items.' WHERE id='c4563926-d9c4-44c9-86d8-91bccad45cd1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A track record mode for MIDI in which newly played events are merged into the existing item without erasing what is already there, so successive passes accumulate. It suits layering parts and capturing controller data, such as fader moves or sustain-pedal messages, over an already-recorded performance while it plays back.' WHERE id='0bbc3abd-396c-4b19-be77-63d35e3b4026' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A track record mode for MIDI in which existing events within the recorded time span are overwritten by the new pass, so previously recorded material in that range is replaced rather than merged. It is used to re-record a section cleanly while leaving MIDI outside the recorded span untouched.' WHERE id='e9ae6989-f2da-4a82-8d32-45452efd40af' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A track record mode for MIDI that replaces existing events only at the moments where new notes or events are actually played, leaving the surrounding existing material intact. Unlike full replace, which clears the whole recorded span, it overwrites only where the performer plays, making it useful for fixing individual notes or phrases within an otherwise good take.' WHERE id='270fce49-98cc-4732-a5ba-6248d4d4aa95' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The routing of a track''s live input signal to its output so it can be heard while recording or rehearsing, toggled per track and cycled through its monitoring states. REAPER offers input monitoring on, input monitoring off, and an automatic tape-style behaviour, letting the engineer choose when the input is audible relative to playback. Monitoring choices affect only what is heard, not what is recorded.' WHERE id='f5df8955-1936-4ab9-abc7-d5b61da96571' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A track monitoring state in which the live input is not routed to the track output, so the input is silent even when the track is armed. It is used when monitoring is handled externally, for example through the audio interface''s direct-monitoring path or a hardware mixer, to avoid hearing the input twice or through added software latency.' WHERE id='825e712d-4d4a-4343-ae38-f7d5d82448da' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A track monitoring state in which the live input is routed through the track to the output while active, so the armed input is heard continuously, including during playback and recording, with the track''s effects applied. It provides constant software monitoring of the input and is the mode used when latency is low enough to monitor through REAPER.' WHERE id='bf46b13e-af18-451b-97a5-c3b11e36c773' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A facility that stores multiple render jobs so they can be processed together rather than one at a time. A project''s current render settings can be added to the queue and the queued jobs run later in a batch, which is convenient for exporting many projects, stems, or format variants unattended. Each queued render retains its own settings until it is processed.' WHERE id='4c8f3913-3a0b-41b5-b4f1-e3290ca40a4a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A point placed within a media item that pins a position in the source audio to a position on the timeline, enabling localized time-stretching. Dragging one stretch marker compresses or expands the audio between it and the adjacent markers, so the timing of individual beats or syllables can be adjusted without moving the item as a whole. Multiple stretch markers allow detailed, non-destructive timing correction within a single item.' WHERE id='26f99fb7-6f94-4a90-9480-2997e278a93c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The workflow of assembling a composite performance from several recorded takes held in an item''s take lanes. With takes shown in lanes, the user auditions the alternatives and selects the best portion in each region, splitting the item and promoting the chosen take per section, to build one continuous comp that can then be flattened or glued. It is the standard method for compiling a final vocal or instrumental part from multiple passes.' WHERE id='7c59ec77-1ffe-414c-a876-64f8e35713eb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A per-take envelope that varies the pitch, or transposition, of a take over time, drawn on the take within its item. Adding it lets the take''s pitch be automated, correcting or shaping intonation across the take, independently of other takes and of track-level automation. As a take envelope it applies only to that take and follows the item when it is moved.' WHERE id='c1be450e-5504-471d-b0d2-22bc8e9840bb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A per-take envelope that automates the playback level of a specific take across its duration, drawn on the take within its item. It allows level rides, such as balancing phrases or riding a vocal, to be committed to the individual take rather than the track, so different takes can carry different level shapes. Being a take envelope it moves with the item and affects only that take.' WHERE id='07df054d-ddb2-4ee3-a159-f2e91f0a9f84' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A track monitoring behaviour, modelled on a tape machine, in which the live input is heard while the transport is stopped and while recording but is muted during ordinary playback, so the recorded audio is heard on playback instead of the input. It switches automatically between monitoring the input and monitoring the recorded material according to transport state, mirroring how a tape machine''s record head behaves.' WHERE id='ec874279-0517-4f00-a003-cc6988788fc7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A sensitivity control in REAPER''s transient-detection settings that sets how prominent a level change must be to be recognised as a transient. Raising it makes detection ignore softer onsets so that only strong hits are found, while lowering it detects more subtle transients; the resulting transient positions drive features such as transient navigation, dynamic split, and stretch-marker placement. It is adjusted to match the material so that detected transients correspond to the intended note onsets.' WHERE id='93e5e09f-41a0-481f-97d3-5fbe2239f8f8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Audio Bend is Studio One''s non-destructive audio-warping system: it analyses an audio event to detect transients and places Bend Markers at them, so the timing of the material can be adjusted by moving those markers. Once markers are present, the audio between them is time-stretched or compressed as they are dragged or quantized to the grid using a selectable stretching algorithm, letting performances be tightened or re-grooved without altering the underlying file.' WHERE id='638bab40-e303-4ec7-b11e-a3ba00de67c4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Bend Marker is an anchor point placed on an audio event, typically at a detected transient, that Studio One''s Audio Bend uses to stretch or move the audio. Dragging a single marker shifts the whole event, whereas with several markers only the audio up to the neighbouring markers on either side is bent, so the markers define the segments that are independently time-stretched when audio is nudged or quantized.' WHERE id='3adc3359-f3db-4881-a347-ccadfb1d9d05' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Bend transient-detection Threshold is a sensitivity control in the Audio Bend detection panel that sets how prominent a peak must be to register as a transient and receive a Bend Marker. Raising or lowering it yields fewer or more detected transients, letting the user tune marker density so markers land on genuine note onsets rather than on noise, or conversely catch softer hits.' WHERE id='db4bc4bd-cf94-4566-afa1-13bd1cc2365a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The Bend tool is the editing tool, chosen from the toolbar, used to create and drag Bend Markers directly on an audio event for manual timing correction. With it, moving a marker compresses the audio on one side and stretches it on the other, with the waveform changing colour to show the direction of the bend, giving hands-on control over Audio Bend warping.' WHERE id='a79ad5d1-3a1b-488b-8201-9e72dee2d4c4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In Studio One a track can hold multiple Layers, or take lanes, and loop recording captures each pass as a separate Take stored on its own layer; comping is the process of selecting the best sections across these layers to assemble a composite performance on the main track. A comping layer is therefore one of these take-holding lanes from which chosen portions are promoted, so alternate takes can be auditioned and combined without extra tracks or duplicated routing.' WHERE id='a813e906-7e21-4376-a1f3-2b0d66727874' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Event Gain is the per-event volume adjustment represented by the handle at the top-centre of an audio event, which raises or lowers that event''s level independently of the mixer channel fader and of other events. It works non-destructively on the event container rather than on the source file, and in newer versions it is complemented by an event gain envelope for drawing level changes within the event.' WHERE id='45502fbe-d742-491f-a662-914bfff89b9b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Export Stems is a Song-menu command that renders a project to multiple separate audio files at once - one per selected track or per mixer channel - rather than to a single mix. It can output raw track audio with no inserts, or, when channels are chosen, print each channel through its full effects chain, including buses and the main output, producing stems suitable for delivery, collaboration, or remixing.' WHERE id='a9cd7e82-30ba-4fed-9cf9-c8351bf283f5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Input Quantize is a recording option that applies the current Quantize settings to incoming notes in real time as a MIDI performance is recorded, so note timing conforms to the chosen grid, including any swing or humanize, as it is captured rather than afterward. It is toggled from the quantize controls in the toolbar and removes the need for a separate post-recording quantize step.' WHERE id='0eb2fee5-8804-4995-b6d1-3af4ce92ca84' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Studio One integrates Celemony''s Melodyne through ARA (Audio Random Access), a technology co-developed by PreSonus and Celemony that lets Melodyne exchange full audio and timing data with the host and behave as an integral part of the track rather than a conventional insert. Invoked on an audio event, for example through the Audio menu or an Edit with Melodyne command, it analyses the audio into editable note blobs so pitch and timing can be edited non-destructively in place, without a separate render-and-return step.' WHERE id='19dbc7ac-518a-4dda-846c-7124b2303197' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Replace is a recording mode in which recording over existing material creates new content that overwrites the overlapped portion of the original rather than being merged with it. While recording in Replace mode you do not hear the previously recorded material within the punch region, since the mode''s purpose is to substitute the new performance for the existing event across that span.' WHERE id='3431b147-82ea-4bf5-ae97-30bf300ddde9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Scratch Pad is an auxiliary arrangement area, opened alongside the main timeline via the Arranger track, in which alternate arrangement ideas - radio edits, section reorderings, loop variations - can be sketched using the same tracks and mixer as the main song without disturbing it. Multiple Scratch Pads can be kept and compared, and content can be moved between a pad and the main arrangement, making it a sandbox for experimenting with song structure.' WHERE id='adf78765-f932-4686-b6d0-f9544e108b27' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Timestretch is an audio-track Tempo mode that causes the track''s audio events to be time-stretched to follow the Song''s tempo and tempo changes, keeping them in sync when the tempo is altered, in contrast to the Don''t Follow mode which leaves events unstretched. The quality of the stretching is set by a selectable algorithm suited to the material, such as percussive, sustained or monophonic sources, so events conform to tempo without being re-recorded.' WHERE id='6a1ef875-e8dd-49ae-8b90-dd4b667175eb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Track Transform renders a track to a fixed audio state to save CPU while remaining reversible: transforming an Instrument Track to audio, or an audio track to Rendered Audio, prints the track together with its insert effects to an audio file that replaces the live track, freeing the processing those plug-ins consumed. The track can later be transformed back to Realtime, or to an Instrument Track, to restore the original instrument, parts and effects for further editing, with Studio One preserving the state needed to switch between the realtime and rendered forms.' WHERE id='401d4bba-f1f0-4a36-b90d-ffd554a0a097' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A passive magnetic component that couples an audio-frequency signal from a primary winding to one or more secondary windings through a shared laminated-iron or ferrite core, transferring the signal by mutual induction while providing galvanic isolation between the circuits it connects. Its turns ratio sets the voltage and impedance transformation between windings, so it is used for impedance matching, voltage step-up or step-down, and conversion between balanced and unbalanced connections. Its usable bandwidth, distortion and phase response are bounded by core loss, winding resistance, leakage inductance and interwinding capacitance, and by core saturation at high levels and low frequencies.' WHERE id='88f4c7b8-5988-4b4d-bab0-1ca7c147b66a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A potentiometer whose resistive element is graded so that the output varies approximately logarithmically with shaft rotation, matching the ear''s roughly logarithmic perception of loudness so that a level control sounds smoothly progressive over its travel. Also called a logarithmic or log-taper potentiometer, it is commonly manufactured by joining two or three linear track segments of differing resistance-per-degree to approximate the ideal law. It is specified for volume and gain controls, where a linear taper would crowd most of the audible change into one end of the rotation.' WHERE id='d105e1d8-10be-4512-860c-ade9b4909ce0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transformer used to interface between balanced and unbalanced audio circuits, or to derive a balanced floating connection, by presenting a symmetrical or centre-tapped winding whose two legs carry equal and opposite signals with respect to ground. Because it responds only to the differential voltage across its winding, it rejects common-mode interference and ground-referenced noise. The galvanic isolation it provides also breaks ground loops between interconnected pieces of equipment.' WHERE id='d53220e9-bf21-4268-b9f8-632730dfaa5d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A full-wave rectifier built from four diodes connected in a bridge (Graetz) configuration that conducts on both halves of the AC input cycle without needing a centre-tapped source. On each half-cycle two diagonally opposite diodes conduct and route current through the load in the same direction, so the full secondary voltage is used on both half-cycles and the output ripple appears at twice the input frequency. It is the most common rectifier arrangement in linear power supplies feeding a reservoir capacitor.' WHERE id='6aa9f62f-7ba8-45e1-b8d7-2166731d1f24' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A precision resistive attenuator, typically a rotary stepped or continuously variable pad, used in broadcast and studio audio to reduce signal level by a defined, repeatable amount while maintaining specified source and load impedances. It is realised as a balanced network such as a T, H or bridged-T section, calibrated in decibels, and is built for low distortion, accurate tracking and constant impedance so that inserting it does not disturb line matching.' WHERE id='3fdd3d38-af3d-4a0f-a34b-6d98d59be99b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A hardware equalizer intended for the broadcast signal chain, providing frequency-selective boost and cut to shape the spectral balance of programme material or to correct the response of sources and transmission links. It is implemented as passive LC or active filter sections with switchable or continuously variable band centres and gains, and is built to broadcast interface requirements for impedance, headroom and low noise so that it can sit in a line-level path without degrading the signal.' WHERE id='5496b4d5-3583-4777-8ab3-299bdf59363d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dynamics-processing unit placed in the broadcast chain to impose a firm ceiling on programme level, preventing the peaks that reach it—most critically the modulator of a transmitter—from being overdriven. It applies rapidly increasing gain reduction above a set threshold with a very high ratio, holding peaks down while leaving lower-level material largely unaffected, and is engineered to guard against overmodulation and to maximise perceived loudness within the permitted peak limits.' WHERE id='1cd743ed-700d-49f4-8041-c6ab68fb87ec' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The permitted deviation of a capacitor''s actual capacitance from its nominal marked value, expressed as a percentage (or, for very small values, in picofarads) and frequently encoded by a single-letter tolerance code. It is specified at a stated measurement frequency and temperature, and the tolerance class attainable reflects the dielectric technology: precision film and C0G/NP0 ceramic parts offer tight, symmetric tolerances, whereas high-permittivity ceramics and electrolytics have wide and often asymmetric tolerances.' WHERE id='49748c1f-d21f-4eb6-99a9-17a3f86354a2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A potentiometer whose resistive element is a track of carbon composition—carbon particles in a resin binder—printed or moulded onto an insulating substrate, over which a wiper sweeps to tap off a variable fraction of the applied voltage. It is inexpensive and available in linear and logarithmic tapers, but compared with conductive-plastic or cermet elements it shows higher contact noise, coarser resolution, greater sensitivity to temperature and humidity and a shorter wear life, so it tends to become scratchy as the track wears.' WHERE id='4a1ea2e9-cc96-4c0e-9c75-88a65012177b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transformer or inductor winding provided with an additional connection brought out at its electrical midpoint, dividing the winding into two nominally equal, series-connected halves relative to the end terminals. The centre tap is commonly grounded or used as a reference so that the two ends carry equal and opposite (push-pull) voltages. This arrangement is exploited in full-wave centre-tapped rectifiers, push-pull amplifier stages and balanced audio interfaces.' WHERE id='33981998-35ef-4e88-a0e0-0497c95564de' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An inductor used specifically to impede alternating or radio-frequency current while passing direct or lower-frequency current, exploiting the rise of inductive reactance with frequency. Wound on an iron, ferrite or powdered-iron core—or air-cored at high frequencies—it is applied to smooth rectified supplies, to block RF from entering or leaving a circuit, and to present a high AC source impedance. Its usefulness is bounded by winding resistance, self-resonance and, in cored types, core saturation.' WHERE id='13fc13f2-d75a-40b9-ac2d-ad70bd3e9dd8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The insulating structure, also called a bobbin, on which a coil or transformer winding is wound and supported, fixing the geometry of the turns and providing the clearance and creepage separation between the winding and the magnetic core. Moulded from a thermoplastic, phenolic or similar dielectric material, it carries the winding turns, locates the assembly on or around the core, and often incorporates terminal pins or tabs to which the wire ends are attached.' WHERE id='77f8a980-8f0e-4b8e-9e01-59e0d6ec5792' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A choke comprising two (or more) windings on a shared magnetic core, wound so that the differential signal or supply current in the conductors produces opposing fluxes that cancel, while common-mode current shared by the conductors produces aiding fluxes that see a high inductive impedance. It therefore suppresses common-mode noise and interference on power and signal lines with little effect on the wanted differential signal, and is a standard element of EMI/RFI filtering.' WHERE id='34485867-fb6e-48fb-9c43-6cc29b869ba1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dual potentiometer in which two independent resistive sections are operated by two coaxial shafts—an outer sleeve shaft and an inner solid shaft—so that two separate controls are stacked and adjusted independently from a single mounting hole. Each layer is keyed to its own knob, allowing two related parameters, such as bass and treble or level and pan, to share one panel position while remaining separately variable.' WHERE id='79ee0208-a56e-4f6a-820f-0e63b7fc18d9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A slide (linear-motion) potentiometer whose resistive track is a conductive-plastic film over which a multi-finger wiper travels, used as a level control on mixing consoles and other audio equipment. The conductive-plastic element gives smooth, low-noise operation, fine resolution and long mechanical life, and the track can be shaped to an audio (logarithmic) law calibrated in decibels; typical construction includes a dust-sealed slot and, in motorised versions, a servo drive and a position-sensing track.' WHERE id='830e0d56-4f25-475f-b22f-1ee215bdb27a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A potentiometer whose resistive element is a film of conductive plastic—conductive particles dispersed in a plastic binder—giving a continuous, essentially infinite-resolution track with low contact noise and long rotational life. Compared with carbon-composition elements it offers lower noise, better linearity and far greater wear endurance, which is why it is preferred for high-quality audio level and control applications.' WHERE id='3bc64762-6a75-43b6-be93-04bc45788876' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The power dissipated within the magnetic core of a transformer or inductor when it carries alternating flux, comprising hysteresis loss—the energy expended each cycle in reversing the magnetic domains, proportional to frequency and to the enclosed B-H loop area—and eddy-current loss, the resistive heating from currents induced in the core and limited by using thin insulated laminations or high-resistivity ferrites. It rises with flux density and frequency, contributes to heating and reduced efficiency, and in audio components is a source of level-dependent distortion, particularly at low frequencies where flux density is greatest.' WHERE id='f1149749-3f63-4e16-b201-5acc9391b5c3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dual in-line package containing an array of small manually set switches—usually slide or rocker single-pole single-throw elements—sharing a body with the pin spacing and footprint of a DIP integrated circuit for direct mounting on a printed circuit board. Each switch sets one bit of a configuration held on the board, so DIP switches are used to select addresses, options or operating modes that are changed rarely and set by hand rather than under program control.' WHERE id='caa3a60a-4ab4-4525-80e9-874b5b012b3b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The isolation and matching transformer at the heart of a passive direct box (DI), used to connect a high-impedance, unbalanced instrument-level source to a low-impedance, balanced microphone input. Through its turns ratio it steps the impedance and voltage down, converts the unbalanced signal to a balanced one and provides galvanic isolation that breaks ground loops between a stage instrument and the mixer; its bandwidth and level handling must be sufficient to pass the instrument signal without core saturation.' WHERE id='6a88c932-ca66-4e67-8859-b6492b64bca7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An equalizer whose gain and filtering stages are built from discrete components—individual transistors, resistors and capacitors—rather than from integrated-circuit operational amplifiers, so that the active gain elements are realised in a discrete topology. Such designs are chosen for their headroom, drive capability and sonic character, and the term distinguishes the signal-path implementation from op-amp-based equalizers of otherwise similar function.' WHERE id='c7af9276-99bf-4431-b431-a46b0d6b6dd1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A potentiometer containing two separate resistive sections mounted on and operated by a single common shaft, so that both wipers move together and track one another as the shaft is turned. It is used where two channels or paths must be adjusted simultaneously and kept matched—such as the left and right channels of a stereo level control—and its channel-to-channel tracking tolerance across the rotation is a key specification.' WHERE id='01edd582-469c-4223-97b8-54cc7d5863f1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dedicated hard-surfaced, acoustically reflective room used to create reverberation by electro-acoustic means: a loudspeaker reproduces the dry signal into the room and one or more microphones capture the resulting reverberant field, which is then mixed with the direct signal. The room''s dimensions, surface materials and loudspeaker and microphone placement set the reverberation time and character; the method predates and informed later mechanical (plate and spring) and digital reverberation devices.' WHERE id='181c5046-3825-4148-80aa-ca02afd4e41a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transformer built on a laminated core stacked from interleaved E-shaped and I-shaped steel laminations, the classic shell-type construction in which the windings surround the centre limb of the E while the I pieces close the magnetic circuit. Interleaving and butting the E and I stampings minimises the effective air gap and stray flux, and the laminations are thin and insulated to limit eddy-current loss; the geometry is widely used for mains and audio transformers because it is economical and mechanically robust.' WHERE id='2b72dc48-31b0-4859-bdd0-f190073f5e41' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A grounded conductive layer—often called a Faraday shield—placed between the windings of a transformer, or around a signal conductor, to intercept capacitive coupling by giving electrostatically coupled currents a low-impedance path to ground. In a transformer it reduces the interwinding capacitance that would otherwise pass high-frequency noise and common-mode interference from primary to secondary, improving isolation and noise rejection without affecting the intended magnetic coupling.' WHERE id='a9e81b3e-b4cd-4024-94ce-2e85a7bbbeca' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A rotary electromechanical control that converts shaft rotation into a digital output rather than a variable resistance, using optical or mechanical (contacting) sensing of a coded or slotted disc. An incremental encoder outputs two phase-quadrature pulse trains whose count gives relative movement and whose phase relationship gives direction, while an absolute encoder outputs a unique code for each position; unlike a potentiometer it typically rotates continuously without end stops and often includes detents and an integral push switch.' WHERE id='92378672-9423-4fb1-a8af-63f434a2fab3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The control knob fitted to the shaft of a rotary encoder, providing the tactile grip a user turns—and often presses—to generate the encoder''s digital output. Because an encoder usually rotates endlessly without hard stops, its knob bears no fixed pointer relationship to a value and instead commonly works with the encoder''s detents to give discrete, felt steps; on control surfaces it may incorporate an illuminated collar or a push action.' WHERE id='db106065-6acd-44c0-8d87-5ae01417b2dc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The single equivalent resistance placed in series with an ideal reactive element in a component''s model to account for its total resistive (dissipative) losses at a given frequency, most commonly quoted for capacitors. In a capacitor it lumps the resistance of leads, electrodes and contacts together with dielectric loss into one figure that governs self-heating under ripple current, the depth of the impedance minimum at self-resonance and the high-frequency performance; it is frequency- and temperature-dependent.' WHERE id='9d363d14-d9ef-449a-a957-952743699077' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The common abbreviation for equivalent series resistance, the lumped series resistance in a component model that represents its total dissipative losses at a specified frequency, quoted chiefly for capacitors. A low ESR minimises self-heating from ripple current and lowers the impedance at self-resonance, which makes it a critical selection parameter for power-supply smoothing and decoupling capacitors; it rises as aluminium electrolytic capacitors age and lose electrolyte.' WHERE id='8f7a17f9-69bf-4b5e-bf04-b6f71839622b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An inductor placed in the smoothing filter of a rectified DC power supply, in series with the load, to oppose changes in current and so reduce the ripple remaining after rectification. Working with a following (and sometimes preceding) reservoir capacitor to form an LC low-pass filter, it presents a high reactance to the ripple frequency while passing the DC component with only its winding resistance, and is built on a gapped iron core so it can carry the DC bias without saturating.' WHERE id='50db80ad-bb46-4df0-8d95-a15a3dbb86fe' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A switch designed to be operated by foot pressure so that the hands remain free, used for functions such as engaging an effect, muting a channel, operating talkback or controlling transport. It is available in momentary or latching action and in single- or multi-way forms, built into a rugged enclosure or pedal able to withstand repeated foot actuation, and may be wired as a simple contact closure or as part of a controller.' WHERE id='d7fa4d1f-9169-4297-a1ec-de021b45ab57' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A rectifier that converts both halves of an alternating input into a unidirectional output, delivering current to the load on every half-cycle rather than only one. It is realised either with two diodes and a centre-tapped secondary or with four diodes in a bridge, and because output pulses occur twice per input cycle the ripple frequency is double the supply frequency and is easier to smooth, giving higher average output and better transformer utilisation than a half-wave circuit.' WHERE id='fde7c0dd-6e24-4cb5-9ae2-c625a7dabd72' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistive attenuator arranged in an H-shaped network—series resistors in both legs of a balanced line at the input and output with a shunt resistor bridging their midpoints—that reduces signal level by a defined amount while presenting matched impedances at both ports. It is the symmetrical, balanced form of the T-pad, maintaining balance to ground on each side, and is used to pad down balanced audio lines by a calibrated number of decibels without disturbing source and load matching.' WHERE id='306c4970-626d-4d20-b77a-a188811444cd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The simplest rectifier, a single diode in series with the load that conducts on only one polarity of the AC input and blocks the other, so current flows through the load during just one half of each cycle. The output is a train of unidirectional pulses at the supply frequency with large gaps, giving high ripple that requires heavy filtering, poor transformer utilisation and a DC magnetising component in any supply transformer; it is used only where current and ripple demands are modest.' WHERE id='9ef1b85b-0ed2-4354-8c60-c3a8b6859f87' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An auxiliary or oppositely-phased winding arranged so that hum voltages induced by stray magnetic fields—chiefly at the mains frequency—are equal and opposite in the two winding sections and therefore cancel at the output. Used in transformers, in the supplies of valve equipment and in the sensing coils of instruments, the technique relies on symmetry so that externally coupled interference, being common to both halves, subtracts while the wanted signal, developed differentially, is preserved.' WHERE id='115315f0-88eb-4e71-8932-943bcc45fa68' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A manually operated switch that incorporates an internal light source—an incandescent lamp or, more commonly, one or more LEDs—so that the actuator or a legend can be lit to indicate the switch''s state or to draw attention. The illumination may be wired to follow the switch contacts or driven independently, and such switches are used on control panels where an operator must see at a glance whether a function is engaged.' WHERE id='0e053bac-5b2b-4ec3-b86e-7bdf4bc0fc77' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A light-emitting diode whose semiconductor junction is engineered to emit in the infrared band, typically at wavelengths of the order of 850 to 950 nm and invisible to the eye. Made from materials such as gallium arsenide or gallium aluminium arsenide, it emits incoherent infrared when forward-biased and is used as the emitter in optocouplers, slot and reflective sensors, remote controls and other opto-electronic links, paired with a matched photodiode or phototransistor.' WHERE id='c8ce218d-d7a3-4e27-beb2-646823ee4db4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transformer placed at the signal input of an amplifier, mixer or other device to interface the incoming line to the circuit, providing impedance matching, voltage step-up and conversion of a balanced source to the circuit reference while galvanically isolating the input. By floating the input and responding only to the differential signal it rejects common-mode noise and breaks ground loops, and its turns ratio and core determine the available gain, bandwidth and distortion of the front end.' WHERE id='ee4d2402-67b4-4fcd-8421-ed06b4113ee4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transformer used to couple the output of one amplifier stage to the input of the next, transferring the signal while providing impedance transformation, DC isolation and, where centre-tapped, a means of driving a push-pull stage in phase opposition. Once common in valve equipment for matching a stage''s high output impedance to the following grid circuit and for phase splitting, its bandwidth and distortion are limited by leakage inductance, winding capacitance and core nonlinearity.' WHERE id='d9342f17-4ae7-493e-99ff-cbb256aaeb44' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The parasitic capacitance that exists between the separate windings of a transformer, for example between primary and secondary, arising because adjacent turns at different potentials are separated only by thin insulation. It forms an unwanted electrostatic coupling path that lets high-frequency noise and common-mode interference pass across the isolation barrier and degrades high-frequency response and isolation, and it is deliberately reduced by winding geometry and by inserting a grounded electrostatic (Faraday) shield between the windings.' WHERE id='1af1cae4-811f-4a52-a940-912a2e213a28' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A control cap fitted to the projecting shaft of a potentiometer, rotary switch or encoder, giving the operator an appropriately sized, comfortable grip to set the control and, where marked, a pointer or skirt indicating its position. Moulded or machined from plastic or metal and secured by a push-fit collet, a set screw against a flatted (D-shaped) shaft or a splined bore, its diameter and profile determine the mechanical advantage and resolution of adjustment.' WHERE id='7b811822-07f1-4a2d-abee-d5e341a61c18' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An attenuator built from two resistors in an L-shaped arrangement—one in series and one in shunt—used to reduce signal level; in its constant-impedance loudspeaker form the two resistors are varied together so that the amplifier continues to see a constant load while the power delivered to the speaker is changed. Unlike a symmetrical T- or H-pad it matches impedance correctly at only one port, so it is applied where a defined source or load impedance must be held on one side, classically as a loudspeaker level control.' WHERE id='21a82b31-7172-44d7-9dac-df004e19bdf2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A packaged display subassembly that combines a liquid-crystal display panel with its driver and controller electronics, and usually a backlight and connector, so that it can be driven from a host through a simple digital interface. Character modules built around controllers such as the Hitachi HD44780 present a fixed grid of alphanumeric cells addressed over a parallel or serial bus, while graphic modules address an array of pixels; the LCD itself only modulates transmitted or reflected light and emits none, so a backlight is added for low-light legibility.' WHERE id='8be00408-c20f-43b5-9f18-d5fefbebda42' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The component of a transformer''s inductance produced by flux that links one winding but does not couple into the other, so it is not shared between primary and secondary and behaves as an inductance effectively in series with the windings. It arises from imperfect magnetic coupling and depends on winding geometry; in audio transformers it limits high-frequency response and, together with load and winding capacitances, can cause resonant peaking, so it is minimised by interleaving the primary and secondary sections.' WHERE id='ae43041f-eec9-4e05-a192-c53d3bac5f0a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A display device consisting of a linear row of closely spaced light-emitting diode segments that are lit progressively to represent a signal level as an illuminated bar or a moving dot. Driven by a level-sensing circuit or a dedicated driver IC that lights successive segments as the input crosses stepped thresholds, it serves as a level, meter or status indicator, and its segment colours are often graded so that the upper, overload segments read red.' WHERE id='786e2304-798c-4e4b-9cd5-244f34cf8e96' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An electromechanical switch actuated by the physical movement or presence of a machine part, used to detect the position or end of travel of that part and to signal or interrupt a control circuit accordingly. A moving lever, plunger or roller mechanically operates the internal snap-action contacts when the monitored element reaches a set position, providing position sensing, interlocking or motion limiting; ruggedised versions are specified for industrial and motion-control use.' WHERE id='4ee40126-f043-4fb9-a9be-774c816297b5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A potentiometer whose resistance between the wiper and an end terminal varies in direct proportion to shaft position, so that equal increments of rotation produce equal increments of resistance along a uniform resistive element. It is used where a proportional, even relationship between control setting and resistance or voltage is wanted—such as bias, balance, panning and instrumentation adjustments—rather than the perceptually weighted response of an audio (logarithmic) taper.' WHERE id='b8f664ce-d565-4705-ac0b-5386fd4ba625' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An echo chamber whose interior surfaces are hard and highly reflective, or acoustically ''live'', giving a long reverberation time and dense reflections when a loudspeaker feeds signal into it and a microphone returns the reverberant field. The reflective treatment maximises reverberant energy and decay, distinguishing it from a more absorptively treated (''dead'') space, and its dimensions and surface finish set the character of the reverberation added to a recording or mix.' WHERE id='21599ea1-af21-4192-9d79-3c622deba47c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A power transformer whose primary winding is connected directly to the AC mains supply, providing the voltage transformation and, critically, the galvanic isolation between the hazardous mains and the lower-voltage secondary circuits of the equipment. It steps the mains voltage up or down to the levels the power supply requires, and because its primary sits at mains potential it must meet safety requirements for insulation, creepage and clearance distances, dielectric strength and thermal protection so that a single fault cannot make accessible parts live. The isolation between primary and secondary is fundamental to operator safety and must never be compromised.' WHERE id='b0c31971-d7f6-423d-803f-d9595f089912' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Artificial reverberation produced by launching the audio signal into a physical, vibrating mechanical medium and recovering the delayed, multiply-reflected result, rather than using an acoustic room or a digital algorithm. In the spring type a transducer twists a helical spring whose torsional waves reflect back and forth before a pickup recovers them; in the plate type a driver excites bending waves in a large suspended metal sheet sensed by contact pickups, with damping setting the decay. Both convert the electrical signal to mechanical vibration and back, imparting a characteristic reverberant decay.' WHERE id='badaa2bd-aeab-45eb-b8a7-7d9fac48c4c1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A moving-coil (d''Arsonval) volume-unit meter in which a pointer is deflected across a calibrated scale by current through a coil in a magnetic field, driven from the audio signal through a copper-oxide or semiconductor rectifier. Its defined dynamic response—a 300 ms rise to 99 percent of a steady tone with specified overshoot, giving an approximately average-responding, loudness-related reading calibrated in volume units—distinguishes it from a peak meter, and its standardised ballistics are what make readings comparable between installations.' WHERE id='670a7188-90a7-4b1f-871e-72d59fd1007f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small audio transformer used at a microphone''s output, or at a preamplifier''s microphone input, to step up the low-level, low-impedance signal, present a balanced and galvanically isolated connection, and match impedances between the microphone and the following stage. Its turns ratio provides a noise-free voltage gain ahead of the first active stage while its balanced secondary rejects common-mode interference on the cable; performance is bounded by core saturation at low frequencies and by leakage inductance and winding capacitance at high frequencies.' WHERE id='1a14583c-ffbc-4ed1-81b4-abce83b26c57' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An analogue indicating instrument of the d''Arsonval type in which a lightweight coil suspended on jewelled pivots or a taut band sits in the field of a permanent magnet, so that current through the coil produces a torque that deflects an attached pointer against a restoring spring. Deflection is proportional to the average current, giving a linear scale for DC; to read AC or audio signals the current is first rectified, and shunts and multipliers extend the basic movement to measure larger currents and voltages.' WHERE id='abaff110-f6c3-42a1-a721-f8b4427c7ec1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A potentiometer assembly comprising several independent resistive sections (gangs) stacked on and operated by a single common shaft, so that all sections are adjusted together and track one another as the shaft turns. It is used where three or more channels or parameters must move in unison—such as multi-channel level control—and the inter-section tracking tolerance across the travel is a defining specification.' WHERE id='78c8d03b-320c-4024-b3c7-eff84ab7f387' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A trimmer (preset) potentiometer whose wiper is driven through a lead-screw or worm mechanism, so that many turns of the adjusting screw—commonly of the order of a dozen or more—are needed to move the wiper across the full resistive element. The high mechanical reduction gives very fine setting resolution and stable, vibration-resistant adjustment for calibration presets on a circuit board, at the cost of slower adjustment than a single-turn trimmer.' WHERE id='acd0ab72-20be-4deb-9ef3-bb03bc09e8c5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A display built from organic light-emitting diodes, in which layers of organic semiconductor emit light directly when current passes through each addressed pixel, so no backlight is required. Because every pixel is self-emissive it can be switched fully off to give true blacks and very high contrast with wide viewing angles and fast response; the panel may be passive- or active-matrix addressed and is used for small equipment displays as well as larger screens.' WHERE id='17ae0a4a-5cdf-4c59-b9c9-fec9a37806f1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A component that transfers a signal between two electrically isolated circuits by light, packaging a light-emitting diode facing a photodetector—photodiode, phototransistor, photo-Darlington or photo-triac—within one opaque body. The input LED converts the electrical signal to light that the detector reconverts to current with no ohmic connection between input and output, providing galvanic isolation that blocks high voltages and ground-loop currents while passing the wanted signal; its key parameters are current-transfer ratio, isolation voltage and switching speed.' WHERE id='b6a1c262-d002-437c-99a7-4f65e0236fca' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A circuit that captures and holds the maximum instantaneous amplitude reached by an input signal, typically by charging a capacitor through a diode—or a precision op-amp rectifier—up to the signal peak and holding that voltage until it is reset or allowed to decay. It provides the fast-attack, slow-release envelope needed to register transients, and is the sensing element behind peak-reading meters, overload and clip indicators and the level-detection side-chains of limiters and compressors.' WHERE id='44f3ea36-ea3b-4cdc-b485-def33f5effb7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An indicator LED driven by a peak-sensing circuit that lights when the instantaneous signal level reaches or exceeds a set threshold, warning that the signal is approaching clipping or overload. Positioned to trip a few decibels below the clipping point, it flashes on transients that a slower averaging meter would miss, giving the operator an immediate visual cue to reduce gain.' WHERE id='6d6c1d91-eb63-48f3-9847-0bbc05756a8d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A level meter whose ballistics follow the instantaneous peak of the signal, using a fast attack to reach the true peak of brief transients and a deliberately slow decay so the reading can be seen. In contrast to the average-responding VU meter it shows the peaks that risk overload or clipping; the standardised broadcast form, the quasi-peak programme meter (PPM), has defined integration and fall-back times that make readings consistent between installations.' WHERE id='d73fa9cc-f704-44e1-b939-c1beb7adbe3b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A professional slide fader manufactured by Penny + Giles using a conductive-plastic resistive track and a precision multi-finger wiper, long regarded as a reference-grade level control on high-end mixing consoles. The conductive-plastic element gives low noise, fine resolution, an accurately shaped audio (dB-calibrated) law and long operating life, and the range includes dust-sealed, dual-track and motorised (automation) versions; the name has become a byword for high-quality studio faders.' WHERE id='68476760-2b8c-4674-be00-33f1f2841660' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone-line audio transformer designed to operate correctly while 48 V phantom powering is present on its winding. The winding on the powered side is center-tapped or otherwise balanced so that the phantom feed current divides equally between the two signal legs, and the equal, opposing direct currents in each half produce no net magnetizing flux, preventing core saturation and the distortion that unbalanced DC would cause. Such transformers are electrostatically shielded and wound for tight interwinding balance so that high common-mode rejection is preserved under phantom conditions.' WHERE id='c6532489-248e-4405-97d2-13ed260e054b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An electromechanical reverberation device that generates artificial reverberation by exciting a large, thin suspended sheet of steel with a moving-coil transducer driven by the audio signal. Bending waves propagate across the plate and reflect from its edges, building a dense, diffuse field that one or more contact pickups convert back to an electrical signal, while a movable damping pad set near the plate adjusts the decay time. Introduced commercially in the late 1950s, the plate offers high echo density and a bright, smooth tail that made it a studio standard before digital reverberation.' WHERE id='94348055-a4f6-4048-aea1-a8991d2931cc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A three-terminal variable resistor in which a wiper contact moves along a resistive element, tapping off a voltage that is an adjustable fraction of the voltage applied across the element''s two ends. Connected across a source it acts as a continuously variable voltage divider, while use of the wiper and one end terminal alone yields a two-terminal variable resistance. The resistive element may be carbon composition, conductive plastic, cermet, or wound wire, and the relationship between resistance and wiper travel, called the taper or law, may be linear or logarithmic to suit the application.' WHERE id='769acdcb-6896-4122-a59b-ee31e326465b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An iron-cored inductor used in a power supply to oppose changes in current and thereby smooth the pulsating output of a rectifier. Placed in series in the supply, it presents high reactance to the ripple frequencies while passing direct current with low resistive loss, so residual hum at the output is reduced. Its core is usually built with an air gap to prevent saturation under the steady direct current it carries in addition to the ripple.' WHERE id='637e4783-95a7-479c-ae60-e23a7c288296' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An electromechanical relay built to switch substantial load currents and voltages, in which a coil-driven electromagnet actuates one or more contact sets to make or break a power circuit under the control of a lower-power signal. Its contacts are rated for the make and break currents and inrush of resistive, inductive, or motor loads and are sized and spaced to interrupt the resulting arc and to isolate the control side from the load side. Contact material, load rating, and expected electrical life are specified for defined categories of load.' WHERE id='6de53894-f2f2-42a9-a4d2-4b5c9ea83ca0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transformer that transfers electrical energy between circuits by mutual magnetic induction between windings on a shared core, used in equipment power supplies to convert the incoming mains voltage to the higher or lower voltages the circuitry requires while providing galvanic isolation from the supply. The ratio of secondary to primary voltage follows the ratio of the respective turns, and the core and windings are sized for the volt-ampere throughput and the operating frequency. Its isolation, insulation class, and thermal limits are fixed by its construction and rating.' WHERE id='ffaf2049-40b8-4715-bfab-297310bc14e1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small potentiometer intended to be adjusted only occasionally, typically once during calibration or servicing rather than by the end user. Usually mounted directly on the circuit board and turned with a screwdriver or tuning tool, it sets a fixed operating point such as bias, gain, or offset. Preset types are made in open carbon and sealed cermet single-turn forms and in multiturn leadscrew forms, trading a limited adjustment life for compactness and setting stability.' WHERE id='e7d899cc-e6c8-4a04-b617-f6f4dc5fd064' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The winding of a transformer to which the source or input energy is applied; energized by the driving circuit, its alternating current establishes the changing magnetic flux in the core that induces voltage in the other windings. Its turn count relative to the secondary sets the voltage and impedance transformation, and its wire gauge and insulation are chosen for the current it carries and the voltage it withstands. In a signal transformer the primary presents the source with the reflected load impedance scaled by the square of the turns ratio.' WHERE id='9914a6a4-0bba-4522-b04e-11fd0e3e3241' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A limiter that acts on a complete program signal to hold its level below a defined ceiling, protecting a following stage such as a broadcast transmitter, recorder, or transmission link from overload. It applies rapid, high-ratio gain reduction only when the signal exceeds a set threshold, with attack and release times chosen to catch peaks while remaining sonically unobtrusive on the overall mix. In broadcast use it guards against overmodulation and enforces peak level limits.' WHERE id='bc837fe7-4218-4413-92c1-52779af04565' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A rotary control that combines an incremental rotary encoder with a momentary pushbutton switch actuated by pressing the shaft along its axis. Rotation produces a two-channel quadrature output whose pulse sequence and phase indicate the number of steps and the direction, while the integrated switch provides a separate momentary contact for select or enter functions. Commonly detented and free to rotate without end stops, it is widely used as a single-knob menu and parameter control on audio and electronic equipment.' WHERE id='9513d7a9-f07f-4a48-8adb-e741e868f4cb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A switch actuated by axial pressure on a plunger or cap that opens or closes one or more contact sets, either only while it is held (momentary) or alternately latching between states on successive presses (push-push or alternate action). Contact arrangements range from a single pole and throw to several, and the mechanism may give tactile or audible feedback at the point of actuation. It is specified by its contact configuration, electrical rating, actuation force, and mechanical life.' WHERE id='fbc9be76-d4fe-4ada-b0eb-2be0fd7c8290' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transformer built on a core formed from a continuous strip of grain-oriented silicon steel wound and machined to a round cross-section, giving a gapless closed magnetic path with a circular section. The round former allows tightly and evenly wound bobbins with a short mean turn length, yielding low leakage flux, low external stray field, and low audible mechanical hum, which makes the type popular in high-quality audio power supplies. It offers efficiency and low-noise behaviour comparable to a toroidal core while retaining an easily wound bobbin.' WHERE id='fa6985ae-421a-48fe-b33e-85f192f5f655' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A series assembly of rectifier elements connected and packaged as a single unit so that their reverse-voltage ratings add, allowing the stack to block a higher peak inverse voltage than any one element could withstand. Early stacks were built from series selenium plates; later ones series-connect silicon diodes, sometimes with voltage-sharing resistors to equalize the reverse voltage across each element. Such stacks are used chiefly for high-voltage rectification, as in valve equipment and high-tension supplies.' WHERE id='c6ce5c84-21a2-409d-8fad-a138711e944d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A relay that uses one or more reed switches as its contacts, operated by the magnetic field of a surrounding energizing coil rather than by a moving armature. When the coil is energized the ferromagnetic reeds inside a sealed glass envelope magnetize and their overlapping ends attract and close; de-energizing lets their own stiffness spring them open. The hermetically enclosed, low-mass contacts give fast operation, long mechanical life, and clean switching of low-level signals, though at modest current ratings.' WHERE id='6f459c9c-7b1a-4b23-8464-afcb5432bc78' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A switch consisting of two ferromagnetic contact blades, or reeds, sealed inside an inert-gas or evacuated glass tube with their ends overlapping and slightly apart. An external magnetic field from a permanent magnet or a coil magnetizes the reeds so that the opposite poles formed at the gap attract and the contacts close; removing the field allows the reeds'' stiffness to spring them open again. The hermetic enclosure protects the contact surfaces from contamination, giving long life and reliable operation.' WHERE id='4644622f-d0e2-44cf-91db-59bfcbcf336a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A potentiometer whose resistance-versus-rotation law is the mirror image of a standard logarithmic (audio) taper, so that most of the resistance change occurs early in the travel and becomes gradual toward the end. Also called an anti-log or reverse-audio taper, it is used where the required control action is the reflection of an audio-taper response, for example on a control wired to operate from the opposite direction or one that must complement a logarithmic element in ganged or balance circuits. The taper is set by the graded conductivity of the resistive track.' WHERE id='3784f83f-51c3-4f45-ad2f-34e928c48d26' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A two-terminal variable resistor used to control the current in a circuit by inserting an adjustable amount of resistance in series with the load. It is functionally a potentiometer connected using only the wiper and one end terminal, and it is constructed, often as a wirewound element, to dissipate the power produced by the load current it carries. Moving the wiper varies the resistance in circuit and therefore the current.' WHERE id='419b79b8-942c-4946-9cf7-14947998f7e1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The maximum root-mean-square value of the alternating (ripple) current that a component, most commonly an electrolytic filter capacitor, may carry continuously without exceeding its permitted internal temperature rise. Ripple current flowing through the component''s equivalent series resistance dissipates power as heat, so the rating is quoted at a stated frequency and ambient temperature and is derated under other conditions. Operating above it shortens the component''s service life through excessive self-heating.' WHERE id='b074e4d4-eb46-4d77-af2e-505b3eacdc17' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A switch whose actuator is a pivoted lever that rocks, or seesaws, about a central axis, so that pressing one end raises the other and moves the contacts between positions. It gives a clear on/off action through a snap mechanism, is available in single- and multi-pole and in momentary or maintained forms, and is commonly panel-mounted with an integral legend or illumination. It is rated by its contact configuration, voltage and current, and mechanical life.' WHERE id='c697e590-2f91-4e6a-a961-1b75f17fb0e5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A rotary control that reduces signal level by an amount set by the shaft position, either continuously by means of a potentiometer element or in discrete calibrated steps by means of a switched resistor network. In precision and professional audio use the switched form arranges fixed resistors around a rotary switch to give repeatable, accurately known attenuation with close channel-to-channel matching and, in suitable topologies, constant input or output impedance. It is characterized by its attenuation range, step size, and terminating impedance.' WHERE id='b2daf2e7-7440-4b55-a0b4-bacf4bfd0366' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A rotary electromechanical or opto-magnetic transducer that converts shaft angular position or motion into an electrical output. Incremental types emit two quadrature pulse trains whose counted edges give displacement and whose phase relationship gives direction, while absolute types output a coded word unique to each shaft position. In control-panel use the detented incremental mechanical form provides endless, stop-free adjustment of a digital parameter, unlike a potentiometer with fixed end limits.' WHERE id='91104c45-a19c-490e-928d-1dc2e7c18851' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A potentiometer in which the wiper is moved along an arc-shaped resistive element by rotation of a shaft, typically over an angle of the order of 270 degrees for a single-turn part. Turning the shaft varies the wiper position and hence the output voltage ratio or the resistance in circuit according to the element''s taper. Single-turn and multiturn, single and ganged, and detented or continuous versions are made for panel controls and calibration.' WHERE id='f8c0ea15-4c7e-4e90-b170-c801d6ed0fea' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A multi-position rotary switch used to select one of several circuit paths or settings by turning a shaft that steps a wiper between fixed contact positions. Each detented position connects the common, or pole, terminal to a different contact, and several switch wafers stacked on a common shaft allow multiple circuits to be selected together in step. It is defined by its number of poles and positions and by whether it is shorting or non-shorting between adjacent positions.' WHERE id='e58688b9-d53b-4588-92a3-f4053971619d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A switch operated by rotating a shaft that carries a moving contact past a set of stationary contacts arranged around it, connecting the common terminal to a selected fixed contact at each detented angular position. Built from one or more wafers, or decks, to provide multiple poles, and made in shorting (make-before-break) or non-shorting (break-before-make) forms, it is used to route signals or select ranges among several discrete states. Its rating covers contact configuration, current and voltage, and mechanical life.' WHERE id='749b4375-9d55-4923-bfcf-ecc94f46f2a5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The winding of a transformer in which voltage is induced by the alternating magnetic flux set up by the primary, delivering the transformed energy to the load. Its voltage relates to the primary voltage in proportion to the ratio of secondary to primary turns, and a transformer may carry several secondaries, tapped or separate, to provide multiple output voltages. Its wire gauge and insulation are chosen for the secondary current and for its voltage relative to ground and to the other windings.' WHERE id='896e7c34-4e99-4062-aee6-72dcd93e1d0d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A numeric display made of seven elongated light-emitting segments arranged in a figure-eight pattern, usually with a decimal point, that are selectively energized to form the digits zero to nine and a limited set of letters. It is implemented most commonly with light-emitting diodes wired in common-anode or common-cathode form, or with liquid-crystal or fluorescent elements, and is driven directly or through a decoder that maps a binary-coded value to the segment pattern. In multi-digit arrays the digits are usually driven in rapid succession by multiplexing.' WHERE id='e83fc0aa-f7ee-4aa9-a959-8be64f15ea44' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A light-emitting diode used as an indicator to show the presence, state, or level of a signal rather than to provide illumination. Forward current through the diode''s semiconductor junction produces light whose colour is set by the material system, and a series resistor or a constant-current drive limits the current to a safe value. In audio equipment such indicators show signal-present, peak or clip, and channel-activity conditions.' WHERE id='71f7dd98-f059-4941-bda8-bb6f0e5e7b5e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small electromechanical relay designed to switch low-level signal currents and voltages rather than power loads, in which a coil actuates lightweight contacts to route signals under electrical control. Its low contact mass and gold or other low-resistance plating give reliable make and break of small currents with low, stable contact resistance, suiting audio routing, muting, and instrumentation. It is rated for a modest switching current and for defined minimum, or dry-circuit, loads below which reliable contact is still assured.' WHERE id='66279d2f-0b08-4890-aa66-d72d631be047' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A potentiometer whose wiper is moved in a straight line along a linear resistive element by a sliding actuator, so that the knob position maps directly to a length of travel. The linear layout permits a long throw and precise setting and makes the position readable at a glance, which is why the fader form is used for level control on mixing consoles. Single and dual ganged elements and various tapers are available.' WHERE id='4e8c8f75-3f9e-4e8f-87d9-5194d146c1a0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A switch actuated by sliding a button or knob in a straight line between two or more detented positions, which moves an internal contact to connect the common terminal to different fixed contacts. Made in single- and multi-pole and two- and multi-position forms, it provides a maintained, latching selection in a low-cost, low-profile body. It is specified by its pole and throw configuration, contact rating, and mechanical life.' WHERE id='0d997282-a44c-47d8-a842-17d7f3f2bd56' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An electromechanical reverberation device that produces artificial reverberation by launching torsional and longitudinal waves along one or more helical springs. An input transducer converts the audio signal into mechanical vibration that travels down the spring with dispersion and repeated reflections at its terminations, and an output transducer reconverts the delayed, diffused motion back to an electrical signal. Compact and inexpensive, it became common in guitar amplifiers and organs and gives a characteristic springy, dispersive reverberant tail.' WHERE id='525f6b78-9388-4a7c-8f51-fe89ec9193ea' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A light-emitting diode used to indicate the operating condition or mode of a device, such as power on, standby, ready, fault, or link activity. Its colour together with steady, blinking, or multi-state behaviour encodes discrete status information for the user, with the forward current set by a series resistor or a driver. Bi-colour and red-green-blue devices allow several states to be shown from a single indicator position.' WHERE id='45b7252b-1dc3-43bc-9abf-248c8fb882f2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transformer whose secondary winding has fewer turns than its primary, so that the secondary voltage is lower than the primary voltage in proportion to the turns ratio while the available secondary current is correspondingly higher. It is used to derive lower operating voltages from a higher supply, such as reducing the mains voltage to the levels an equipment''s circuitry needs, and it also provides the galvanic isolation set by its construction. Apart from core and winding losses the volt-ampere throughput is conserved between the windings.' WHERE id='c5098c29-2d43-4226-8f3f-91338e37f4eb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transformer whose secondary winding has more turns than its primary, so that the secondary voltage is higher than the primary voltage in proportion to the turns ratio while the available secondary current is correspondingly lower. It is used to raise a voltage, for example to feed a high-tension stage or to match into a higher-impedance load, while providing galvanic isolation. Apart from core and winding losses the volt-ampere product is preserved between the windings.' WHERE id='aa0d2874-3087-4222-b55c-2357c848304b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A level-control device that changes attenuation in discrete, precisely defined steps by using a rotary switch to select among fixed resistor networks rather than a continuous resistive track. Each switch position inserts a calculated resistor combination, giving repeatable, accurately known gain steps with excellent channel matching for stereo and multichannel work and, in suitable topologies, a constant input or output impedance. It is favoured where recallability and matching matter more than continuous adjustment.' WHERE id='02f06eaa-8171-4e98-8f89-d2bc8b3ef6aa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistive attenuator built from three resistors arranged in the shape of the letter T, comprising two series arms and one shunt arm to the common line, used to reduce signal level by a fixed amount while presenting matched impedances at both ports. Its element values are chosen so that the network gives the required attenuation and at the same time matches a specified equal source and load impedance, making it a symmetrical, purely resistive pad whose loss is essentially independent of frequency. It is a standard building block for impedance-matched attenuation in a single-ended line.' WHERE id='e34f99e9-a1b9-4ace-951d-b320514b5da5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small momentary pushbutton switch that gives a distinct tactile click when actuated, produced by the snap action of a metal dome or spring that provides positive feedback at the instant the contact closes. Usually a surface-mount or through-hole circuit-board component with a short travel, it closes a single-pole contact only while it is pressed and is specified by its actuation force, travel, and a mechanical life reaching into the millions of operations. It is ubiquitous as a front-panel and keypad momentary control.' WHERE id='80f1d4f5-ce77-47f5-a871-5063acb60c54' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An electromechanical delay-and-echo device that records the input signal onto a moving loop or reel of magnetic tape and replays it a short time later from one or more playback heads spaced away from the record head. The delay time is set by the tape speed and the head spacing, and feeding the replayed output back to the record head produces a train of repeating echoes whose character carries the tape''s limited bandwidth, saturation, and wow and flutter. It was a mainstay studio and guitar effect before electronic and digital delays.' WHERE id='aaad73fb-49c1-4897-8dcf-e3adf7546a74' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transformer having one or more additional connections, or taps, brought out along a winding so that the effective number of turns, and hence the voltage or impedance seen at that connection, can be selected. A centre tap divides a winding into two equal halves for push-pull operation, full-wave rectification, or a balanced phantom feed, while several taps allow a choice of output voltage or of impedance match. The tap points are fixed by the winding at manufacture.' WHERE id='0ecba05e-867b-4674-a59f-e5773ed62cfb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A switch operated by a projecting lever moved through an arc to snap the contacts between two or more maintained positions, its spring-loaded over-centre mechanism giving a rapid make or break regardless of how slowly the lever is moved. It is made in many pole-and-throw combinations and in maintained or momentary actions, is typically panel-mounted, and is rated by its contact configuration, voltage and current, and mechanical life. The snap action shortens the time the contacts spend arcing.' WHERE id='9f83fe86-5a0d-4f68-a930-0f8e9155e5ee' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small potentiometer intended for infrequent, fine adjustment of a circuit parameter during setup or calibration, turned with a tool rather than a knob. It is made in single-turn open carbon or cermet form and in multiturn leadscrew form, the latter moving the wiper only a small fraction of the track per shaft revolution to give high setting resolution and stability. It trades a limited adjustment life for compact, board-level precision.' WHERE id='49a50505-849f-4aff-9ec6-4e98b9049047' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The ratio of the number of turns in one transformer winding to the number in another, conventionally primary to secondary, which in an ideal transformer equals the ratio of their voltages and the inverse of the ratio of their currents. Because impedance is transformed as the square of this ratio, the turns ratio determines the transformer''s voltage, current, and impedance-matching action. It is a defining parameter fixed by the winding construction.' WHERE id='2bd9ca56-ae3e-4026-b888-3cf1eda63aaf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistive opto-isolator in which a light source, usually a light-emitting diode and historically an incandescent or neon lamp, is optically coupled inside a sealed housing to a photoresistor, or light-dependent resistor. The controlling current sets the emitter''s brightness, which varies the photocell''s resistance smoothly and with full electrical isolation between the control and signal sides, and the photoresistor''s slow, memory-like response gives characteristically soft, click-free changes. It is used for gain control, filtering, and compression in audio and in analog synthesizers. The name began as a trade name and is now used generically for the emitter-and-photocell device.' WHERE id='e15a3218-2cc4-4e0d-a27e-2a3ed0a35d3b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A compressor or limiter whose gain-control element is a variable-mu, or remote-cutoff, vacuum tube, a valve whose amplification factor falls as its grid is biased more negatively. A side-chain rectifies the audio to develop a control bias that reduces the tube''s gain as the signal level rises, giving smooth, program-dependent gain reduction without a separate gain-reduction cell. The topology, embodied in classic units, is valued for its gentle, low-distortion limiting character.' WHERE id='20bd4b0f-ad16-4068-8454-83924c372daf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A vacuum fluorescent display, an emissive display in which electrons emitted from a heated filament cathode are accelerated by a grid and strike phosphor-coated anodes shaped as segments or dots, causing them to glow with a characteristic blue-green light. Operating inside an evacuated glass envelope, it offers high brightness, a wide viewing angle, and fast response, and it is driven by switching the grid and anode voltages for each character or segment. It was widely used on the front panels of audio and consumer equipment.' WHERE id='3f6a84d6-32df-4933-a747-7ffaef9dc000' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dynamic-range compressor of a design or era regarded as classic, typically using an older gain-control technology such as a variable-mu tube, an optical photocell element, or a field-effect or diode-bridge cell rather than a modern voltage-controlled amplifier. Such units are valued for the program-dependent, often nonlinear character that their gain element and signal transformers impart, and their attack and release behaviour and harmonic colouration are widely emulated in software. The term denotes both the original hardware and its sonic signature.' WHERE id='491f3e0c-7d9b-4a23-9dc0-4093d9f80312' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An equalizer of classic design or era, typically a passive or valve or discrete-transistor active tone-shaping unit built with inductor-capacitor networks and signal transformers, such as the switched-band program equalizers of mid-twentieth-century broadcast and recording practice. Their fixed or stepped frequency choices, broad musical curves, and the colouration of their inductors and transformers give a character distinct from modern parametric designs and are widely modelled. The term covers both the original hardware and its tonal character.' WHERE id='79f3cd61-6740-460e-856f-fb1aa06b5f97' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A peak or program limiter of classic design or era, employing an older gain-reduction method such as a variable-mu tube, an optical attenuator, or a diode or field-effect element together with signal transformers rather than a modern voltage-controlled amplifier. Such limiters are sought for their smooth, program-dependent action and for the harmonic and transient colouration of their circuitry, characteristics widely emulated in software. The term refers to the original units and to their sonic signature.' WHERE id='ca077c09-8f93-4eb3-829d-8d4da13a1b7e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A device or console section of older design that governs the control-room monitoring path, selecting sources, setting the monitor level, and switching between loudspeakers without lying in the main recording signal path. In vintage practice this function usually resided in the monitor section of the recording console or in a dedicated passive or relay-switched level controller, valued for a simple, high-quality gain and switching path with minimal active electronics. The term denotes such older monitor-control hardware and its transparent character.' WHERE id='2e04410b-27c7-49ea-8379-35d2a7dc6e8d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A mixing console of classic design or era, typically built with discrete-transistor or valve amplifier stages and input and output signal transformers, providing the input preamplification, equalization, routing, summing, and monitoring for multitrack recording. Such desks are prized for the tonal colouration of their transformers and class-A or discrete circuitry and for their channel-strip workflow, and many are closely emulated. The term covers both the original hardware and its sonic character.' WHERE id='ac5b80a3-05ff-484b-b1c3-cc86de7faca2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A collective term for audio recording hardware of an earlier era, including consoles, tape machines, microphones, outboard processors, and their supporting electronics, typically built with valve or discrete-transistor circuitry and signal transformers. Such equipment is valued for the sonic colouration and behaviour of its older technology, which is widely sought after and emulated even though it demands specialist maintenance. The term is descriptive rather than denoting a single defined device.' WHERE id='19838edb-bff0-4f1c-b466-356170d207b4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A loudspeaker of an older design or era used for critical monitoring in a recording or mastering room and representative of the reference monitors of its period. Such monitors are associated with particular voicings and driver and enclosure technologies that engineers came to know as mixing references, and some remain in use or are emulated for their familiar tonal balance. The term denotes both the original loudspeakers and their characteristic sound.' WHERE id='b263324e-469d-4c78-b0c9-484a599dd78a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An analog magnetic tape recorder of an earlier era that records audio as a varying magnetization on moving tape by way of a record head and replays it through a playback head, with bias and equalization set by its electronics. It is valued for the sonic effects of analog tape, including head and electronics colouration, gentle compression, saturation, and wow and flutter, as well as for its transport and alignment characteristics, and it is widely emulated in software. The term covers both the original hardware and its sonic signature.' WHERE id='58a18500-4587-4cd1-878c-8e739d3bec3e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A potentiometer whose resistive element is a length of resistance wire wound as a coil on an insulating former, over which the wiper travels. The wound construction gives a low temperature coefficient, good long-term stability, and the power handling of a metal element, at the cost of a resolution limited to discrete steps as the wiper moves from turn to turn and of a higher self-inductance than film elements. It is chosen where power dissipation and precision matter more than fine, continuous resolution.' WHERE id='6891359f-372a-4970-868c-8056553451bb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The maximum steady voltage that may be applied continuously across a component, or between specified points, in normal operation without exceeding its ratings, often quoted for capacitors as the direct-current working voltage. It is set below the component''s breakdown or test voltage to leave a safe margin over the expected service life, and applying more than the working voltage risks insulation breakdown or premature failure. The rating is defined at a stated temperature and is derated at higher temperatures.' WHERE id='8a842182-8e7a-44c6-b3cd-bdd2bf18db35' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The pre-session process of generating and refining the metronomic click that musicians hear on headphones so a recorded performance locks precisely to picture. It involves deriving a tempo, or a sequence of tempos, that places musical downbeats and accents on the desired frame-accurate sync points, choosing the click subdivisions and accent pattern, and inserting count-offs together with streamers, punches, and any free-time, accelerando, or ritardando passages. The prepared click is embedded in the score''s tempo structure and routed to the podium and players so the conductor can hold ensemble timing against the film while the recordist captures a synchronized take.' WHERE id='44e3d28b-67e0-4045-8974-f9bf008b91b4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An orchestral ensemble assembled specifically to record the musical score for a motion picture, typically composed of freelance session players contracted for the dates of a scoring session rather than a standing concert orchestra. Its instrumentation is tailored to the demands of each score and may range from a small string section to a full symphonic complement with augmented brass, percussion, and specialty instruments. The ensemble performs on a scoring stage under a conductor while synchronized to picture, and its recorded performance becomes the orchestral component of the film''s music tracks.' WHERE id='688f48e0-0bfc-4978-a4a3-f25c158b76a1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording facility designed for capturing music to picture, centered on a large acoustically treated live room (the scoring stage) sized to hold an orchestra together with picture projection or large monitors visible to the conductor and players. It is equipped for synchronizing the musical performance to a running film via timecode, click, and visual cueing systems, and pairs the stage with a control room able to record the many simultaneous microphone channels of an orchestral setup. Such studios provide the reverberant acoustics, sightlines, and monitoring infrastructure required for orchestral film-score recording.' WHERE id='b90d9ee0-a3ad-4a0a-8816-a25d15315868' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A scheduled block of studio time in which an assembled orchestra performs and records music, run according to the standard session structure of rehearsal, recording of successive takes, and playback review under a conductor and producer. In film work it is conducted to picture with the players synchronized by click and streamers, while the recording team captures the ensemble across sectioned microphone arrays. Session length, personnel, and the number of recorded minutes permitted are typically governed by musicians''-union agreements.' WHERE id='65cc2095-69a2-4b84-93e5-789a34b64546' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An incomplete measure of music that precedes the first full metric bar of a phrase, containing one or more notes (an anacrusis or upbeat) that lead into the following downbeat. Its note values fill only part of the notated time signature, with the remaining beats understood to be completed by the final bar of the phrase or section. In score and part preparation the pickup bar is written ahead of bar 1 and is not usually assigned a measure number.' WHERE id='cc3eba2f-9b10-4cc4-a2be-189a1f209006' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A partial measure at the start of a phrase or piece that carries the upbeat notes leading to the first downbeat, equivalent to an anacrusis and holding fewer beats than the prevailing time signature indicates. The missing beats are conventionally accounted for by shortening the final measure so the two together equal one complete bar. Also called a pickup bar or upbeat measure, it is placed before the first numbered bar of the notated score.' WHERE id='658dd649-4d88-43a4-aebc-0dc343433bac' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The professional process of producing accurate, performance-ready conductor''s scores and instrumental parts from a composer''s or orchestrator''s manuscript or notation file, carried out by a music copyist or preparation service. It encompasses engraving or transcribing the notation, laying out page turns and rehearsal markings, adding bar numbers and timings and any click or streamer indications for sync work, transposing and extracting individual parts, and proofreading for errors before the recording session. The goal is a clean, unambiguous set of materials that lets an orchestra sight-read and record efficiently.' WHERE id='dab4abc7-3f0c-4d18-8338-81af3f65721b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A discrete, self-contained passage of music composed to accompany a specific segment of a film, identified by a cue number (commonly a reel-and-sequence designation such as 1M1) and spotted to begin and end at defined picture points. Each cue is written, timed, and recorded as an individual unit with its own tempo structure and sync references, and the assembled cues constitute the film''s original score. The term denotes both the compositional unit and the corresponding recorded music element delivered to the dub.' WHERE id='aefd89eb-8d7d-4640-833f-c7df9e8e1c26' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The orchestra engaged to perform and record a film, television, or game score on a scoring stage, made up of session musicians contracted for the recording and configured to the score''s orchestration. It plays under a conductor in synchronization with picture and click, and is recorded by section through dedicated microphone setups to yield the live orchestral tracks of the score.' WHERE id='ff380b7d-6318-48a6-9a08-340cfd4eda0a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording session devoted to capturing a musical score to picture, in which the scoring orchestra or smaller ensemble performs the composed cues on a scoring stage while synchronized to the film by timecode, click, and streamers. The session proceeds cue by cue through rehearsals and successive takes under a conductor, music producer, and scoring mixer, and is bound by union rules governing hours and recorded minutes. Its output is the recorded, sync-locked music later mixed and dubbed into the soundtrack.' WHERE id='7a84559e-86e2-4f85-a25b-ccc1258c27d4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A single continuous recorded performance of a scoring cue, or a portion of it, captured during a scoring session and identified by cue and take number for later selection or editing. Multiple takes of the same cue are recorded to obtain a clean, well-synchronized, musically satisfying performance, and takes may be full passes or inserts and pickups covering only specific bars. The chosen take, or a composite edited from several, becomes the master recording of that cue.' WHERE id='f73af10b-1ad8-45ef-a56f-03bd1482a962' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The control room adjoining a scoring stage from which the scoring mixer and music producer record and monitor the orchestra, equipped with a large-format console or DAW capable of handling the many simultaneous microphone channels of an orchestral setup. It provides sightlines or video of the stage, timecode and click distribution, talkback and cue-mix systems, and calibrated monitoring for judging balance and blend. From here takes are recorded, reviewed, and logged against picture.' WHERE id='9c7c1837-73b6-4374-ba8b-24286e85185b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The main recording floor of a scoring stage: the large, acoustically designed live room where the orchestra is seated and performs to picture. It offers the volume, ceiling height, and reverberant character suited to orchestral recording, along with sightlines to the conductor and projected or monitored picture, riser layouts for the sections, and provisions for microphone placement and headphone and click distribution to the players.' WHERE id='09837a5e-2a95-480b-8b25-2b8892bb56de' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An acoustically isolated enclosure on or adjacent to a scoring stage used to separate particular instruments or vocalists from the main orchestra so their signals can be captured with minimal leakage. Rhythm-section instruments, loud percussion, soloists, or a vocalist are placed in the booth to control spill and allow independent level control and processing in the mix, while sightlines and headphone feeds keep the isolated performer in sync with the conductor and ensemble.' WHERE id='67389700-35ac-483c-9af0-e00d583369cc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A body of professional session musicians engaged to record in a studio setting, typically freelancers assembled for specific dates rather than a permanent concert ensemble, valued for strong sight-reading and the ability to deliver polished recorded performances quickly. In film, television, and commercial work the studio orchestra records to picture and click under a conductor, and its size and instrumentation are configured per project.' WHERE id='0f27f83b-5f34-4f68-a5ec-799e653bba68' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The process of constructing the sequence of tempos, time signatures, and bar positions in a sequencer or notation environment so that musical beats fall on the frame-accurate sync points required by the picture. It entails placing hit points at key visual events, deriving the tempos and any accelerandi, ritardandi, or meter changes that land downbeats and accents at those frames, and generating the corresponding click and streamers. The finished tempo map underlies the click track, score alignment, and MIDI or orchestral synchronization for the scoring session.' WHERE id='643bba2f-5621-4623-9bde-a5d76cf6a247' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A document listing the Foley to be performed for a given reel or scene, enumerating each cue with its timecode or footage location, a description of the action or prop, the character or object involved, and notes on surface or performance. Prepared by the Foley editor or supervisor from the spotting session, it guides the Foley artist and mixer through the recording so that every required footstep, movement, and prop sound is covered. It also serves as a record for tracking and later editing of the recorded cues.' WHERE id='ecea8f51-dead-4af4-90fa-080f574271c1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The process of screening the picture and identifying each action that requires a Foley sound, marking its position and character so it can be performed and recorded. Cueing establishes what will be covered — footsteps, cloth movement, and specific prop actions — and organizes those cues into passes and onto the cue sheet for the session. It is the planning stage that precedes the Foley performance.' WHERE id='6ad45865-e099-4e82-8a30-1ed1424a47e2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The sound editor responsible for preparing Foley cues before the session and for editing the recorded Foley into precise sync with picture afterward, trimming, tightening, and placing each footstep, move, and prop sound. The Foley editor spots and cue-sheets the material, guides or logs coverage during recording, then conforms and cleans the tracks and lays them out for the re-recording mix. The role bridges the Foley-stage performance and the finished, mix-ready Foley units.' WHERE id='623306ab-96bd-4a78-8d84-887ff9d0c002' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The editorial discipline encompassing the preparation, syncing, and track layout of Foley recordings within sound post-production. It covers spotting and cue-sheeting the Foley, supervising or logging the recorded passes, and then editing the performances into frame-accurate sync, organizing footsteps, moves, and specifics across tracks as mix-ready units. Foley editorial is distinct from effects and dialogue editorial and delivers the Foley units and stems for the dub.' WHERE id='4bf99459-1760-4f55-be53-0441ebd1a45b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The performance area of a Foley stage, comprising the assorted walking surfaces — Foley pits set with materials such as concrete, gravel, dirt, wood, tile, and water — together with the open floor space and prop stores where the Foley artist works. Its varied surfaces let the artist match the footsteps and movement of on-screen characters across different environments, performed in view of the picture and captured by a close microphone. The floor is designed for quiet operation and flexible reconfiguration of surfaces and props.' WHERE id='a8822bf7-9f2a-40e2-8e3f-9d53769537a8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The category of Foley covering the sounds of body movement and clothing — the rustles, brushes, and cloth friction produced as characters shift, gesture, and move — as distinct from footsteps and from specific prop ''specifics.'' Performed by the Foley artist manipulating garments and fabrics in sync with the actor''s motion, moves add the subtle continuous presence of a body in space that supports the reality of a scene, and are typically recorded in their own pass.' WHERE id='019c5a5e-d128-4a88-8ab4-fd6a54ccb305' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A single recording take through a reel or section that covers one category or grouping of Foley — for example a footsteps pass, a moves or cloth pass, or a specifics and props pass — performed by the Foley artist in sync with picture. Breaking the work into passes lets the artist and mixer concentrate on and isolate each type of sound on its own track, building complete coverage layer by layer. Multiple passes may be recorded per character and per surface.' WHERE id='7491a46c-6bd0-43e7-ba3e-ab7d3c8fa424' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The real-time performance of sound effects by a Foley artist who watches the picture and physically reproduces characters'' footsteps, movements, and prop handling in sync with the image, recorded on a Foley stage. It is an interpretive craft in which the artist matches timing, weight, texture, and character through choice of shoes, surfaces, props, and technique to make the actions sound believable. The recorded performance becomes the Foley element of the soundtrack.' WHERE id='03c5ae3f-14ef-42a5-ad12-b767bfb88c6f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An arrangement of acoustic resistances, compliances and absorptive elements distributed within a loudspeaker system to dissipate acoustic energy and thereby control resonant behaviour. Realised physically through damping fill (fibreglass, long-fibre wool, foam), lined panels, resistively vented apertures or aperiodic membranes, it broadens and lowers the amplitude of internal standing-wave and enclosure resonances and raises the effective loss factor of the acoustical system. In lumped-element modelling the network appears as resistive branches added to the acoustical equivalent circuit, allowing the system''s total Q and its impedance/response shape to be tuned by design.' WHERE id='2681954e-9c7f-4d29-8a27-527596d0ae39' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An electroacoustic system that reduces unwanted acoustic noise by generating a secondary sound field of equal amplitude and opposite phase so that the two superpose destructively at a target point such as the entrance to the ear canal. A controller derives a cancelling signal from one or more reference and/or error microphones and drives a control loudspeaker; feedforward implementations sense noise upstream, feedback implementations sense residual pressure at the ear, and hybrid designs combine both. Cancellation is most effective at low frequencies where the wavelength is large relative to the transducer spacing, and its performance is bounded by causality, controller stability and acoustic path uncertainty.' WHERE id='d2688b50-11b2-40a7-a8d0-74f4de6d76a3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A production quality-control procedure that detects unintended air leaks in a sealed loudspeaker enclosure or drive-unit assembly, for example around gaskets, cabinet seams, terminal cups, the dust cap or the surround. Leakage is inferred from the rate of pressure decay after the sealed volume is pressurised or evacuated, or from characteristic anomalies and air-rush noise in the acoustic and electrical-impedance signatures. Because uncontrolled leaks lower the effective enclosure compliance and add losses, they shift the low-frequency alignment and system Q and can cause audible chuffing, making leak screening a standard end-of-line check.' WHERE id='4d507002-4cad-4e72-9670-4f4f24e4d554' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of air-motion (Heil) transformer transducers, in which a diaphragm folded into an accordion-like pleat pattern carries a conductive trace and sits within a transverse magnetic field. Audio current in the trace makes adjacent pleats alternately squeeze together and open, expelling and drawing in air from the folds at a velocity several times greater than the diaphragm''s own mechanical velocity, a velocity-transformation effect that gives the mechanism its name. The resulting low moving mass and large effective radiating perimeter yield high efficiency and extended, low-distortion high-frequency response, and the engineering task centres on pleat geometry, diaphragm mass and tension, magnetic-gap flux and thermal handling of the trace.' WHERE id='d2a412c5-4ea4-4861-aae1-a3e61ad1a382' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An operating mode of headphones or in-ear monitors in which external microphones capture the surrounding sound field and mix a controlled amount of it into the earpiece so the wearer retains situational awareness while wearing otherwise isolating earphones. Also called transparency or hear-through mode, it manages the passthrough gain and its frequency shaping, and in active designs compensates for the occlusion and passive isolation of the earpiece to approximate an open-ear timbre. Latency, self-noise and the balance between naturalness and amplification are the principal engineering constraints.' WHERE id='4121b66c-c125-4446-b23d-1e5bf5b02bf6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of the back plate, the ferrous rear member of a moving-coil driver''s magnetic circuit that sits behind the permanent magnet and, together with the front plate and centre pole piece, completes the magnetic return path and defines the voice-coil gap. Its geometry sets the flux density in the gap and hence the force factor, while features such as a bumped or extended pole, a centre vent and back-plate relief provide clearance for large coil excursion and a path for cooling airflow. Careful shaping controls flux distribution, magnetic saturation and the symmetry of the field seen by the moving coil.' WHERE id='b0512fd2-0768-457d-b4bb-e2f208a07e76' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A miniature electromagnetic transducer, widely used in hearing aids and in-ear monitors, in which a ferromagnetic armature or reed is pivoted within a magnetic field between two pole pieces and surrounded by a drive coil. Signal current modulates the flux so that the balanced armature deflects toward one pole or the other, and its motion is coupled through a drive rod to a small diaphragm that radiates into an acoustic port. The design offers high sensitivity and very small size at the expense of limited bandwidth per unit, so wideband systems combine several armatures tuned to different bands.' WHERE id='d4a9bd9e-7403-4464-96f8-bc3fd024b5af' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An in-ear earphone that uses one or more balanced-armature drivers sealed into the ear canal, frequently in multi-driver arrangements with passive crossovers and tuned acoustic tubes so that separate armatures handle low, mid and high bands. Because each armature couples to the sealed residual ear-canal volume, the earphone achieves high sensitivity, compact packaging and a response that can be closely tailored, though it depends on a good canal seal for its low-frequency output. Design work addresses driver selection and matching, acoustic damping in the sound bores and the summation of the driver outputs at the eardrum reference point.' WHERE id='03e583e4-08c0-4e42-8b8b-7a7541af633d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A structural resonance of the loudspeaker basket, the frame or chassis that carries the motor assembly and anchors the suspension, excited by the reaction forces of the moving system. When the frame''s natural modes fall within the driver''s operating band they add spurious peaks and dips to the response and can radiate audible coloration, so basket resonance is minimised through stiff cast or heavily ribbed frames, added mass or damping, and by placing the dominant modes out of band. It is characterised by accelerometry or laser vibrometry on the frame during drive.' WHERE id='dbaee126-9e6a-42bf-b36d-64a87572b4e1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A power-handling test in which short, high-level bursts of tone or band-limited noise, separated by defined off periods to form a duty cycle, are applied to a drive unit to evaluate its short-term and peak power capability and its mechanical robustness without reaching thermal steady state. The regime stresses the suspension, voice-coil former and lead-out wires under large transient excursion while limiting average heating, and pass/fail is judged from parameter shift, audible defects or mechanical failure. It complements long-term continuous-power testing in the standardised power-rating framework.' WHERE id='423475eb-6a82-47fa-be3b-f3c4a4b3d7ed' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The scattering and re-radiation of sound at the edges and boundaries of a loudspeaker enclosure. As the wavefront radiated by a driver reaches a cabinet edge it launches secondary edge waves that interfere with the direct radiation, producing frequency-response ripple and, over the transition from full-space to half-space loading, the roughly six-decibel baffle step. Diffraction depends on baffle dimensions and driver placement and is mitigated by rounding or chamfering edges, applying absorptive felt, using offset or asymmetric driver locations and tapering the cabinet.' WHERE id='e21bf70f-5fbb-42f0-9e1a-3129a2e1af59' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A quality-control test that verifies the structural and airtight integrity of a completed loudspeaker enclosure, confirming that panels are properly bonded, joints and gaskets are sealed and the cabinet does not leak air or generate buzzes and rattles when driven. It combines air-leak or pressure-decay checks with acoustic and vibration screening under signal, flagging loose bracing, unsealed seams or resonant panels against pass/fail limits. The test protects the intended low-frequency alignment, which depends on a well-sealed and rigid enclosure.' WHERE id='862d4f8d-1f36-48a2-a64b-8e3832b626b7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The measurement and interpretation of the vibrational resonances of a loudspeaker enclosure, comprising both the structural bending modes of the cabinet panels and the acoustic standing-wave modes of the air enclosed within it. Panel and internal-air resonances radiate energy through the walls or back through the cone and port, colouring the reproduced sound, and are identified with accelerometry, laser Doppler vibrometry, near-field acoustic measurement and modal or finite-element analysis. The results guide bracing, panel damping, wall construction and the placement of absorptive material to move or suppress the offending modes.' WHERE id='123b6c81-8c8a-4e17-9ffa-57f8cce85908' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of circumaural, around-the-ear headphones whose earcups fully enclose the pinna and seal to the side of the head through a compliant cushion. The volume trapped between diaphragm, pinna and cushion, together with any deliberate or leakage porting and the quality of the seal, governs the low-frequency extension, the resonance structure and the passive isolation. Design decisions balance transducer selection, cup acoustics, clamp force and pad compliance against target response, isolation and wearing comfort, with performance verified on a standardised ear and head simulator.' WHERE id='3f8c6036-b89b-4d8a-a871-7e20f54a3a36' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The measurement of the clamping force a headphone''s headband exerts on the wearer''s head, typically expressed in newtons and obtained by spreading the earcups to a representative head width on a jig or force gauge. Clamp force sets the quality and consistency of the ear-cushion seal, which in turn affects passive isolation and low-frequency response, and it trades directly against long-term wearing comfort and pressure fatigue. It is a routine parameter in headphone design and production control.' WHERE id='6038d0a5-8f96-4cfa-ba52-fe7d2d26d3cf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of headphones whose earcups form a sealed, acoustically closed enclosure behind the diaphragm, isolating the wearer from ambient sound and preventing rear radiation from escaping. The sealed rear cavity loads the driver and introduces its own resonances, so the designer must damp and dimension that volume to control midrange coloration and to set the bass balance, while accepting the isolation and reduced leakage that distinguish the closed-back type from open designs. Cup damping, internal absorption and controlled leak ports are the principal tools.' WHERE id='7d36050e-659f-4ad8-9fee-cf3f009a079b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of coaxial drive units in which two transducers covering different frequency bands share a common axis, most commonly a high-frequency compression driver or tweeter mounted at the apex of, or radiating through the pole of, a low-frequency cone. Placing the acoustic sources on one axis makes the assembly behave more nearly as a coincident point source, improving the consistency of the combined response across listening angles and easing time alignment. The engineering challenges include the horn or waveguide formed by the surrounding cone, diffraction at the transition and the crossover between the two sections.' WHERE id='c2097c51-e696-4f01-bd66-0bff3b1fb03e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The assessment of the physical comfort of headphones or earphones during wear, combining subjective panel ratings with objective measures such as headband clamp force, mass and its distribution, ear-cushion pressure and contact area, and thermal build-up under the pad. Comfort evaluation informs the choice of cushion material and geometry, clamp force and weight balance, since these also influence the acoustic seal and therefore the reproduced response, linking ergonomic and acoustic design. Repeatable fixtures and standardised wear protocols are used to compare designs.' WHERE id='3ca9dcce-3195-4f8c-b362-8ff40ca7770a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The measurement of the mechanical compliance of a loudspeaker driver''s suspension, that is the diaphragm displacement produced per unit of applied force, in metres per newton, and the reciprocal of the suspension stiffness contributed by the spider and surround. It is a fundamental Thiele-Small parameter, denoted Cms, and is determined from the free-air resonance together with added-mass or added-compliance methods, or directly by static or laser displacement measurement. Because compliance sets the resonance frequency and the equivalent compliance volume, its accuracy underpins enclosure design.' WHERE id='5cc78e9d-b4e2-4d25-9332-f5c59d1f38b6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A test that quantifies power compression, the reduction in a loudspeaker''s acoustic output relative to the electrical input as drive level rises, caused chiefly by the increase in voice-coil resistance as the coil heats and by other level-dependent losses. Output is measured at successive input levels and the loss in sensitivity, in decibels, is reported against power or against coil temperature, revealing thermal limiting well before mechanical or thermal failure. The result characterises how faithfully a driver maintains its rated sensitivity under sustained high power.' WHERE id='64fb8e57-9b68-4a64-98a6-cc7c769feeb0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of horn-loaded high-frequency compression drivers, in which a dome or annular diaphragm driven by a voice coil radiates into a phasing plug whose channels sum coherently at a throat of much smaller area than the diaphragm, the ratio of the two areas being the compression ratio. Concentrating the radiation into the small throat raises the acoustic loading and hence the efficiency and lets the driver couple to a horn that sets the directivity. Design work addresses diaphragm material and geometry, phasing-plug channel design to avoid path-length cancellation, gap flux and thermal management.' WHERE id='2aad9874-e869-4485-9a19-59124e097cac' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of concentric drive units, a coaxial arrangement in which the high-frequency and low-frequency transducers are mounted on the same axis with their acoustic centres as nearly coincident as possible, the low-frequency cone often serving as the flare for the high-frequency horn. The coincident-source geometry gives uniform directivity and integration through the crossover region and a stable stereo image, at the cost of managing the moving cone as a time-varying horn boundary and the mechanical packaging of one driver within the other. It is exemplified by dual-concentric professional and monitor loudspeakers.' WHERE id='be1b0447-1f15-413a-85dc-3b34edba3348' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The study of cone break-up, the frequency region in which a loudspeaker diaphragm ceases to move as a rigid piston and instead flexes in modal, standing-wave patterns across its surface. These bending modes produce peaks and dips in the response and non-linear distortion, and their onset and severity depend on the cone material, geometry and the damping of its terminations. They are analysed by finite-element modelling and measured by laser Doppler vibrometry or full-surface scanning, which map the modal shapes so that material, profile and damping can be optimised or the break-up region moved above the driver''s passband.' WHERE id='53b58f5b-6d8c-46f4-9155-d8915a1c3de2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The set of physical shape parameters of a loudspeaker cone, including its diameter, depth, half-angle and the roll geometry at its edge, which together determine the effective radiating area, moving mass and stiffness of the diaphragm. Cone geometry governs the piston-band sensitivity through radiating area, the high-frequency directivity through diameter and depth, and the onset of break-up through the way the shape distributes stiffness. It is chosen in concert with material and surround design to meet targets for bandwidth, dispersion and distortion.' WHERE id='96c0e228-dbc7-40a3-b304-45c8aac4bb88' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The cross-sectional contour of a loudspeaker cone along its radius, ranging from a straight-sided conical profile to curvilinear forms such as exponential or flared shapes. The profile sets how stiffness and mass are distributed between apex and rim, and therefore trades rigidity and a higher, cleaner break-up onset against controlled decoupling that can smooth the upper response and widen dispersion. Selecting a profile is a central diaphragm-design decision, made together with the choice of cone material and edge treatment.' WHERE id='f361df4f-c0ef-446b-a50d-ba291e215d60' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A power-handling test in which a continuous, steady-state signal, typically band-limited pink noise shaped to a specified spectrum and crest factor, is applied to a drive unit for a defined duration to verify its long-term thermal power capacity. Sustained excitation drives the voice-coil and motor to thermal equilibrium, so the test exposes thermal failure modes such as adhesive softening, former deformation and coil damage, and it establishes the long-term or nominal power rating within the standardised rating scheme. It complements short-term burst testing that instead stresses peak and mechanical limits.' WHERE id='f06dcc9b-439b-4206-9f37-80c7cc118b99' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A headphone equalisation objective derived from the diffuse-field frequency response of the human head and ear, that is the average head-related transfer function for sound arriving uniformly from all directions in a reverberant field. A headphone tuned to the diffuse-field target aims to present at the eardrum the same spectral shaping that a diffuse sound field would, providing a physically grounded reference for headphone tuning and measurement on an ear simulator. It is one of the standard targets alongside free-field and listener-preference curves.' WHERE id='823b89fe-aca9-41a6-846b-f5e64b9d7a03' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The engineering of a loudspeaker''s angular radiation characteristics, describing how the radiated sound is distributed in space and quantified by the polar response, coverage angle, directivity factor and directivity index. Directivity is shaped by the size and spacing of the radiating apertures, by horns and waveguides, and by the arrangement of drivers into arrays, and it is designed to deliver a specified coverage while controlling off-axis energy and its interaction with the room. Because directivity generally rises with frequency for a fixed aperture, controlling its variation across the band is a central design goal.' WHERE id='05505711-fc9c-47cd-bedd-2d18bdba7b54' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An electrical filter network that divides the audio band into adjacent frequency ranges and routes each to the driver best suited to reproduce it, setting the crossover frequencies, filter slopes and the relative level and phase of the sections so that their outputs sum to the intended system response. Passive networks placed between amplifier and drivers use inductors, capacitors and resistors and must account for the drivers'' own impedance, while active networks filter at line level ahead of separate amplifiers. Good crossover design ensures a smooth acoustic transition and controlled off-axis behaviour through the overlap region.' WHERE id='e5351ceb-6039-45d8-9738-f342500632af' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The discipline of designing loudspeaker drive units, integrating the motor structure of magnet, pole pieces and voice coil, the suspension of spider and surround, and the diaphragm assembly into a transducer that meets defined electroacoustic targets. Design work sets the Thiele-Small parameters, the force factor, sensitivity, frequency response, distortion and power handling through choices of magnetic circuit, coil and gap geometry, moving mass and compliance, and diaphragm material and profile. It couples magnetic, mechanical, acoustic and thermal analysis with measurement and non-linear parameter identification.' WHERE id='942468dc-2480-4e76-9fc3-b8d50020d9a3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The selection and pairing of drive units whose measured parameters, such as sensitivity, frequency response, impedance and Thiele-Small values, lie close together, so that units used in a stereo pair, a multi-way system or an array behave consistently. Matching preserves accurate imaging between channels and coherent summation within arrays, and it is enforced in production by measuring each unit and grouping or binning those that fall within a specified tolerance. It rests on the same parameter measurements used to characterise the driver.' WHERE id='73b217f9-29d6-4668-abc1-898ae7ca8ff8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The permissible deviation between drive units that are to be paired, expressed as a tolerance band on the parameters used for matching, for example a maximum allowed difference in sensitivity in decibels or a percentage window on resonance frequency and other Thiele-Small values. The tolerance defines the pass/fail limits for binning units in production and represents a compromise between the imaging and array-summation accuracy demanded by the application and the manufacturing yield that tighter limits sacrifice. It is set from the characterisation measurements of the drivers.' WHERE id='127417a4-53e1-4e73-8eaf-1a362befc4a8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A moving-coil transducer used in headphones, in which a voice coil bonded to a lightweight diaphragm sits in the radial field of a permanent-magnet gap; audio current in the coil produces a force proportional to the product of gap flux density and coil length, driving the diaphragm to radiate sound. It is the most common headphone driver type, valued for robustness, efficiency and low cost, and its response is shaped by the diaphragm mass and compliance, the magnetic circuit and the acoustic loading of the earcup or ear-canal cavity. Design centres on extending bandwidth and reducing distortion within the small available volume.' WHERE id='d410a7c6-68ed-426e-b0a0-f2f8a7d6bec0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The mechanical compliance, or softness, of a headphone ear cushion, that is how readily the pad deforms under the clamping force of the headband. Ear-pad compliance governs the quality and stability of the acoustic seal against the head or pinna, and therefore the passive isolation and the low-frequency response, while also distributing clamp pressure and so influencing comfort. It depends on the cushion''s foam or gel filling, its cover material and its cross-sectional geometry, and it is characterised by force-deflection measurement.' WHERE id='d628b979-4d61-4811-b1df-89faeb3329d7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The measurement of the electrical quality factor of a loudspeaker driver, Qes, which characterises the damping of the fundamental resonance contributed by the electrical side of the motor, principally the interaction of the force factor with the voice-coil resistance. It is derived from the electrical-impedance curve about the free-air resonance, usually together with the mechanical quality factor Qms to obtain the total Q, using the height and bandwidth of the impedance peak. As a core Thiele-Small parameter it directly informs the choice and alignment of an enclosure.' WHERE id='df95345d-d6cf-4618-9a1f-38e2a8bedaa9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of electroacoustic transducers, the devices that convert electrical signals into sound or sound into electrical signals, spanning electrodynamic, electrostatic, piezoelectric, balanced-armature and other transduction principles. The discipline applies the electromechanical and acoustical analogies to model the coupling between electrical, mechanical and acoustic domains, and optimises efficiency, bandwidth, sensitivity, linearity and impedance matching to the medium. It underlies the engineering of loudspeakers, microphones, headphones and receivers alike.' WHERE id='817d6034-4e9c-455a-a358-09aa72283f07' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transducer in which a very thin, electrically conductive diaphragm is suspended under tension between two perforated fixed electrodes, or stators. A high fixed polarising voltage charges the diaphragm, and the audio signal applied across the stators creates an electrostatic force that moves the diaphragm nearly uniformly over its area, so that it radiates as a low-mass, large-area planar source with very low distortion and extended high-frequency response. The design requires a bias supply and step-up interface, and its output at low frequencies and high levels is limited by the available diaphragm excursion and electrode spacing.' WHERE id='06eb56c2-a8b6-4782-8b48-5304f5427ed1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A headphone that reproduces sound with electrostatic drivers, in which a charged, tensioned diaphragm moves between perforated stator electrodes under the signal field, requiring a dedicated energiser to supply the polarising bias and the high stator drive voltage. The very low moving mass yields low distortion and an extended, airy high-frequency response, and the open, acoustically transparent stator structure typically gives an open-backed character. Design concerns include diaphragm tension and coating, electrode spacing, safety of the bias voltage and the matching transformer or amplifier interface.' WHERE id='bd3fe888-708b-401c-9fd7-c63aa107706a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A test that identifies and characterises the resonances of a loudspeaker enclosure, both the structural bending modes of the cabinet walls and the acoustic standing-wave modes of the enclosed air. Resonances are detected through anomalies in the driver''s electrical impedance, through accelerometry or laser vibrometry on the panels, or through near-field acoustic measurement inside and outside the box, and the results direct the placement of bracing and absorptive material. Controlling these resonances prevents unwanted radiation through the walls and back through the cone that would colour the reproduced sound.' WHERE id='9f8af5b7-8f3c-4e91-8028-a9ebfe1d596e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An automated quality-control test performed at the end of the production line that rapidly screens every finished loudspeaker against pass/fail limits before shipment. Within a short cycle it checks characteristics such as frequency response and sensitivity, polarity, rub-and-buzz and other impulsive defects, air leaks and, where required, Thiele-Small parameters, using psychoacoustically weighted defect detection to catch audible faults reliably. It provides the final gate that separates conforming units from those with assembly or component defects.' WHERE id='233392ee-e1fe-4e4a-9e0e-67cd55477d71' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A reliability process in which finished transducers or loudspeaker assemblies are deliberately subjected to environmental and operational stresses, such as temperature cycling, humidity, mechanical vibration and power cycling, to precipitate latent and infant-mortality defects that would otherwise fail early in service. Unlike qualification testing, which characterises a design''s limits, screening is applied to production units to weed out weak items, and the applied stresses are chosen to expose defects without consuming the useful life of good units. Units surviving the screen proceed to shipment.' WHERE id='9ab5f61e-7049-4ad1-bbe2-0c0f5f661f59' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A defined frequency-response correction curve, giving gain as a function of frequency, that is applied to a transducer or loudspeaker system so that its output meets an intended target response. The profile may flatten an uneven raw response, compensate for enclosure or room effects, or steer a headphone toward a chosen target such as a diffuse-field or preference curve, and it is implemented with analogue filters or digital signal processing. It is derived from measured response relative to the target and stored as a set of filter parameters or a correction table.' WHERE id='f83420a7-6a45-46c5-88db-b0e22ff7aed0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The measurement of the equivalent compliance volume of a loudspeaker driver, Vas, defined as the volume of air whose acoustic compliance equals the mechanical compliance of the driver''s suspension. It is a fundamental Thiele-Small parameter, obtained from the free-air resonance together with an added-mass or added-compliance perturbation, or from a sealed-box or known-volume measurement, and it expresses the suspension stiffness in a form directly usable for enclosure sizing. A larger Vas indicates a more compliant suspension and generally calls for a larger enclosure.' WHERE id='90033cd7-c984-4bcd-a5a4-7d90b0d2bb6e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A formal quality document that records the measured test results and the pass/fail acceptance status of a product or production batch against its specification before it is released or delivered from the factory. For a loudspeaker or transducer it summarises the outcomes of the applicable acceptance tests, such as response, sensitivity, defect screening and any environmental or power checks, providing traceable evidence that the units conform. It is the reporting artefact of the factory acceptance testing that concludes manufacture.' WHERE id='cfe0a295-a375-4c68-91db-4ae84c738371' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An active-noise-cancellation topology that places an error microphone inside the earcup, close to the ear, and feeds its signal back through a controller that drives the earphone transducer to null the residual sound pressure sensed there. Because it regulates the pressure actually present at the ear, the feedback loop is robust to fit and leakage and cancels well at low frequencies, but its bandwidth and gain are constrained by loop stability, and it can amplify noise or ring near the loop''s frequency limits. It is frequently combined with a feedforward stage in hybrid designs.' WHERE id='2fd05f87-3406-4617-a250-6a128840f5ee' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An active-noise-cancellation topology that uses a reference microphone on the outside of the earcup to sense incoming noise before it reaches the ear, so that a controller can compute and radiate an anti-noise signal timed to cancel that noise at the ear. Sensing the disturbance upstream lets the feedforward path act over a wider bandwidth than a feedback loop, but its accuracy depends on the fixed model of the acoustic path from the external microphone to the ear and is therefore sensitive to fit, leakage and the direction of the noise. It is commonly paired with a feedback stage in hybrid systems.' WHERE id='5a16ef42-6367-45eb-a8d6-550187498735' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The magnetic flux per unit area in the voice-coil gap of a loudspeaker motor, denoted B and expressed in tesla, representing the strength of the magnetic field through which the coil conductor moves. Together with the length of coil wire lying in the gap it forms the force factor Bl, so gap flux density directly determines the motor''s force per ampere and hence the driver''s efficiency and sensitivity. It is set by the magnet, the pole geometry and the gap dimensions, and is limited by magnetic saturation of the pole material.' WHERE id='572ccb50-6a35-4e5d-b35e-6d294a0590bb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The measurement of a loudspeaker driver''s force factor, Bl, the product of the magnetic flux density in the gap and the length of voice-coil conductor within it, which quantifies the electrodynamic coupling between current and mechanical force and is expressed equivalently in tesla-metres or newtons per ampere. It is obtained statically, for example by measuring force per unit current, or dynamically from the electrical parameters, and modern characterisation also maps its variation with displacement, Bl(x), to capture motor non-linearity. Force factor governs sensitivity, damping and large-signal distortion.' WHERE id='3c86251c-4a75-4db0-9b79-af59c2b2925c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The measurement of a loudspeaker driver''s free-air resonance frequency, fs, the fundamental resonance of the moving system when the unit is suspended in open air without baffle or enclosure loading. It is read from the peak of the electrical-impedance magnitude versus frequency and reflects the interaction of the moving mass with the suspension compliance, making it a basic Thiele-Small parameter and the starting point for deriving the quality factors and equivalent compliance volume. Care is taken to mount the driver so that no added air load shifts the result.' WHERE id='cfa41279-0db4-4258-9b6f-54bff5074bb9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A headphone response target, developed through listener-preference research at Harman International, that specifies the preferred frequency response measured at a standardised ear or head simulator. It is grounded in the response of an accurately calibrated loudspeaker auditioned in a reference listening room, translated to the headphone measurement point, and refined by controlled double-blind preference testing, yielding a curve that a majority of listeners prefer. It has become a widely cited reference against which headphones are tuned and evaluated.' WHERE id='6c816cf6-da75-48e1-b2a3-4c9829bb6e08' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The formal testing and approval of headphones or earphones against the standards required to place them on the market, covering electrical safety, electromagnetic compatibility and, notably, limits on sound-exposure level intended to protect hearing. Certification verifies conformance to the applicable regulatory and standards requirements, such as the volume-limiting and dose provisions for personal music players and the general product-safety framework, and documents the results for regulatory acceptance. Because it concerns exposure levels that bear on hearing safety, the specified limits and test conditions must be taken from the governing standards rather than assumed.' WHERE id='9f7388ea-1625-4199-8f21-ebdd36c45a8e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The engineering discipline of designing headphones and earphones, encompassing the choice and design of the transducer, the acoustics of the earcup or in-ear coupling and any porting, the coupling to the ear and the resulting response at the eardrum reference, together with isolation, comfort and the tuning to a chosen target response. It relies on measurement using standardised ear and head simulators and on the electromechanical-acoustical modelling of the small cavities that load the driver. The work balances acoustic performance against ergonomic and mechanical constraints.' WHERE id='cbda5efb-bb21-400d-97a3-6779b896101f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The intended frequency response that a headphone is designed to achieve at a defined reference point, typically the eardrum reference point or the drum-reference point of an ear simulator, against which its measured response is judged. Common targets include the diffuse-field and free-field responses derived from the acoustics of the human head, and preference-based curves such as the Harman target, each specifying the desired spectral balance at the measurement point. The target guides both the tuning of the design and the interpretation of headphone measurements.' WHERE id='3f2261ac-880f-4bd5-8c26-750303a0efa5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The analysis of how a horn acoustically loads the driver mounted at its throat, whereby the gradually expanding passage transforms the low radiation impedance at the large mouth to a high impedance at the small throat, improving the match between the driver and the air and thereby raising efficiency over the horn''s operating band. The analysis relates the flare profile and rate, the throat and mouth areas and the length to the low-frequency cutoff, the loading impedance and the directivity, using horn-equation and equivalent-circuit models. It also addresses higher-frequency distortion arising from non-linear propagation in the compressed throat air.' WHERE id='94cc43e6-4f1d-458a-bd14-10941c61b8cd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A measurement in which the electrical impedance of a loudspeaker driver or system is recorded as a function of frequency, usually as magnitude and phase, by exciting the unit and monitoring the resulting voltage and current. The impedance curve reveals the fundamental resonance and its peak, from which Thiele-Small parameters are extracted, and it exposes the effects of enclosures, crossovers and defects such as shorted turns or leaks. It is a standard characterisation and quality-control tool because so much electromechanical behaviour is visible in the impedance versus frequency.' WHERE id='9618bfda-b895-48d0-a27b-cd2e7d54a061' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of in-ear monitors, sealed earphones inserted into the ear canal that use balanced-armature drivers, dynamic drivers or a hybrid of both, frequently split by passive crossovers and routed through tuned sound bores and acoustic dampers to shape the response. Because the driver couples to the small sealed residual canal volume, the seal quality governs low-frequency output and isolation, and designs are often custom-moulded to the ear for fit and repeatability. The engineering balances driver count and integration, acoustic tube design and the summation of outputs at the eardrum reference.' WHERE id='8bc0e805-2325-4e32-8fdc-9eadeafa90e3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The set of techniques used to suppress acoustic standing waves, the resonant modes of the air enclosed within a loudspeaker cabinet or transmission line, which would otherwise be re-radiated through the cone or port and colour the midrange. Control measures include filling or lining the enclosure with absorptive material, choosing internal proportions that avoid coincident dimensions, adding internal bracing or partitions, and tapering or stuffing transmission-line paths so that the offending modes are damped or shifted out of the audible passband. The aim is a smooth internal acoustic environment that does not imprint its resonances on the driver''s output.' WHERE id='073cd2a9-663c-4070-8018-48846f8ed759' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The measurement of the passive attenuation that a headphone or earphone provides against external sound, quantifying how much ambient noise is blocked by the earpiece and its seal as a function of frequency. It is obtained as an insertion loss, comparing the sound reaching the eardrum reference of an ear or head simulator with the earpiece present and absent, and it depends strongly on the fit, the seal and the earpiece construction. Isolation measurement characterises the passive component distinct from any active noise cancellation.' WHERE id='d55a7c0e-3ac8-4e11-9f49-231daa61be6a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The measurement of acoustic leakage at a sealing interface, most commonly the seal between a headphone or in-ear earphone and the head or ear canal, quantifying how imperfect or broken seals allow air paths that reduce low-frequency output and passive isolation. It is assessed by observing the change in the measured response, or in the sealed-cavity impedance, as the seal is varied on an ear or head simulator, and analogous leakage checks apply to loudspeaker enclosures. The measurement quantifies the sensitivity of a design''s bass and isolation to real-world fit.' WHERE id='24482617-559d-416e-bf2a-f047de478f4b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The matching of the left and right transducers of a stereo pair or headphone so that their sensitivity and frequency response agree within a specified tolerance, ensuring a balanced and correctly centred stereo image. Deviations between channels shift the perceived image and alter tonal balance, so units are measured and selected, or their electronics trimmed, to keep the interchannel difference within limits. It is a routine element of production quality control for paired transducers.' WHERE id='24c3c8d4-6298-4f0d-b986-12a30b02fecd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A reliability procedure in which a loudspeaker is driven continuously with a specified test signal, typically band-limited pink noise shaped to a standardized spectrum, at its rated power for an extended interval on the order of hours, to confirm that the driver survives sustained thermal and mechanical stress without permanent degradation. It targets long-term failure mechanisms such as voice-coil overheating, adhesive softening, former deformation, and gradual parameter shift, distinguishing steady-state power endurance from short-duration or peak capability. Pass or fail is judged by post-test changes in impedance, sensitivity, and Thiele-Small parameters together with the absence of audible defects.' WHERE id='b5a5c2b3-f915-4895-af3c-c34dc427fcb8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The applied discipline concerned with the design, modeling, and manufacture of electroacoustic transducers and the systems that house them, integrating electromagnetics, mechanical vibration, and acoustics to convert electrical signals into radiated sound. It encompasses motor (magnet and voice-coil) design, diaphragm and suspension mechanics, enclosure and port alignment through the electromechanical-acoustical analogy and Thiele-Small theory, crossover networks, and the measurement and reliability testing used to verify performance.' WHERE id='ddad4879-0c1d-4540-ad2a-544e79461b49' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The final, largely subjective stage of loudspeaker development in which the system''s frequency response, crossover, and tonal balance are fine-tuned by ear, supported by measurement, to achieve a target sonic character. Voicing adjusts driver levels, crossover slopes and component values, and equalization to reconcile measured on- and off-axis response with the intended perceptual result, reflecting design intent and expected use rather than a single objectively flat curve.' WHERE id='23e4a323-b032-4905-8fd0-540efab80c00' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The narrow annular air gap in a loudspeaker''s magnetic circuit, formed between the center pole piece and the top plate, within which the voice coil is suspended and where the radial magnetic flux is concentrated. Its width, height, and the flux density it develops set the force factor Bl together with the coil, while its dimensional precision governs available linear excursion, mechanical clearance, and susceptibility to rubbing.' WHERE id='98601d46-9c0e-40ba-91f5-31a1cdb9551b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The quality process of confirming that produced drivers or systems fall within specified allowable deviations for critical parameters, such as resonance frequency, DC resistance, sensitivity, and Thiele-Small values, relative to the design nominal. It combines dimensional inspection with electroacoustic measurement against upper and lower limits so that unit-to-unit variation stays within bounds that preserve pair matching and system alignment.' WHERE id='e53aea19-32a4-44f9-b3b1-312bb7e6278c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Determination of Qms, the dimensionless quality factor describing the sharpness of a driver''s fundamental resonance as governed by mechanical losses alone, namely suspension damping and internal friction, with electrical damping excluded. It is derived from the free-air impedance curve by locating the resonance frequency fs and the frequencies at which impedance falls to a defined level relative to the peak, a higher Qms indicating lower mechanical loss.' WHERE id='64fe6349-06d1-4b92-abc1-c6b606018b8d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A robustness test in which a driver, finished loudspeaker, or its packaged unit is released from a defined height onto a hard surface at specified orientations of faces, edges, and corners to evaluate resistance to the mechanical shock encountered in handling and transport. Outcomes are assessed by inspection and by pre- and post-test electroacoustic measurement for cracked frames, dislodged magnets, coil misalignment, or parameter shift.' WHERE id='f920e29a-5804-48aa-a6f0-acdbffdca441' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of transducers optimized to reproduce the middle of the audio band, roughly a few hundred hertz to several kilohertz, where much of the ear''s sensitivity and vocal and instrumental energy lies. It balances a light, rigid, well-damped diaphragm and controlled breakup behavior with a motor and suspension tuned for low distortion and smooth, wide dispersion across the passband, and often includes enclosure isolation or a sealed sub-chamber to decouple the midrange from woofer pressure.' WHERE id='7dfd42f7-24d9-462b-857c-838fe9846664' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Environmental testing that exposes a loudspeaker or driver to controlled high humidity, often combined with elevated or cycling temperature in a damp-heat regime, to assess degradation of cones, adhesives, foams, and metal parts from moisture ingress and condensation. Failure indicators include cone sag or delamination, corrosion of the voice coil or terminals, adhesive weakening, and the resulting shifts in compliance and resonance.' WHERE id='aaf1af60-4cd9-4aff-b64a-2c3e89553728' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Determination of Mms, the total effective moving mass of the vibrating assembly, comprising the diaphragm, voice coil, former, and the moving portion of the suspension together with the acoustic radiation mass of the air loading both faces of the diaphragm. It is commonly obtained by the added-mass or added-volume method, measuring the resonance frequency before and after a known mass is attached or a known enclosure volume is applied and solving the resulting relations, Mms being fundamental to sensitivity and to Thiele-Small modeling.' WHERE id='2f2a51ca-d471-4d33-a419-3a83f71e349d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A headphone architecture in which the rear of each earcup is acoustically open, through perforations or a grille, so the diaphragm radiates freely to the outside air rather than into a sealed rear volume. Removing the trapped rear air lowers and linearizes the compliance-controlled load and reduces internal resonances and pressure buildup, yielding lower distortion and a more open, spacious presentation, at the cost of little passive isolation and significant sound leakage.' WHERE id='38505b89-6792-434c-b908-bbcc3f8a3f9d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The verification that a product''s protective packaging adequately preserves a loudspeaker or system through the distribution environment, using a defined sequence of simulated hazards, including drop and shock, vibration, compression and stacking, and climatic exposure, applied to the packaged unit. Validation confirms, by post-test functional and cosmetic inspection, that the pack design keeps the product within damage limits over the expected shipping cycle.' WHERE id='e977fd33-b4bb-4708-a80a-34583739b240' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The attenuation of external ambient sound achieved purely by the physical construction of a headphone or earphone, through earcup sealing, cushion compliance, ear-canal fit, and mass, without active cancellation. It arises from acoustic sealing and transmission loss and is inherently frequency-dependent, providing strong attenuation at high frequencies and progressively less toward low frequencies where seal leakage dominates.' WHERE id='7ebfb425-0e79-447c-9c18-f1af26aab3e5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The alignment of a loudspeaker system that uses an undriven diaphragm, the passive radiator or drone cone, in place of a vented port to load a woofer, where the radiator''s moving mass and suspension compliance set the box tuning frequency in a manner analogous to a Helmholtz port. Tuning selects and trims the radiator''s mass, and hence its resonance in the enclosure, so that its output reinforces the driver near the low-frequency cutoff, and it requires the radiator''s compliant excursion capability to exceed the driver''s to avoid limiting.' WHERE id='a0f4548d-a0dd-449f-aa53-fef932d7cf7b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A test that applies brief, high-amplitude signal bursts to establish the maximum instantaneous power or voltage a loudspeaker can withstand without immediate mechanical or electrical damage, as opposed to sustained thermal endurance. It probes short-term limits such as suspension over-excursion, coil bottoming, lead-wire fatigue, and dielectric breakdown, with pass or fail based on survival and the absence of parameter change or audible defect after the bursts.' WHERE id='ed932f75-d584-4e9b-a323-4ddfed0e9e61' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The tailoring of a headphone or loudspeaker''s frequency-response correction to an individual listener, based on measurements of their hearing, their ear and head acoustics such as ear-canal or head-related transfer functions, or their elicited preference, rather than a single population-average target. Implemented in DSP, it applies a per-user filter so that the sound reaching the eardrum matches the desired target curve, compensating for individual anatomy and hearing differences.' WHERE id='daf64cee-c616-4cac-9285-67a6ffa6f250' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A headphone using a planar or isodynamic transducer in which a large, very light flat diaphragm carries an etched or bonded conductive trace and is suspended in a magnetic field produced by arrays of bar magnets on one or both sides. Current through the distributed conductor exerts a nearly uniform driving force across the whole membrane, so it moves almost as a piston with low mass, low distortion, and fast transient response, at the cost of lower sensitivity and greater weight than dynamic types.' WHERE id='5c574750-8723-49f7-a38c-a16d63d5b2d5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The shape and dimensions of the ferromagnetic center pole in a loudspeaker motor, including features such as an extended or T-shaped pole, shorting-ring seats, undercuts, and pole-face profiling, which together shape the flux distribution across the magnetic gap. Optimized geometry linearizes and symmetrizes the force factor Bl over excursion, reduces flux modulation and reluctance distortion, and manages heat conduction, directly affecting linearity and thermal capacity.' WHERE id='bf9ac621-e77c-4c29-8bd4-12a58cce8d79' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The process of selecting a vent''s cross-sectional area and length so that the enclosed air mass in the port resonates with the box compliance at a target Helmholtz frequency, aligning the vented system''s low-frequency roll-off to a chosen response such as a maximally flat alignment per Thiele-Small tables. Optimization also sizes the port to keep air velocity low enough to avoid audible turbulence and dynamic compression at high output, frequently flaring the ends to that end.' WHERE id='34f921d8-4a5d-4b90-8c06-7f46ca2e2b5f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A measurement of the loss of acoustic sensitivity that occurs as drive level increases, caused chiefly by voice-coil heating: rising coil temperature raises the coil resistance, reducing the current and force produced for a given input voltage and compressing output relative to the small-signal expectation. The test steps power over a range and reports the sensitivity reduction in decibels at each level, characterizing thermal behavior and effective dynamic capability.' WHERE id='6d96e34e-9b0d-4e19-b586-f2c7500e69ce' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='End-of-line testing performed on manufactured loudspeakers to decide whether each unit, or a sampled lot, conforms to specification before release, typically an automated sequence measuring frequency response, sensitivity, impedance and resonance, polarity, and defect signatures against pass and fail limits. It functions as the gate separating conforming product from rejects and supplies yield and process-control data.' WHERE id='92203bf3-4847-4747-9db2-5c9ee3312a37' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The routine measurement of loudspeakers as they are built, applying a standardized stimulus and evaluating the response for conformance and for audible or measurable defects such as rub, buzz, and air leaks, within a short cycle time on the production line. It emphasizes repeatable, fast, go/no-go assessment suited to high volume rather than exhaustive laboratory characterization.' WHERE id='ee157320-7f3c-4275-87c8-e508d89ebd1d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The statistical practice of inspecting a selected subset of a production lot, rather than every unit, and using the results to accept or reject the lot according to a defined sampling plan with a specified sample size and acceptance number. Such plans set the producer''s and consumer''s risks and the acceptable quality limit, allowing variation to be controlled economically when full inspection is impractical.' WHERE id='a4ac42f3-3d45-4576-a133-36543202f700' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Accelerated life testing in which a loudspeaker is subjected to repeated stress cycles, whether thermal, power on and off, humidity, or mechanical excursion, to precipitate wear-out failure mechanisms and estimate durability over its intended service life. Repeated cycling reveals fatigue, adhesive and solder-joint failure, suspension aging, and progressive parameter drift that single-exposure tests do not surface.' WHERE id='2d6db404-b59d-4b52-bda6-67cbe845a12d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of transducers whose radiating element is a thin, low-mass corrugated metal foil, the ribbon, suspended directly in a magnetic field and carrying the audio current so that the entire conductor is the diaphragm. Because the ribbon''s mass and electrical impedance are extremely low, it is driven through a step-down transformer for impedance matching and yields very high frequency extension and low moving mass; the engineering centers on ribbon tension and corrugation, field strength, and transformer and acoustic-loading design.' WHERE id='a91caaf6-2518-46b0-80e0-c7d97fe9d7cd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A production and diagnostic test that detects mechanical defects producing spurious, non-harmonically-related noise, such as a voice coil rubbing in the gap, loose particles, buzzing lead wires, or a cracked diaphragm, which are only weakly reflected in ordinary total-harmonic-distortion figures. It excites the driver, often with a swept sine, and analyzes the residual for the impulsive, high-order distortion and noise signatures characteristic of these faults.' WHERE id='05f2057e-52e1-44e2-9921-6f3f49791546' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A corrosion test that exposes a loudspeaker or its metal parts to a fine atomized saline mist in a controlled chamber for a specified duration to evaluate resistance to salt-laden or marine atmospheres. It accelerates corrosion of grilles, baskets, terminals, and plating, with degradation assessed by inspection and by electrical and acoustic checks for terminal or voice-coil corrosion.' WHERE id='e4ae59cf-3c03-4891-a8c8-4147ec1b0113' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A headphone construction intermediate between a sealed closed back and a fully open back, using a partially vented earcup that relieves some rear pressure while retaining a degree of enclosure. It seeks a compromise, offering more controlled low-frequency loading and better isolation than open designs but less internal resonance and a more spacious sound than fully closed ones, achieved by tuning the size and damping of the rear venting.' WHERE id='0fe36076-730c-4fba-bb29-ca6449e926a1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The selection or trimming of drivers so that units intended to work together, whether a stereo pair or the several drivers of a system, produce the same output level for a given input, keeping sensitivity differences within a tight tolerance. Matching preserves stereo imaging and correct crossover summation and is achieved by measurement-based sorting into bins or by adjusting attenuation, since normal manufacturing spread in force factor, magnetization, and moving mass causes sensitivity to vary.' WHERE id='f8379788-0f29-4a53-aec9-68e72c4047e6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Laboratory reproduction of the mechanical and climatic stresses a packaged loudspeaker experiences in the distribution chain, including random and fixed-frequency vibration, shock and drop, compression, and sometimes temperature and humidity, applied in a representative sequence to predict field damage before shipment. It uses vibration tables and drop testers driven by standardized distribution profiles to validate the product and its packaging together.' WHERE id='0aec552d-79e5-4951-bc1e-067f61e4b9a5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A conductive ring, typically copper or aluminum and also called a Faraday ring, placed on or around the pole piece within a loudspeaker motor so as to act as a shorted single turn linking part of the gap flux. Eddy currents induced in it oppose changes in the magnetic field, stabilizing gap flux against modulation by the voice-coil current and reducing the driver''s voice-coil inductance and its variation with excursion and frequency, thereby lowering flux-modulation and inductance distortion.' WHERE id='f3a5038e-1516-4731-831e-52e5c1010e42' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The mechanical compliance, or inverse stiffness, contributed by the spider, the corrugated inner suspension that centers the voice-coil former and diaphragm at the rear while permitting axial motion. As one of the two suspension elements together with the surround, its compliance combines with the surround''s to set the driver''s total mechanical compliance Cms and hence its resonance, and its progressive stiffening at large excursion is a principal source of suspension nonlinearity.' WHERE id='18b562c4-3cfe-44cb-8515-e668afd1cbc0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The use of statistical methods, principally control charts that track a process characteristic over time against control limits derived from its inherent variation, to monitor a manufacturing process, distinguish common-cause from special-cause variation, and signal when intervention is needed. Applied to loudspeaker production it keeps parameters such as resonance frequency, voice-coil resistance, and sensitivity centered and stable, detecting drift before nonconforming units are made.' WHERE id='21eddc42-baee-4bff-b875-631cc6e5aad3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A headphone worn with its cushions resting on the outer ear rather than enclosing it, that is, an on-ear rather than a circumaural design. The pad seals against the pinna instead of against the head around it, giving a smaller, lighter form and only a partial acoustic seal, which affects low-frequency response and passive isolation and makes the acoustic coupling more sensitive to placement and clamping force.' WHERE id='424dc9b6-e19a-44b6-b4c6-3d2c77ddd012' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The mechanical compliance contributed by the surround, the outer suspension roll that joins the diaphragm rim to the frame, sealing and supporting the diaphragm''s outer edge while permitting axial excursion. It combines with the spider''s compliance to define the total mechanical compliance Cms and the resonance frequency, and its material and roll geometry govern restoring-force linearity, the damping of edge resonances, and maximum excursion.' WHERE id='8292f95d-50ea-4b05-a5b0-69654f17c3bf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Cms, the total mechanical compliance of the driver''s suspension system, being the combined effect of the spider and surround expressed as displacement per unit force in metres per newton and equal to the inverse of the suspension''s mechanical stiffness. Together with the moving mass Mms it sets the driver''s free-air resonance frequency, and its nonlinearity with excursion is a major determinant of large-signal distortion.' WHERE id='a78d7236-6d8b-4ec3-b4f8-af48e87a4b3a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A measurement quantifying the reduction in loudspeaker output caused specifically by voice-coil heating, in which power is applied until the coil reaches thermal equilibrium and the resulting rise in coil resistance, and the consequent drop in sensitivity, is measured. It characterizes the driver''s thermal resistance and its power compression, informing the maximum usable output and the thermal design of the motor.' WHERE id='a3ca6a57-8ead-4b32-97d2-0598e9c878e6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The determination of the small-signal electromechanical parameters, including fs, Qes, Qms, Qts, Vas, Re, Bl, Cms, Mms, and Sd, that characterize a driver''s low-frequency behavior and predict its performance in sealed and vented enclosures. It is typically done from the free-air impedance curve to obtain the resonance frequency and the quality factors, followed by an added-mass or added-volume perturbation to separate compliance from moving mass and thus derive the equivalent compliance volume and the force factor.' WHERE id='3152f7aa-2e90-447e-895e-151fbebb1512' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Determination of Qts, the driver''s total quality factor at resonance, which combines the mechanical damping Qms and the electrical damping Qes as their parallel combination, equal to Qms times Qes divided by their sum. Qts summarizes the overall damping at the resonance frequency and, together with Vas and fs, is a primary input to the selection of an enclosure alignment.' WHERE id='9d793be6-71b8-4c8b-b49a-ff760a579a85' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A feature of active noise-cancelling headphones and earphones that deliberately passes external ambient sound through to the listener by capturing it with the external microphones and mixing a processed, level-corrected version into the playback, letting the user hear their surroundings without removing the device. It is effectively the inverse of active noise cancellation, using the same microphone array and DSP to reproduce rather than cancel the outside field and compensating for the passive occlusion of the fitted earpiece.' WHERE id='581d7216-61c4-4719-9d45-58b6d5c34f70' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of drivers dedicated to the highest audio frequencies, from a few kilohertz up to and beyond twenty kilohertz, employing a small, light, stiff dome or other diaphragm on a low-mass motor to achieve the fast response and wide dispersion those frequencies demand. It addresses ferrofluid or other cooling and damping, diaphragm material and its first breakup, waveguide loading for directivity control, and protection against thermal and excursion overload given the tweeter''s limited power handling.' WHERE id='777e6cbe-4f3e-4340-8392-ea60c78d7048' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Accelerated weathering testing that subjects loudspeaker materials, including cones, surrounds, foams, grilles, adhesives, and finishes, to controlled ultraviolet radiation, often with heat and moisture cycling, to assess photodegradation such as embrittlement, discoloration, and loss of surround compliance. It predicts outdoor and sunlight aging by reproducing, in an accelerated timeframe, the ultraviolet dose that drives polymer breakdown.' WHERE id='796f15fc-0091-4c11-9a99-10cd8309ffd9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The tubular bobbin, wound with the voice-coil wire, that couples the coil to the diaphragm and rides in the magnetic gap, also called the coil bobbin. Made from materials such as polyimide film, paper, glass fiber, or aluminum, it must be dimensionally stable, light, and, when conductive, may aid heat dissipation, and its stiffness and thermal properties influence power handling and high-frequency mechanical behavior.' WHERE id='76c177fc-4096-4f5a-88af-e4cafb1c08a5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Measurement of Re, the DC ohmic resistance of the voice-coil winding, obtained by applying a small direct current or a low-frequency method that excludes the coil''s inductive and motional impedance. Re is the asymptotic low-frequency floor of the impedance curve, is required to compute Qes and other Thiele-Small parameters, and rises with temperature, which is the mechanism underlying power compression.' WHERE id='f99b1094-0556-4114-8a39-846cdcdd3608' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The coil of insulated conductor, copper or aluminum and of round or flat ribbon wire, wound on the former, which carries the audio current and, in the magnetic field of the gap, generates the axial motor force. Its number of turns, wire gauge and material, layer count, winding height relative to the gap, and total conductor length in the field set the coil resistance, inductance, and mass and the force factor Bl, trading off sensitivity, power handling, and linearity.' WHERE id='4b88e429-e67f-4f04-b5a4-39e007ec9c1c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The shaping of a horn-like guide in front of a driver, commonly a tweeter or compression driver, to control directivity, match acoustic impedance, and smooth the response by tuning the mouth size, throat, and flare profile toward a target coverage pattern and constant directivity over the passband. Optimization trades off directivity control, loading gain, and diffraction-related response smoothness, often adopting constant-directivity profiles.' WHERE id='2176e206-9bae-4787-9b45-49ed8c82f94f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of drivers optimized for low-frequency reproduction, using a relatively large, long-excursion diaphragm on a motor and suspension able to move a substantial volume of air with linear control at high displacement. It centers on maximizing linear excursion, force-factor and compliance linearity, moving mass and equivalent compliance volume for the intended enclosure alignment, thermal power handling, and cone rigidity to defer breakup, in concert with sealed or vented box design through Thiele-Small theory.' WHERE id='43b03b17-0bf0-45b7-b743-6e62df820571' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A manufacturing document that defines how the parts of a printed-circuit assembly are placed and oriented on the board, showing component outlines, reference designators, polarity and pin-one indicators, and any assembly notes. It is used by assembly operators and inspectors to build the unit and to verify correct part placement and orientation against the design.' WHERE id='073f2659-2ae8-4119-8cb3-1335518d10df' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A bill of materials: the structured list of every part required to build an assembly, giving for each line item the reference designators, manufacturer part number, value or description, package, and quantity. It is the authoritative source that drives procurement, kitting, and the placement of components during assembly, and it is version-controlled together with the design.' WHERE id='432b415b-3bdf-4453-b5d9-4db95cdb0239' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The permissible deviation of a component''s actual value from its nominal (marked) value, stated as a plus-or-minus percentage or absolute range within which the manufacturer guarantees the parameter. Tolerance defines the spread that a design must accommodate, is coordinated with the preferred (E-series) value scheme so that adjacent nominal values do not overlap, and is indicated by marking codes on the part.' WHERE id='ae4148fa-e0de-47ae-aa5e-5d9273502bda' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The nominal electrical rating that identifies a passive component, such as resistance in ohms, capacitance in farads, or inductance in henries, as specified and marked on the part. Standard components are made in preferred (E-series) value steps whose spacing is matched to their tolerance, and the marked value together with the tolerance defines the guaranteed range of the actual parameter.' WHERE id='94374795-7eb4-4ae8-9585-029cc8f9c90e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The manufacturer''s technical document for a component or device that specifies its electrical, mechanical, thermal and environmental characteristics, including absolute maximum ratings, recommended operating conditions, package and pin-out, and performance curves. It serves as the authoritative reference for part selection, circuit design and qualification, and its parameter definitions follow standardised presentation and test conditions.' WHERE id='1a6a2d02-a64b-47de-8659-bf88dff1044d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A manufacturer''s marking indicating when a component was produced, most commonly a four-digit year-and-week code (YYWW) or an equivalent coded form. It supports lot traceability, shelf-life and moisture-sensitive-device floor-life management, and quality investigations by tying a part back to its date of manufacture.' WHERE id='12a5e1a3-9c72-46b3-9169-811c5888a768' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A designator: the standardised alphanumeric label assigned to an individual component on a schematic and circuit board to identify it uniquely, formed from a class letter denoting the component type (for example R for resistor, C for capacitor, U for integrated circuit) followed by a sequence number. It correlates the same part across the schematic, bill of materials, assembly drawing and physical placement; the full form is the reference designator.' WHERE id='b8154198-c7bc-4597-8f35-8d93ffa5ee11' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A data file that describes one layer or feature set of a printed circuit board for photoplotting and fabrication, encoding copper, solder-mask, silkscreen and drill features as draw and flash commands referencing an aperture list. The extended Gerber format (RS-274X, and later Gerber X2 with embedded attributes) is the de facto interchange standard between PCB design tools and board fabricators.' WHERE id='5cbf1d56-a589-438b-8de9-9b794e668ec6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A code marked on a component or its packaging that identifies the specific production batch (lot) from which it came, linking the part to the manufacturer''s records of materials, process conditions and test results for that run. Lot codes underpin traceability, quality investigation and recall management, allowing affected units to be isolated by production batch.' WHERE id='f8d98dfe-d32f-4ac6-a82d-6a948fbe00f5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The unique alphanumeric identifier that a component manufacturer assigns to a specific part, distinguishing an exact combination of value, tolerance, package, temperature grade and other attributes from every other product in its catalogue. It is used for precise sourcing and procurement and is distinct from a distributor''s ordering number or a company''s internal part number.' WHERE id='95aaa235-11e3-4589-adc0-cab31f2c30d8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The abbreviation for manufacturer part number: the unique alphanumeric identifier assigned by a component manufacturer to one exact part, encoding a specific combination of value, tolerance, package and grade. It provides an unambiguous reference for procurement and for matching a bill-of-materials line to the correct catalogue item, separate from distributor or internal part numbers.' WHERE id='b74b0142-0658-4da4-83c6-93aff74972c4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A version identifier for a printed-circuit-board design that denotes a specific released iteration of its layout and artwork. It is incremented under configuration control whenever the board changes, so that fabrication, assembly, documentation and inspection all reference the same version, and it is commonly marked in the copper or silkscreen of the finished board.' WHERE id='4cde7826-471a-470a-81e5-1d59e11e3e85' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A manufacturing data file, also called a centroid or XY file, that lists for each surface-mount component its reference designator, X and Y placement coordinates, rotation angle and board side. It is used to program automated pick-and-place machines so they position each part correctly on the board during assembly.' WHERE id='5a68b079-d1b4-4ba9-bda8-bc04fc9d0cb8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A standardised alphanumeric label, such as R1, C4 or U3, assigned to each component in a schematic and on the board to identify it uniquely and to correlate the schematic, bill of materials, assembly drawing and physical placement. The leading class letter denotes the component type according to recognised conventions, and the number distinguishes individual parts of that type.' WHERE id='bb3c1825-96fc-4dff-9e24-826f7da6b182' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An identifier denoting a specific released version of a document, drawing, schematic or board design within a configuration- and change-control system. It is incremented on each approved change so that all parties work from the same, current iteration, and the change history records what differs between successive revisions.' WHERE id='9f8d5ef2-5c69-4fd9-814c-d6a4e77a9e48' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A standardised graphical representation of an electronic component or function used in a circuit diagram, conveying the part''s type and the arrangement of its terminals independently of its physical form or package. Standard symbol sets ensure that schematics are read consistently across designers, tools and organisations.' WHERE id='8c1a0156-c00a-4a4a-85a8-53d684b06499' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The manufacturer-specified range of ambient or operating temperatures over which a component is guaranteed to meet its published specifications, together with any wider storage-temperature limits it can withstand without damage. It reflects the part''s materials and construction, is verified by environmental test methods, and is stated in the datasheet, often as a named grade (for example commercial, industrial or automotive).' WHERE id='c91eb89c-27fd-48e9-8151-28faf56e19f5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The maximum voltage a component is rated to withstand continuously across its terminals without breakdown or damage, such as the working (rated) voltage of a capacitor or the maximum working voltage of a resistor. It is defined in the datasheet and is normally applied with a design derating margin below the rated figure to ensure reliability.' WHERE id='7f890e79-ed8b-4335-bede-e8ae51c9331f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The maximum electrical power, in watts, that a component (typically a resistor) can dissipate continuously as heat at a specified reference ambient temperature without exceeding its temperature limits. Above that reference temperature the allowable power falls according to a derating curve, and designs apply a margin below the rated value for reliability.' WHERE id='6a9cdaee-f5b5-40fa-957d-b04b692bbe9d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In vinyl pre-mastering, bass summing is the practice of progressively combining the low-frequency content of the two stereo channels toward mono below a chosen crossover frequency, so that bass energy appears as lateral (horizontal) rather than vertical groove modulation. Because out-of-phase low frequencies drive the cutting and playback styli vertically and can cause the stylus to break contact with the groove, summing the bass to mono keeps the cutter tracking and permits a hotter, more consistent low end. It is normally implemented with an elliptical equaliser in the cutting chain and is a standard step in preparing a mix for disk cutting.' WHERE id='f1cb3d04-1fb5-4f89-a725-ad6019555279' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Bypass comparison is the level-matched A/B evaluation technique in which the engineer alternately switches a processor, or an entire signal chain, in and out of circuit to judge its effect on the program. Because added gain or loudness is itself perceived as improvement, a valid bypass comparison requires that the processed and unprocessed signals be matched in loudness, so that only tonal, dynamic, or spatial changes rather than level differences inform the decision. It is a fundamental quality-control method throughout mastering for confirming that each processing stage is a genuine net benefit.' WHERE id='260414e4-2d85-43ea-aee8-042933dbc38a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In disk mastering, an elliptical filter, or elliptical equaliser, is a device that increasingly sums the stereo low frequencies to mono below a set corner frequency, converting difference-signal (vertical) bass into sum-signal (lateral) bass. Its purpose is to limit vertical groove modulation at low frequencies, which the cutter head cannot reproduce without the stylus breaking contact, thereby protecting groove integrity and allowing greater cutting level and playing time. In this context the term denotes the stereo bass mono-summing processor of the vinyl cutting chain and is distinct from the elliptic (Cauer) filter of general network theory.' WHERE id='2d98d9c7-4fd0-4636-9696-ec29cd0038f2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='File validation is the quality-control step in the mastering delivery chain in which a finished master file or disc image is checked against the target specification before release or replication. Typical checks confirm the file format, sample rate, bit depth, and channel count; verify data integrity, for example through checksums or DDP CRC verification; and inspect for clipping, inter-sample overs, truncation, encoding faults, and correct metadata. It ensures the deliverable conforms to the requirements of the distributor or pressing plant so that no defects are introduced downstream.' WHERE id='9f8c9813-ec7f-4194-827d-1463ecad1ca6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Groove depth is the vertical dimension of the cut groove in a phonograph record, namely the distance the cutting stylus penetrates into the lacquer. It is governed by the depth setting of the cutter head together with the vertical, difference-signal component of the modulation; sufficient depth is required so that the playback stylus sits securely and the groove walls retain enough material, while excessive depth wastes lacquer and radial space. Depth is managed in concert with pitch by the lathe''s depth-and-pitch computer to balance level, playing time, and tracking security.' WHERE id='b0d0a0dd-8318-4f79-b418-ff6d53e58f7a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Groove pitch is the packing density of the groove spiral on a disc, expressed as the number of grooves, or lines, per inch of radius; its reciprocal, the radial distance the groove advances per revolution, is the groove spacing. On a variable-pitch lathe the pitch is continuously adjusted by the pitch computer, opening the spacing ahead of loud, low-frequency passages and closing it for quiet ones, so as to fit the required playing time onto the side without adjacent grooves cutting into one another. Higher pitch, meaning more lines per inch, yields longer playing time at the expense of maximum attainable level.' WHERE id='e924b56a-405e-4f6c-89ba-29c48c780db4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Groove spacing is the radial distance between successive turns of the groove spiral, the land that separates one revolution of the groove from the next. It is the inverse expression of groove pitch and must be kept wide enough that heavily modulated grooves do not cut through into their neighbours, an overcut, yet narrow enough to achieve the needed playing time. On variable-pitch lathes the spacing is varied dynamically in response to the program level supplied by the preview signal.' WHERE id='a25140bf-c067-44e2-81f9-a9ea8e0ff6ae' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Horizontal, or lateral, modulation is the side-to-side component of groove displacement in a disc recording. In the 45/45 stereo system it corresponds to the sum (L+R) mono signal and is the component that a monophonic lateral-cut record carries in its entirety; it is generally more robust and can be cut at higher levels than the vertical component. Because mono and low-frequency energy is carried laterally, engineers deliberately steer bass toward horizontal modulation, through bass summing and elliptical filtering, to protect groove tracking.' WHERE id='5e884de5-54a6-42f6-8678-881983815e27' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A lacquer cut is the operation of inscribing the audio program onto a lacquer, or acetate, master disc using a cutting lathe, and by extension the resulting cut disc itself. A heated cutting stylus driven by the cutter head engraves a modulated spiral groove into the nitrocellulose lacquer coating of an aluminium substrate; this lacquer master is then electroplated to produce the metal stampers used to press vinyl records. The lacquer cut is the physical culmination of the vinyl mastering chain, and its quality sets the ceiling for the pressed product.' WHERE id='91d18123-5675-4603-95cc-b4ead512bc63' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The preview head is a supplementary playback head on the source machine feeding a disk-cutting lathe, positioned so that it reproduces the program a fixed interval, one lathe revolution, ahead of the main program head. This advance, or preview, signal is fed to the lathe''s pitch-and-depth computer so that groove spacing and depth can be opened up in anticipation of loud or bass-heavy passages before they are actually cut, enabling automatic variable pitch. Originally a second head on a tape machine offset by one turntable revolution, the same look-ahead function is provided by a delay in modern digital cutting systems.' WHERE id='b537d3a5-f969-497c-9b4b-44275e0bc48e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Side timing is the determination and allocation of the total playing time for each side of a record during vinyl pre-mastering and cutting. Because groove pitch, maximum cutting level, and low-frequency content all trade against playing time, the cutting engineer uses the side''s running time to set the achievable level and pitch: shorter sides allow louder, wider-groove cuts with extended bass, whereas longer sides force tighter pitch and reduced level. Timing the sides, and balancing the program across the sides of an album, is therefore a key planning step that constrains the sonic result.' WHERE id='cb7f3bba-dfd8-4901-8709-545b653abbb3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Vertical modulation is the up-and-down, or depth, component of groove displacement in a disc recording. In the 45/45 stereo system it carries the difference (L-R) signal; excessive vertical modulation, particularly at low frequencies or when the signal is out of phase, drives the stylus toward the groove floor or lifts it clear of the groove, causing mistracking or skipping. Controlling vertical modulation, by phase correction, bass mono-summing, and elliptical filtering, is essential to producing a cuttable and reliably playable master.' WHERE id='8d8ad8e4-54ff-486d-a03a-83c8b5bb66bf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A vinyl pre-master is a version of the finished master prepared specifically for disc cutting rather than for digital release. It typically differs from the digital master by avoiding brickwall limiting, taming sibilance and harsh high frequencies, summing or centring the low bass, and checking mono compatibility and absolute phase, and it supplies correct song sequencing, spacing, and side splits with running times for the cutting engineer. The vinyl pre-master thus gives the cutting engineer a source optimised for the mechanical constraints of the medium.' WHERE id='b8a74c28-af8f-45fe-9b38-0bd11fd85b88' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A reference instrumental part recorded to give performers a pitch, harmonic, and timing framework to play or sing against during tracking, not intended to appear in the final master. Commonly a guide piano, keyboard, or guitar laid down with the rhythm section or to a click, it outlines the song''s chords and form so subsequent overdubs stay in tune and in time, and is usually replaced or muted once the keeper parts are recorded.' WHERE id='68ee5e3b-3703-49da-b49c-b954baceb8bf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A preliminary reference performance captured to steer the recording of a song, providing tempo, feel, phrasing, and structure for the musicians who will lay down the keeper parts. Whether a rough full-band take or a single guide part, it functions as a temporary scaffold, often recorded to a click, and is generally not used in the final master, being replaced by the definitive performances built around it.' WHERE id='090337d3-b8ca-44f3-91d9-ca4227be086a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A reference vocal recorded early in production so that instrumentalists and later overdubs can follow the song''s melody, lyrics, phrasing, and structure, typically not intended for the final master. Often cut live with the rhythm section or to a click to convey feel and timing cues, the guide (or scratch) vocal is usually re-recorded as the lead vocal once the backing tracks are complete, though an exceptional guide take is sometimes kept.' WHERE id='68a1c3b8-ae1e-4968-ba2c-05b8217456aa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The contractual and practical procedure by which a completed recording (the master) is reviewed and formally accepted as satisfactory before it is released, mastered, or paid for. In recording agreements the master must typically be approved by the record company, and sometimes the artist, as technically and commercially satisfactory, a condition that can gate delivery, advances, and release and that governs revisions, re-recording, and sign-off. It establishes when a recording is deemed final and who holds the approval rights.' WHERE id='9f33094e-df98-4fde-9d3e-b2e0dd73811a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The overall process of creating a finished recorded piece of music, spanning pre-production and arrangement, the recording of performances through tracking and overdubbing, editing, mixing, and mastering, together with the creative and technical direction that shapes the record''s sound. Guided by a producer, it turns a song or composition into a released master through choices of arrangement, instrumentation, performance, and sonic treatment, coordinating artists, musicians, and engineers toward the intended artistic and commercial result.' WHERE id='11c098af-856f-4555-bfd1-f87b61818df4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A temporary, non-final guitar part recorded as a reference to establish chords, groove, and timing for the rest of a session, comparable to a scratch or guide vocal. Laid down with the rhythm section or to a click, it gives the other players a harmonic and rhythmic guide and is normally replaced by the definitive guitar overdub, though a strong scratch take is occasionally kept.' WHERE id='b9732b40-f7a0-43a9-8722-e50f7a3addc6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The organization of a song''s musical elements into its final form: the choice and ordering of sections such as intro, verse, chorus, bridge, and outro, the instrumentation and voicings, and the distribution of parts, dynamics, and textures across the performance. Arrangement determines how the composition is realized and paced, shaping which instruments play what, when, and in what register to serve the song, and is a key pre-recording decision that defines the track''s structure and feel.' WHERE id='516e614d-a633-4e35-bb8e-63137b90782d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The digital emulation of a guitar or bass amplifier''s sound, including its preamp gain staging, tone stack, nonlinear distortion and power-amplifier behaviour, by a DSP model that transforms a clean direct signal so it sounds as though played through the modeled amp. Approaches range from white-box circuit or component modeling, which simulates the underlying electronics, to black-box, data-driven identification, increasingly using neural networks trained on the amplifier''s measured input and output.' WHERE id='8e0a1d06-06f7-4793-a63a-669143132aab' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A common shorthand for amplifier simulator, meaning a software plug-in or hardware unit that reproduces the sound of guitar or bass amplifiers, and frequently their cabinets and effects, through digital signal processing. Historically such simulators used circuit-based or analog modeling of the amplifier''s stages, while more recent products increasingly rely on machine learning and neural modeling; the term is used interchangeably with amp modeling when referring to the product.' WHERE id='b79e66df-be04-45c2-8ad4-d81c25efd841' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The emulation of the tone imparted by a speaker cabinet together with its loudspeaker and the microphone capturing it, which together colour the amplifier''s output. Because this chain is very nearly linear and time-invariant, it is most commonly modeled by convolving the signal with an impulse response that captures the chain''s frequency response, though neural methods are also used; cabinet modeling is normally applied after amp modeling in the signal chain.' WHERE id='a276fe20-d1f9-4e64-91d0-aa2030e2456e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An open-source neural amp modeling system, created by Steven Atkinson and commonly abbreviated NAM, that trains a deep neural network, in a WaveNet-derived architecture, on paired recordings of a clean input signal and the same signal passed through a real amplifier or distortion device. The trained model file captures that device''s nonlinear behaviour and is played back in real time by the NAM plug-in, letting users capture and share models of specific gear.' WHERE id='174423fa-8bdb-4b77-8e2f-bd647e6944a7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The general technique of capturing an amplifier''s or distortion device''s nonlinear input-to-output behaviour with a trained neural network, using paired clean and processed audio for supervised, black-box system identification, and then running the network in real time as an audio effect. It contrasts with circuit-based modeling by learning the response from measurement rather than simulating components; the widely used open-source implementation of the idea is the Neural Amp Modeler software.' WHERE id='469b0109-4a51-41bd-a1a4-aee6e2862206' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The process of capturing a real amplifier''s sonic character by sending reference or test signals through it, measuring the resulting response, and reconstructing that behaviour in a digital model, so that the profile can be recalled without the original hardware. Popularised by the Kemper Profiler, it is the capture and identification stage of amp modeling, conceptually similar to the training step used by neural approaches, and produces a portable model of a specific amp and its settings.' WHERE id='330de2e3-b18b-43ae-be79-b3114e19bc72' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Brass-section mixing is the balancing and processing of the orchestral brass, the horns, trumpets, trombones, and tuba, within an ensemble mix so that their power and directivity are integrated without overwhelming the other sections. Because brass is highly directional and dynamic, radiating strongly on axis and hardening in timbre at high levels, it is captured and balanced with attention to microphone distance and placement, controlled dynamics, and reverberation so that it blends into the orchestral image while retaining its brilliance and impact. In the classical seating and depth picture it sits behind the strings and woodwinds yet must cut through at climaxes.' WHERE id='6456fd31-fe03-4d86-a1ba-598abdaa9f97' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Decca Tree balance refers to setting the levels and image produced by the Decca Tree, a main microphone array of three spaced omnidirectional microphones, a left and a right with a centre microphone mounted forward on a T-shaped frame above and behind the conductor, used to capture an orchestra. The centre microphone anchors the middle of the stereo image and fills the hole in the middle that widely spaced pairs can produce, while the outriggers provide width; balancing the tree involves its height, spread, and distance from the ensemble together with the relative level of the three capsules. It yields a broad, stable, warm stereo image that forms the foundation of many orchestral recordings.' WHERE id='8564d779-178f-469f-90c0-5ea2905f987a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Main-array balance is the process of achieving the desired musical balance and stereo image from the principal microphone system, the main pair or tree, that captures the whole ensemble, as distinct from the spot or accent microphones. It is set primarily by the array''s type, position, height, and distance from the ensemble, which together determine the relative pickup of the sections, the direct-to-reverberant ratio, and the width and depth of the image. Because the main array supplies most of the recorded sound in classical work, getting its balance right at the source minimises later corrective mixing.' WHERE id='99e11ff7-635a-4e78-8cb2-989e7918a1aa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Main-array equalisation is the application of frequency-response correction to the signals from the main microphone array capturing an ensemble, used to compensate for the room''s coloration, the microphones'' characteristics, and the balance of the captured sound. In classical practice such equalisation is applied sparingly, as broad and gentle adjustments to correct tonal tilt, tame room resonances, or restore air, so as to preserve the natural timbre of the instruments and the integrity of the array''s spatial image. It thus contrasts with the more aggressive, per-track equalisation typical of multitrack pop mixing.' WHERE id='e34f1304-74b8-4b31-8089-3d686b34f2c4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Natural orchestral balance is the internal balance among the sections of an orchestra as it is produced acoustically in the hall by the players and conductor, which a classical recording seeks to capture and preserve rather than reconstruct artificially. Achieved chiefly by choosing an appropriate main-array type and distance so that the ensemble''s own balance and blend reach the microphones intact, it treats the orchestra as a single self-balancing instrument, with spot microphones used only to reinforce detail without upsetting that balance. The goal is a mix that sounds as a listener would hear it from a good seat in the hall.' WHERE id='771ca217-9c94-4d8f-b866-34a01b3897fa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Orchestral depth is the perceived front-to-back dimension of the reproduced orchestra, the sense that some sections are nearer and others farther away, arranged in receding planes. It arises from differences in direct-sound level, the ratio of direct to reverberant energy, high-frequency air absorption, and the timing of early reflections for each source, all of which cue distance; in a recording it is captured through microphone distance and preserved or shaped in the mix by level and reverberation. Convincing depth reproduces the natural layering of the orchestra from the front-desk strings to the rear percussion.' WHERE id='3307b987-354a-4efa-81df-ffb22ef5bfae' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Orchestral width is the perceived lateral extent of the reproduced orchestra across the stereo stage, from the leftmost to the rightmost sources, together with the apparent breadth of the ensemble and its ambience. It is governed by the main array''s angling and spacing, which map source direction to inter-channel level and time differences, by the panning of spot microphones, and by the amount and decorrelation of captured reverberation that broadens the image. Appropriate width places the sections believably from left to right without stretching the image into a gap in the centre or collapsing it toward mono.' WHERE id='b2ae20dc-f459-418c-9430-a8b28fd432e4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Reverberant-field control is the management of the proportion and character of reverberant, diffuse reflected energy relative to direct sound in an ensemble recording, so as to convey the appropriate sense of hall and space without obscuring clarity. It is exercised by choosing microphone distance and the direct-to-reverberant ratio at capture, by adding dedicated ambience or room microphones, and by supplementing or shaping the tail with artificial reverberation in the mix. Balancing this field sets the recording''s perceived size, intimacy, and blend while preserving definition and intelligibility.' WHERE id='01b6b579-b289-4ef8-befe-c7fdef2900b7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Stereo localisation is the listener''s perception of the direction and position of a sound source within the stereo image and, in recording practice, the control of that perceived position. Over a loudspeaker pair it is produced by inter-channel level and time (phase) differences between the two channels, summing localisation, which the auditory system interprets as a phantom image at a point between the speakers; microphone arrays and panning create these differences to place sources across the stage. Accurate localisation gives each section and instrument a stable, well-defined place in the orchestral image.' WHERE id='83f98f0d-a24d-4c96-a119-be4fda5e6700' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='String-section mixing is the capture, balancing, and processing of the orchestral strings, the violins, violas, cellos, and double basses, so that they form the warm, blended foundation of the orchestral image. Because the strings comprise many players spread across the front of the stage, they are recorded and balanced with attention to seating layout and stereo placement, favouring blend over sharp definition, with generous but controlled reverberation, while spot microphones add presence and bite without spotlighting individuals. The aim is a cohesive, wide, and sustained string body with appropriate depth relative to the other sections.' WHERE id='91717f39-f8ec-42c5-988c-49802f01d698' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Symphonic mixing is the discipline of combining the many microphone signals of a large orchestra, the main array, section and accent (spot) microphones, and ambience microphones, into a coherent stereo or surround image that reproduces the balance, depth, and space of the ensemble in its hall. It prioritises the natural balance captured by the main array, using spot microphones only to reinforce detail, usually delayed to align with the main pickup, and reverberation to preserve the sense of the room, with restrained equalisation and dynamics. The objective is a realistic, unified orchestral picture rather than the foregrounded, heavily processed sound of pop production.' WHERE id='e7ccddba-5a9f-4761-86a7-30d5732507fa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Woodwind-section mixing is the balancing and placement of the orchestral woodwinds, the flutes, oboes, clarinets, bassoons, and their relatives, within the ensemble mix so that their individual colours remain audible from their seated position behind the strings. Because the woodwinds sit in the middle of the stage and are easily masked by strings and brass, they are commonly supported with spot microphones for presence and intelligibility, balanced and panned to their orchestral locations, and given depth consistent with their distance while retaining their characteristic timbres. The goal is clear, well-placed woodwind lines that blend into, but are not buried by, the surrounding sections.' WHERE id='8dfbf8a3-e143-4f57-a768-ef067df7a029' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An inductor whose winding is wound on a non-magnetic former or is self-supporting, so that the magnetic flux path is entirely air with a relative permeability of approximately one. Because there is no ferromagnetic core, inductance is comparatively low and is set solely by coil geometry (number of turns, diameter and length), but the device is free of core saturation, hysteresis and eddy-current losses, giving highly linear, temperature-stable behaviour and inductance that stays constant up to very high frequencies. It is chosen where low distortion and stable high-frequency operation matter, such as radio-frequency tuned circuits and high-power loudspeaker crossovers.' WHERE id='f1de53a0-ac15-41b0-9726-266325916bc1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A polarized capacitor that uses an etched aluminum anode foil whose surface carries a thin, anodically grown aluminum-oxide dielectric, with a liquid or solid electrolyte forming the effective cathode connection. Etching greatly increases the effective surface area, so these parts achieve high capacitance and stored energy per unit volume, at the cost of relatively high equivalent series resistance, appreciable leakage current, wide tolerance, limited high-frequency performance and a finite service life governed by loss of electrolyte. Correct polarity must be observed because reverse voltage degrades the oxide and can lead to venting or failure.' WHERE id='c5e92f86-5a09-4108-89b5-c8ffb6993fec' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An inductor optimized for operation across the audio band of roughly 20 Hz to 20 kHz, typically with an inductance large enough (millihenries to henries) to present significant reactance over that range. It usually employs a laminated iron or ferrite core to obtain high inductance in a compact size and is designed for low winding resistance and low core loss and distortion at audio frequencies. Typical applications include loudspeaker crossover networks, LC equalizers and audio filters.' WHERE id='63a4876f-7c12-4e01-a9b9-320b2f8bef93' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode designed to operate in reverse breakdown by the avalanche mechanism, in which carriers accelerated by the high reverse-bias electric field gain enough energy to create additional electron-hole pairs through impact ionization, producing a sharp and controlled breakdown at a specified reverse voltage. Above roughly five to six volts this avalanche process dominates and gives a positive temperature coefficient, distinct from the tunneling (Zener) mechanism at lower voltages. Such diodes clamp voltage and absorb transient energy, and are used for voltage references and overvoltage or transient protection.' WHERE id='11dc55ea-42c5-4d65-aef8-44da972509f7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small-value resistor placed in series with the base lead of a bipolar junction transistor, mounted close to the base terminal, to suppress parasitic high-frequency oscillation. Together with the transistor''s input capacitance and the lead and wiring inductance it damps the resonant loop and lowers loop gain at very high frequencies, preventing spurious oscillation while having negligible effect on the intended lower-frequency operation. Values are typically from tens to a few hundred ohms.' WHERE id='5116f8dc-3eec-49cf-9b53-e4e41864e478' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A non-polarized electrolytic capacitor built with two anodized, oxide-coated foils, so that it behaves as two polarized electrolytic capacitors connected back-to-back within a single case and tolerates voltage of either polarity. This provides the high capacitance density of an electrolytic for alternating or bipolar-signal applications where polarity is undefined, at the expense of larger size than an equivalent polarized unit of the same value. It is commonly used in loudspeaker crossovers and in audio coupling where no DC bias is present.' WHERE id='d1372c8f-9ce8-4c14-9a83-b80e635b1804' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An aluminum electrolytic capacitor whose case top or scored vent has swollen outward, an external symptom of internal gas pressure produced by electrolyte decomposition or overheating that indicates degradation or end of life. The bulge arises when gas is generated inside the can because the part has been overstressed, overheated, subjected to reverse or excessive voltage, or has simply aged, and it typically accompanies rising equivalent series resistance and falling capacitance. It signals imminent or actual failure and the need for replacement.' WHERE id='2ceae6b2-7e8a-4df2-bbba-dcba7571cad1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A large-value capacitor placed on a power-supply rail to store energy and smooth the low-frequency ripple that remains after rectification, supplying transient load current and holding the rail voltage between charging cycles. It is usually a high-capacitance aluminum electrolytic whose value, together with load current, sets the peak-to-peak ripple voltage, and it works alongside smaller decoupling capacitors that handle high-frequency demand. It is sized from its required hold-up and its ripple-current rating.' WHERE id='cbbb8e57-361b-42da-8dde-d4d6f6f40fca' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor connected to provide a low-impedance alternating-current path around a component or to a reference rail, shunting unwanted signal or ripple to ground while leaving the DC conditions undisturbed. By presenting low reactance at the frequencies of interest it stabilizes bias points, for example when placed across an emitter resistor to restore a stage''s AC gain, and diverts high-frequency noise. Its effectiveness depends on its impedance over the frequency range, including equivalent series resistance and inductance.' WHERE id='f76f1ec4-ee8e-4ed2-99c7-f3d1c08542ad' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A Class 1 ceramic capacitor using a paraelectric dielectric formulation with the EIA C0G (NP0) temperature characteristic, meaning its capacitance changes by no more than about plus or minus thirty parts per million per degree Celsius, nominally zero, over the operating temperature range. These parts exhibit extremely low loss and high Q, negligible dependence of capacitance on applied voltage, frequency or age, and excellent long-term stability, at the cost of low capacitance density. They are used where precision and stability matter, such as filters, timing and oscillator circuits.' WHERE id='07eba159-8943-4dd9-bb6b-7c8b3dd4afb1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The small DC current that flows through a capacitor''s dielectric when a steady voltage is applied, arising from the dielectric''s finite insulation resistance and, in electrolytics, from conduction and repair of the oxide layer. It is modeled as a resistance in parallel with the capacitance and is specified either as a leakage current or as an insulation-resistance figure; it is very low for film and Class 1 ceramic parts and comparatively high for electrolytics. Leakage causes stored charge to decay and imposes an unwanted load on high-impedance and sample-and-hold circuits.' WHERE id='dda4becc-a472-4431-80a3-cf6760a3f50e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The property of a polarized capacitor, such as an aluminum or tantalum electrolytic, that requires its anode terminal to be held positive with respect to its cathode, because the dielectric oxide is formed and maintained only under correct-polarity bias. Reverse or excessive alternating voltage causes the oxide to conduct and generates heat and gas that can lead to leakage, venting or explosive failure. Polarity is indicated on the body, for example by a stripe marking the negative terminal or a plus symbol at the anode.' WHERE id='c0e28bfe-c1b1-4408-b695-d537415bfe50' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A conditioning procedure in which a long-stored or aged aluminum electrolytic capacitor is subjected to a gradually increased DC voltage through a current-limiting resistor to rebuild, or re-anodize, its aluminum-oxide dielectric, restoring insulation resistance and reducing leakage before full operation. During storage the oxide partially dissolves and leakage rises; controlled reforming regrows the oxide layer and limits the inrush current and heating that would otherwise damage the part. It is recommended by manufacturers after extended shelf storage.' WHERE id='8de75ecb-eacf-4a64-9fd6-e2c1a5e3277b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A fixed resistor whose resistive element is a molded rod of finely divided carbon mixed with a non-conductive binder, the ratio and geometry of which set the resistance. It offers good pulse and surge energy handling and low inductance, but suffers from poor tolerance, large temperature and voltage coefficients, higher current noise, and a tendency to drift with age, humidity and load. It has largely been superseded by film types except where high transient-energy absorption or vintage authenticity is required.' WHERE id='c29a7f94-92dd-4cc9-af02-9a9ef16ef027' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A fixed resistor made by depositing a thin film of pyrolytic carbon onto a ceramic rod and then helically trimming the film to set the resistance. Compared with carbon composition it gives better tolerance, lower current noise and a more defined, negative temperature coefficient, with moderate stability, and it is an inexpensive general-purpose type. The helical trim introduces some series inductance that limits high-frequency use.' WHERE id='548cb821-252f-4c32-a866-a863d937d87a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A non-polarized capacitor whose dielectric is a ceramic material, most commonly built as a multilayer chip (MLCC) of interleaved metallized ceramic layers to raise capacitance density. Ceramics are grouped by dielectric class: Class 1, such as C0G/NP0, for stability and low loss, and Class 2, such as X7R and Y5V, for higher capacitance density at the cost of dependence on voltage, temperature and age. They offer low equivalent series resistance and inductance and are ubiquitous for decoupling, filtering and coupling.' WHERE id='f59e0337-6ba2-48b2-a4d2-a8a9472fc4f0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode connected to limit, or clamp, the voltage at a node to a defined level by conducting whenever that voltage rises above a reference rail plus a diode drop, or falls below a lower reference, thereby diverting the excess current. Typical uses include catching inductive kickback, protecting inputs by clamping signals to the supply rails, and restoring a DC level; the clamp voltage is set by the reference to which the diode returns. It is also called a catch diode.' WHERE id='99d81290-cba7-43b8-9da6-c04940f6b367' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A ceramic capacitor using a ferroelectric, high-permittivity dielectric such as X7R, X5R or Y5V, giving much higher capacitance density than Class 1 but with significant dependence of capacitance on temperature, applied DC bias voltage, frequency and time, the last through ageing caused by crystalline relaxation. EIA character codes specify the temperature range and the allowed capacitance change, for example X7R covering minus fifty-five to plus one hundred twenty-five degrees Celsius within plus or minus fifteen percent. It is suited to decoupling and bulk filtering where stability is secondary to obtaining a large value.' WHERE id='1955aa64-f510-4d5d-a4d2-8331c111d74a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor connected between the collector of a bipolar transistor and its supply rail that converts the transistor''s collector current into an output voltage, thereby setting the stage''s DC operating point and, in the common-emitter configuration, its voltage gain, which is proportional to this collector load. Its value trades voltage gain and available output swing against bandwidth and supply current, and it also serves as the load for the amplifying device.' WHERE id='4d55866c-3018-4ce6-beff-32dd278baf85' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor used as a reactive element in a loudspeaker crossover network, where its frequency-dependent reactance forms a high-pass or band section that routes higher-frequency energy to the appropriate driver while blocking lower frequencies. Audio crossover use favours low-loss non-polar types, such as film or bipolar electrolytic, with adequate voltage and ripple-current rating and tight tolerance so that the designed crossover frequency and slope are maintained.' WHERE id='a5d2208b-e8a1-465b-a78c-6202eb36aebb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An inductor used as a reactive element in a loudspeaker crossover, its reactance rising with frequency to form a low-pass or band section that passes low frequencies to a woofer while attenuating higher frequencies. It is chosen for low DC resistance, to preserve damping factor and efficiency, together with adequate current handling and freedom from saturation; air-core types avoid core distortion while iron or ferrite cores give higher inductance in a smaller size.' WHERE id='5d260c82-d018-48ef-8511-902503b96379' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A low-value, high-precision resistor placed in series with a load or supply so that the voltage developed across it, equal to the current multiplied by the resistance, provides a measure of the current flowing. It is designed for low resistance in the milliohm range, tight tolerance, low temperature coefficient and adequate continuous and pulse power rating, and is often a four-terminal (Kelvin) part to eliminate lead and contact-resistance error. It is used for current monitoring, regulation and protection, and is also called a shunt resistor.' WHERE id='3bf7347a-c7f2-42c9-861e-08b140af85b3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor placed close to an active device''s supply pins to isolate, or decouple, it from the shared power rail by supplying transient switching current locally and shunting rail noise to ground, so that the local supply stays stable. Its usefulness over frequency is limited by its equivalent series resistance and inductance and by the loop inductance to the device, so low-inductance ceramics are used, often paired with bulk capacitors that cover lower frequencies. It is closely related to bypassing.' WHERE id='fe592958-7e8b-4fff-9679-6e86eee34704' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A two-terminal semiconductor device formed by a p-n junction, with an anode and a cathode, that conducts current preferentially in one direction: forward bias beyond the junction threshold of roughly 0.6 to 0.7 volts for silicon permits conduction, while reverse bias blocks current up to the reverse breakdown voltage. This rectifying, nonlinear characteristic underlies rectification, clipping, clamping, detection and current steering. It is characterized by forward voltage, reverse leakage, breakdown or peak inverse voltage, junction capacitance and switching speed.' WHERE id='d1688b02-698e-4a4c-b7d5-4bc487f8a848' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode-based demodulator or rectifier that recovers the amplitude envelope of a modulated signal, or converts an alternating signal to a DC level, by rectifying it and filtering the result with an RC network so the output follows the signal''s envelope. The nonlinear diode conducts on one polarity while the following capacitor holds the peak and the resistor sets the discharge time constant. The classic application is AM envelope detection and radio-frequency level or peak sensing.' WHERE id='8f9c7cb7-10a5-4963-b548-61630f30095f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A closed loop of four diodes connected to form a balanced or double-balanced passive mixer, or ring modulator, in which a local-oscillator drive switches the diodes so that two input signals are effectively multiplied, producing sum and difference frequencies while suppressing the input carriers at the output. The symmetric arrangement provides port-to-port isolation and suppression of even-order products. It is used for frequency conversion and for ring modulation.' WHERE id='6b5557ce-ce10-449a-b1b4-38352c1f853b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An aluminum electrolytic capacitor that has lost a significant fraction of its liquid electrolyte through evaporation and diffusion over time, a process accelerated by heat, causing its capacitance to fall and its equivalent series resistance to rise until it can no longer perform its filtering or decoupling function. This gradual wear-out is the dominant ageing mechanism of wet electrolytics and is worsened by high ambient temperature and self-heating from ripple current, and it is a common cause of power-supply failure, showing up as increased ripple and instability.' WHERE id='f5ba3b0b-32b1-4b78-ab73-35e296580b7a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor connected across the emitter degeneration resistor of a common-emitter stage to provide a low-impedance path for the signal while leaving the resistor''s DC bias-stabilizing action intact, thereby restoring the stage''s full alternating-current gain at signal frequencies. Its value together with the resistance sets a low-frequency corner below which gain falls, so it must be large enough to bypass the lowest frequency of interest; its effectiveness is limited by its impedance and equivalent series resistance.' WHERE id='136fed4f-b2d6-45f6-aeb7-7c018ebdf98b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor in the emitter leg of a bipolar transistor stage that introduces local series negative feedback, stabilizing the DC operating point against changes in temperature and device parameters and setting the emitter current for a given base bias. In alternating-current terms it degenerates the gain, which becomes approximately the ratio of collector to emitter resistance unless the resistor is bypassed, while improving linearity, input impedance and thermal stability.' WHERE id='7bc977bc-13e3-4e5a-84af-a3472542b196' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor connected from an amplifier''s output back to its input node to apply negative feedback and set the closed-loop gain; in an inverting operational-amplifier stage the gain is the ratio of this feedback resistance to the input resistance. It fixes gain, bandwidth and impedance largely independently of the device''s open-loop parameters, and its value, together with the input resistor and any associated capacitance, also influences noise and stability.' WHERE id='be82e795-2271-42e5-8a95-7efa13bae3c6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A three-terminal capacitor built so that the signal or power conductor passes straight through the component while the capacitance is formed between that through-conductor and an outer electrode bonded to the chassis or bulkhead, giving a very low-inductance shunt to ground for EMI filtering. Because the ground connection is coaxial and short, it provides high-frequency attenuation far beyond that of an ordinary two-terminal capacitor, and it is mounted through a panel to filter lines entering a shielded enclosure.' WHERE id='7aa1af32-ac61-4b49-b69a-dd45065f5818' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An inductor wound on a core of ferrite, a sintered ceramic of iron oxide combined with other metal oxides such as manganese-zinc or nickel-zinc, offering high permeability together with high electrical resistivity. The high resistivity suppresses eddy-current loss, making ferrite suitable up to radio frequencies, while the permeability raises inductance and allows compact designs; the core can saturate at high flux and its permeability varies with frequency, temperature and drive level. It is used in RF chokes, EMI suppression and switching-supply magnetics.' WHERE id='a988c78c-8559-4372-a1ac-633fca236311' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A non-polarized capacitor whose dielectric is a thin plastic film, such as polyester (PET), polypropylene (PP) or polycarbonate, with either separate foil electrodes or metal deposited directly onto the film. Film types offer low loss, high insulation resistance, a low and stable temperature coefficient, high ripple-current and voltage capability, and, in the metallized form, self-healing of localized dielectric faults, though at lower capacitance density than ceramics or electrolytics. They are used for coupling, filtering, timing, snubber and audio applications.' WHERE id='4767e78d-1443-4a70-a824-b1eb3633b6a1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor whose resistance value is established during manufacture and cannot be adjusted, in contrast to a variable resistor or potentiometer. It is specified by nominal resistance, tolerance, power rating, temperature coefficient of resistance, and voltage and overload ratings, and is realized in composition, film or wirewound constructions. It is the basic passive element for setting currents, dividing voltages and providing loads.' WHERE id='8d4752ee-2eca-447d-8dfd-36d4f1101563' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor constructed and coated, often as a wirewound or metal-oxide film type with a non-combustible or flame-retardant coating, so that under a fault overload it will not flame, emit burning particles or propagate fire, but instead fails safely. It is designed to meet flammability and safety requirements so that an overstress which opens the element does not create a fire hazard, and it is used where safety-agency approval demands non-flammable behaviour.' WHERE id='c6bceb86-8ab9-459c-a4f2-e24cc0204eec' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode connected across an inductive load such as a relay coil, motor or solenoid to provide a path for the stored inductor current when the driving switch opens, clamping the inductive voltage spike, or back-EMF, to about one diode drop and protecting the switching device. It is also called a freewheeling, catch or snubber diode; it must carry the load current and, where switching speed matters, recover quickly so that it turns off cleanly.' WHERE id='ab9096d3-3c73-454b-b60b-7b1bc3c5544c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor designed to act as a fuse under sustained overload, opening the circuit safely, and often non-flammably, when its dissipation exceeds a limit, so that it provides both a defined resistance and fault protection. Its resistive element is made to open cleanly rather than burn, protecting downstream circuitry, and it is typically used as a low-value series element at a power input, combining current-limiting and fusing functions in one part.' WHERE id='cb53d785-fdcb-4d28-bfae-0b11cbb9143f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor, or resistors, whose value determines the closed-loop gain of an amplifier stage, for example the feedback-to-input ratio of an operational amplifier or the single external resistor that programs the gain of an instrumentation amplifier. Its absolute value, tolerance and temperature coefficient, and its matching where a ratio is involved, directly set the gain accuracy and drift of the stage.' WHERE id='d636d780-3921-40bf-a634-cb918c922250' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small series resistor placed at the gate of a MOSFET or other field-effect transistor, close to the gate terminal, to damp the resonant loop formed by the gate capacitance and the lead and trace inductance, suppressing parasitic high-frequency oscillation and controlling switching speed and ringing. It limits peak gate current and slows the voltage slew rate at the cost of some switching loss; values are typically from a few to a few tens of ohms. It is the field-effect-transistor analogue of a base-stopper resistor.' WHERE id='2656b2da-8e0a-4ee4-9ae5-a6e88d529d44' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode whose semiconductor is germanium rather than silicon, characterized by a low forward voltage drop of roughly 0.2 to 0.3 volts together with relatively high reverse leakage and marked temperature sensitivity. The low turn-on voltage makes it useful for small-signal detection and demodulation and for low-level rectification, and its soft, asymmetric clipping is prized in audio effects, while its poor reverse and thermal behaviour limits power use. It has largely been superseded by silicon and Schottky types.' WHERE id='29020de6-4da6-4f5c-b88d-6bdc53ae6a2f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The ratio between the impedances measured at two ports or points, most commonly the ratio of the impedance reflected through a transformer to the actual load impedance, which for an ideal transformer equals the square of the turns, or voltage, ratio. It quantifies impedance transformation and matching, describing how a transformer or matching network maps a load impedance to a source, and it is a dimensionless quantity. It is central to power transfer and matching in audio and radio-frequency work.' WHERE id='1de37158-76c8-40a5-be7b-8b6457430b36' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A passive two-terminal component that stores energy in a magnetic field when it carries current and exhibits the property of inductance, whereby a changing current induces an opposing voltage equal to the inductance times the rate of change of current. It is realized as a coil, optionally wound on a magnetic core, and its reactance rises with frequency as two pi times frequency times inductance, so it passes low frequencies and opposes high ones. It is characterized by inductance, DC resistance, self-resonant frequency, quality factor and saturation current, and is used for filtering, energy storage, tuning and EMI suppression.' WHERE id='33ae9c91-6363-4305-80e5-b9d54da57d4f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An equalizer whose frequency-selective elements are inductors, used with capacitors and resistors to form the resonant networks that produce the desired boost and cut response. In such passive LC equalizers the inductive and capacitive reactances create the resonant peaks and notches, and the topology sets the centre frequencies, bandwidths and amount of boost or cut; this approach underlies classic passive program and graphic equalizers valued for their characteristic sonic behaviour.' WHERE id='ce8f908f-2bd2-407d-b9c8-c6693873759e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor placed in series with the input of a stage to pass the alternating signal while blocking any DC component or offset between the source and the stage''s biased input, so that the operating point is not disturbed. Together with the input resistance it forms a high-pass filter that sets the low-frequency cutoff, so its value is chosen large enough to pass the lowest frequency of interest, and a non-polar or correctly biased type is used to avoid reverse voltage.' WHERE id='85ebee30-fa84-49ff-abab-31573176611b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In an operational-amplifier or amplifier stage, the resistor through which the input signal is applied to the summing or input node; in an inverting configuration it sets the input current for a given input voltage and, together with the feedback resistor, fixes the closed-loop gain as the ratio of feedback to input resistance and largely determines the input impedance seen by the source.' WHERE id='ee2b765c-17e8-49b1-bcab-110b6053fd7f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An inductor wound on a ferromagnetic core of iron, usually laminated or made of powdered iron to reduce eddy currents, exploiting iron''s high permeability to obtain large inductance in a compact winding. Lamination or powdering limits eddy-current loss, but the core is still subject to hysteresis loss, nonlinearity and saturation at high flux, and it is best suited to lower power and audio frequencies. It is used in mains transformers, chokes and audio inductors.' WHERE id='9ce3c90f-c63d-4098-9182-c82e2bcec558' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor exhibiting excessive leakage: either a wet electrolytic whose electrolyte has physically escaped from the case and can corrode the board, or, more generally, a capacitor whose dielectric passes abnormally high DC current because of degradation, indicating loss of insulation resistance. Both senses denote a faulty or degraded part, whether the leakage is physical electrolyte escape or electrical conduction, that compromises circuit function and warrants replacement.' WHERE id='bec04fb3-9035-4cb6-997d-137c78e5110b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A light-emitting diode used deliberately as the clipping element in an amplifier''s signal path, typically within the feedback loop or shunting to ground, exploiting its higher forward voltage of roughly 1.5 to 2 volts or more to clip the signal at a larger threshold than a silicon diode and so give a different, generally louder and harder, clipping character. It is common in guitar overdrive and distortion circuits, where the diode''s forward drop sets the clipping level and the device may also emit light on signal peaks.' WHERE id='7a57b60b-1243-4005-9488-cf08e96ed461' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor whose resistance varies with incident light, made from a photoconductive semiconductor, commonly cadmium sulphide, whose resistance falls as illumination increases because absorbed photons generate additional charge carriers. Its resistance is high in darkness, up to megohms, and low in bright light, down to hundreds of ohms, with a slow, wavelength-dependent and somewhat nonlinear response. It is used for light sensing, automatic level or gain control and, in audio, optical attenuators, and it is synonymous with the photoresistor or photocell.' WHERE id='5c04d371-bab6-4bf4-9326-9d8d5c737752' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode that emits light when forward-biased, as electrons and holes recombine across the junction of a direct-bandgap semiconductor and release energy as photons whose wavelength, and hence colour, is set by the material''s bandgap. It conducts like an ordinary diode above a colour-dependent forward voltage, from about 1.6 volts for red to over 3 volts for blue and white, and it requires external current limiting, with light output roughly proportional to forward current. It is used for indication, illumination and optoelectronics.' WHERE id='7ef728df-5a4b-4a2a-95d7-b99e263c8dda' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor connected to a source or output to draw a defined current and dissipate power, providing the load across which an output voltage is developed or establishing a minimum load for correct operation, as in a bleeder, dummy load or the DC return for a current output. Its value sets the operating current and the power it must dissipate, equal to the square of the voltage divided by the resistance, and it converts a current, or the drive from a source, into a usable voltage.' WHERE id='2ea3a059-31f1-42fd-b760-fc647c2ac55a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Two resistors manufactured and specified so that their resistance values track closely in ratio and over temperature, characterized by tight relative tolerance and low tracking temperature coefficient rather than by absolute accuracy. Such matching is essential where a resistance ratio sets performance, as in differential and instrumentation-amplifier gain and common-mode rejection, precision voltage dividers and bridges, since ratio and drift matching dominate the error. The pair is often supplied in a single package so the two elements track thermally.' WHERE id='e2aa4694-7a1f-4b72-9245-82d90eda7aa7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A fixed resistor made by depositing a thin metal or metal-alloy film, typically nickel-chromium, onto a ceramic substrate and trimming it, often helically, to the required value, giving tight tolerance, a low temperature coefficient, low current noise and good long-term stability. It is the standard precision general-purpose resistor, superior to carbon film in accuracy and noise, with moderate power and pulse handling.' WHERE id='9725d22e-cc22-4f7b-adea-206c4cb00e5e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A fixed film resistor whose resistive layer is a metal oxide, typically tin oxide, fired onto a ceramic core, giving good stability at elevated temperature, high surge and pulse capability and higher power ratings than thin metal-film types, with moderate tolerance and temperature coefficient. It is used where robustness, high operating temperature and surge or overload handling are required.' WHERE id='2dd14e9a-dbb6-4fee-a7b1-4c9d7bdd86ec' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An alternating-current capacitor connected continuously in the auxiliary winding of a single-phase induction motor to create the phase shift needed for efficient running torque, sized for continuous AC current and rated in microfarads and AC working voltage. Unlike a start capacitor, which provides a brief high-capacitance duty, it is a lower-capacitance, continuous-duty, self-healing part, usually metallized polypropylene, rated for the sustained reactive current and voltage of normal running.' WHERE id='4427953c-b356-42b7-ad23-0ec0d67cc37c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor with no polarity requirement, so it may be connected either way round and can withstand alternating or bipolar voltage across it, in contrast to a polarized electrolytic. Film and ceramic capacitors are inherently non-polar, while electrolytics can be made non-polar, or bipolar, by an internal back-to-back construction. Such a capacitor is required wherever the applied voltage reverses, for example in AC coupling and loudspeaker crossovers.' WHERE id='7636fd07-c299-42f9-a4ca-7d4119ee572b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A thermally sensitive resistor with a negative temperature coefficient, made from a metal-oxide semiconductor ceramic whose resistance decreases sharply and nonlinearly, approximately exponentially and described by a B (beta) constant, as temperature rises. It is used for temperature measurement and compensation and, in a power inrush-limiting form, to present a high cold resistance that limits switch-on surge current before falling as the device self-heats. It is characterized by its resistance at twenty-five degrees Celsius, its B value and its tolerance.' WHERE id='1a6ee028-e65f-43a8-86c6-0dcbbac3ccd4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor whose dielectric is paper, often impregnated with wax or oil, held between metal-foil electrodes and rolled into a cylinder, an early construction now largely replaced by plastic-film types. It offers high voltage capability but relatively high loss, moisture sensitivity and drift; in modern practice paper is used mainly, impregnated, in high-voltage and power capacitors, while paper types persist in vintage audio equipment.' WHERE id='f3d63f70-1fb5-4e05-b959-c0db0e3bc756' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode optimized to convert incident light into an electrical signal, operated in reverse bias in the photoconductive mode or at zero bias in the photovoltaic mode, so that absorbed photons generate electron-hole pairs that produce a reverse photocurrent proportional to optical power. It is characterized by responsivity, spectral range, dark current, junction capacitance and speed, and PIN structures give fast, low-capacitance response. It is used for light detection, optical communication and sensing.' WHERE id='aa068955-e53b-409e-b0fd-8a43f6c59139' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor whose resistance decreases with incident light, a photoconductive device commonly made of cadmium sulphide or selenide in which absorbed photons free additional charge carriers and so lower the resistance; it is synonymous with the light-dependent resistor. Its resistance is high in darkness and low under illumination, with a slow, nonlinear and wavelength-dependent response, and it is used for light sensing and optical level control.' WHERE id='8e5b733e-1128-4636-a4a5-21a4e0063437' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A film capacitor that uses polycarbonate as the dielectric, usually in metallized construction. It is distinguished by very high insulation resistance, a low and stable dissipation factor, low dielectric absorption and a small, near-linear temperature coefficient, which together give excellent capacitance stability and tight tolerances over wide temperature and frequency ranges. It was favoured for precision timing, filtering and sample-and-hold circuits, although availability declined sharply after the base film ceased to be manufactured, so it is now largely superseded by polypropylene and polyester types.' WHERE id='05685ae0-6ddd-422c-a957-eddbcb986e4a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A film capacitor using polyethylene terephthalate as the dielectric, commonly known by the trade name Mylar. It offers high capacitance per unit volume, good self-healing behaviour in metallized form and adequate insulation resistance at moderate cost, but exhibits a comparatively high dissipation factor, noticeable capacitance drift with temperature and greater dielectric absorption than polypropylene or polystyrene. It is widely used for general-purpose coupling, decoupling and non-critical filtering where stability requirements are relaxed.' WHERE id='117589ee-2f1a-42d1-b0cb-ce5cf078da25' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A film capacitor using polypropylene as the dielectric, available in film/foil and metallized constructions. It exhibits a very low dissipation factor, extremely low dielectric absorption, high insulation resistance and a small negative temperature coefficient, together with high tolerance of high frequencies, high peak currents and steep voltage slopes. These properties make it the preferred choice for audio signal paths, resonant and snubber circuits, pulse and AC applications, and precision filters.' WHERE id='32db475d-7d52-4eb9-92cb-49c631d11ac5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A film capacitor using polystyrene as the dielectric, typically in film/foil construction. It provides one of the lowest available dissipation factors, very high insulation resistance, low dielectric absorption and a predictable, near-linear negative temperature coefficient, giving outstanding stability and low loss. Its usefulness is limited to relatively low operating temperatures because the dielectric softens with heat, and it is applied chiefly in precision timing networks, active filters and high-quality tuned circuits.' WHERE id='da02aeb2-e6d8-498d-ba05-e4d1e82fa027' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor designed to dissipate a comparatively large amount of electrical energy as heat, typically rated from about one watt upward, using constructions and materials chosen for thermal performance such as wirewound elements, thick-film-on-ceramic bodies or aluminium-housed types intended for heatsink mounting. Its rated power must be derated as ambient or case temperature rises according to the manufacturer''s derating curve, and its maximum working voltage and surface temperature are also specified. It is used wherever significant current and voltage combine, including current sensing, braking, bleeder, load and snubber duties.' WHERE id='5e22aa2d-8036-4ea2-a59b-b45e8eba6805' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor manufactured to a tight resistance tolerance, commonly 0.1 percent or better, with a low temperature coefficient of resistance expressed in parts per million per degree, low long-term drift, low thermal electromotive force and low current noise. Such performance is typically achieved with metal-foil, thin-film or precision wirewound technology and careful trimming. It is specified where absolute accuracy and stability of a resistance value are critical, such as in instrumentation, reference dividers, gain-setting networks and measurement bridges.' WHERE id='7b6a71f9-f340-487a-ae39-c85a96574aa4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode placed in a circuit specifically to protect other components from a damaging electrical condition rather than to process a signal. According to its arrangement it may block or safely conduct under reverse-polarity connection, provide a freewheeling path for the current of an inductive load when switching turns off, or clamp an overvoltage to a safe level. Its selection is governed by the reverse voltage, forward current, clamping behaviour and speed required to absorb or divert the fault energy without exceeding the ratings of the protected devices.' WHERE id='5a1738e6-304a-4567-9f09-1dd66e61a59a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A thermistor exhibiting a positive temperature coefficient of resistance, so that its resistance rises with temperature; in the widely used ceramic switching type the resistance increases very steeply above a defined reference or Curie temperature. This self-limiting behaviour is exploited for resettable overcurrent and short-circuit protection, inrush-current limiting, self-regulating heating and motor-start functions, where rising current heats the device and drives its resistance up to restrict further current. Key parameters include reference temperature, resistance at reference, maximum voltage and rated current.' WHERE id='a724b3a7-e661-4d31-8762-b06f10bb5196' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor connected between a circuit node and ground so that, when no active source is driving the node, it holds the node at a defined logic-low level and prevents it from floating to an indeterminate voltage. It also provides a controlled discharge path for node capacitance and a defined current for inputs or open-drain outputs. Its value is a compromise: low enough to overcome leakage and noise reliably, yet high enough to limit static current and not overload the driving source.' WHERE id='ae987913-86d6-4cba-9d64-a7263260bbb6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor connected between a circuit node and the positive supply rail so that, in the absence of an active low-driving source, it holds the node at a defined logic-high level. It supplies the current needed by open-drain or open-collector outputs and by bus lines such as those used in serial interfaces, and it prevents inputs from floating. Its value trades static power consumption and rise-time speed against noise immunity and the current the pulled-up devices must sink.' WHERE id='48744aaf-8ca2-4c16-9323-be718c9dfd94' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode optimised for power rectification, converting alternating current to unidirectional current by conducting on forward bias and blocking on reverse bias. It is built with a large junction area and robust packaging to handle high average forward current and high repetitive reverse voltage, generally at the expense of switching speed, so standard silicon types show relatively slow reverse recovery. It forms the core of half-wave, full-wave and bridge arrangements in power supplies, and is rated primarily by average forward current and peak repetitive reverse voltage.' WHERE id='99d8830a-a78b-40d2-9f64-b3277d5e3470' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A large-value capacitor connected across the output of a rectifier that stores charge on each conduction peak and delivers it to the load during the intervals when the rectified waveform falls below the capacitor voltage, thereby holding the direct voltage up and reducing ripple. Its capacitance is chosen from the load current, mains frequency and permissible ripple, and it must withstand high repetitive charging current and the peak rectified voltage. It is normally the first, largest element of a power-supply smoothing filter.' WHERE id='d355403a-7210-429d-a6f0-ab2c5a0c0964' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A passive two-terminal electronic component that presents a defined electrical resistance, opposing the flow of current so that the voltage across it is proportional to the current through it in accordance with Ohm''s law, and converting the associated electrical energy into heat. It is characterised by its nominal resistance value, tolerance, rated power dissipation, temperature coefficient of resistance, maximum working voltage and noise. Resistors are fundamental to setting currents and voltages, providing biasing, loading, termination and feedback throughout electronic circuits.' WHERE id='8396610a-aed5-4f09-b2ea-9ddbf01257bc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A network of resistors interconnected in a repeating series-and-shunt pattern that produces a set of precisely weighted voltages or currents, most commonly the R-2R configuration in which only two resistor values are needed. By switching ladder nodes between reference and ground under digital control it performs binary-weighted digital-to-analog conversion, and the ratio matching of its resistors, rather than their absolute values, governs its linearity and accuracy. It is also used to build accurate voltage dividers and attenuators.' WHERE id='4fa4bd75-da50-42f4-bc13-e0238ac124fa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A single package containing several resistors with a defined internal interconnection, supplied either as electrically isolated elements or with a shared common terminal in bussed configurations. Integrating the resistors on one substrate saves board space and assembly steps and, because the elements are made together, provides close resistance matching and tracking of temperature coefficient that discrete parts cannot easily achieve. Such networks are used for pull-up and pull-down arrays, terminations, dividers and other applications where matching or density is important.' WHERE id='ed6cbc7c-c829-4282-afc7-92785d3d7ff6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor certified for connection directly to the AC mains supply, where a failure could otherwise expose a user to electric shock or create a fire hazard, and therefore subject to mandatory safety approvals and testing. It is divided into Class X types, connected between line conductors where failure does not present a shock risk, and Class Y types, connected between a line conductor and accessible or earthed parts where the capacitor must fail safely and withstand high transient overvoltages. Its construction, insulation, endurance and impulse withstand are governed by the relevant safety standard and it must never be substituted with an ordinary capacitor.' WHERE id='606da64a-0c92-41d9-9029-73343e04dbe8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The storage capacitor in a sample-and-hold or track-and-hold circuit that captures and retains the instantaneous value of an analog voltage during the hold interval, typically charged through a switch and buffered by high-impedance amplifiers. Its value is a compromise: larger capacitance reduces droop from leakage and switch charge injection but lengthens the acquisition time needed to track the input, while a low-leakage, low-dielectric-absorption dielectric is chosen to minimise voltage error during hold. It is critical to the accuracy of analog-to-digital converters and analog memory circuits.' WHERE id='e57685ed-4454-43db-96dc-c2b58c9e56c3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode formed from a metal-semiconductor junction rather than a conventional p-n junction, so that conduction is carried almost entirely by majority carriers. This gives a low forward voltage drop, typically a few tenths of a volt, and extremely fast switching with negligible reverse-recovery charge, at the cost of higher reverse leakage and a comparatively low reverse breakdown voltage. It is used for efficient low-voltage rectification, freewheeling and clamping, output rectifiers in switching supplies, and high-frequency detection and mixing.' WHERE id='ef0e3561-8ef7-4cfd-bb77-45ea0911590d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small-signal diode intended for low-power switching, detection, steering and logic functions rather than power handling, built with a small junction to give low capacitance and fast response. Compared with rectifier diodes it carries only modest forward current and blocks modest reverse voltage, but its speed and low charge storage make it suitable for high-frequency and pulse work, the 1N4148 being a classic example. It is applied in wave shaping, protection, demodulation and general low-current signal processing.' WHERE id='c27c73cc-733f-41f1-81e1-853f3b15b22d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A semiconductor diode whose rectifying action is provided by a p-n junction formed in silicon, the dominant material for general-purpose diodes. In forward conduction it exhibits a characteristic threshold voltage of roughly 0.6 to 0.7 volts and an exponential current-voltage relationship, while in reverse it blocks current up to its rated breakdown voltage apart from a small leakage. Its stability, low leakage and wide temperature range make it the standard choice for rectification, switching, clamping and protection.' WHERE id='a7ffc902-ea18-4727-9283-1a10df8d4abd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor that uses thin sheets of natural mica as the dielectric with silver electrodes deposited directly onto the mica surfaces. It offers exceptional long-term stability, a very low and consistent dissipation factor, a small predictable temperature coefficient, tight tolerances and excellent performance at radio frequencies. Available typically at small capacitance values, it is used in resonant and tuned circuits, oscillators, precise timing and filter networks where stability and low loss at high frequency are essential.' WHERE id='f297fe23-62b5-48d4-a44b-52ec3c407af0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor network built in a single in-line package, in which several resistors are moulded into one body with a single straight row of through-hole leads. It is commonly offered in a bussed configuration, where one end of every resistor connects to a shared common pin, and in an isolated configuration of independent resistors, allowing arrays of pull-ups, pull-downs, terminations or dividers to be placed compactly with matched values. Its advantages are board-space saving, reduced assembly count and close resistor matching.' WHERE id='ed0c4695-18c1-4c38-b7b5-3d2db260c0af' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor placed in a power-supply filter after rectification to smooth the pulsating rectified waveform toward a steady direct voltage by absorbing charge at the peaks and releasing it during the troughs, thereby reducing ripple presented to the load or to a following regulator. The term is often used for the main reservoir capacitor and for additional filter capacitors in multi-stage arrangements; its value follows from load current, ripple frequency and allowable ripple voltage, and it must be rated for the working voltage and ripple current.' WHERE id='1709c14e-6e4d-461b-9360-a56b3efde315' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor, usually combined with a series resistor to form an RC snubber, connected across a switching device, relay contacts, rectifier or inductive load to absorb and slow voltage transients, suppress ringing and limit the rate of rise of voltage. By providing a temporary low-impedance path for the energy released when current is interrupted, it protects semiconductors and contacts and reduces electromagnetic interference. It must tolerate high peak currents and steep voltage slopes, so low-loss film dielectrics are typically specified.' WHERE id='012d7b35-e709-439a-aad6-b84fd91ccca4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor connected in series with the source terminal of a field-effect transistor, corresponding to the emitter resistor of a bipolar transistor. By developing a voltage proportional to the device current it sets or stabilises the operating bias point and introduces local negative feedback, or degeneration, that linearises the stage, raises its output impedance and improves thermal and device-to-device stability at the expense of some gain. Its value is chosen from the desired quiescent current and the amount of feedback required.' WHERE id='9cf021d3-f55b-4417-a2a9-a4ad1385beef' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode used to route current along a selected path according to signal polarity or voltage, effectively directing charge to or away from a node while blocking unwanted return paths. Such diodes appear in diode-OR combining networks, in clamp arrangements that steer overvoltage into supply rails, and particularly in electrostatic-discharge protection structures where paired diodes steer transient current to the power or ground rail to protect an input. Selection depends on low capacitance, low forward drop and adequate peak-current handling for the intended duty.' WHERE id='3842a15d-45d6-4369-9fcc-c1d36eeda6d4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor connected between a supply rail and ground, placed physically close to the power pins of an integrated circuit or other load, that provides a local reservoir of charge and a low-impedance path for the high-frequency return currents drawn by the device. By supplying transient current demands locally it prevents those currents from disturbing the supply distribution, reducing rail noise and coupling, and it is frequently combined with larger bulk capacitance to cover a range of frequencies. Effectiveness depends on low series inductance and short connections.' WHERE id='5cc7be12-95b2-4b5c-852c-061ebbb12d83' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode designed to change rapidly between its conducting and non-conducting states, achieving short forward-recovery and reverse-recovery times so that it can follow fast pulses and high-frequency waveforms with little stored-charge delay. It is built with a small, fast junction and is used for logic gating, wave shaping, clamping, steering and general high-speed signal switching, the 1N4148 being a common example. Its key ratings include reverse recovery time, junction capacitance, forward current and reverse voltage.' WHERE id='66e3eab3-7a3a-4d73-a0aa-c1a741ec7b67' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A polarised electrolytic capacitor whose anode is a porous slug of sintered tantalum, on which an extremely thin tantalum-pentoxide dielectric is formed by anodisation, giving very high capacitance in a small volume together with stable capacitance and low, stable leakage. Because the dielectric is thin, the device is sensitive to reverse voltage, overvoltage and surge current, and solid types can fail short with a characteristic ignition mode if these limits are exceeded, so voltage derating is standard practice. It is widely used for bulk decoupling and filtering where compact, stable capacitance is required.' WHERE id='77c97ecb-6920-45d6-bfd3-0a6985af8476' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor connected at the end of a transmission line, or across a bus, whose value equals the characteristic impedance of the line so that signal energy arriving at the termination is absorbed rather than reflected. By matching the impedance it suppresses reflections, ringing and standing waves that would otherwise corrupt fast digital signals or high-frequency transmissions, and it may be arranged as a single series or parallel element, a Thevenin pair or an AC termination. Its value, tolerance and power rating are set by the line impedance and signalling levels.' WHERE id='8657dcc0-4383-4a31-b7b7-22dbd3942078' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor deliberately made from semiconducting ceramic material so that its resistance varies strongly and reproducibly with temperature, available as negative-temperature-coefficient types whose resistance falls as temperature rises and positive-temperature-coefficient types whose resistance rises. Its large, nonlinear resistance-temperature relationship makes it far more sensitive than a metallic sensor over a limited range, and it is characterised by nominal resistance at a reference temperature and a material constant describing the slope. It is used for temperature measurement and compensation, inrush-current limiting and overcurrent or over-temperature protection.' WHERE id='41536af5-59ee-42da-9908-3263392dee31' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor formed by screen-printing a paste of conductive and glass particles, typically a ruthenium-oxide-based composition, onto a ceramic substrate and firing it at high temperature to leave a fused resistive film that is then laser-trimmed to value. It is the basis of the common surface-mount chip resistor, offering low cost, robustness and a wide resistance range, but with a higher temperature coefficient, looser tolerance and higher current noise than thin-film equivalents. It dominates general-purpose applications where extreme precision is not required.' WHERE id='760154d4-5308-4e55-a269-d90537aafb45' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor made by depositing a very thin metallic film, commonly nickel-chromium, onto a substrate by sputtering or evaporation and then patterning and trimming it to the required value. The uniform, fine-grained film yields tight tolerances, a low temperature coefficient of resistance, low current noise and good long-term stability, substantially better than thick-film construction, though generally at higher cost and lower maximum power. It is chosen for precision, instrumentation and high-stability circuits.' WHERE id='160c8f3e-7e20-4927-a7d8-e3a343553cb7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor that, in combination with a resistor or a controlled current source, establishes the time constant that sets the period or frequency of a timing or oscillator circuit, its voltage rising and falling as it charges and discharges to define the switching instants. Because the timing depends directly on the capacitance, the stability, tolerance, temperature coefficient, leakage and dielectric absorption of this capacitor determine the accuracy and drift of the resulting interval or frequency. It is central to relaxation oscillators, monostable and astable timers and RC clock generators.' WHERE id='c4ac3bad-c9df-4b22-aac9-11150ec7c362' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor used in a passive tone-control network, most familiar in electric guitars and basses, connected with a potentiometer so that it progressively shunts higher audio frequencies to ground and thereby rolls off treble as the control is turned. Together the capacitor and the pot form a variable low-pass filter whose corner frequency is set by the capacitance and resistance, so a larger capacitor removes more high-frequency content. Its value is selected to give the desired darkening of the tone rather than for precision.' WHERE id='9b6ac783-c966-4e82-b84e-0d88b70a766c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An inductor whose winding is placed on a ring-shaped, or toroidal, magnetic core so that the magnetic flux follows a closed path entirely within the core. This closed geometry gives high inductance for a given number of turns and confines the flux almost completely inside the core, producing very low leakage flux and radiated interference and reducing coupling to nearby circuits. It is widely used in power supplies, filters and radio-frequency circuits, with core material, permeability and saturation flux density selected for the frequency and current involved.' WHERE id='96ead89d-6da7-4f10-9525-0b1ce53fd4b2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode designed to protect circuits from short-duration overvoltage transients by clamping, in which it presents a high impedance below its rated standoff voltage and then conducts heavily through avalanche breakdown when the voltage exceeds its breakdown level, diverting the surge current and holding the terminal voltage to a defined clamping value. It responds extremely quickly and is rated principally by standoff voltage, breakdown voltage, clamping voltage and peak pulse power or current, and is available in unidirectional and bidirectional forms. It guards against surges, load-dump and electrostatic-discharge events on signal and supply lines.' WHERE id='e397f77d-2e5d-4dd6-ac2d-12f5d8beb5c9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small variable capacitor intended for occasional adjustment, used to set or trim a circuit value during calibration or alignment rather than for continuous tuning by the user. Its capacitance is varied over a limited range by mechanically altering the overlap or spacing of its electrodes, using air, ceramic or film dielectrics, and it is typically adjusted with a tool and then left in place. It is applied to fine-tune resonant frequency, phase, timing or balance in oscillators, filters and radio-frequency circuits.' WHERE id='df310d00-5bae-4349-a9e6-b9d761bb5118' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transient-voltage-suppressor diode, a protection component that clamps overvoltage transients by remaining nonconducting below its standoff voltage and then breaking down to conduct surge current once its breakdown voltage is exceeded, limiting the voltage seen by the protected circuit to a specified clamping level. It reacts very fast and is characterised by standoff, breakdown, clamping and peak-pulse-power ratings, and comes in unidirectional and bidirectional versions for DC-biased or AC lines. It is a standard defence against surges, electrostatic discharge and other fast transients.' WHERE id='660a5209-ec99-4557-b836-7cfc3a4d94d4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A capacitor whose capacitance can be deliberately varied, in the classic mechanical form by rotating one set of interleaved plates relative to another to change their effective overlapping area, with air, film or ceramic as the dielectric. Larger, continuously adjustable types were the standard tuning element in radio receivers and transmitters, and smaller versions serve as trimmers, while the value is specified by its minimum and maximum capacitance. It is used wherever adjustable capacitance is needed to tune resonant circuits, match impedances or set frequency.' WHERE id='a6fb8439-4939-4d1f-97f0-743b63b4f7c2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An electrolytic capacitor, most commonly of aluminium construction, fitted with a deliberately engineered pressure-relief vent, such as a scored pattern in the can or a rubber seal, that opens in a controlled way if internal gas pressure builds up during overload, reverse voltage or end of life. By releasing gas and electrolyte vapour gradually it prevents the violent rupture of a sealed case, improving safety and containing failure. The vent design and its operation are part of the device''s safety specification and endurance rating.' WHERE id='19e0ae7c-c93c-4481-82c4-19f2ef3f3fc1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A resistor made by winding a length of resistance wire, typically a nickel-chromium or similar alloy, around an insulating core and protecting it with a coating or housing. The construction allows precise resistance values, a low temperature coefficient, low noise and high power dissipation, but the coil gives the component appreciable self-inductance that limits its usefulness at high frequencies unless a non-inductive winding is employed. It is used for power dissipation, current sensing and precision applications at low to moderate frequencies.' WHERE id='2469e7ce-ed11-430a-93da-5637db1a193f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A class of mains safety capacitor intended for connection between the line and neutral conductors, across the supply, principally to suppress differential-mode electromagnetic interference. It occupies a position where a capacitor failure cannot by itself expose a person to electric shock, so it is permitted a relatively large capacitance, but it must withstand the high transient overvoltages present on the mains and is subdivided into subclasses according to the peak impulse voltage it is designed to survive. Its ratings and testing are defined by the mains-capacitor safety standard, and only an approved part may be used in this position.' WHERE id='4948455e-88a6-4d4c-869d-db9613363be9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A class of mains safety capacitor intended for connection between a line conductor and an accessible or earthed part, where it typically suppresses common-mode interference. Because a short-circuit failure in this position could place a hazardous voltage on touchable metalwork and cause electric shock, the capacitor must fail safe and withstand very high impulse voltages, and it is therefore restricted to comparatively small capacitance and divided into subclasses by rated voltage and impulse withstand. Its construction, insulation and testing are governed by the mains-capacitor safety standard, and substitution with a non-approved capacitor is prohibited.' WHERE id='1fac29cc-268a-4c27-90e4-aa7d78e85b43' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diode designed to be operated in reverse breakdown, where beyond a specified reverse voltage it conducts strongly while maintaining a nearly constant voltage across its terminals, the effect arising from Zener tunnelling at low voltages and avalanche multiplication at higher ones. Provided the current is limited by an external element and kept within its power rating, it holds this reference voltage over a wide current range, and its parameters include nominal breakdown voltage, tolerance, dynamic impedance and temperature coefficient. It is used for voltage references, regulation, level shifting and overvoltage clamping.' WHERE id='d423445a-9678-4f12-9c67-6220a1888618' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A component made in a standard resistor body but with essentially no resistance, functioning as a wire link or jumper that can be inserted, placed and soldered by the same automated equipment that handles ordinary resistors. It is used to bridge printed-circuit tracks, to provide configuration or option links, and to allow a single board layout to be adapted by fitting or omitting the link, and although its resistance is nominally zero it has a small residual resistance and a specified maximum current-carrying capability. It carries no signal-processing function beyond connection.' WHERE id='c0ece9ed-607b-47f2-9355-7dbaad6e1b6d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The resistor in a Zobel network, a series resistor-capacitor branch connected across a load such as a loudspeaker or the output of an amplifier to compensate the rising impedance caused by an inductive element. Across a loudspeaker it works with its series capacitor to counteract the rise of voice-coil impedance with frequency, presenting the crossover or amplifier with a more nearly resistive load, while at an amplifier output it helps ensure stability by defining a controlled high-frequency load. Its value is calculated from the load''s resistance and inductance so that the network flattens the combined impedance.' WHERE id='417738c4-3b73-4bbb-830e-b187d77edda4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A through-hole component package in which the two leads emerge from opposite ends of a cylindrical or tubular body along its central longitudinal axis, so the part is mounted lying flat and parallel to the board with each lead bent down into a plated hole. Typical of fixed resistors, non-polarised capacitors, diodes, and small inductors, the lead-to-lead span sets the required hole pitch, and the parts are commonly supplied taped for automated axial insertion. The axial lead configuration is one of the standard descriptors used in interconnect terminology and diode outline registrations.' WHERE id='ade1dc4a-f610-4a0f-b585-b2c7c0adec79' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A surface-mount integrated-circuit package whose interconnections form an area array of solder balls on the underside of the body rather than peripheral leads, providing high input/output count in a small footprint with short, low-inductance connections. During reflow the balls wet the matching board lands and partially collapse to form the joints; because those joints lie hidden beneath the body they are evaluated by X-ray or cross-section rather than direct visual inspection. Ball pitch, count, and body size are fixed by registered package outlines, and assembly is governed by dedicated area-array implementation guidance.' WHERE id='00b88193-6524-423c-9f37-954f138e3cb6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A through-hole package with two parallel rows of leads running along the long sides of a rectangular body, spaced at a 2.54 mm (0.1 in) pitch and, for the common form, 7.62 mm (0.3 in) between rows, inserted into plated through-holes or a socket. It was the dominant package for logic, op-amp, and microcontroller ICs in through-hole assembly and is defined by a registered JEDEC mechanical outline. Pin count is even, numbered counter-clockwise from a keyed or notched end that marks pin one.' WHERE id='5567443a-e95d-48b9-850d-c6915c3bab89' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A JEDEC-registered family of molded surface-mount packages for two-terminal devices such as rectifier and transient-suppression diodes, comprising DO-214AC (commonly called SMA), DO-214AA (SMB), and DO-214AB (SMC) in ascending body and current-handling size. Each has a molded body with metal end terminations formed to solder onto two board lands, the larger variants providing greater junction area and thermal capacity for higher forward-current and power ratings.' WHERE id='1ef82625-8b75-4c0b-8648-8bfd60540c80' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A JEDEC-registered axial-leaded through-hole package for diodes, having a cylindrical molded or glass body on the order of 5 mm long with axial leads of roughly 0.86 mm diameter emerging from each end. It is the standard body for common 1 A general-purpose silicon rectifiers such as the 1N4001 to 1N4007 series, mounted flat to the board with leads bent into plated holes.' WHERE id='0dd071e6-81eb-4dcd-a029-12b239e63164' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A surface-mount package with gull-wing leads on all four sides of a thin, square or rectangular molded body, standardised at a nominal 1.4 mm seated height and offered in fine lead pitches, commonly 0.4 to 0.8 mm. It provides moderate-to-high pin counts for microcontrollers and peripheral ICs and is registered under a JEDEC quad-flat-pack mechanical outline, differing from the thicker QFP and the thinner TQFP chiefly in body height.' WHERE id='036fa002-fa80-4988-8ffa-9665f1ad210b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A near-chip-scale surface-mount package with no protruding leads; peripheral lands on the underside, and usually a large central exposed pad, are soldered directly to the board, giving a small footprint, low parasitic inductance, and a good thermal path. The exposed pad typically requires a soldered thermal and ground connection to the board, and because the terminations sit under the body it is classed as a bottom-termination component with its own assembly guidance.' WHERE id='f13ee69f-4041-4254-bb7f-d0d76c480dd2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A surface-mount package with gull-wing leads projecting from all four sides of a flat, square or rectangular body, enabling high pin counts for large logic and processor devices. Members of the family are distinguished mainly by body thickness and lead pitch, including the thin TQFP and the low-profile LQFP, and their dimensions are fixed by registered quad-flat-pack outlines.' WHERE id='659c049e-25f4-4838-a59d-2c50324b6010' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A through-hole component package in which both leads exit from the same face of the body, so the part stands vertically on the board on parallel leads set to a defined spacing. It is characteristic of aluminium electrolytic capacitors, many film and disc/multilayer ceramic capacitors, and some inductors, and radial parts are supplied taped for automated radial insertion. The radial lead configuration is one of the standard lead descriptors in interconnect terminology.' WHERE id='a5620570-78a7-4f3e-9d17-760c41fcedb8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A through-hole package with a single row of leads along one edge of the body, inserted into one line of holes so the part mounts vertically or at a slight angle. It is used for resistor networks, some linear regulators, and small modules where a compact single-row footprint is wanted, and is one of the in-line package styles covered by package designation standards.' WHERE id='ca987eef-a60d-453f-a614-969b12ff1d2e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A component designed to be placed and soldered directly onto lands on the surface of a printed board rather than inserted through holes, using compact terminations such as leadless chip ends, gull-wing or J-leads, or ball arrays suited to solder-paste reflow and automated pick-and-place. Surface-mount devices are the basis of surface-mount technology, permitting smaller assemblies, double-sided population, and high placement throughput, and their acceptable soldered condition is defined by assembly acceptability standards.' WHERE id='19881d6a-9565-4013-9b95-578f6f977896' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A surface-mount integrated-circuit package with gull-wing leads on its two long sides at a 1.27 mm (0.05 in) pitch and a low profile, functioning as the surface-mount counterpart to the dual in-line package. Narrow-body and wide-body variants, differing in body width to suit different die sizes and pin counts, are registered as distinct JEDEC mechanical outlines.' WHERE id='005842c2-e667-47ad-bdf2-cd8bc44512ca' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A surface-mount package for small-power transistors and low-dropout regulators, having three gull-wing leads on one side and a wide metal tab on the opposite side that usually forms a fourth terminal and acts as a heat-spreading and mechanical anchor for the enlarged board land. Its larger pad area gives higher power dissipation than smaller small-outline transistor packages while remaining compatible with reflow assembly.' WHERE id='be36c833-aaec-40db-8797-b08f50f05b31' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small three-terminal surface-mount package for transistors, diodes, and low-pin-count ICs, with gull-wing leads soldered to board lands; five- and six-lead variants extend it to small analog and logic parts. The baseline three-lead SOT-23 is registered by JEDEC as outline TO-236, and its compact body supports high-density placement.' WHERE id='ca24962b-9e00-4367-8224-b3860e4e41ea' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A surface-mount package resembling the small-outline IC but with a shrunken body and finer lead pitch, commonly 0.65 mm, carrying gull-wing leads on its two long sides. It offers higher pin density than the SOIC in a smaller area and is registered under a JEDEC shrink-small-outline mechanical outline.' WHERE id='90ab4858-8194-4196-ad28-b4c9fbee8257' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A component whose terminations are engineered for placement and soldering onto copper lands on a board surface, without leads passing through holes, so that it can be assembled by solder-paste reflow and automated placement. This construction is the foundation of surface-mount technology, enabling smaller size, double-sided boards, and high-throughput production, and the term is used interchangeably with SMD. Acceptable termination, wetting, and joint condition for such parts are specified in assembly acceptability standards.' WHERE id='81275f9e-b0a8-44ab-bb9f-34aa3a75d9ad' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A component package whose leads or pins pass through drilled, plated holes in the board and are soldered to the annular ring and barrel, typically on the side opposite to the body, giving a strong mechanical attachment that resists shock and vibration. The category includes axial, radial, dual and single in-line, and many power packages, and the acceptable condition of through-hole solder joints, including barrel fill, is defined by assembly acceptability standards.' WHERE id='abfb0975-5b1f-4cef-a22e-e552d1219079' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A JEDEC-registered package for power semiconductors such as transistors, MOSFETs, linear regulators, and rectifiers, comprising a molded body with a metal mounting tab that carries a hole for bolting to a heatsink and, typically, three leads on a 2.54 mm pitch. Through-hole (TO-220) and surface-mount derivatives allow the device to dissipate substantial power when properly heatsinked.' WHERE id='a5849bb3-e064-4fef-b022-d6dd74d2c7ea' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A JEDEC-registered through-hole power package larger than the TO-220, with a thick molded body, wide, robust leads, and a metal-backed tab with a mounting hole, used for high-current, high-power discrete devices such as IGBTs, power MOSFETs, and rectifiers. Its greater size and thermal mass support higher dissipation and voltage ratings than the TO-220.' WHERE id='228cc81f-1295-4c50-aed8-dbf2043ba429' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A JEDEC-registered metal-can power package with a flanged oval body bolted directly to a heatsink or chassis through two mounting holes, from which two pins emerge through the base while the metal case itself forms a third terminal, for example the collector of a power transistor. It was long used for high-power transistors and regulators such as the 2N3055.' WHERE id='32025981-e76b-4c8e-ac44-dff22b371d35' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A JEDEC-registered through-hole package with a small molded epoxy body having one flat face and three leads emerging from the base, used for low-power transistors, small-signal and voltage-reference devices, and temperature sensors. The flat face and lead order identify pin assignment, and the leads may be formed to different spacings for insertion.' WHERE id='25b4f7d9-b22c-446b-8a9a-7fa93512106f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A surface-mount package with gull-wing leads on its two long sides at fine pitch, commonly 0.65 mm or 0.5 mm, and a thin body of roughly 1.0 to 1.2 mm height, giving a small, low-profile footprint for logic and analog ICs. It is registered under a JEDEC thin-shrink-small-outline mechanical outline and is thinner than the SSOP from which it is derived.' WHERE id='c4162307-644b-4893-a762-be5b5bd019f2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A vocal delivery performed with little or no prior rehearsal, in which a host, narrator, or voice performer reads copy essentially at first sight rather than after study and repeated practice. In podcast and broadcast voice work it is used to obtain a spontaneous, conversational quality, is common for host-read advertisements and unscripted reactions, and serves as a standard audition and studio technique for gauging a performer''s sight-reading fluency, phrasing, and interpretive instinct.' WHERE id='285f0fb7-eac6-49cd-9602-f0e801d52201' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Recorded spoken narration produced for an advertising message, in which a voice performer delivers scripted promotional copy that is mixed over or between program content. In podcasting it spans both pre-produced spot advertisements and host-read endorsements inserted into episodes, and it is characterised by a persuasive delivery style, tight timing to a target duration, and conformance to the campaign''s approved brand and legal copy.' WHERE id='812ab6de-fd8a-4b60-954d-5d4df2fee1a3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A distribution technique in which advertising audio is stitched into a podcast episode at defined ad markers at the moment the episode is requested, rather than being permanently baked into the recorded file, so that different listeners can receive different, individually targeted or time-current advertisements. The hosting platform inserts spots at pre-roll, mid-roll, and post-roll positions signalled in the episode, enabling ads to be updated, targeted, and measured after the episode has been published.' WHERE id='775be098-4228-4838-97c3-0a72103a15ba' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The still image associated with an individual podcast episode, distinct from the show-level cover art, so that podcast directories and apps can display a dedicated image for that episode. For Apple Podcasts it is expected as a square RGB JPEG or PNG between 1400 by 1400 and 3000 by 3000 pixels, and it is carried either as an embedded image frame inside the episode''s audio file or by an item-level image reference in the RSS feed.' WHERE id='420efcdb-e978-447c-88b0-466c11336245' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The detection and removal of transient mouth noises — lip smacks, clicks, and saliva sounds — from a spoken-word recording, carried out either by manual waveform editing or by dedicated de-click and mouth-noise processing so the artefacts are attenuated without audibly disturbing the surrounding speech. It is a routine dialogue-cleanup step in podcast and voice-over post-production, improving intelligibility and the perceived polish of the voice track.' WHERE id='7221acce-e1ea-4825-9ea3-32dcd12d7a14' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The assembly of a single continuous narration performance from multiple recorded takes by selecting the best portions of each and combining them into one composite track. Using a digital audio workstation''s take-folder or playlist tools, the editor auditions alternate reads of the same lines and splices chosen words and phrases together so that the final voice track is seamless and free of the errors present in any single take.' WHERE id='d03844a8-c33c-459a-9659-6ec92afac342' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The voice performer who delivers the spoken narrative of a podcast, providing the unseen voice that recounts events, links segments, and guides the listener through the program. In scripted, documentary, and audio-drama podcasting the narrator reads authored narration to carry the storyline between interviews, actuality, and music, and the role centres on interpretation, clarity, and sustained tonal consistency across the episode.' WHERE id='e5adfc8d-f122-4e85-bdb0-a5df5ff36524' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A low-level, often loopable music track played underneath speech to establish mood and continuity while remaining subordinate to the voice, its level held well below the dialogue and frequently ducked further whenever speech is present. Beds run beneath narration, interviews, or advertisements and are distinguished from foreground theme music by their sustained, background supporting role rather than a signature identifying one.' WHERE id='36506720-e7da-4111-8b5d-cecf9e49c2f4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small, acoustically treated and sound-isolated enclosure in which a host or voice performer records speech, designed to exclude external noise and suppress room reflections so the captured voice is dry, close, and intelligible. It typically contains the microphone, pop filter, headphones, and the talent, and may be a purpose-built vocal booth or a treated small room used for the same purpose.' WHERE id='8efa13b0-4af2-4d3e-a82e-a59a1710bf6a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A short audio element, commonly combining a spoken tag with music or a sound effect, used to bridge between segments or to mark the move into and out of a break within an episode. Bumpers usually announce what is coming next or reinforce the show''s identity and run only a few seconds, giving the program structural signposts and continuity.' WHERE id='4f1cdc96-2e1c-490e-9cd6-70b829160e0b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A presenter who shares hosting duties on a podcast with one or more other hosts, participating on broadly equal footing in leading discussion, interviewing guests, and carrying the show''s continuity. The co-hosted format depends on conversational interplay between hosts, and each co-host is typically recorded on a separate microphone and track to allow independent editing and balancing.' WHERE id='1611a235-d737-4f79-a195-9f4681782192' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The person responsible for the post-production editing of a podcast episode: assembling the recorded material, removing mistakes and dead air, restructuring and tightening content, and shaping pacing before the program is mixed and released. The role is primarily editorial and narrative, distinguishing it from the engineer''s technical focus, and it determines the final running order and flow of the episode.' WHERE id='06d120f3-79c4-43c6-8f37-689131db4123' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The audio technician responsible for the technical capture and processing of a podcast''s sound, including setting up and operating microphones, preamplifiers, and recording systems, and handling signal processing, mixing, and loudness-compliant delivery. Distinct from the editor''s narrative role, the engineer safeguards technical audio quality — clean gain structure, low noise, and conformance to distribution level and format specifications.' WHERE id='1e954c10-2ffc-417e-8bd5-030ca06fb879' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The opening section of an episode that introduces the show, typically combining theme music with a spoken welcome, the show title, and host identification, establishing the program''s identity and setting expectations before the main content begins. It provides a consistent, recognisable start that brands successive episodes and cues the listener that the program has begun.' WHERE id='e2cdc5a1-091a-4b7e-9daa-80b6fff8c267' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The specified integrated program loudness, expressed in LUFS as measured by the ITU-R BS.1770 loudness algorithm, to which a finished podcast is normalised so that level is consistent across episodes and platforms. Common targets cluster around -16 LUFS for spoken-word podcasts on Apple Podcasts, near -14 LUFS on some music-oriented streaming services, and -18 LUFS for speech under the AES streaming recommendation, paired with a maximum true-peak ceiling of about -1 dBTP to prevent clipping after lossy encoding.' WHERE id='49bfda34-86a3-4092-b8df-fdc904bd48f0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The final post-production stage that prepares the mixed episode for distribution, applying corrective and finishing processing — equalisation, dynamic-range control, and especially loudness normalisation to a target integrated loudness with true-peak limiting — before exporting to the delivery file format. Its aim is a clean, consistent program that plays back at the intended, platform-appropriate level and headroom rather than to add new creative content.' WHERE id='4522bac6-d58a-4d9a-a29d-c4613a9147a8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The descriptive and technical data that accompanies a podcast and its episodes, carried in the RSS 2.0 feed with the Apple/iTunes podcast namespace tags and in embedded ID3 tags within the audio file. It includes elements such as title, description, author and owner, category, artwork reference, a globally unique episode identifier, publication date, duration, and the enclosure pointing to the media file, and it is what directories and apps read to list, describe, order, and deliver the show.' WHERE id='e9490463-77a7-4d9e-9a60-b54c275843ce' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The closing section of an episode, typically pairing closing or theme music with a spoken sign-off that thanks the audience, gives calls to action such as subscribing or visiting a link, and credits the production, bringing the episode to a defined and branded end. Like the intro it is a recurring structural element that reinforces the show''s identity across episodes.' WHERE id='ed62a5bb-948a-4516-ac8e-bb2086064185' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The process of making an episode available to listeners by uploading the finished audio to a host and issuing or updating the RSS 2.0 feed so that directories and apps such as Apple Podcasts and Spotify ingest the new item and deliver it to subscribers. Publishing concerns the feed enclosure, episode metadata, and directory distribution rather than the recording or editing of the content itself.' WHERE id='f7a9e4fa-27e8-475b-bc72-1a12a31b4e44' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The systematic checking of a finished episode before release against technical and editorial standards, verifying integrated-loudness and true-peak conformance, absence of clipping, noise, dropouts and audible editing errors, correct metadata and artwork, and a valid feed enclosure. Its purpose is to confirm the program meets platform specifications and production standards so that it plays back correctly and consistently for all listeners.' WHERE id='27367df5-e297-489f-8631-93b49ff7298f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The capture of the spoken-word, and any accompanying, audio for an episode to a storage medium using microphones and a recording device or digital audio workstation, whether in a single location, a studio, or remotely. It produces the raw source tracks — ideally each voice on its own channel — that subsequent editing, mixing, and mastering refine into the finished program.' WHERE id='f8046b10-5e78-4426-84ec-51046b48e355' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A discrete, self-contained section of an episode devoted to a particular topic, format, or recurring feature, delimited from adjacent sections by transitions such as bumpers, stingers, or music. Segmenting lets an episode be structured into ordered, often recurring parts, aiding pacing, navigation, and the placement of advertisements or breaks.' WHERE id='7203eba4-6983-4ec6-a013-4cc65dad34ad' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A very short musical or sound-effect punctuation, often a single chord, hit, or brief phrase, used to accent a moment, close a segment, or punctuate a transition. It is distinguished from a bed or theme by its brevity and its accenting function, giving emphasis or a structural marker without occupying sustained time.' WHERE id='1490a42f-9ba8-43c6-9b13-d5527420caa2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A brief promotional excerpt or specially produced clip that previews upcoming content to draw listeners in, placed either at the very start of an episode as a highlight cold open or released separately to promote a show or episode. Its function is to entice the audience by foreshadowing the most compelling material without disclosing the whole.' WHERE id='90afa9a2-2257-466b-9ca1-1d286eec011e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The signature musical piece identified with a podcast, played prominently in the intro and outro and sometimes at segment transitions to brand the show and cue its structure. It functions as foreground music that carries the program''s identity, as opposed to a bed, which sits subordinate beneath speech.' WHERE id='7c1a62ce-bbce-44d5-8f9c-34d5df567a22' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A short, standalone promotional episode that introduces a podcast or a forthcoming season and is published in the feed so prospective subscribers can preview the show. In the Apple Podcasts specification a trailer is a defined episode type, flagged by the item''s episode-type value so that apps present it distinctly from regular episodes.' WHERE id='fd280b6a-a2e0-4630-bc5d-ac991bcec0ae' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An audio device — music, a sound effect, a bumper, or a stinger — used to move smoothly between segments, topics, or scenes within an episode, signalling a change of section while maintaining flow and pacing. Transitions give the program clear structural boundaries and prevent abrupt or jarring cuts between differing content.' WHERE id='0226a543-cbd0-4b6d-8e4c-da05c2b5a250' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The chain of signal processing applied to a recorded podcast voice to improve its clarity and consistency, typically including high-pass filtering, broadband and spectral noise reduction, de-essing, corrective and tonal equalisation, and dynamic-range compression, ending in level control toward the delivery loudness target. Applied judiciously it yields an even, intelligible, and present spoken-word track suitable for varied listening environments.' WHERE id='2d44b299-b346-4073-8ae1-07d179eb77a6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The recording of a podcast whose participants are in different physical locations, connected over the internet, in which each speaker''s audio is captured for later assembly. To avoid the quality loss of a compressed real-time call, the preferred method records each end locally at full quality — a double-ender — and synchronises the separate tracks in post-production, giving studio-grade audio for every participant.' WHERE id='2e8c46cb-472b-4283-9184-65a0d0d3026a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A performer who supplies a character or narrative voice through recorded speech, relying on vocal technique — characterisation, accent, timing, and interpretation — rather than physical or on-camera performance. In podcasting, voice actors perform roles in scripted and fiction podcasts and provide narration and character work, and they typically read from a script under a director toward a defined performance intent.' WHERE id='b2d92893-4013-49c8-8663-ed26e2eb8dde' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The person who directs voice talent during a recording session, guiding interpretation, pacing, emphasis, and characterisation so that performances match the script and production intent, and selecting and shaping the usable takes. In scripted podcast and audio-drama production the voice director is responsible for performance quality much as a director is on a film set, working with the engineer to capture the intended read.' WHERE id='ef41f619-f64d-4a1e-aa4a-13462e23b284' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The deliberate stressing of particular words or syllables in a spoken delivery — achieved through increased loudness, pitch movement, lengthened duration, or sharper articulation — to highlight meaning and steer the listener''s attention. It is a core interpretive tool of narration and voice performance, shaping how a sentence is understood by marking its most important elements.' WHERE id='2049bb21-36ad-4ab1-9ec0-60dd890f2f44' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The variation of pitch across an utterance — the rising and falling melodic contour of speech — used to convey meaning, emotion, and grammatical sense, for example distinguishing a question from a statement, and to keep delivery from sounding flat or monotone. Controlled inflection is fundamental to expressive, natural-sounding narration and hosting.' WHERE id='aa0a25d0-9691-40d0-89b3-ac077e8dba16' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The rate and rhythm of a spoken delivery — the speed of speech together with the placement and length of pauses — managed by the performer to aid comprehension, sustain interest, and shape emphasis and mood. Appropriate pacing varies with content and register, slowing for important or complex material and using pauses to punctuate and let ideas register.' WHERE id='e501b903-0db4-4a7c-b4e0-e65079467806' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The performer engaged to provide the spoken voice for a production — narration, hosting, character, or commercial delivery — that is, the professional whose voice is recorded. The term is used both for an individual performer and collectively for such performers, and it emphasises the voice itself as the hired creative asset.' WHERE id='2cb06dab-ada8-4cd1-81c6-5eea47fd65c6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The performer''s control of when and for how long words, pauses, and beats occur in a delivery, including hitting a required overall duration and synchronising the read to music, cues, or edited picture and content. In voice-over it commonly means reading copy so that it fits an exact time slot while preserving natural phrasing and emphasis.' WHERE id='c6bf9260-5e0b-417e-b081-9bb2b4d0cf8e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The final, approved, mixed-down version of a voice-over recording — processed, edited, and level-normalised — from which distribution and delivery copies are made. As the definitive rendered file of the finished narration, it embodies the agreed performance and technical treatment and serves as the reference source for any subsequent encoding or re-versioning.' WHERE id='355f0343-1d42-4ea2-9d4c-45faa8d73d03' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An isolated submix containing only the voice-over or dialogue elements, bounced as a separate track so the spoken content can be handled independently of music and effects during mixing, delivery, localisation, or re-versioning. Providing the voice as its own stem lets later stages adjust, replace, or translate the narration without disturbing the rest of the mix.' WHERE id='7eb90342-d96c-450b-b719-ba83fcbcd405' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A facility or acoustically treated room equipped for recording voice-over and narration, comprising an isolated recording space or booth and a control and monitoring position with microphone, preamplifier, and digital audio workstation. Its design prioritises clean, low-noise spoken-word capture with controlled reflections, making it suited to narration, commercial, and podcast voice recording.' WHERE id='124d89e7-b194-4f60-a2e8-c991083df289' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An output stage that presents its signal across two conductors carrying equal-magnitude, opposite-polarity voltages (or one driven leg plus a reference leg) relative to ground, so that only the voltage difference between the conductors constitutes the wanted signal. Its defining characteristic is balanced source impedance: the two legs present nominally identical impedances to ground, allowing a downstream differential receiver to reject interference coupled equally onto both conductors as common-mode. Practical realizations include transformer outputs, electronically balanced differential drivers, and impedance-balanced outputs in which one leg is driven and the other is a matching resistance to ground.' WHERE id='9b300cba-9b36-4049-9cc7-deecd3e3349f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An input impedance made deliberately much higher than the source''s output impedance, so the input bridges across the line and draws negligible current, imposing minimal loading and voltage drop on the source. In professional practice a bridging input is typically at least ten times the nominal source impedance, for example ten kilohms or more bridging a low-impedance line, and it embodies the modern constant-voltage (voltage-transfer) interfacing convention rather than matched power-transfer termination.' WHERE id='4374d85e-c594-4c3f-b90a-e43212de815e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone preamplifier whose gain devices are biased so that they conduct current over the entire signal cycle, the operating point sitting near the centre of the load line so no device ever cuts off. Because conduction never ceases, the stage avoids the crossover and switching nonlinearities inherent in push-pull operation, at the cost of high quiescent current, greater power dissipation, and low efficiency.' WHERE id='7a925b49-765c-4674-a23d-61e17d748941' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone preamplifier whose output or gain devices are biased so that each conducts for more than half but less than the whole signal cycle, a small quiescent bias keeping both halves of a push-pull pair conducting through the region around the zero crossing. This biasing minimizes the crossover distortion of pure Class B while achieving substantially higher efficiency and lower dissipation than Class A.' WHERE id='5040905e-70ba-4195-8c43-0c0bf957d552' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The primary, large-increment gain adjustment of a preamplifier, setting overall input sensitivity in relatively wide steps or over a broad continuous span. It establishes the approximate operating gain and is normally used together with a fine or trim control that resolves levels between its coarse increments.' WHERE id='0405ddb2-2d3e-420c-854a-78638e35d9d7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The ratio of a differential amplifier''s differential gain to its common-mode gain, conventionally expressed in decibels, quantifying how effectively the stage amplifies the wanted difference signal while suppressing any signal common to both inputs. In a balanced microphone or line input it measures the input stage''s ability to reject hum, ground-referenced noise, and interference coupled equally onto both legs of the balanced line; higher values indicate better rejection.' WHERE id='b11cad34-6b71-4639-962f-8549adf8cd83' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A gain control that varies amplification smoothly across its range without discrete steps, typically implemented with a potentiometer acting in the feedback or attenuator network so any value between the end limits can be selected. It offers effectively infinite resolution but poorer channel-to-channel matching and repeatability than a switched, stepped control.' WHERE id='a4e3a596-29df-409a-a669-6db51eda612f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The input stage of an amplifier, such as a long-tailed transistor pair or an instrumentation-amplifier front end, that responds to the voltage difference between its two inputs while ideally ignoring any voltage common to both. In an audio preamplifier it forms the balanced-line receiver, providing common-mode rejection of interference and establishing the noise and input-impedance characteristics of the channel.' WHERE id='96423154-2031-49f6-add2-0cac0f257dc5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone preamplifier whose gain stages are built from individually packaged (discrete) active and passive components such as transistors, FETs, resistors and capacitors, rather than from monolithic integrated gain blocks. Designers choose discrete construction for direct control over device selection, biasing, noise, headroom, and overload character.' WHERE id='2ff1ca7e-6ed0-40b9-b46e-d6e0dffc5c22' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The intrinsic noise of an amplifier referred back to its input, expressed as the input-referred noise voltage that, multiplied by the stage''s gain, would account for the noise measured at the output; for a microphone preamplifier it is specified over a stated bandwidth and source resistance, commonly a one-hundred-fifty-ohm source with twenty-kilohertz or A-weighted bandwidth, so intrinsic noise can be compared independent of gain. Its theoretical lower bound is set by the thermal (Johnson) noise of the source resistance, about minus one hundred thirty-one dBu for a one-hundred-fifty-ohm source in a twenty-kilohertz band at room temperature.' WHERE id='c1ad989f-f6ae-4011-a0f6-3a8de922ee91' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A secondary, small-range gain adjustment that resolves level between the wider increments of a coarse or stepped control, giving precise trimming for tasks such as matching the sensitivities of a stereo pair or aligning a channel to a reference level.' WHERE id='6cfdd6a7-4e87-48da-9702-44b6985b00ca' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An adjustable stage providing a limited range of gain or attenuation used to normalize signal level rather than supply primary amplification, for example to align channel sensitivities or bring a signal chain to a defined reference operating level. It complements the main gain block, allowing fine calibration of level at a point in the path.' WHERE id='78d9f266-e95e-4c07-aaa7-9f74b7c71bc4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The maximum gain that can be applied to a given signal before the amplifier''s output reaches its clipping ceiling, equivalently the headroom of the stage expressed as allowable gain. It is set by the ratio between the stage''s maximum undistorted output level and the level of the applied signal, and it bounds how far a source can be amplified while remaining free of overload distortion.' WHERE id='1a5c1857-d16f-4284-8761-02427e4d1ff8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An input presenting a high impedance, commonly of the order of a megohm or more for an instrument input, so that it only lightly loads sources of high output impedance such as passive electric-guitar or bass pickups and piezo transducers. The high impedance prevents the source impedance from interacting with input loading to cause level loss and high-frequency roll-off.' WHERE id='9754d7bd-963e-424e-94fb-58934957f37e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The electrical load an amplifier input places on its source, determined by the input impedance (its resistance and capacitance) appearing in parallel across the source. This impedance forms a divider with the source impedance that can attenuate level and alter frequency and transient response, and for some transducers, such as dynamic and ribbon microphones, the load also changes electrical damping and hence tonal balance.' WHERE id='e2b84b14-ea5a-4382-b045-cb4d863eedf8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The point in a channel signal path at which an externally processed signal is returned into the chain, forming the receive side of an insert send-and-return break. It is typically presented on the return contact of a TRS insert jack or a dedicated return input, re-entering the channel immediately after the point from which the corresponding insert send was taken.' WHERE id='c1cd0f18-e598-4b35-b5fa-d0b0347f4f49' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The point at which a channel''s signal is tapped and routed out to an external processor, forming the send side of an insert break. It is commonly located after the input gain stage and ahead of the channel''s main processing and fader, so that the external device is placed in-line within the channel path.' WHERE id='e5372047-e181-4530-8f8c-fa9833704b3e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An input designed to accept the unbalanced, high-impedance signal taken directly from an electric instrument''s pickup, presenting a high input impedance so the pickup is not loaded and its level and high-frequency response are preserved. It feeds the preamplifier either directly or through a dedicated direct-input stage that conditions the signal to the internal operating level.' WHERE id='ad2cd528-5cc3-468c-8787-022a690930b5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The front-end stage optimized for instrument-level sources, providing a high input impedance, an appropriate gain range, and sufficient headroom to accept the wide level and dynamic range of electric-guitar and bass pickups. It converts the unbalanced, high-impedance instrument signal to the device''s internal operating level without loading the pickup.' WHERE id='687cde87-ba15-420b-b4ec-20520c591579' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone or line preamplifier whose amplification is provided by monolithic integrated circuits, such as operational amplifiers or purpose-designed integrated preamplifier and instrumentation-amplifier gain blocks, rather than by discrete devices. Integration offers closely matched on-chip components, compactness, low cost, and predictable performance.' WHERE id='26a89858-a1d6-4c17-b9b4-6eabb33b4cae' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The input stage designed to receive line-level signals at a nominal professional or consumer reference level, presenting a bridging (high) input impedance and providing low, unity, or modest gain rather than the high gain of a microphone input. It is commonly implemented as an electronically balanced differential receiver so as to reject common-mode interference on the incoming line.' WHERE id='dbf02ecd-86d2-47b3-af93-3d6e49ed79bd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An input presenting a comparatively low impedance; in the microphone context it is the balanced low-impedance microphone input, whose nominal input impedance of roughly one to two kilohms receives a microphone of low output impedance, typically one to two hundred ohms. This follows bridging practice, the input impedance being several times the source impedance so the microphone is not power-matched but voltage-transferred.' WHERE id='c606fdbb-ae09-43e1-92c6-c8d3d140c6e0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The load impedance a preamplifier presents to a microphone. For moving-coil and ribbon microphones this load, together with the transducer''s source impedance, sets electrical damping and shapes level and frequency response, which is why some preamplifiers provide selectable input impedance so the loading can be varied to alter tonal character.' WHERE id='c25f629f-fe05-47b4-9a6b-c1bc11139247' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The circuit architecture of a microphone preamplifier: the arrangement of its input stage (transformer, discrete, or integrated differential), its gain-setting network and feedback scheme, and its output stage, together with how they are coupled. The chosen topology, for example transformer-input, single- or dual-op-amp instrumentation, discrete Class-A, or integrated-circuit preamplifier, determines the unit''s noise, headroom, common-mode rejection, and distortion behavior.' WHERE id='a9279308-c6f0-410b-8f2a-21a5bd921088' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Reduction of signal level at a preamplifier or amplifier output, applied by a fixed or switched resistive divider (a pad or attenuator) placed after the gain stage. It lowers the delivered level, for example to match a sensitive downstream input or to avoid overloading the following device, without altering the preceding gain structure.' WHERE id='8aec2725-4f9b-472a-ae04-f9aeeddaa9be' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The final stage of a preamplifier or line-level device that buffers the internal signal and drives the output line, presenting a low output impedance and supplying the current needed to drive cable capacitance and downstream input loads without level loss or added distortion. It may take the form of an electronically balanced differential driver or a transformer-driven output.' WHERE id='e48489db-b73d-4ed4-8398-7f7d8e10ac90' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The margin, expressed in decibels, between a device''s nominal operating level and the maximum output level it can deliver before clipping. It defines how large a transient peak above the reference level the output can pass without overload distortion, and together with the noise floor it bounds the device''s usable dynamic range.' WHERE id='0168b003-f256-4146-9677-92cc821bd1c3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A metering element, usually an LED, that illuminates when the signal at a monitored point approaches or reaches the onset of clipping. It is normally set to trigger a defined margin below the actual clip point so that the operator is warned before overload becomes audible, and it is a common way to police headroom at preamplifier inputs and outputs.' WHERE id='2e134a65-c5f3-485d-8baf-a450cb31ce30' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A relay used to switch a fixed attenuation pad into or out of the signal path, for example a microphone-input pad placed ahead of the preamplifier. The relay provides a clean, low-resistance contact that can be controlled by logic or from a remote panel, inserting the attenuator to prevent overload from high-output or high-SPL sources.' WHERE id='c53af521-dc83-49a3-84d3-42920eeff002' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The circuitry that applies or removes phantom power at a balanced microphone input, phantom power being a direct-current supply, standardized nominally at forty-eight volts with lower-voltage variants, fed equally to the two signal legs through matched feed resistors and returned via the cable shield to power condenser microphones and other active transducers. Because the two legs receive identical voltage, the DC is common-mode and does not appear as signal; the switching must manage the resulting turn-on and turn-off transients so as not to damage inputs or produce loud thumps.' WHERE id='ef907bc1-a6f1-47dd-b24b-e7a7a63f9ff3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A switch, often marked with the polarity symbol, that inverts the polarity of a signal by one hundred eighty degrees, in a balanced circuit by interchanging the two signal legs and in an unbalanced circuit through an inverting stage. It is used to correct polarity mismatches between microphones or sources so that summed signals reinforce rather than partially cancel; strictly it is a frequency-independent polarity inversion, not a time or phase delay.' WHERE id='51224276-9241-4f9a-a0ec-e86f57f3cdf3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The process of adjusting a preamplifier so that its gain, output level, and metering correspond to known reference values: setting gain trims so a defined reference input produces a specified operating level, matching the gains of paired channels, and aligning the preamplifier''s output to the reference of the following console or converter. Calibration establishes repeatable, documented level relationships across the signal chain.' WHERE id='011f1c54-334d-4881-a317-160b67814cfa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone preamplifier that provides gain using semiconductor active devices, such as bipolar transistors, field-effect transistors, and operational amplifiers, rather than vacuum tubes. It is the mainstream modern approach, offering low noise, wide bandwidth, low power consumption, and stable, reliable operation.' WHERE id='5114da27-8df8-4cc1-b1f2-d0689b0306a9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A rotary or switched gain control that selects amplification in discrete, precisely defined increments, usually by switching fixed resistor values into the feedback or attenuator network. Compared with a continuous potentiometer it gives highly repeatable, closely channel-matched settings, at the expense of only being able to choose the provided steps.' WHERE id='d851cff5-fb5d-4012-aecd-f951eda30523' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An output stage that couples the signal to the line through an output transformer, providing galvanic isolation, an inherently floating and balanced output, breaking of ground loops, and impedance transformation. The transformer also imposes its own bandwidth limits and low-frequency saturation behavior, which contribute to the characteristic sound and overload character of such outputs.' WHERE id='5ccb76a3-a6ba-445b-943e-8ee4b31d424e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A preamplifier that uses transformers to couple the signal into the gain circuitry, out of it, or both, thereby providing balanced interfacing, common-mode rejection, and galvanic isolation. An input transformer can also give a noise-free voltage step-up that improves the noise figure with low-impedance microphones, though at the cost of the transformer''s bandwidth and low-frequency distortion limits.' WHERE id='3dc3ff11-4a87-4902-9920-66ff4eb5b93c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A preamplifier that dispenses with input and output transformers, using active electronic balanced input and output stages to achieve balancing and common-mode rejection. This avoids the cost, weight, and low-frequency distortion of transformers but makes common-mode rejection dependent on the precise matching of the circuit''s components.' WHERE id='ceba4653-e889-42a1-a43f-8ef1bf25fa4b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone preamplifier whose gain is provided by vacuum tubes (thermionic valves), characteristically employing high-impedance voltage amplification and, usually, transformer coupling at input and output. Its overload behavior is comparatively soft and its distortion spectrum is dominated by low-order harmonics, a character often sought for its subjective sound.' WHERE id='4725a90c-3ba7-49bc-a1fe-dfa68905638d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The practice of setting the gain of the analog stages in a signal chain so that the nominal operating level sits an appropriate margin below each stage''s clipping point, leaving enough headroom for transient peaks while keeping the signal well above the noise floor. It amounts to distributing analog levels through the chain for the best signal-to-noise ratio consistent with freedom from overload.' WHERE id='ef66406d-e097-489a-8220-3b1d20e76a55' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The reference signal level to which a system is aligned during calibration, establishing a consistent correspondence across the chain; for example the analog level made to read a defined value on a meter, or the analog level made to correspond to a specified level below digital full scale at a converter. It is the anchor level against which gain, metering, and headroom are set.' WHERE id='bb474268-79a0-4986-ba96-fb93a575e7d5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The provision of a backup timing reference in a digital audio system so that, if the primary word clock or its distribution fails, connected devices can fall back to an alternate reference and remain sample-synchronous without dropouts or loss of lock. It is a resilience measure applied to the synchronization layer of a digital installation.' WHERE id='cf175746-a844-4528-b01a-2a1b5562f378' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The alignment that defines what analog level corresponds to a given digital reference in an analog-to-digital or digital-to-analog converter, typically stated as the analog level in dBu that produces a specified number of decibels below digital full scale. It fixes the converter''s headroom above the reference and the scaling between the analog and digital domains.' WHERE id='6940900d-4606-4317-822f-ba9ccf81e132' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The practice of setting recording levels so that peaks land a deliberate margin below digital full scale, preserving headroom for unexpected transients and inter-sample overs while keeping levels high enough relative to the converter and quantization noise floor. Unlike analog gain staging, exceeding full scale in the digital domain produces hard clipping rather than gradual overload, so the retained margin is a firm safety allowance.' WHERE id='6ec4116c-c053-41c1-a42a-caaccea375b3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The arrangement of the gain and loss of every stage in a signal chain so that each operates near its optimum level: enough gain established early to secure a good signal-to-noise ratio, but not so much that later stages are driven toward overload. Correct gain structure maximizes the usable dynamic range end-to-end, keeping the signal above the noise floor at every stage while retaining headroom for peaks.' WHERE id='79be6a97-e619-4785-b7a5-7767b57bd8b1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design and topology of the monitoring signal paths in a recording system: how monitor and playback sources are selected, summed, level-controlled, and routed to control-room loudspeakers and to headphone or cue feeds, independently of the record path. It defines what performers and engineers hear and how those feeds are derived without affecting the recorded signal.' WHERE id='13394474-10fe-4211-b86a-8bf9080b4739' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In a networked audio system, a scheme that provides duplicate transport paths, typically primary and secondary networks on separate switches and cabling, so that the failure of a cable, port, or switch does not interrupt audio. Devices that support it transmit or can switch to the secondary path automatically, giving seamless or near-seamless changeover on fault.' WHERE id='33843132-58cb-43cc-8052-6e5a6f2dd01b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The provision of backup electrical power to a signal chain, through redundant or dual power supplies, uninterruptible power sources, or alternate mains feeds, so that failure of one power source does not interrupt operation. Critical equipment continues to run on the alternate supply, protecting a recording or broadcast against power-related loss.' WHERE id='d3af4582-c48f-4335-b384-e32fcdc20f63' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The structural arrangement of the signal-processing resources in a recording or mixing system: where dynamics, equalization, and effects are inserted in the path, how processing is distributed between host CPU and dedicated DSP, and the order and routing of the processing blocks. It determines how and where signal is treated as it flows through the system.' WHERE id='f3f756c4-86d2-4181-97a7-e0050de61831' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The overall structural design of a recording system''s signal chain: the arrangement and interconnection of sources, preamplifiers, converters, routing, processing, and storage that together determine how signal flows from transducer to recorded medium. It is the high-level plan of the capture path within which gain structure and routing are configured.' WHERE id='daa64d1a-ba46-42f0-8f4d-8c7a7462ced1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The capture of the same program through duplicate or parallel record paths or devices, for example a simultaneous backup recorder or a mirrored recording, so that a failure in one path does not lose the take. It is a safeguard applied to the recording process itself rather than to the signal quality.' WHERE id='706e784e-232d-4fe6-a526-19b0acb265a8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The preservation of the recorded signal''s fidelity throughout the chain: freedom from added noise and distortion, correct levels, stable clocking, and the absence of dropouts or data corruption, so that the stored signal faithfully represents the source. It encompasses both the analog quality of the capture path and the error-free handling of the digital data.' WHERE id='b690ea39-b934-4363-9567-3dfc9599d349' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The standard nominal signal level to which a system is aligned and against which its meters are referenced, such as the professional or consumer analog reference level, or a specified level below digital full scale. It serves as the alignment point around which headroom above and noise floor below are reckoned, and it lets interconnected equipment share a common notion of nominal level.' WHERE id='ab9dd3f9-028f-4461-bc86-c89eea4da64f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The design of how signals can be interconnected within a system: the matrix, bus, and patch structure that determines which sources may be sent to which destinations, whether inputs, buses, inserts, or outputs, and how those paths are configured and recalled. It defines the flexibility and constraints of signal distribution in a console or DAW.' WHERE id='019a0479-64dd-423c-8dbc-9784a4ad1b21' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Measures taken within a recording session to guard against loss of work, such as mirrored or duplicate session recording, automatic backup of the session file and its media, or parallel capture, so that a hardware or software failure does not destroy the session. It targets the integrity of the session as a working entity rather than only the audio signal.' WHERE id='e7624ec0-a04b-4efb-a219-e0efc3d881c2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A documented layout of the signal flow for a recording session: the assignment of sources to inputs, preamplifiers, converter channels, and recorder or DAW tracks, together with the routing and monitoring paths. It serves as the reference chart of how signal is connected for that session, aiding setup, recall, and troubleshooting.' WHERE id='c9059c1f-ab42-4af7-9342-5b880683b38d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A verification procedure in which a dedicated audio test instrument generates precisely characterized stimulus signals—typically sine tones, multitone bursts, or swept sines—applies them to a device under test, and analyzes the returned output to quantify parameters such as gain, frequency response, total harmonic distortion plus noise, signal-to-noise ratio, crosstalk, and intermodulation distortion. The generator and measurement paths are calibrated so that the analyzer''s own residual distortion and noise sit well below the device''s, allowing measured impairments to be attributed to the equipment being serviced. In repair work it confirms that a unit meets its published performance specification before and after component-level intervention.' WHERE id='36010d90-710f-428b-9e52-5f657be48abc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The controlled evaluation of a device or module at a service workbench, removed from its normal operating installation, using laboratory power sources, signal generators, meters, and monitoring instruments to exercise the unit and confirm correct operation or localize a fault. Bench conditions let the technician apply known stimuli, reach internal test points, and observe behavior repeatably without the confounding variables of the field environment. It is typically both the first step of diagnosis and the final step of confirming a completed repair.' WHERE id='c60a9049-201d-482f-916e-46da7444076c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The practice of exercising and measuring electronic equipment at a dedicated service bench using controlled power, signal sources, and instrumentation, so that performance can be assessed and faults reproduced independently of the system in which the unit normally operates. Bench testing provides repeatable access to internal nodes and test points, enabling systematic stimulus-and-measure diagnosis, comparison against specification, and confirmation of repairs before a unit is returned to service. Working away from the installation removes environmental variables and lets the technician safely isolate power and signal conditions.' WHERE id='5e6807cc-391a-4a7d-a33a-06102d50b48f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The service procedure of setting the quiescent, no-signal current flowing through an amplifier''s active devices—most commonly the output stage of a power or headphone amplifier—to the value specified by the manufacturer, so the stage operates in its intended class and crossover distortion is minimized without excessive standing dissipation. The technician monitors the current, usually as the voltage across an emitter or source degeneration resistor, and trims a bias potentiometer or resistor while allowing the stage to reach thermal equilibrium. Correct bias balances low distortion against device heating and long-term reliability.' WHERE id='004410c7-e4fb-44e2-993a-4bd605273a13' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A region of discoloration, scorching, or carbonization on a printed circuit board, component body, or conductor caused by localized overheating, signalling a past or ongoing fault such as excessive current, a short circuit, or a failed part. During inspection a burn mark serves as a diagnostic clue that directs the technician toward the overstressed component or trace, though the visible damage is often a symptom rather than the root cause. Its extent and location help distinguish a single failed device from wider collateral damage, and carbonized residue may itself be conductive.' WHERE id='2528da86-76f8-4243-99ff-c298a2e85a63' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A reliability screening procedure in which a device or assembly is operated—often at elevated temperature, voltage, or load—for an extended, defined period so that weak components prone to early, so-called infant-mortality failure are stressed to the point of failing before the unit enters or returns to service. Units surviving the interval without degradation are considered to have passed the steep early portion of the bathtub failure-rate curve. In repair it confirms that a rebuilt unit is stable under sustained operation rather than merely functional at switch-on.' WHERE id='dccff55c-3616-42e3-9582-fd82e67ee652' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The practice of subjecting electronic equipment to prolonged operation under controlled stress conditions to expose latent manufacturing or repair defects that would otherwise cause premature failure in normal use. By accelerating early-life failure mechanisms through sustained thermal and electrical loading, burn-in testing separates units with marginal solder joints, weak semiconductors, or out-of-tolerance parts from those likely to give stable service. It is commonly the final qualification step before a repaired or manufactured unit is released.' WHERE id='c82ce752-e846-45fc-b54e-1ebd213e2f45' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The determination of a capacitor''s capacitance, usually with an LCR meter, dedicated capacitance meter, or a multimeter function that applies a known AC or stepped stimulus and derives the value from the resulting current, charge, or time constant. In servicing it verifies that a capacitor remains within its rated tolerance and has not drifted, and it is often combined with equivalent-series-resistance and leakage checks because capacitance alone does not reveal every degradation mode. Accurate results require accounting for test frequency, lead effects, and any in-circuit parallel paths.' WHERE id='d3ca711d-52c7-4350-8c9f-ff273f0921db' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An electronic component whose body has been blackened, carbonized, or physically degraded by excessive heat, indicating that it has been driven far beyond its safe dissipation—commonly a resistor, semiconductor, or connector that has passed fault current or sustained an overload. Charring often destroys the part''s markings and can make any measured value meaningless, so the technician must identify the component and its cause from the circuit and surviving evidence. Because carbonized residue can be electrically conductive, adjacent board material may also require cleaning or replacement.' WHERE id='d4dc1643-953f-4265-85a9-580411f7e112' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The process of following the physical and electrical connections of a circuit—using the schematic, board layout, and continuity or signal measurements—to identify a specific component, map how it is wired to the rest of the circuit, and understand its role in the signal or power path. It underpins diagnosis by letting the technician correlate a schematic node with its actual location on the board and confirm that the built circuit matches the documentation. Tracing is especially important on dense or undocumented boards where component designators are not obvious.' WHERE id='8813bbfe-d042-4a16-b1f2-50d508c2ebda' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The repair operation of removing a damaged, worn, or intermittent electrical connector and installing a replacement, restoring reliable mechanical and electrical continuity between mating assemblies or cables. It requires matching the connector type, contact count, current and voltage rating, and contact plating to the original, and executing a sound termination—soldered, crimped, or insulation-displacement—so that contact resistance and mechanical retention meet the original design. Correct strain relief and pin-out orientation are essential to prevent recurrence.' WHERE id='4136b233-b2be-464f-9a92-ed672f7a2174' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A test that confirms an intended electrical path is unbroken and of suitably low resistance, typically performed with a multimeter''s continuity or low-ohms function that signals a complete circuit audibly or numerically. In servicing it verifies conductors, traces, fuses, switch contacts, and solder joints, and—carried out with power removed—distinguishes an open connection from a component or supply fault. Reliable results require the circuit to be de-energized and any parallel paths to be taken into account.' WHERE id='2ae5e318-3991-4356-9e95-3af3a99588a4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The application of a specialized contact-cleaning and conditioning fluid of the DeoxIT type to electrical contacts, switches, potentiometers, and connectors to dissolve oxidation and contamination, lower contact resistance, and leave a protective film that inhibits further oxidation. It is a common remedy for the crackle, dropout, and intermittent behavior caused by degraded contact surfaces, and is applied sparingly with the contact exercised to distribute the fluid. It restores rather than replaces surfaces and is unsuitable where mechanical wear, not oxidation, is the underlying fault.' WHERE id='139e5684-791b-4d08-b42e-4b6c5935263c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The repair of removing aged, dried-out, leaking, or out-of-specification aluminum electrolytic capacitors and fitting replacements of correct capacitance, voltage rating, ripple-current rating, temperature grade, and polarity, often carried out as preventive recapping of power supplies and older equipment. Because electrolytics degrade with time and heat—losing capacitance and gaining equivalent series resistance—their replacement frequently restores performance and prevents secondary damage. Correct polarity orientation and lead dressing are critical, since reversed or under-rated electrolytics can fail violently.' WHERE id='ef0fea82-5c12-4621-9a69-225f1d41689d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The measurement of a capacitor''s equivalent series resistance—the small effective series resistance that models its dielectric, electrode, and lead losses—typically with a dedicated ESR meter or LCR bridge operating at a defined frequency such as 100 kHz. Rising ESR is a primary degradation signature of aluminum electrolytic capacitors and often causes power-supply ripple, instability, or failure while measured capacitance still appears near nominal, so ESR testing catches suspect parts that a capacitance check alone would pass. Many ESR meters permit in-circuit testing because their low test voltage does not turn on most semiconductor junctions.' WHERE id='aacf6e80-098b-4842-800a-437630305fc2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The systematic process of narrowing a malfunction to its specific origin by dividing the system into functional sections and testing to determine which stage or component is responsible, progressively excluding the healthy portions of the circuit. Techniques include signal tracing, signal injection, half-splitting the signal path, and comparing measured node voltages and waveforms against expected values or a known-good reference. Effective isolation converts a general symptom into an identified defective part or connection ready for repair.' WHERE id='52a7fcf7-9fbf-4963-b54d-b9cd8fe66e9e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A test method that uses a function generator—an instrument producing sine, square, triangle, and pulse waveforms of adjustable frequency, amplitude, and offset—to inject a known stimulus into a circuit or stage so its response can be observed with a meter or oscilloscope. By sweeping frequency or selecting waveform shape, the technician evaluates gain, bandwidth, slew rate, filtering, triggering, and linearity of the device under test. It is a fundamental bench technique for exercising audio and control circuitry during diagnosis and alignment.' WHERE id='f0019f9e-d898-44a9-b953-2979c0ffc4b7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The check that confirms a fuse is intact and of the correct type and rating for the circuit it protects, performed by inspecting the element, measuring continuity with power removed, and confirming the marked current rating, voltage rating, and breaking characteristic such as fast- or slow-blow against the equipment''s specification. A blown fuse is treated as evidence of an underlying overcurrent condition that must be found before replacement, and fitting a fuse of higher rating or wrong type defeats the protection and creates a fire and shock hazard. Ratings are taken from the manufacturer''s documentation or the marking, never assumed.' WHERE id='f3610955-bb69-413d-b862-15411352fd84' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The determination of an inductor''s or winding''s inductance, normally with an LCR meter that applies a known AC excitation at a specified test frequency and computes inductance from the measured voltage-current phase relationship. In servicing it verifies coils, chokes, and transformer windings against their rated values and, combined with Q or resistance measurements, can reveal shorted turns or core problems. Results depend on test frequency and drive level, and in-circuit measurements must allow for coupled or parallel components.' WHERE id='434b0ae4-19bf-41d4-87a6-251529ca1917' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A malfunction that occurs only sporadically or under particular conditions—such as temperature, vibration, humidity, or mechanical flexing—and is absent when the equipment is otherwise tested, making it among the most difficult faults to diagnose. Common causes include cracked solder joints, corroded or loose contacts, marginal semiconductors, and connectors that lose contact under movement. Diagnosis relies on provoking the fault by applying heat, cold, tapping, flexing, or prolonged monitoring so the failure can be captured while measurements are being taken.' WHERE id='ccbedaa7-2cb1-41b6-9eea-762b28a12f84' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A handheld test instrument that indicates the logic state of a digital node—high, low, open or high-impedance, and pulsing—usually through indicator lamps and a pulse stretcher that makes brief transitions visible, drawing its reference and power from the circuit under test. It offers a quick way to check for activity, stuck levels, and clock or data pulses without the setup of an oscilloscope, and it is matched to the logic family''s threshold voltages. While it cannot show waveform detail or timing, it efficiently confirms the presence or absence of digital activity during troubleshooting.' WHERE id='4cdf13ca-8644-4ec6-a32a-b3d247428074' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Testing that operates equipment continuously over an extended period—from hours to weeks—to reveal degradation, drift, thermal, and wear-related failures that only appear over time and would be missed by brief functional checks. By logging performance and environmental conditions throughout, it characterizes stability, uncovers intermittent and cumulative faults, and gives confidence that a repaired or manufactured unit will hold specification in service. It is related to but broader than burn-in, addressing long-term parametric stability rather than only early-life failure screening.' WHERE id='aa006193-807c-42c8-b62b-117e6ab5925f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The service procedure of trimming an amplifier stage''s output or input DC offset to zero, or to a specified value, typically by adjusting a null potentiometer or offset-trim network at the inputs of an operational amplifier or the DC-coupled stages of an audio path. Input offset voltage—the small differential voltage needed to bring the output to zero—arises from device mismatch and drifts with temperature, and nulling it prevents unwanted DC at the output, reduces switch-on thump and control noise, and keeps DC-coupled stages centered. Adjustment is made with inputs grounded and the circuit thermally settled.' WHERE id='c1a0e625-d96c-4753-a411-7057b27aa616' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The technique of connecting an oscilloscope to circuit nodes through an appropriate probe to display voltage waveforms against time, enabling observation of signal shape, amplitude, timing, noise, and distortion during diagnosis. Correct probing requires matching probe type and attenuation such as a compensated 10:1 passive probe, establishing a short local ground return to avoid picked-up noise and ringing, compensating the probe, and accounting for its loading on high-impedance or high-frequency nodes. Sound probing preserves signal fidelity and keeps measurement artifacts from being mistaken for circuit faults.' WHERE id='04c18734-41af-48e3-b7a7-a98bdb3c9468' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The measurement of the voltages present on a circuit''s power-distribution rails—the regulated and unregulated supply lines feeding a device''s stages—to confirm they are present, correct, and free of excessive ripple or noise, using a meter for DC value and an oscilloscope for ripple and transient content. Because supply faults propagate to every dependent stage, rail measurement is an early diagnostic step, and comparing measured values against the service documentation quickly localizes supply, regulation, and loading problems. Rails referenced to the mains or carrying stored energy present a shock hazard, so measured values must be checked against the manufacturer''s figures rather than assumed.' WHERE id='1215a0bf-9e1d-451d-883e-f4344289f84c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The comprehensive overhaul of a power supply in which aged or failed parts—typically electrolytic capacitors, rectifiers, regulators, and sometimes the transformer or switching devices—are replaced and the unit is realigned and retested to restore its original specification. It combines component-level repair with preventive replacement of parts known to degrade, followed by controlled power-up and verification of each output rail under load. Because power supplies contain mains-referenced circuitry and capacitors that store dangerous energy after disconnection, the work requires discharging stored charge and observing the manufacturer''s safety and rating data throughout.' WHERE id='ebffe036-bb49-4428-89e9-9aea003e0e7d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An amplifier built into or at the tip of a measurement probe that buffers and, where needed, amplifies the signal close to the point of measurement, presenting a high input impedance and low capacitance to the circuit while driving the instrument''s input and cable. By minimizing the loading and cable effects that degrade high-impedance or high-frequency measurements, a probe amplifier—as in an active oscilloscope probe or a signal-tracing probe—preserves signal fidelity that a plain passive probe would corrupt. It requires its own power and has a bounded input-voltage and dynamic range.' WHERE id='f84b1393-0818-4130-9603-af152549bc33' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The repair of removing a failed or out-of-tolerance voltage regulator—linear or switching, discrete or integrated—and fitting a functionally equivalent replacement matched in output voltage, current capability, dropout, package, and pinout, restoring stable regulated supply to the downstream circuitry. Correct installation includes verifying the associated input, output, and any compensation components and ensuring adequate heatsinking, because poor thermal management or an unsuitable substitute can cause instability or repeat failure. The output is confirmed against the service documentation after replacement.' WHERE id='f9c63ca9-93c2-42d8-96e4-7403fad8314a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The verification of an electromechanical relay''s operation by confirming that its coil energizes at the specified voltage, that its contacts transfer cleanly with acceptably low closed contact resistance and true open isolation, and that it switches reliably without sticking, chatter, or excessive bounce. Testing may measure coil resistance, pull-in and drop-out behavior, and contact continuity in each state, and it often includes inspecting for contact erosion or contamination that raises resistance or causes intermittent operation. Findings are compared with the relay''s datasheet ratings to judge serviceability.' WHERE id='9bf72e22-89d9-4574-9ed9-730e27fdcd55' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The final stage of a service action in which the repaired equipment is tested to confirm that the original fault is resolved, that no new faults have been introduced, and that the unit meets its performance and safety specification before return to service. It typically combines functional checks, measurement against published parameters, and a period of operation or burn-in to expose any residual instability, and it may include electrical-safety testing where mains-connected equipment is involved. Verification closes the diagnose-and-repair loop and documents that the unit is fit for use.' WHERE id='43ed5370-97a1-409d-9fb9-ac009d700ac7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The determination of a component''s or circuit''s resistance, typically with the ohmmeter function of a multimeter, which forces a known small current through the element and measures the resulting voltage, or with a four-wire Kelvin method for low values where lead resistance would otherwise dominate. In servicing it verifies resistor values, checks conductors and windings, and detects opens and shorts, and it is performed with the circuit de-energized because any applied voltage would corrupt the reading or damage the meter. In-circuit measurements must allow for parallel paths and semiconductor junctions.' WHERE id='23dbf266-8fbd-4482-97d7-2496e5a98cb2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The repair of removing and replacing a flat, multi-conductor ribbon cable and its insulation-displacement or crimp connectors, restoring reliable parallel interconnection between boards or assemblies. It requires matching conductor count, pitch, and connector type, maintaining correct pin-one orientation and conductor sequence, and seating insulation-displacement connectors squarely so every conductor is pierced and contacted. Careful routing and strain relief prevent the flexing-induced conductor fractures that commonly cause intermittent digital faults.' WHERE id='c8263d7d-0f3e-4024-a838-f6ce09534373' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An instrument that characterizes discrete semiconductors or junctions by applying controlled voltages and currents and measuring the device''s response to confirm type, polarity, and key parameters—such as a diode''s forward voltage, a transistor''s current gain and junction integrity, or a device''s identity in a component analyzer. Ranging from simple in-circuit junction testers and multimeter diode functions up to bench curve tracers and semiconductor parameter analyzers that plot characteristic curves, such testers let a technician establish whether a device is good, leaky, shorted, or open. They are central to component-level diagnosis and to matching or selecting replacements.' WHERE id='87ce6140-aab5-4257-a17d-b54f96d89865' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The technical documentation supplied to support the repair and maintenance of equipment—schematics, block diagrams, parts lists, board layouts, adjustment and alignment procedures, voltage and waveform reference charts, and disassembly instructions—that enables a technician to diagnose faults and restore a unit to specification. It provides the authoritative reference values and procedures against which measurements are compared and is essential for safe, correct component-level servicing. Accurate, version-matched documentation is what distinguishes systematic repair from guesswork.' WHERE id='859f6c40-c59d-4d90-b13b-c4c740743bb5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A diagnostic technique in which a known test signal is introduced at successive points along a signal chain—typically working from the output back toward the input—so the technician can find where the signal is lost by noting at which injection point normal output ceases. It complements signal tracing, which works forward from the input, and is effective for localizing a dead stage in an amplifier or receiver. The injected signal''s level and frequency are chosen to suit each stage so as not to overload the circuit or misrepresent its behavior.' WHERE id='7bde1ab6-a843-49f9-b43c-7f72d01bfdbb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A portable test amplifier, usually with a shielded probe and its own loudspeaker or meter, used to follow an audio or test signal through the successive stages of a circuit and hear or read its presence, level, and quality at each node. By moving the probe from input toward output, the technician identifies the stage at which the signal disappears or becomes distorted, isolating the fault. Its input is high-impedance and often AC-coupled with selectable gain so it can monitor both small-signal and line-level points without loading them.' WHERE id='6f373a5d-e0ce-4e39-81c4-101faa4e811f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The verification that a switch makes and breaks its circuit correctly—confirming low, stable contact resistance when closed, true isolation when open, and clean, reliable actuation across its positions without intermittency, bounce, or sticking. Testing typically uses a meter''s continuity or low-ohms function with power removed, exercising the switch through its full travel while watching for erratic readings that indicate worn, oxidized, or contaminated contacts. The findings decide whether cleaning, contact treatment, or replacement is required.' WHERE id='cb2ee8b1-a933-4f75-8118-6954d453f18d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A non-contact diagnostic method that uses an infrared thermal imager to map the surface-temperature distribution of a powered circuit or assembly, revealing abnormally hot components, shorted devices, overloaded conductors, and poor connections as thermal anomalies against the expected pattern. Because many electrical faults show as localized heating before outright failure, thermographic inspection can locate a problem quickly and without physical contact, and comparison with a known-good board highlights deviations. Accurate interpretation accounts for component emissivity, airflow, and normal operating temperatures.' WHERE id='56bff464-0eaf-44ed-918e-6fc48d4b0f4b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The selection of two or more transistors with closely matched key parameters—such as base-emitter voltage, current gain, or threshold voltage—so they behave identically in circuits where symmetry is critical, for example differential input pairs, push-pull output stages, and current mirrors. Matching is done by measuring candidate devices under identical bias and temperature and grouping those within a chosen tolerance, and the matched devices are often thermally coupled so the match is maintained as temperature changes. Good matching reduces offset, distortion, and thermal drift in the resulting stage.' WHERE id='09f2b284-4b34-4538-b435-03db6989875c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A servicing procedure in which a device is powered up gradually through a variable autotransformer of the Variac type, raising the mains-derived supply voltage slowly from zero while monitoring current draw and behavior, so that faults in a repaired or long-dormant unit reveal themselves before full voltage is applied. Slow ramping limits fault current, lets aged power-supply capacitors reform their oxide, and allows the technician to abort at the first sign of excessive current, smoke, or abnormal operation. Because a Variac is an autotransformer it provides no galvanic isolation from the mains, so its output remains a live shock hazard and it is commonly used together with an isolation transformer and current monitoring.' WHERE id='e72839b1-24d0-4417-856e-58eb02c0c23c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The elevated structural steelwork at or near the roof of a venue—the beams, girders, and grid from which overhead rigging is suspended—and, by extension, the working position of riggers who climb into that structure to install and connect flown equipment. Work in the high steel is carried out at height, frequently on beams without permanent walkways, and it is where up-rigging teams attach bridles, hoists, and points to the building''s load-bearing members. The structural capacity and permitted attachment methods there are set by the building''s engineering and the governing rigging standards, not assumed by the rigger.' WHERE id='4ad660a2-87b3-4ba2-9b2d-4f98ae77b94c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The permanent structural steel of a venue''s building—its roof girders, beams, and trusses engineered as part of the structure—regarded from the rigger''s standpoint as the fixed load-bearing members from which production rigging can be hung. Because it belongs to and is certified as part of the building, its usable rigging capacity and permitted attachment points are defined by the venue''s structural engineering and rigging-point documentation rather than by the visiting crew. Riggers connect to house steel using beam clamps, wraps, or designated points, and only within those documented limits.' WHERE id='78faf184-e532-495f-ad6e-093785ea8726' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A truss that is permanently installed and owned by the venue as part of its fixed rigging infrastructure, as distinct from the touring truss a production brings in, used to provide house lighting, audio, or scenery hanging positions with a known, documented capacity. Because it is a fixture of the building, its rated load, span, and support arrangement are established by the venue and its structural documentation, and productions rig to it within those stated limits. It may serve as the primary hanging structure or as intermediate support for touring equipment.' WHERE id='d6ea7ae8-f506-4dc6-8b65-9987643e37ae' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The provided means and route by which riggers reach a venue''s roof structure or high steel—such as ladders, catwalks, hatches, or dedicated stairways—so that overhead rigging points can be installed and connected. Safe roof access is a prerequisite of up-rigging and is governed by working-at-height practice, including fall protection and access control, because the routes lead to elevated and often hazardous positions. Its availability and configuration shape how a rig is planned and how loads are reached and attached.' WHERE id='1256731f-710a-47fe-8287-4db6e3b9f22f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An access opening, fitted with a closable cover, in a roof or high ceiling that provides entry from below into the roof structure, void, or onto the roof itself, used by riggers to reach overhead steel and to pass rigging lines or equipment between levels. As an opening at height it is both an access route and a fall hazard when uncovered, so its use is controlled under working-at-height and edge-protection practice. Its position and size influence where and how overhead points can be reached and lines dropped.' WHERE id='254c083b-1fe0-439c-8a20-7f9a616a04d4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The structural steel members forming a venue''s roof—trusses, purlins, and girders—considered as the overhead load-bearing structure from which production rigging is suspended. Its capacity to carry flown loads at any given point is determined by the building''s structural engineering and set out in the venue''s rigging documentation, and attachments are made only to members and points rated for the intended load. Riggers treat roof steel as the ultimate anchor for bridles and hoists, working strictly within the engineered limits rather than assuming capacity.' WHERE id='a9f4959d-34a3-419d-b2ea-519658503a31' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In bridle rigging, the horizontal distance a bridle apex is offset from its overhead anchor—the out dimension that, together with the vertical up dimension, defines the geometry and therefore the tensions of a bridle. Increasing the take-out lengthens the horizontal run and raises the tension in the bridle legs for a given load, so it is a controlled variable when positioning a pick point away from directly beneath the steel. Riggers calculate the resulting leg forces from the up-and-out geometry rather than estimating them, because shallow bridle angles multiply the leg loads substantially.' WHERE id='31715f9b-10c1-4918-b8c0-256858d8290e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A rigging and flying call directing the hoist operator or flyperson to take up on the line or motor until it bears the load, transferring the weight of a piece from its current support onto the lifting device in a controlled manner. The instruction is part of the standardized verbal communication used to coordinate lifts so that load transfer, slack removal, and readiness are unambiguous among the crew. It marks the point at which the rigging becomes load-bearing and is given only when the lift path and all connections are confirmed clear and secure.' WHERE id='87693a84-309c-432e-8960-9143ba602438' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A purpose-built structural ceiling grid—common in television studios and multipurpose performance spaces—engineered as a dense array of load-rated hanging positions, often with a walk-on access surface, from which lighting, audio, scenery, and services can be suspended and repositioned across the whole ceiling area. Unlike incidental building steel, a technical ceiling is designed and certified for repeated rigging use, giving distributed, documented point capacities that productions rig to within the stated limits. It typically integrates power, data, and rigging provision to allow flexible overhead layouts.' WHERE id='b3720aaa-49b1-49c5-a3a6-4af7a4cbc47a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='10-4 is a radio ten-signal (ten-code) used in two-way voice communication to signify acknowledgment: that a transmission has been received and understood, roughly equivalent to ''OK'' or ''message received.'' It belongs to the numeric ten-code scheme standardized for public-safety and land-mobile radio, in which a ''10-'' prefix plus a number encodes a fixed operational phrase so routine exchanges stay brief and unambiguous over congested or noisy channels. On a touring production it is spoken over the crew''s two-way radios to confirm receipt of an instruction without tying up the channel, serving as the ten-code counterpart of the receipt acknowledgment otherwise expressed by the proword ''roger.''' WHERE id='7d80c457-bf76-446f-acbb-f0611769a541' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='''Copy,'' as in ''copy that,'' is a radiotelephone procedure expression signifying that the speaker has received and understood the preceding transmission; it acknowledges reception of the message without in itself implying compliance, and in formal procedure corresponds to the receipt proword otherwise rendered as ''roger.'' The usage derives from telegraphy and early radio practice, in which to ''copy'' a signal was to write down or successfully receive its content. On a touring crew it is used over intercom and two-way radio so the sender knows a cue, count, or instruction was heard clearly, keeping channel traffic short and confirming the message got through.' WHERE id='d4750d81-cbe0-4bf5-9be0-9453fa0c3627' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An input patch list is the reference document that maps every audio source for a session or performance to the specific physical input it occupies on the stage box, splitter, or console, typically recording for each channel the source or instrument, the microphone or DI type, any phantom-power or pad requirement, the connector or stage-box number, and the console input to which it is routed. It is prepared during planning and used by the crew to wire and label inputs consistently, to verify signal flow during setup, and to recall or rebuild the patch quickly, including at an unfamiliar console. As the authoritative record of what is connected where, it underpins the line check, soundcheck, and troubleshooting.' WHERE id='f8974161-d27b-4a14-9322-a438918d9564' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An outdoor recording setup is the configuration of equipment and signal flow for capturing audio in the field rather than in a controlled studio, arranged to cope with the additional demands of the environment. It addresses reliable power from batteries or conditioned generators, weather and wind protection for microphones and electronics, longer and well-managed cable runs or wireless links, secure mounting and stable monitoring, and safeguards against humidity, temperature, and radio interference. The goal is a robust chain from transducer to recorder that preserves signal integrity and provides dependable monitoring under conditions a studio would otherwise control.' WHERE id='e348f344-a458-43ac-9fc0-8dbb8ad2a7dc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An output patch list is the reference document that maps each of a console''s outputs to its physical destination, recording for every mix, matrix, group, auxiliary, or direct output the connector or stage-box number and the device it feeds, such as an amplifier or loudspeaker processor, a stage monitor or in-ear feed, an effects unit, a broadcast or recording split, or a send to another position. Prepared alongside the input list during planning, it lets the crew wire and label outputs correctly, confirm routing during setup, and recall or rebuild the output patch reliably. It is the counterpart to the input patch list and is essential to verifying that every mix reaches its intended destination.' WHERE id='e60af68e-a749-4d08-a4c3-b52d89e4053b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A technical line check is the systematic pre-performance verification that every input is correctly connected and passing signal to the intended console channel, carried out before soundcheck and often without the full band present. Working through the input patch list, the engineer confirms channel by channel that each microphone or direct source produces signal on the expected channel at a healthy level, with correct phantom power, gain, and labelling, and that there are no wiring faults, mispatches, hum, or dead lines. It isolates and resolves signal-flow problems in advance so that soundcheck can be spent on musical balance rather than fault-finding.' WHERE id='73b2c49d-8d44-4837-a5d5-bba07abd2900' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording that captures the characteristic background soundscape of an airport, such as the layered murmur of travellers, public-address announcements, rolling luggage, and distant aircraft and ground vehicles, recorded as a continuous atmosphere rather than as isolated effects. It is slated and logged as a specific location type so it can establish a sense of place, provide continuity under dialogue or picture, and be edited or looped seamlessly in post-production.' WHERE id='4aaf7be1-68d9-4af1-83f7-5e903657863f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A review pass, carried out during sequencing and mastering of a multi-track release, that confirms the individual tracks form a coherent whole by verifying consistency of relative level, tonal balance, and perceived loudness from song to song, checking the spacing and any crossfades or segues between tracks, and confirming a consistent running order, fades, and metadata across the program. It ensures the record plays as an intended, unbroken listening experience before it is approved for delivery.' WHERE id='9ecee30c-f367-4c43-a76f-0f6a767f37fc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A direction, during tracking or overdubs, to record an additional version of a part already captured so that a different interpretation, phrasing, dynamic, or technical execution is available for later selection or comping, without discarding the earlier take. The request is logged on the take sheet or track log against the relevant take numbers so the producer and editor can compare and choose among the options.' WHERE id='a86417ba-b107-4bc0-87a4-9e27976bee18' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A stem consisting of the mixed ambience, room, and atmosphere elements of a production bounced as an independent submix, kept separate from the dialogue, music, and effects stems so background atmosphere can be balanced, replaced, or muted independently during final mixing, re-versioning, or foreign-language delivery. Like other stems it is delivered time-aligned to the full mix so that the stems sum back to the approved master.' WHERE id='597ec5e2-7e29-4934-ae76-db733d71d29f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The capture of the continuous background sound of an environment — its tone, reverberation, and characteristic activity — as a sustained recording rather than a discrete sound effect, typically made at the location so it represents the natural acoustic character of the space. Such recordings establish a sense of place, provide a bed beneath other material, and supply room tone for smoothing edits and filling gaps in post-production.' WHERE id='c4e208de-78da-4fe7-a609-0948a5b13e16' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The documented, chronological record of review decisions on a project, listing each version or deliverable submitted for approval, who reviewed it, the date, the outcome such as approved, rejected, or revisions requested, and any notes, so the current status and the path to it are auditable. It underpins client sign-off and version control by making clear which version is authorised for the next stage or for release.' WHERE id='f47867be-7536-437d-9e81-90570815d2b6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The controlled transfer of a completed project''s session data and assets from active production into long-term archival storage or to an archiving party, in which the session files, source audio, mixes, and documentation are consolidated, verified, and accompanied by the metadata needed to retrieve and reopen the project later. A proper handoff confirms that all elements are present, readable, and described before the working copies are retired.' WHERE id='c7f69680-95ad-4cdb-8537-aedf67dc3d38' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Comments recorded from or for the performing artist during a session — reactions to takes, preferences, requested changes, and lyric or arrangement adjustments — captured in the session documentation so the artist''s creative intentions are preserved and can be acted on during subsequent tracking, comping, and mixing.' WHERE id='5fd37454-b50b-4d07-819f-834226af0251' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A monitored playback of recorded material to the performing artist during a session, typically over the control-room monitors, so they can evaluate a take or a rough mix, confirm the performance, and decide whether to keep it or record further. It is a distinct workflow step from tracking, used to guide take selection and creative direction.' WHERE id='2b541761-0aeb-4567-8ef8-f2283b518d2a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In field and post-production practice, an atmos (short for atmosphere) recording is a capture of the continuous background atmosphere of a location or scene — its ambient tone and characteristic low-level activity — recorded as a sustained bed rather than as spot effects. It is used to establish environment, maintain continuity beneath dialogue and picture, and provide fill for edits.' WHERE id='d1c7574b-02d7-42fe-b6f1-d1e226ab4bf0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The recording of the sustained ambient sound that characterises a place or scene — the layered, continuous background tone and activity of an environment — captured as a bed rather than as isolated effects so that it conveys location and mood. Such recordings provide continuity, a sense of space, and material for smoothing edits in post-production.' WHERE id='2a8a62ff-88b7-4ffb-9a8b-37a203a40e75' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A room optimised for high-fidelity critical listening, in which loudspeaker placement, listener position, room dimensions, and acoustic treatment are controlled to achieve an even frequency response, well-defined stereo imaging, and low coloration from reflections and room modes. It is intended for evaluating reproduction quality rather than for recording, and follows the same acoustic principles as a control or mastering room.' WHERE id='d7d14586-f4da-48b9-9a37-b830a1e87d20' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The low-level, continuous background sound of an environment, recorded or placed beneath foreground elements to establish a sense of place and fill silence, sitting under dialogue, music, or featured effects without drawing attention. As a bed it provides acoustic continuity across edits and unifies otherwise disparate material.' WHERE id='2c1ca5d5-3ba5-4d7f-9de3-90be51ad8cb7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The procedure of confirming that a redundant, parallel recorder capturing the same program as the primary system is armed, receiving signal at the correct levels, and writing valid, complete files, performed before and during a session so the safety recording can be relied upon should the primary fail. Verification typically includes checking routing, record status, media capacity, and a playback confirmation.' WHERE id='61761d3e-2f5e-4da2-9060-e3359921624c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A project or home studio located in a building''s basement, valued for isolation from outside noise and for structural mass that aids low-frequency containment, but requiring attention to room modes in a typically small, low-ceilinged space, to moisture and humidity control, and to acoustic treatment that manages reflections. The term denotes the location and attendant acoustic constraints of the recording space rather than a class of equipment.' WHERE id='cb9ee529-9656-4a6e-b00e-78fcf7d929ea' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small home studio set up in a domestic room such as a bedroom, characterising the compact, budget project-studio environment in which tracking, overdubbing, and mixing are done in an untreated or lightly treated space using nearfield monitors or headphones. The term implies working within the acoustic and level limitations of a domestic room.' WHERE id='daed2164-3eec-4a9f-8289-d507ed344443' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A self-contained, transportable rack or set of cases housing a complete production or recording system — such as a mixer or console, routing, recorders, monitoring, and interconnect — assembled so it can be flown or trucked to a location and quickly deployed as a temporary control room for remote broadcast or recording. It packages a fixed installation''s core signal chain into a rugged, rapidly riggable form.' WHERE id='0a5e23f4-3154-4dfe-8386-218f2760ecdb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A session document that lists, for each console or recorder channel, the source assigned to it — the instrument or microphone, often the mic or DI type, and any notes — so that inputs are wired, labelled, and routed consistently and can be checked during setup and recalled later. It is the authoritative record of what occupies each channel for a session.' WHERE id='686bc7a9-32e7-4cbe-9f95-44d47af253d7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording capturing the characteristic sound of a church interior — its long reverberation, quiet background tone, and incidental activity — recorded as a sustained atmosphere that conveys the reverberant, spacious acoustic of such a space. It is used to establish location and to provide a reverberant bed or continuity in post-production.' WHERE id='82b308e5-57b4-43ce-b81b-7e259ea38b45' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the general background sound of an urban environment — distant traffic, crowds, and the diffuse hum of activity — captured as a continuous atmosphere rather than discrete effects, used to establish an urban setting and to provide a background bed and continuity in editing.' WHERE id='4da73e8d-1806-4891-8a7b-d5e6ffc1618e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The defined sequence by which work is submitted to a client for review and sign-off, comprising delivering a version, collecting the client''s feedback or approval, implementing revisions, and re-submitting until the client authorises the deliverable, with each decision recorded. It governs how a project advances from review to accepted release and establishes the authorised version at each gate.' WHERE id='9a84421c-1d84-4f48-ad74-bc86676255c0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dedicated, comfortable area within a recording facility, separate from the control room and live room, where clients, guests, and non-performing personnel can wait, relax, and hold discussions without disturbing the session. It is an element of studio layout and workflow design rather than the signal chain.' WHERE id='02d3843f-507c-4cc6-801a-89040fa8a77a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A monitored playback of recorded or mixed material presented to the client for evaluation and approval during or after a session, over the studio''s monitoring, so the client can assess the work and approve it or request changes. It is a defined review step distinct from the engineer''s own checking.' WHERE id='1ca80200-b382-4157-8afb-7edcf9c7859f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A scheduled session whose purpose is to audition completed or in-progress material for the client on the studio''s monitoring system so they can review, comment on, and approve the work, as opposed to a tracking or mixing session. It formalises client review as a distinct calendared event with associated feedback and sign-off.' WHERE id='f9a26b5f-f1aa-4aa3-a5fd-c9c446b59380' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The part of a studio facility set aside for clients to listen to and evaluate work, whether the control-room listening position or a separate room, arranged so clients can assess playback and give feedback. It denotes the designated space for client auditioning within the facility''s layout.' WHERE id='7959f72d-e025-4577-a6cb-354637779a8b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A backup of a project''s session files and associated media copied to remote, network-based storage so a redundant copy exists off-site, protecting against loss of local media and often enabling recovery and sharing across locations. As part of a backup strategy it complements local copies with geographically separate redundancy.' WHERE id='61d9776f-a5d8-4c23-ac91-bd187f362dd5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A networked service that lets multiple contributors to a recording project share session files, audio, and revisions and exchange feedback, providing version tracking, commenting, and controlled access so that geographically separated participants can work on the same material. It supports remote workflow and asset exchange rather than being part of the audio signal chain.' WHERE id='3ded7ebd-9387-481a-b7d8-8c2e1d0300b1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An agreed scheme for assigning colours to tracks, regions, markers, or channels in a session so that categories — such as instrument groups, take types, or statuses — are visually distinguished for fast navigation and consistent organisation across a project or facility. Digital audio workstations support user-defined track and clip colours for this purpose.' WHERE id='46cd472b-972a-4599-b0ad-57d5a663d3e9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In a collaboration or review tool, a display that positions reviewers'' comments at the specific time points in a recording to which they refer, so feedback is anchored to exact moments in the audio and can be navigated in sequence. It ties written notes to timecode positions for precise, time-referenced review.' WHERE id='259911cc-a2b5-41f1-a2a4-247ea7c7639a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A take, or a section of a take, identified during comping as a possible choice for the final composite performance, marked or rated so the best portions of multiple takes can be compared and assembled into a single continuous performance. It denotes material shortlisted for the comp rather than the finished composite.' WHERE id='e8d06048-69d6-4820-bd18-414cde0b47e9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The complete set of records that captures the state of a mixing console so a mix or session can be rebuilt later, comprising the settings of channel and output controls, routing, and processing together with any recall sheets, stored scenes, or snapshots. On analogue consoles this relies on written recall sheets and photographs, whereas on digital consoles the state is stored as recallable data.' WHERE id='47016c35-29c9-4149-9a10-2636a3c7d421' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A printed template diagramming a console''s channel strips and controls on which an engineer records every knob position, switch state, and routing assignment before teardown, so that an analogue console with no automated memory can be reset to the identical configuration to continue or revise a mix. It is the manual record used to recall a non-automated console.' WHERE id='fa91add8-fae6-44bc-8127-5815558bef21' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The system and practice of talking between the control room and performers or other areas during a session, principally via the talkback microphone routed into the studio''s headphone or monitor feeds, so the engineer and producer can direct performers, count takes, and coordinate the session. It is the operational voice link that manages the flow of a recording session.' WHERE id='d11eccb8-a51a-4673-b2dd-93b519377578' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Playback of recorded material over the control-room monitors, at the engineer''s primary listening position, for evaluation and decision-making by the production team, as distinct from headphone monitoring in the live room. It is the reference listening used to judge takes and mixes.' WHERE id='8e35b952-27b1-4c47-bd87-81000f05a987' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A room designed and treated for accurate, analytical monitoring, in which acoustics, monitor placement, and the listening position are controlled to give a neutral, reliable frequency response and stereo image so that fine differences in a recording or mix can be heard and judged. It is the environment in which mixing and evaluation decisions are trusted.' WHERE id='6bdd60e1-d290-4e48-9e37-42ddaf9d6e4c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the collective background sound of a group of people — the diffuse murmur, movement, and reaction of a crowd — captured as a continuous atmosphere used to convey the presence and size of a gathering and to provide a background bed. Recordings may be made as neutral, non-specific background voices or as a specific reactive crowd.' WHERE id='07f1bb5b-2785-4105-8aa9-848da9ff4ea7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The checking of a cue sheet — the document listing each musical or audio cue in a production with its identifying details, timings, and usage — to confirm the entries are complete and accurate before the sheet is used for synchronisation, delivery, or rights reporting. It is a verification step over the cue documentation.' WHERE id='ed6da753-e1dc-46e5-818f-097f89169c54' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A document compiled at the end of each session day that records what was accomplished — songs and takes worked on, personnel present, hours, equipment used, issues encountered, and notes — providing a running administrative and creative log of the project''s progress for the producer and studio.' WHERE id='28d0c477-0ae1-48d4-9fc5-f6ccf8a0f7af' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the characteristic background sound of a location during daytime — the pattern of activity, traffic, and natural sound typical of daylight hours — captured as a sustained atmosphere and logged as a time-of-day variant so it can convey a daytime setting and match continuity.' WHERE id='7ea95980-9659-4cff-98f6-c771470f733e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The organised tracking and control of the set of files a project must produce — such as masters, stems, instrumentals, and their associated metadata and documentation — ensuring each required deliverable is created to the correct specification, versioned, verified, and delivered to the right recipient. It coordinates what must be handed off and confirms completeness against the delivery requirements.' WHERE id='da7af69d-d4f1-421b-a08e-410b0e7d5381' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A checking pass over the finished deliverables before release, in which each file is confirmed against the delivery specification — correct format, sample rate and bit depth, level and loudness, channel configuration, edits and spacing, and metadata — so that errors are caught before the material leaves the studio. It is the quality-control gate on outgoing files.' WHERE id='5d7a747f-1a75-4534-86c0-a5b23aa2bd2a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The formal approval marking that a project''s deliverables have been reviewed, accepted, and authorised for release or hand-off, recording who approved them and when, and closing the delivery stage. It is the point at which responsibility passes and the delivered material is deemed final.' WHERE id='64c8f064-9040-40f8-a75b-63c9ff3c924e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the sparse, quiet background sound characteristic of a desert environment — wind, distant natural sound, and a generally low ambient level — captured as a sustained atmosphere used to convey an arid, open setting and to provide a subtle background bed.' WHERE id='86658f60-3c7a-4679-8a05-c52bbf0f01e9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The complete, specification-compliant set of files delivered to a distributor for release, comprising the finished audio masters in the required format together with cover art and the metadata — track and artist information, identifiers such as ISRC and UPC, and credits — needed to catalogue and distribute the release. Its contents and formats follow the distributor''s or standard delivery requirements.' WHERE id='06707f71-2f3a-4397-8563-8d0bb7483617' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A room used for recording drums, chosen or designed for its acoustic character and isolation; larger, more reverberant rooms impart a lively ambience captured by room microphones, more controlled rooms give a tighter sound, and the space provides separation from other instruments. It denotes the acoustic space for drum tracking rather than equipment.' WHERE id='688ad45c-f24e-4d26-9d32-a5d1ce39927c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A studio configured principally for producing electronic music, centred on a digital audio workstation, synthesizers, drum machines, samplers, and controllers with MIDI and audio integration rather than on large-room acoustic tracking. Its design emphasises the production, sequencing, and sound-design environment, together with accurate nearfield monitoring.' WHERE id='f916118f-6e64-47ea-ab9d-78c81512cf79' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording room large enough to accommodate a group of musicians playing together, allowing simultaneous performance with visual contact and a shared acoustic while providing enough space and treatment to manage separation and blend. It supports capturing a live ensemble in one space.' WHERE id='e0852ba4-66ea-42fa-aabd-a5830271fce8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A continuous background recording of an environment''s ambient sound laid down as an underlying layer beneath foreground elements, establishing location and providing acoustic continuity across a scene or program. As a sustained bed it unifies edited material and fills gaps.' WHERE id='31035cf2-25a3-47ac-9afc-a2ede6f8cb9b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The recording of the sounds of a natural or built environment in the field, capturing its ambient character and characteristic events as they occur on location, for use as atmospheres, beds, or source material. It denotes location capture of an environment''s soundscape rather than studio recording.' WHERE id='5605e582-bde8-4baa-9fd2-aa08caedaa79' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Photographs taken of equipment settings — console channel strips, outboard front panels, and patching — to document their exact state as part of a recall record, so that the positions of controls that cannot otherwise be stored can be reproduced when the setup is rebuilt. They supplement written recall sheets as a visual reference.' WHERE id='1da0073f-8b27-43f2-8a44-6b00a919e72f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the background sound of an outdoor location, capturing the open-air atmosphere — weather, distant activity, and natural or urban sound — as a sustained bed used to convey an outdoor setting and to distinguish exterior from interior acoustics.' WHERE id='e962d56d-4481-419a-8e89-bb15bf85ba20' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the characteristic background sound of an industrial or factory environment — the continuous hum and rhythm of machinery and processes — captured as a sustained atmosphere used to convey an industrial setting and provide a background bed.' WHERE id='a9765e42-6b20-41cb-a938-50f5a7744e8f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A remotely located input and output unit that connects microphones and lines to a mixing system over a fibre-optic link, converting the audio to digital and transporting many channels bidirectionally on a single lightweight cable over long distances with immunity to electromagnetic interference. It extends a console''s I/O to the stage or location while reducing copper cabling.' WHERE id='c164e983-cdd3-4fd1-b9d3-db5a3a7a1887' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The structured arrangement of a project''s files into a consistent hierarchy of folders — separating session files, recorded audio, bounces, and documentation with clear naming — so that assets are reliably located, kept together, and can be backed up, transferred, and archived without loss. Consistent structure underpins session management and safe hand-off.' WHERE id='2d8099b9-a742-48f4-94fa-18924aeca1eb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the background sound of a forest or wooded environment — birdsong, wind in foliage, and other natural sound — captured as a sustained atmosphere used to convey a woodland setting and provide a natural background bed.' WHERE id='e7913a04-cb2b-44f3-9404-1df33c81e857' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The checking of the durations of the silent gaps between tracks in an assembled album or program to confirm the spacing is as intended before delivery, ensuring the pacing between songs and any indexing meets the master''s specification. It is part of quality control on a compiled master.' WHERE id='cd32ea37-ca7e-4d46-bde5-016207f2b584' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A project studio located in a garage, a space offering room size and isolation from the house but typically requiring acoustic treatment and climate and noise control to become a usable recording environment. It denotes a converted, non-purpose-built recording space.' WHERE id='4e220809-076b-4dc4-b52c-fe32b77e54ff' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording setup supplied by a portable engine-driven generator rather than utility mains, used where mains power is unavailable, requiring clean, stable, and properly earthed power so that noise, hum, and interference are not introduced into the audio chain. It enables remote and location recording at the cost of managing power quality and grounding.' WHERE id='c5fe5b55-3292-4d98-932d-f238f6e35ef6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A room set up for high-fidelity playback and evaluation, with loudspeaker and listener placement and acoustic treatment arranged to give accurate, low-coloration reproduction for critical listening. It follows the acoustic principles of a critical-listening space but is used for reproduction rather than recording.' WHERE id='5e9a09f1-c6bb-48d0-a3fe-ee9ec637888d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording facility set up in a private residence, ranging from a modest project setup to a well-equipped room, typically based on a digital audio workstation and interface with nearfield monitoring, in which the domestic space imposes acoustic and level constraints addressed through treatment and monitoring choices.' WHERE id='8ee6981a-300c-4c8a-b7e0-8925b9d516d7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The practice of saving successive, separately numbered copies of a session as work proceeds, rather than overwriting a single file, so that earlier states are preserved and can be reverted to if a later version is corrupted or an earlier arrangement is needed. Digital audio workstations provide a save-as-new-version function that increments the file name for this purpose.' WHERE id='11669f3f-7ec2-419f-a38a-c01bd94731da' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the background sound within an enclosed space, capturing the room''s characteristic tone, reverberation, and low-level activity as a sustained bed used to convey an indoor setting and to distinguish interior from exterior acoustics.' WHERE id='06aa063e-0b5e-4461-956c-5cad8acafca2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The checking that the International Standard Recording Code embedded in or delivered with each recording is present, correctly formed, and correct for that recording. An ISRC is a twelve-character identifier comprising a five-character prefix of a country code plus a registrant code, a two-digit year of reference, and a five-digit designation code, uniquely identifying a specific sound or music-video recording; verification confirms each track carries its unique, valid code before release.' WHERE id='5c6ffe33-cb71-47bb-8471-ae33580670fe' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The complete set of finished masters, artwork, and metadata assembled and formatted to a record label''s specification for hand-off, containing the audio deliverables together with the credits and identifiers needed to catalogue, manufacture, and release the material.' WHERE id='f19f90a0-c36b-48d2-ac30-60eff3713e15' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A room used for the controlled reproduction and evaluation of audio, in which loudspeaker placement, listener position, and acoustic treatment are arranged to provide accurate, reliable monitoring for judging recordings and mixes. Standardised listening-room characteristics support consistent subjective assessment.' WHERE id='0c572c56-ac2a-447e-8cbe-9b9505d26ba4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Communication between the control room and performers in the live room during a session, carried by the talkback and headphone cue system so instructions and cues pass between engineer, producer, and musicians while they are acoustically isolated in the recording space.' WHERE id='632e3b22-22f5-468d-ace3-7918966ea93e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The planned timetable for delivering and setting up equipment at a venue or studio before a session or event, specifying when gear arrives and the order and timing of getting it in and rigged, so that setup is coordinated and complete before recording or performance begins.' WHERE id='d781ce6d-e339-4ea9-af2d-0ad116f48f72' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The archiving of a project''s session data and media for durable, future retrieval, addressing stable storage media, redundant copies, open or documented formats, and complete metadata so the material remains readable and the project can be reopened years later despite technological change. It applies preservation practice to recording sessions.' WHERE id='101b578d-cdf8-4f47-bccc-ad1569d16cf3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The transfer of the approved final master, with its associated documentation and metadata, to the next party in the chain — such as the label, manufacturer, or distributor — as the authoritative source for manufacturing or release, confirming that the correct final version and its specifications are passed on.' WHERE id='981f8b4d-5121-41c5-8419-b871b536f0cb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Written notes accompanying material sent to or produced during mastering that convey the client''s or engineer''s instructions and observations — reference levels, requested tonal or loudness targets, sequencing, spacing, and any issues — so the mastering engineer understands the intent and the delivery requirements.' WHERE id='01c17ff4-f540-4507-9d74-48302b4bc3e2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A room designed and acoustically treated for mastering, providing an especially neutral, full-range, and accurate monitoring environment so that fine spectral and level decisions applied to a final master can be judged reliably; it typically represents the most acoustically controlled listening space in a facility.' WHERE id='0b10a42d-0b68-4c0d-8ad4-07ee1e2919be' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The checking that the descriptive and technical metadata attached to deliverables — such as track and artist names, credits, identifiers like ISRC and UPC, and technical parameters — is present, correctly formatted, and accurate before release, so the information carried with the files meets the delivery specification.' WHERE id='3ee7e2d3-5a29-4d06-8c37-34952feed723' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Confirmation that microphones and their signal paths are in consistent absolute polarity, so a positive acoustic pressure produces a consistent-polarity electrical signal across channels and combined or multi-microphone sources sum correctly rather than partially cancelling. It typically checks cabling and any polarity switches to avoid phase-related loss, especially when several microphones capture one source.' WHERE id='ef26f5a6-1f7f-47e3-a03a-9ef981298442' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The transfer of completed mixes, usually with stems and documentation, from the mixing stage to the next party such as the mastering engineer, delivering the approved mix files in the required formats and levels together with the notes needed to master or further process them.' WHERE id='5e5794ec-a4f0-4670-a03d-33b17f4acfda' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A room designed and treated for mixing, with monitor placement, listening position, and acoustics controlled to give an accurate, neutral response so that balance, panning, dynamics, and spectral decisions translate reliably to other systems. It is the control-room environment in which mix decisions are made.' WHERE id='15dad724-93c7-42d4-97ab-4b71994e451b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The backing up of recorded session data to portable storage while working on location or away from the studio, creating redundant copies of the captured material in the field so recordings are protected before they reach permanent storage.' WHERE id='4db9c112-2dbb-409b-aeee-1f7e6095e56f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The organised way of working when recording or producing away from a fixed facility, covering transport and setup of portable equipment, power and signal management on location, capture, redundant backup, logging, and safe transfer of material back to the studio. It adapts studio workflow to the constraints of location work.' WHERE id='276a320f-d16d-4b99-9cb9-81c58400dd59' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transportable rack housing recording equipment — interface or preamplifiers, recorder, and supporting gear — assembled and protected for transport so a recording chain can be quickly deployed on location. It packages the core recording signal path into a portable, rugged unit.' WHERE id='c3d461b7-f1f7-4854-9e71-b7499ab58d9b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The scheduled time at which a musician is required to arrive at a session, set on the session plan so that personnel are present when needed and tracking can proceed on schedule. It is a basic element of session scheduling and documentation.' WHERE id='0c38dd4b-3ced-4c75-82d7-46943522076c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the background sound of a natural environment, free of obvious man-made noise, capturing the characteristic sounds of nature as a sustained atmosphere used to convey an outdoor natural setting and provide a background bed.' WHERE id='f887d266-45f6-4ee1-9b92-f96e68c7d613' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A remote input and output unit that connects microphones and lines to a mixing system over a digital audio network, converting audio to and from a networked digital format and carrying many channels bidirectionally on standard network cabling using an audio-over-IP protocol. It extends console I/O to the stage or location over a single network link.' WHERE id='5a4bbc69-9faf-41c1-b094-aae18d42d395' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the characteristic background sound of a location at night — reduced activity, nocturnal natural sound, and a generally lower and different ambient tone than daytime — captured as a sustained atmosphere and logged as a time-of-day variant to convey a night setting.' WHERE id='3eff911d-d326-44ec-a8bc-469be95d82c4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the background sound of the sea and shore — the continuous wash of waves and related coastal sound — captured as a sustained atmosphere used to convey a seaside or ocean setting and provide a background bed.' WHERE id='ea6f3652-ef11-4942-bee8-c3d48ba0e2f4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the background sound of an office interior — the low hum of equipment, ventilation, and muted activity — captured as a sustained atmosphere used to convey a workplace interior setting and provide a background bed.' WHERE id='d2a72594-9b35-476c-896d-198c82be9cc6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The records that capture the settings of outboard, external hardware processors used in a session — the control positions and patching of each unit — so their exact configuration can be reproduced when the session is rebuilt, since such hardware often cannot store or recall its own state.' WHERE id='4491f348-c8b0-4113-9c7a-4d7d6561d2e3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A form on which the settings of outboard processors are written down or annotated on unit diagrams — every control position, switch, and patch — before teardown, providing the manual record needed to reset external hardware to its previous state for recall.' WHERE id='c03db13b-7340-44d2-baba-d4cbe6f28878' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording space used for overdubbing — adding parts while monitoring previously recorded material — often a smaller isolated room or booth that provides separation and a controlled acoustic suited to capturing individual parts against playback.' WHERE id='8e275941-a17a-49b5-a9e1-acce1764b684' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A field recording of the background sound of a park or green urban open space — birds, foliage, distant activity, and people — captured as a sustained atmosphere used to convey a park setting and provide a background bed.' WHERE id='49aa3b40-006a-44c0-a679-7a12c5cd62f4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A direction to re-record a specific portion of a performance — a phrase, line, or section — to replace or supplement earlier material, logged so the new pickup take can be dropped into or comped with the existing recording. It denotes a targeted partial re-record rather than a full new take.' WHERE id='62b41ef0-2198-4259-ac9b-9f8ba376f3a4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The records or stored data that capture the plug-in processors instantiated in a session and their parameter settings, so the processing can be reproduced when the session is reopened, including noting plug-in names, versions, and presets where the settings are not otherwise embedded in the session file.' WHERE id='5fd8cf07-f802-4d7b-9ede-e922dea10c3d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A plug-in recall sheet is a session documentation record that captures the identity and parameter settings of every signal-processing plug-in used on a track or mix so that the exact processing chain can be reconstructed later. It typically lists, per channel or insert slot, the plug-in name and version, the preset in use, and the key control values (or a screenshot of the interface), together with insert order and any routing notes. It supplements the DAW''s own saved session by providing a human-readable, portable reference for recalling a setup on a different system, revisiting a mix after time has passed, or matching processing across revisions.' WHERE id='0a7d26ce-b2b0-455a-8b8a-ad620b302f66' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A portable clock generator is a compact, often battery-capable master word-clock device that distributes a stable digital timing reference to the converters and digital equipment of a mobile or location recording rig. By supplying a common sample clock, it forces all interconnected digital devices to lock to one timebase, preventing the sample slips, clicks, and drift that occur when units free-run on their independent internal clocks. It is used where a fixed studio clock is unavailable, providing the low-jitter reference needed for reliable multi-device digital capture in the field.' WHERE id='11aa5be0-fc2d-4cf3-852b-1a7d0275016e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A portable multitrack recorder is a self-contained, transportable device that captures several independent audio channels simultaneously to internal media, combining microphone/line preamps, analogue-to-digital conversion, and multichannel storage in one unit for use away from a fixed studio. It records each input as a discrete track, commonly as Broadcast WAV files, so individual sources can be balanced and edited later, and typically provides metering, headphone monitoring, timecode or word-clock options, and battery operation. Such recorders are the core capture tool for location music, film-sound, and remote sessions.' WHERE id='fa1b7fcb-6fe6-4803-80f3-8fb556f0b963' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A portable rackmount recorder is a multichannel recording unit built to standard 19-inch rack dimensions but intended for transport within a portable rack or flight case for remote and location work. It provides the same discrete multitrack capture, conversion, and monitoring as a studio recorder while being ruggedised and integrated with other rack gear such as preamps, converters, and clocking, so setup and teardown can be handled as a single mobile system. The rackmount form allows it to be wired permanently into a remote-recording rig and moved as one protected assembly.' WHERE id='1ff8df56-35d2-47f9-887c-532edc04d6f7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A portable recording workstation is a transportable, integrated system that brings the recording, monitoring, and often editing capability of a studio into a movable package, typically pairing a computer running a DAW (or a self-contained recorder) with a multichannel interface, preamps, and monitoring in a case or compact rack. It lets an engineer track, review, and sometimes mix multitrack audio on location or at a temporary site with studio-comparable signal flow. The emphasis is on a complete, self-sufficient capture-and-review chain that can be deployed and struck quickly.' WHERE id='bf46428f-dab6-485f-94f5-c9e0914e8527' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A portable stagebox is a transportable input/output interface unit placed near the sound sources that concentrates multiple microphone and line connections and carries them back to a console or recorder over a single multicore or digital (networked) link. In a recording or remote context it provides remotely controllable preamps and analogue-to-digital conversion at the stage, reducing long analogue cable runs and simplifying setup by consolidating many channels into one rugged, movable box. It is the field counterpart to a fixed stage panel, enabling rapid deployment of a large channel count.' WHERE id='bd0221c8-3ecf-44eb-ab0c-2155732c611e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A project archival package is the organised, self-contained collection of files and documentation assembled at the end of a project so the work can be reliably stored and fully reopened or reconstructed in the future. Following recognised archival guidance, it gathers the native session/project files, all referenced audio at its original resolution (consolidated, with a fallback set of flat standard-format files), and supporting documentation such as track sheets, recall and instrument notes, and session metadata, typically written to preservation-grade media in verified copies. Its purpose is long-term recoverability independent of the original system or software.' WHERE id='b193e0f8-21f4-4d6a-87fe-82ea142a72f1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Project closeout is the final administrative and technical phase of a production in which all deliverables are completed, verified, and handed over, and the project is formally concluded. It covers confirming that mixes, masters, and required files meet the agreed specification, delivering them with accompanying documentation and metadata, reconciling paperwork such as credits, session logs, and asset lists, and archiving or backing up the project before releasing studio resources. The goal is an orderly end state in which nothing outstanding remains and the project can be reopened from a known, documented condition.' WHERE id='f5971e4b-4f8a-4849-8c5b-0affa8fb5800' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A project studio is a smaller, personally owned recording facility, typically built around a DAW and a modest control environment, used by an artist, producer, or engineer to record and produce their own or others'' projects outside a large commercial studio. Made viable by affordable digital recording and processing, it concentrates on writing, tracking, overdubbing, and mixing for specific projects rather than operating as a full-service commercial room, though its results can be release-quality. The term distinguishes this owner-operated, project-focused setup from both the professional commercial studio and the casual home studio.' WHERE id='43475820-461a-43a3-b344-2428da4caa81' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A publisher delivery package is the set of finished assets and documentation supplied to a music publisher or licensing administrator so a work can be registered, exploited, and paid out correctly. Alongside the approved audio masters and any required alternate versions or stems, it carries the metadata and paperwork the publisher needs, such as song titles and writer/publisher shares, standard identifiers, lyrics, and instrumental or edited versions for synchronisation use. Its purpose is to give the publisher everything required to catalogue, license, and administer royalties for the work.' WHERE id='83568ed1-7750-44d0-8f2d-6aa74b17c337' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Rain ambience is an ambience (atmosphere) recording that captures the continuous background sound of rainfall in a location, used to establish a sense of place and weather beneath dialogue, music, or picture. Like other ambience or atmos recordings, it is captured as a sustained, event-free wash rather than as a specific hard effect, often in stereo or surround and looped or layered in post, and its character depends on the surface the rain strikes and the presence of drips, gusts, or distant thunder. It functions as a background bed that fills a scene with environmental tone.' WHERE id='6a96e5d5-bfa5-4975-8f72-3ebdc40cfdc2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording brief is the concise planning document that sets out the objectives, requirements, and constraints of a recording session or project before work begins. It typically states the intended deliverables and their specification, the material to be recorded, the artistic and technical goals, the personnel, schedule, and budget, and any references or client expectations, so that everyone involved shares a common understanding of what the session must achieve. It guides preparation and provides the yardstick against which the finished recording is judged.' WHERE id='46a9be2e-9eef-4ab6-8ffb-f4dfa82066d3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording log update is the act of adding new entries to the running session log as recording proceeds, keeping the written record of the session current. The recording log documents each take and event, including time, track assignments, take numbers, and comments on performance and technical quality, along with which takes are marked as keepers; updating it means capturing this information take by take so the log accurately reflects what was recorded. Maintaining the log in real time supports later editing, comping, and recall by preserving an authoritative account of the session.' WHERE id='a822b23d-4cc0-4de7-a5f6-1c0fe06086f1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording pass log is a session document that records each recording pass, a single run-through or take of a part, together with its identifying number and notes on the result. For every pass it typically notes the take number, the section or range covered, technical settings or issues, and the engineer''s or producer''s comments on usability, so that passes can be compared and the best material identified during review and comping. It is a granular form of take documentation focused on successive attempts at the same material.' WHERE id='061a92b4-1f07-4060-b954-b7509dbc4f4e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording readiness review is a pre-session check confirming that everything required to begin recording is in place and functioning before the session starts. It verifies signal flow and monitoring, input assignments and levels, recorder/DAW configuration, sample rate and clocking, available storage and backup, headphone and cue feeds, and session documentation, so that faults are found and corrected in advance rather than during valuable session time. It is the technical go/no-go step that ensures the session can proceed reliably from the downbeat.' WHERE id='ab4b72ac-4622-4ac5-a416-cc82cd74d23d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A redundant recording strategy is a planned approach in which audio is captured simultaneously on more than one recorder or medium so that a hardware failure, media error, or dropout on one path does not result in lost material. It commonly involves feeding a duplicate signal to a second recorder (a mirror or safety machine) or writing to two media at once, and it defines how the paths are configured, clocked, and verified. The strategy is standard where a performance cannot be repeated, providing a fallback recording as insurance against failure of the primary capture.' WHERE id='b1bef826-12ba-448d-9833-3de130aa39b0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Redundant recording verification is the process of confirming that a redundant or backup recording was actually captured correctly and is complete and usable, rather than assuming the safety path succeeded. It involves checking that the second recorder ran for the full duration, that files exist at the expected length and format, and that the audio is present and free of dropouts or corruption, ideally by auditioning or comparing against the primary. Performing this check, often before the setup is struck, ensures the redundancy provides genuine protection rather than a false sense of security.' WHERE id='83907ce4-f80e-424c-ae2b-ba5f876c738d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A redundant remote recorder is a secondary recording device deployed at a remote or location session to capture a simultaneous backup of the same audio as the primary recorder. Fed a parallel signal and clocked to the same reference, it runs in tandem so its files can substitute for the primary if that machine fails, its media errors, or a dropout occurs. It is the physical embodiment of a redundant recording strategy in a remote context, providing an independent safety copy of an unrepeatable performance.' WHERE id='39dfabc5-306d-4949-bfe1-5785159245d0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A reference listening room is an acoustically controlled space designed and calibrated so that recordings can be evaluated on a known, neutral monitoring standard. It combines treated room acoustics, defined loudspeaker placement and listening geometry, and calibrated monitors and playback level so that the sound heard is representative and repeatable, allowing critical judgements about balance, tone, and processing to translate reliably to other systems. Such rooms follow recognised recommendations for listening-room acoustics and monitor alignment used in professional evaluation and mastering.' WHERE id='ba8588af-b4f1-43d9-9849-efc7d22e7890' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Remote backup verification is the process of confirming that data backed up at, or transferred from, a remote or location session has copied completely and is intact and restorable. It involves checking that all expected files are present at the correct size and format on the backup medium or destination, validating copies against the source (for example by checksum or by re-reading the data), and confirming the backup can be read back before the originals are cleared or the site is struck. It guards against silent copy failures that would otherwise be discovered only when the material is needed.' WHERE id='55011180-ca84-45a9-bb5c-c9cb7001ced1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A remote mix position is a mixing location set up away from the sound sources or main control room, such as a truck, a separate room, or a spot within a venue, from which the engineer monitors and balances the audio. It provides the console or control surface, monitoring, and communications needed to mix while the performers, stage, or capture equipment are elsewhere, relying on a multicore, network, or transmission link to carry signals to and from the source. It is used in remote broadcast, location recording, and large-venue work where mixing at the source is impractical.' WHERE id='a27942a4-3db9-41bf-8723-0f77a0298a2f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A remote recording engineer is the engineer responsible for capturing multitrack audio at a location or event away from a fixed studio, typically from a mobile rig or recording truck. The role covers planning and specifying the capture, setting up and clocking the recorders and inputs, obtaining a split of the source signals, monitoring and documenting the recording, and ensuring reliable, backed-up files, often under the time and access constraints of a live or on-location session. It combines studio recording skill with the logistics and redundancy demands of working outside the studio.' WHERE id='0330e87f-8cb3-4865-b787-733a72948433' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A remote recording rig is the assembled, transportable set of equipment used to capture multitrack audio on location, comprising the recorders, preamps, converters, clocking, monitoring, power, and interconnection needed to work as a self-contained system. Built into cases or a portable rack for transport and rapid deployment, it takes a split or feed of the source signals and records them reliably away from a studio, usually with backup or redundant paths. The term denotes the whole coordinated capture system rather than any single device.' WHERE id='2c000fee-73db-4b7e-8d87-3b9b70438bff' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A remote recording workflow is the defined sequence of procedures for capturing audio on location, spanning pre-session planning, setup and verification, capture with monitoring and documentation, and post-session backup and handoff. It specifies how signals are obtained and split, how recorders are configured, clocked, and made redundant, how takes are logged, and how files are verified, copied, and delivered, so that a mobile session proceeds reliably despite the constraints of working outside a studio. It is the organising process that ties the remote rig and personnel into a dependable capture operation.' WHERE id='13bf63f2-841e-4546-aa30-047e569a9fe8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Remote signal verification is the check, performed at a remote or location setup, that every intended source is present, correctly routed, and arriving at the recorder at a healthy level and quality before recording begins. Working through the input list, the engineer confirms channel by channel that each microphone or line feed produces signal on the expected channel with correct gain, phantom power, and polarity, and without hum, noise, or dead lines. It is the location equivalent of a line check, isolating wiring and routing faults in advance so the capture is clean and complete.' WHERE id='b097daca-1a2c-494c-8a35-9a1a3b05edf4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A remote synchronization check is the verification, at a location or multi-device setup, that all recording and playback devices are locked to a common timing reference so their audio, and any picture, stays aligned. It confirms that every digital device shares one word-clock or sync source and, where timecode is used, that machines are reading and agreeing on the same timecode, so that multitrack files, cameras, or a second recorder can be conformed together without drift or offset. It prevents synchronisation errors that would otherwise appear only when the material is combined in post.' WHERE id='f4c552ef-0685-4f61-9f35-fb5443c3382f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A remote systems technician is the crew member responsible for the technical infrastructure of a remote or location production, setting up, interconnecting, and maintaining the recording, signal-distribution, synchronisation, power, and communications systems and troubleshooting faults in the field. Distinct from the mixing or recording engineer, this role concentrates on making the whole signal and support system work reliably, including cabling and splits, clocking and timecode distribution, networking, and redundancy, so the capture or broadcast proceeds without technical failure. The position is common on mobile-truck and large remote operations.' WHERE id='eb0e5a2b-47d4-443a-8ecf-3234f07fe3cf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Restaurant ambience is an ambience recording that captures the characteristic background sound of a restaurant interior, the blend of overlapping conversation (walla), clinking crockery and cutlery, movement, and room tone, used to place a scene in such an environment. Recorded as a sustained, non-specific atmosphere rather than distinct events, it is laid under dialogue or picture as a background bed and may be captured in stereo or surround and looped or layered in post. Its density and crowd character are chosen to suggest the size and busyness of the establishment.' WHERE id='021578b2-a1d6-4801-bc0b-0565155934c8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A retake request is a documented or spoken instruction to record a part or section again because the previous take was judged unsatisfactory. Arising during recording or on later review, it identifies the material to be redone and usually the reason, whether a performance error, a technical fault, or a desired change, so that a new take can be captured and tracked against the earlier ones in the take log. It is the mechanism by which unacceptable takes are flagged for replacement within the session workflow.' WHERE id='8501bb23-e1d4-4f95-a480-88a664cb6b12' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Review playback is the act of playing back recorded material during or after a session so that the performers, producer, and engineer can evaluate it and decide whether it is acceptable or needs another take. It provides the critical-listening step in which takes are judged for performance and technical quality, compared against alternatives, and marked as keepers or candidates for retake or comping. Conducted on the room''s monitoring, it is the feedback point around which tracking decisions are made.' WHERE id='026f85eb-9d3d-4258-a921-edf091dc906c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Revision management is the systematic tracking and control of successive versions of a project or its deliverables so that changes are recorded, identifiable, and reversible. In a recording context it means labelling and saving distinct versions of sessions, mixes, or edits with clear version identifiers and notes on what changed, retaining prior versions, and knowing which is current and approved, so that a client''s requested changes can be applied and, if necessary, earlier states restored. It prevents confusion between drafts and protects against loss of a preferred earlier version.' WHERE id='eb26537a-2659-4f4a-a95f-f0012a0bc741' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='River ambience is an ambience recording that captures the continuous background sound of a river or flowing water at a location, used to establish a natural setting beneath dialogue, music, or picture. Recorded as a sustained, event-free atmosphere rather than a specific effect, its character depends on the speed and volume of the water, the proximity of the microphones, and incidental elements such as wildlife or wind, and it is looped or layered in post as a background bed. It provides the environmental tone of a waterside location.' WHERE id='043316af-9662-4206-b53d-63799c5cee60' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Room ambience is the characteristic background sound and acoustic signature of an enclosed space captured with no intentional action occurring, comprising the room''s inherent noise floor and reverberant character. Often called room tone, it is recorded at each location so editors have a matching bed to fill pauses, smooth edits between takes, and maintain a continuous, natural sense of the space under dialogue or performance. Because every room sounds subtly different, capturing its ambience is a standard part of location and studio documentation.' WHERE id='f9618e58-ba23-4c26-a713-7f48f9ab90ad' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A ruggedized recording system is a recording setup built or specified to withstand harsh operating conditions such as shock, vibration, dust, moisture, and temperature extremes, so that it captures reliably in field, industrial, or adverse environments. Ruggedisation typically includes protective enclosures, sealed connectors and controls, shock-mounting, and components rated for wider environmental tolerances, trading some size or cost for durability and dependability where a standard studio system would be at risk. The emphasis is on maintaining recording integrity under physical and environmental stress.' WHERE id='340c1391-2f11-4640-97fa-a23e4ccde3d4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Rural ambience is an ambience recording that captures the background atmosphere of a countryside or agricultural setting, typically a quiet, open soundfield of birdsong, insects, wind through vegetation, and sparse, distant human or animal activity. Recorded as a sustained, event-free wash rather than specific effects, it is used to establish a rural sense of place beneath dialogue or picture and is looped or layered in post as a background bed. Its low density and natural quiet distinguish it from urban atmospheres.' WHERE id='a8f630d7-feb1-4240-aec4-4bf7deae5f68' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='School ambience is an ambience recording that captures the characteristic background sound of a school environment, such as the murmur and voices of children, corridor or classroom activity, and playground crowd noise, used to place a scene in an educational setting. Recorded as a sustained atmosphere rather than distinct events, it is laid under dialogue or picture as a background bed and may be captured in stereo or surround and looped or layered in post. Its crowd character and setting cues are chosen to suggest the type and part of the school.' WHERE id='8147997e-17e3-4676-88cb-6a14ba719d48' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A scoring stage is a large, acoustically designed recording studio built specifically for recording musical scores to picture, typically for film, television, or games, with room enough to accommodate a full orchestra and to display synchronised picture for the players and conductor. It combines a sizeable, favourably reverberant live area with isolation, a control room, and provision for click tracks, streamers and punches, and timecode synchronisation so that music can be performed and captured in time with the visuals. Scoring stages are among the largest recording rooms and are dedicated to media-music production.' WHERE id='30be5539-f64a-421c-ae2f-20a40ea01865' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Session archive verification is the process of confirming that an archived session is complete, intact, and restorable after it has been written to its preservation medium. It checks that all session files, referenced media at full resolution, and supporting documentation are present, that copies match the source (for example via checksums or a test restore), and that the archive can actually be reopened, so the material is genuinely preserved rather than assumed safe. It is the validation step that gives an archive its reliability.' WHERE id='7737e61d-9bcb-4639-93d6-a2c8e8043e8f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A session backup strategy is the defined plan for copying session data during and after a project so that work is protected against loss from hardware failure, media error, or accident. It specifies what is backed up, how often, to how many copies and media, where those copies are kept (including off-site or otherwise redundant locations), and how backups are verified, commonly following the principle of keeping multiple independent copies. The strategy ensures that at any point the session can be recovered from a recent, validated copy.' WHERE id='411e2e85-e611-42ff-a5e3-2e85b8724dc5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A session briefing is the pre-session meeting or communication in which the producer, engineer, and participants are informed of the plan, goals, and requirements for the day''s work. It covers what will be recorded, the running order and schedule, the technical setup and responsibilities, and any special requirements or references, so that everyone begins with a shared understanding and the session runs efficiently. It aligns the team before work starts.' WHERE id='ef30e498-f2a7-4af7-ac5f-faa189814cd6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Session continuity is the maintenance of consistent technical and creative conditions across a recording session or between sessions so that separately recorded material matches and edits together seamlessly. It involves preserving microphone choice and placement, the signal chain and its settings, levels, and room and instrument setup, together with the documentation of these, from one take, day, or location to the next, so that overdubs and later sessions align tonally and sonically with earlier work. Good continuity, supported by thorough session documentation, prevents audible mismatches when parts are combined.' WHERE id='1fb0c301-0375-4480-8440-49bb3564fc28' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A session debrief is the review held after a session in which the team discusses how it went, what was accomplished, and what remains or needs attention. It captures which takes were kept, outstanding issues or fixes, and technical notes, feeding these into the session documentation and the planning for subsequent work. It closes the session with a shared record of outcomes and follow-up actions.' WHERE id='7b25d992-a9dd-4e9c-b769-3e846b7f8d85' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A session handoff is the transfer of a project from one engineer, team, or facility to another, together with the files and documentation needed to continue the work seamlessly. It provides the current session in a known state, the associated media and versions, and the notes, such as track and channel naming, recall sheets, and edit and mix status, along with outstanding tasks, so the receiving party can pick up without loss of information or having to reconstruct the setup. Thorough handoff documentation is what makes a project portable between people and systems.' WHERE id='48b5ffce-b822-4a24-b3af-79150730028b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A session milestone save is a deliberately preserved copy of a session at a significant, named point in its progress, kept as a stable reference distinct from ongoing incremental saves. Made at junctures such as the end of tracking, an approved edit, or a client-approved mix, it captures a known-good state that can be returned to if later work must be undone or compared, forming part of an orderly version history. It provides recoverable checkpoints across the life of a project.' WHERE id='eaaec9de-da32-4ab9-83b1-027d1e929758' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A session recall package is the collected information needed to reopen and reconstruct a session exactly as it was, especially on a different system or after time has passed. It brings together the session/project file, its referenced media, and the documentation of the setup, including track sheets, input and patch lists, plug-in and outboard recall sheets, and instrument or microphone notes, so that the mix or setup can be recalled faithfully rather than approximated. It underpins revisions, transfers, and archival recall.' WHERE id='196afafe-240c-4a96-b507-380f86f98ece' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A session wrap report is the summary document completed at the end of a session, recording what was done and the state in which the project is left. It typically lists the material recorded and the kept takes, the technical setup and any issues, media and backup status, personnel and time, and outstanding tasks, providing an authoritative record for the client, the next session, and the project file. It is the formal close-out note for a day''s or project''s work.' WHERE id='cb6e46e1-98eb-47be-8f7d-ff16fd855711' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A shift change is the handover between crews or operators during a session or operation that runs longer than one team''s working period, transferring responsibility and the current technical and creative state to the incoming personnel. To preserve continuity it relies on documentation and briefing, covering the status of the recording, the settings in use, kept takes, and outstanding tasks, so the new shift can continue without loss of information or consistency. It is the personnel counterpart to session continuity in extended or around-the-clock work.' WHERE id='cedf43e7-9fe4-49ff-94b9-2513d5baf415' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Storm ambience is an ambience recording that captures the turbulent background atmosphere of a storm, typically a blend of gusting and howling wind, driving rain, and distant or near thunder, used to establish severe weather beneath dialogue or picture. Recorded as a sustained, evolving wash rather than as isolated effects, its intensity and the balance of its elements convey the strength and proximity of the storm, and it is looped or layered in post as a dramatic background bed. It provides the environmental tone of inclement weather.' WHERE id='63a30712-8b96-400f-aa75-712ce0515caf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Studio call time is the scheduled time at which musicians, crew, and other participants are required to arrive and be ready at the studio for a session. Borrowed from wider production practice, it sets the start reference around which setup, downbeat, and the session schedule are planned, and against which booked or union hours may be counted. Communicating an accurate call time ensures the session begins on schedule with everyone present and prepared.' WHERE id='3aea1d9b-a1f6-4344-a1d7-cae6829a5fc7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A studio documentation package is the assembled set of written and data records that describe how a session or project was recorded and how it should be reopened or reproduced. It commonly includes track sheets, input and patch lists, microphone and instrument notes, signal-chain and outboard settings, plug-in recall sheets, session logs, and metadata, gathered so that the technical state of the work is fully documented for recall, handoff, delivery, or archive. It is the reference record that accompanies the audio.' WHERE id='5aeb7a75-4df0-4628-992b-7a6056469276' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A studio lockout is an arrangement in which a client books a studio exclusively for a continuous period, typically around the clock over consecutive days, so the room and its setup remain reserved to them alone for the duration. Because no other clients use the room, equipment, patching, and instrument and microphone setups can be left in place between sessions, preserving continuity and saving setup time across a project. Lockouts are common for album projects that need sustained, uninterrupted access.' WHERE id='044e2dc2-733f-4067-9ee3-a297525dbf35' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A studio lounge is a dedicated non-technical space within a recording facility where artists and clients can rest, socialise, and take breaks away from the control and recording rooms. As part of the facility''s ancillary spaces, it supports the comfort and workflow of a session by providing a relaxed area for downtime, refreshment, and informal discussion during long recording days. It is a standard amenity in professional studio design.' WHERE id='bd155b4c-2a05-479c-b57c-daf7e70622a5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Subway ambience is an ambience recording that captures the characteristic background sound of an underground railway environment, such as arriving and departing trains, crowd movement and footsteps, announcements, and the hard reverberation of tunnels and platforms, used to place a scene in a metro setting. Recorded as a sustained atmosphere rather than isolated effects, it is laid under dialogue or picture as a background bed and looped or layered in post. Its reverberant, mechanical character conveys the enclosed, transit environment.' WHERE id='7920cb00-3cc4-4fb6-81e2-79282cbb08e1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A tone bed is a sustained, low-level tonal layer laid continuously under a scene or program segment to provide an unobtrusive atmospheric foundation. As a form of audio bed, a background element over which other sounds sit, it consists of a held, often droning or ambient tone rather than distinct events, giving continuity and mood beneath dialogue, effects, or narration. It is placed and looped in post to fill the sonic space without drawing attention.' WHERE id='0c459c47-1e5e-4061-923a-ed4b13912542' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A track naming convention is an agreed, consistent scheme for labelling the tracks and channels of a session so that their contents are immediately identifiable and portable between people and systems. It standardises how each track is named, whether by instrument, source, microphone, or role, often with take or version indicators and a consistent ordering, so that sessions can be understood, edited, handed off, and delivered without ambiguity. Recognised delivery guidance stresses clear, consistent track naming as essential session documentation.' WHERE id='e0118afa-d29e-40b5-b9f9-2613152f1c6d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Track sequencing verification is the check that the running order of tracks on an album or program, and their spacing, transitions, and levels between items, are correct and as approved before the master is finalised. It confirms the sequence matches the agreed order, that gaps, fades, crossfades, and any segues are right, and that start and end points and timings are correct, so the assembled program plays back in the intended order and flow. It is a quality check on the assembled sequence prior to delivery.' WHERE id='d392c515-f0c8-4e94-a883-d8bb429c1ee0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Traffic ambience is an ambience recording that captures the continuous background sound of road traffic, the blend of passing vehicles, engines, tyres, and distant horns that characterises a roadside or urban thoroughfare. Recorded as a sustained wash rather than as individual vehicle passes, it is used to establish an urban or roadside sense of place beneath dialogue or picture and is looped or layered in post as a background bed. Its density and speed cues suggest the type and busyness of the road.' WHERE id='eb055b05-c66b-47af-a637-ecfc6017142f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Train ambience is an ambience recording that captures the characteristic background sound of trains or a railway environment, which may include the rhythm of a moving carriage interior, passing trains, and the crowd and announcements of a station, used to place a scene in a rail setting. Recorded as a sustained atmosphere rather than isolated effects, it is laid under dialogue or picture as a background bed and looped or layered in post. Its mechanical, rhythmic character conveys the rail environment.' WHERE id='b27b6b04-fe2f-4e33-8d3a-d22583dc8c5f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A UPS-backed recording system is a recording setup powered through an uninterruptible power supply so that a mains failure or disturbance does not interrupt or corrupt a recording. The UPS supplies battery-backed power that carries the system through outages and sags long enough to continue or safely conclude a take and shut down cleanly, while also helping to condition the supply against noise and surges. It protects unrepeatable recordings and open session files from the data loss and equipment stress that abrupt power loss would cause.' WHERE id='f38eb28c-b316-4997-8a21-369a861aad5b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Urban ambience is an ambience recording that captures the general background atmosphere of a city environment, a layered blend of distant traffic, crowd and street activity, and mechanical hum that together convey a built-up setting. Recorded as a sustained, non-specific wash rather than distinct events, it is used to establish an urban sense of place beneath dialogue or picture and is looped or layered in post as a background bed. Its density and constituent elements are chosen to suggest the character and part of the city.' WHERE id='2c2bb922-adb3-4d94-ad51-b4acebcbcb69' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A versioned backup is a backup method that retains multiple dated or numbered copies of a project''s data over time rather than only the latest state, so that any of several previous versions can be restored. By keeping successive generations of the session and its files, it protects against not only media failure but also corruption, mistaken edits, or unwanted changes that a single overwriting backup would simply propagate, allowing recovery of a known-good earlier version. It is a core element of a robust session backup and archival strategy.' WHERE id='e7faa675-273b-4ba8-b1c4-9edad1e8fe57' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A weatherproof recording rig is a location recording setup protected against rain, moisture, wind, and dust so that it can operate reliably in exposed outdoor conditions. Protection typically combines sealed or environmentally rated enclosures, covers and rain protection for recorders and electronics, and wind and moisture shielding for microphones, keeping water and particulates out of sensitive components while preserving signal integrity. It allows recording to continue in adverse weather that would otherwise threaten both the equipment and the capture.' WHERE id='d7f628bb-6820-4b62-abc3-de1720a7ccc5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Wild ambience is background atmosphere recorded wild, that is, captured independently and not synchronised to any picture or specific action, to provide environmental sound for use in post-production. As a wild (non-sync) recording, it is a sustained atmosphere of a location gathered on its own so editors can lay, loop, and layer it beneath scenes, matching the sense of place without being tied to the moment it was recorded. The term joins the established wild, or non-synchronous, recording practice with ambience capture.' WHERE id='592c5cbb-1b37-47ef-aacc-c8e55128ebe1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Wilderness ambience is an ambience recording that captures the background atmosphere of a remote natural environment largely free of human activity, typically a quiet soundfield of wind, distant wildlife, water, or foliage against a very low noise floor. Recorded as a sustained, event-free wash, it is used to establish a natural, untouched sense of place beneath dialogue or picture and is looped or layered in post as a background bed. Its natural quiet and absence of human-made sound distinguish it from rural or urban atmospheres.' WHERE id='b7382436-69d4-464f-8855-d6c37cf21d62' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Wind ambience is an ambience recording that captures the continuous background sound of wind at a location, from a soft breeze to strong gusts moving through trees, structures, or open ground. Because wind striking a microphone directly produces low-frequency rumble rather than the sound of the wind itself, such recordings rely on effective wind protection and capture the wind''s effect on the surroundings; the result is used as a sustained atmosphere and is looped or layered in post as a background bed. It conveys the exposure and mood of a windy setting.' WHERE id='bfd92ea0-7d2d-4034-88fd-5958e863e9d6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A writing room is a space in a studio or facility set aside for songwriting, composition, and pre-production rather than full tracking, typically equipped with a modest recording and production setup for capturing ideas. It gives writers and producers a comfortable, creatively focused environment in which to develop songs and arrangements and record demos and sketches that may later be tracked in a main room. Many commercial and project facilities include such writer rooms to support the early, compositional stage of a project.' WHERE id='3538abda-79f8-4dea-b38e-62bae4300b0c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A plated via that connects an outer surface layer of a multilayer printed board to one or more adjacent inner layers but does not extend through the full board thickness, so it is accessible from only one surface. Blind vias free up routing channels and land area on the opposite side, increasing wiring density in high-layer-count designs, and their structure and plating are governed by rigid-board design and performance standards.' WHERE id='79c39de1-2b06-47af-8d3b-14c38cfbd0c2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A plated via that interconnects two or more inner layers of a multilayer printed board without reaching either outer surface, so it is completely enclosed within the board and not visible externally. Buried vias are formed on inner-layer cores before the board is laminated, adding routing capacity while leaving the outer surfaces free for components and other connections.' WHERE id='6ba3149e-8eca-414a-bd42-39d4c77df790' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The graphic representation, on the board silkscreen and in the CAD footprint data, of a component body''s physical boundary and orientation, marking where the part sits and the keep-out area it occupies on the assembly. It aids placement, orientation, and inspection and is part of the land-pattern data defined for each package.' WHERE id='5803a084-9c56-4425-b38d-1f26d441d913' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A large filled region of copper on a printed-board layer, either tied to a net such as ground or a supply or left as an isolated fill, used to provide low-impedance return paths, shielding, and current-carrying capacity, to spread heat, and to balance copper distribution for more uniform etching and plating. Its width and thickness relative to required current and temperature rise are addressed by board design and current-capacity guidance.' WHERE id='b281751e-f979-4985-aafb-216e83384b11' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A printed board carrying etched copper conductor patterns on both faces of the insulating substrate, with electrical connections between the two sides made through plated through-holes or vias. The second conductor layer roughly doubles the available routing area compared with a single-sided board and supports components on both surfaces.' WHERE id='9ed12cbe-439b-4b32-ace7-f0ecf008dc01' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A copper reference mark on a printed board, typically a bare solder-coated round land surrounded by a clear keep-out, that gives automated placement and inspection equipment a precise optical registration target. Global fiducials locate and correct for the position and rotation of the whole board or panel, while local fiducials near fine-pitch or area-array parts refine local placement accuracy; their size and clearance are specified in land-pattern standards.' WHERE id='fbea8e8d-3615-4991-8b46-4d5d5f4e8496' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The concave, smoothly wetted band of solder that forms between a component lead or termination and the board land, tapering up from the land to the termination. Its geometry, including wetting angle, height, and length of land and termination coverage, is a primary visual indicator of solder-joint quality, and minimum acceptable fillet dimensions for each component class are defined in assembly acceptability standards.' WHERE id='e9d839fc-9f03-4b76-9be8-5846a3a50b31' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The manual formation of solder joints using a temperature-controlled soldering iron to heat a component termination and its board land so that flux-cored solder wire, or separately applied flux and solder, melts and wets both surfaces and, on cooling, forms an intermetallic bond. The process is governed by soldered-assembly requirements and acceptability standards, and it carries intrinsic hazards: burns from the hot iron tip, exposure to lead when tin-lead alloys are used, and inhalation of rosin (colophony) flux fume, a recognised respiratory sensitiser that can cause occupational asthma. Local exhaust ventilation and exposure controls set by occupational-health authorities are required, and no safe threshold for flux fume has been established.' WHERE id='85ff48be-2d7d-4da2-b25c-aeb0de78728b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The arrangement of copper lands, together with associated solder-mask and placement features, designed on a printed board to receive and solder the terminations of a specific component package. Land size, spacing, and shape are dimensioned so that the resulting joints wet correctly, self-align, and can be inspected, and standardised land-pattern geometries are published for common packages; the term is synonymous with footprint.' WHERE id='f01d63e2-e199-47ec-a818-7c0dd51b37e0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A printed board built from three or more conductive copper layers separated by dielectric and bonded together by lamination, with interlayer connections made through plated through, blind, and buried vias. Dedicated internal power and ground planes and additional routing layers make it suited to dense, high-speed, and high-pin-count designs, and its construction and performance are covered by rigid-board design and qualification standards.' WHERE id='92cd44dc-a4a4-4384-9571-018b9db0fc28' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A rigid or flexible insulating substrate, commonly glass-reinforced epoxy such as FR-4, carrying etched copper conductors, lands, and plated holes that both mechanically support and electrically interconnect mounted electronic components. It replaces point-to-point wiring with a reproducible, manufacturable interconnect and forms the structural and electrical base of essentially all modern electronic assemblies; its design and performance are defined by interconnect standards.' WHERE id='d31c6100-0668-48a8-bf80-597f822da882' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The set of copper lands, solder-mask openings, and placement and outline data on a printed board that defines where and how a particular component package is mounted and soldered. In CAD usage it is synonymous with the land pattern, and its dimensions are drawn from standardised package-specific geometries so that joints form and inspect correctly.' WHERE id='f54cf793-d660-4e46-998c-9d27ca211d26' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The process of restoring a damaged printed board or solder joint to a functional and acceptable condition, for example replacing a lifted or missing land, bridging a broken conductor with jumper wire, or restoring laminate and plated holes, following standardised repair and modification procedures. Because it relies on soldering irons, hot-air tools, and solvents, it carries the intrinsic soldering hazards of burns, lead exposure from tin-lead alloys, and inhalation of rosin flux fume, so fume extraction and exposure controls specified by occupational-health authorities apply.' WHERE id='3c856d69-7ea1-4c4a-85bb-a44c7f563001' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The reprocessing of a nonconforming assembly to bring it into conformance with the original requirements, for example removing and replacing a component or reflowing a defective joint, using soldering irons, hot-air or area-array rework stations, and desoldering tools under controlled procedures. It is distinguished from repair, which restores functional capability without necessarily meeting the original configuration, and it carries the intrinsic soldering hazards of burns, lead exposure, and rosin flux fume, requiring fume extraction and the exposure controls set by occupational-health authorities.' WHERE id='1f202116-0bd7-41be-91ec-d667613febc4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A printed board with etched copper conductors on only one face of the insulating substrate, with components generally mounted on the opposite side and their leads or terminations soldered to that single copper layer. It is the simplest and lowest-cost board construction, suited to low-density circuits where routing can be completed on one layer.' WHERE id='aafbbb4f-35d9-4679-8520-e766b6492866' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A designated, accessible conductive feature on a printed board, such as a dedicated land, pad, or via, provided for making electrical contact with a specific net during in-circuit test, board bring-up, or troubleshooting. Test points are commonly contacted by a bed-of-nails fixture, and their placement, size, and access are set by design-for-test and board design guidance to ensure reliable probing.' WHERE id='0718f723-a09c-46b9-9160-1a9db144c7ce' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A plated via that passes completely through the full thickness of a printed board, from one outer surface to the other, providing an electrical interconnection between layers. In through-hole assembly the same plated barrel can also receive and be soldered to a component lead, and its plating and structure are governed by rigid-board design and performance standards.' WHERE id='a563d5e7-4c68-48d1-99c1-869722a8640a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The post-production discipline concerned with selecting, cutting, and synchronizing sound effects to picture, distinct from dialogue and music editorial. Working from a spotted picture, the sound-effects editor sources and edits hard effects, backgrounds and ambiences, and designed elements, placing each in sync and organizing them across tracks in readiness for the re-recording mix. The department delivers the cut effects units and associated documentation as part of the sound deliverables.' WHERE id='7445f7b4-e348-4a23-8c42-c1eb1c674588' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The act of placing and organizing edited sound effects onto the tracks of an editing session in preparation for the mix, laying each effect in sync with picture and grouping like elements — hard effects, backgrounds, prop sounds, and designed material — across appropriately routed tracks. The resulting tracklay, or effects units, presents the re-recording mixer with clearly labeled, premix-ready material. The term is the effects-editorial usage for building the sound-effects tracks for the dub.' WHERE id='5fd159cc-50ee-452b-bb03-e1f343301be8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A synchronous sound effect tied to a specific, visible on-screen action or object — such as a door slam, gunshot, footstep impact, or telephone ring — that must cut in precise sync with the picture. Hard effects are distinguished from ambient backgrounds and atmospheres and from Foley, and are typically drawn from recorded libraries or custom recordings and edited to frame against the action. They form one of the principal categories the sound-effects editor lays for the mix.' WHERE id='f093774d-bfce-46e9-9d36-b71023924c59' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A sound effect heard before the picture cut to the scene it belongs to, so that the audio of the incoming shot precedes its image and the effect laps over from the outgoing scene into the new one. Editors use the prelap to smooth a transition or to prepare the audience for the next location by introducing its sound a beat or more early. It is realized in editorial by extending the head of the effect across the picture cut.' WHERE id='5d9e7851-c436-4352-a21d-0b1b77387054' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An auditory illusion, first described by Diana Deutsch, that arises when the successive tones of a scale are divided between the two ears so that each ear receives alternating high and low notes while an ascending and a descending line are interleaved. Listeners generally do not perceive the alternating input; instead the auditory system regroups the tones by pitch proximity, so one ear seems to carry a smooth higher melodic line and the other a lower one, often mislocalized by frequency. It demonstrates how the perceptual system reorganizes spatial and pitch information in auditory scene analysis.' WHERE id='d1f9b179-ac3d-47bb-a606-34ca5775077f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The process, usually conducted in a spotting session with the director, picture editor, and supervising sound editor, of screening the locked picture to determine where sound effects are required and to specify their nature, placement, and creative intent. Decisions are logged in spotting notes that guide the effects editorial and Foley teams, establishing which moments call for hard effects, backgrounds, or designed sounds. It sets the workload and creative direction for the sound-effects build.' WHERE id='d747c35c-b792-4218-9b71-b467564dcdf2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The post-production term, common in British usage, for editing and placing sound elements — effects, atmospheres, Foley, and dialogue — onto the tracks of an editing session in sync with picture, organized and labeled in readiness for the re-recording mix. The tracklaying editor cuts each element to frame and distributes elements across tracks so the dubbing mixer can balance them efficiently. The output is the set of cut sound units delivered to the dub.' WHERE id='629db8b7-d711-483d-9ef9-b58552403cfa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A sound effect recorded wild — captured without reference to picture or timecode sync — for later editorial placement, as opposed to a sound recorded in sync during shooting. Wild effects are gathered on location or on a recording stage to build a custom library of the exact sounds a scene needs, then edited to picture in effects editorial. Recording wild lets the sound crew obtain clean, isolated, repeatable performances of a sound.' WHERE id='67344034-bbd9-4f3e-9b64-dc5437f5c562' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A recording made without synchronization to the camera or picture — commonly of dialogue lines, room tone or presence, ambiences, or effects — captured separately so it can be laid to picture later in post. Because it carries no sync reference to the image, a wild track is used to supplement or replace sync material, provide atmosphere and room tone, or capture performances free of on-camera constraints. The term derives from the sound being recorded wild, independently of the picture.' WHERE id='fad5f050-1073-41af-b313-40f99dce5d04' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An audio interface whose input and output signal paths pass through series coupling capacitors, which block any steady direct-current (0 Hz) component and pass only the alternating audio band. Because the coupling capacitors act as a high-pass filter, an AC-coupled interface cannot faithfully carry or record steady control voltages, envelopes, or very slow sub-audio modulation, which are attenuated and distorted by the capacitor. It is therefore suited to ordinary audio but not to sending or capturing DC control voltages, in contrast to a DC-coupled interface.' WHERE id='eb601c5a-766d-4c53-a52f-8f35202eeef1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A module or circuit that takes a periodic clock pulse train and outputs pulses at integer sub-multiples of the input rate, emitting one output pulse for every N input pulses so that divisions such as /2, /4, /8 and /16 are produced simultaneously. It is used to derive slower, rhythmically related time bases from a master clock, for example running envelopes or a second sequencer at half or a quarter of the main tempo. The divider counts incoming edges, conventionally the rising edge of the 0/+5 V pulses.' WHERE id='2cedd1f9-662f-47d3-8ae9-72dfb758b56f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A module that outputs pulses at integer multiples of an incoming clock rate, such as x2, x4 or x8, generating faster time divisions than the master clock. Because future pulses cannot be known in advance, a multiplier measures the period of the input clock and interpolates the additional pulses, often using a phase-locked loop, so the multiplied output stays synchronised as the input tempo changes. It is the complement of a clock divider and is used to produce finer rhythmic subdivisions and higher-resolution timing.' WHERE id='75bcac10-41f7-4ac7-ab02-ca4c5b36f9ca' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A single discrete timing event within a clock signal, taking the form of a brief rectangular voltage transition that marks one instant of a regular time grid. Successive clock pulses advance sequencers, sample-and-hold circuits and dividers step by step; in a modular system they are typically 0/+5 V rectangular pulses, and modules conventionally act on the rising edge. The pulse conveys timing only, its rate rather than its width defining the tempo.' WHERE id='8f22cc69-2e32-4c0d-b1da-2c3b402d069c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The reduction of a control voltage''s amplitude by a scaling factor between zero and one, carried out by a passive attenuator such as a potentiometer or resistive divider, or by a voltage-controlled amplifier. Attenuating a modulation voltage reduces its depth without otherwise changing its shape, so an LFO or envelope applied through an attenuator has a smaller effect on the destination parameter. It is a core means of setting modulation amount at each patch point.' WHERE id='5816db14-2613-4f3c-b497-df8813fd99f8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The recording or programming of a time-varying control voltage so that a parameter is driven through a predetermined series of values over time, analogous to parameter automation in a digital audio workstation but expressed as an analog voltage. It is realised by a sequencer, an envelope or a DC-coupled interface that plays stored control data out as voltage, allowing repeatable, tempo-locked changes to pitch, filter cutoff or level in a modular patch.' WHERE id='0f8692a3-59bc-4382-a584-f1cf9092fcfe' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The generation of the negative, mirror-image version of a control voltage about a reference point, usually 0 V, so that a positive input becomes an equal negative output and vice versa. It is produced by an inverting amplifier with a gain of minus one, turning a rising signal into a falling one; applied to an envelope or LFO it reverses the direction of modulation, and applied to an offset it lets one control source raise while another lowers a parameter.' WHERE id='afc8b5b4-34b4-4d47-a5c6-76128d524c20' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The addition of a fixed direct-current voltage to a control voltage, shifting its entire range up or down by a constant amount without altering its shape or amplitude. Offsetting is used to bias a bipolar modulation source into a usable unipolar range, to transpose a pitch voltage by a fixed interval, or to set the resting point of a controlled parameter, and is typically provided by a summing or dedicated offset circuit.' WHERE id='eee71326-a5ae-44e6-8eec-7ca134050c65' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The multiplication of a control voltage by a gain factor, which may attenuate it below unity or amplify it above unity, so as to map the voltage into a desired range or set the depth of modulation. On a pitch input, precise scaling of the voltage is what calibrates an oscillator to track at one volt per octave; more generally it determines how many volts of change at the source produce a given change at the destination.' WHERE id='8da5894a-8e5e-4e17-b582-e5aa5371962c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The linear addition of two or more control voltages into a single composite voltage, performed by a summing amplifier or CV mixer so that several control sources combine additively at one destination. Summing lets, for example, a keyboard pitch voltage, an LFO for vibrato and an envelope for pitch sweep all drive one oscillator; because pitch inputs follow one volt per octave, summing voltages there corresponds to adding musical intervals.' WHERE id='12babf15-115e-4576-9844-3b0af1d2c867' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A hardware unit that converts analog control-voltage and gate signals, and often an analog clock, into MIDI messages. It measures a pitch control voltage referenced to one volt per octave to derive MIDI note numbers, translates the gate into note-on and note-off events, and can map other voltages to controllers, so that a modular or analog instrument can drive MIDI sequencers, sound modules or a computer.' WHERE id='d31fb8fe-8294-4571-b545-94304d033aa7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An audio interface whose signal path contains no series blocking capacitor, so its response extends down to direct current at 0 Hz and it can output or record steady voltages as well as audio. This DC capability is what allows a computer to send precise control voltages, envelopes and slow modulation to a modular synthesiser, or to capture them, which an AC-coupled interface cannot do because its coupling capacitors remove the DC and sub-audio content.' WHERE id='637a36db-424c-4381-86ab-af63bb60dc05' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A unipolar, time-varying control voltage generated by an envelope generator in response to a gate or trigger, which rises and falls through its stages, such as attack, decay, sustain and release, to shape a sound over time. Routed to a voltage-controlled amplifier or filter it contours loudness or timbre for each note; in the Doepfer A-100 the ADSR envelope voltage spans roughly 0 V to +8 V.' WHERE id='8d8cb243-ec75-4ae3-8aac-e6cd08acd077' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The control-voltage output of an envelope follower, a circuit that rectifies an incoming audio signal and smooths it with a low-pass filter to produce a voltage proportional to the signal''s instantaneous amplitude, tracing its loudness contour. This output is used as a modulation source, for instance driving a filter''s cutoff from the dynamics of an external instrument, and its response is set by the follower''s attack and release smoothing.' WHERE id='f1f72ba8-b93a-4474-8f1e-8a39baef485a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The nominal signal level used for audio within the Eurorack modular format, which is considerably hotter than studio line level, typically around 10 volts peak-to-peak, or about -5 V to +5 V. Modules run from plus and minus 12 volt supply rails, which set the available headroom, so patching Eurorack audio to line-level equipment normally requires attenuation. Control voltages share the same rails but are not bound to this audio figure.' WHERE id='927eb827-3c8b-437f-8eda-ea24369fe5f8' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The convention that determines whether a gate signals its active, note-on state with a high or with a low condition. A positive-going gate, also called V-trigger, rests at 0 V and rises to a positive voltage to indicate note-on, which is the standard in Eurorack and most modern and Roland-style gear, whereas an S-trigger, or switch trigger, indicates note-on by shorting its output to 0 V and turns the note off by going open circuit, as used by Moog and some vintage instruments. Connecting sources and destinations of opposite polarity requires a gate converter.' WHERE id='7e41e985-4555-4c54-889d-8e899ba4becd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A sustained on/off control voltage that communicates the duration of a note, held at its active level for as long as a key or step is engaged and returning to its rest level when released. In Eurorack a gate is typically 0 V when off and around +5 V when on, with modules treating anything above about +3 V as high and below about +1 V as low, and tolerating up to +12 V. Unlike a momentary trigger, the gate''s length carries information and is what holds an envelope generator in its sustain phase.' WHERE id='b6a8ddb3-b522-40e7-b744-24e5b5c000a5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A pitch control-voltage convention in which oscillator frequency is directly proportional to the applied voltage, so that doubling the voltage doubles the frequency and therefore raises the pitch by one octave. Because frequency rather than its logarithm scales with voltage, the voltage per octave is not constant but doubles from octave to octave; this standard was used by Korg and Yamaha monosynths, in contrast to the more widespread one volt per octave scheme.' WHERE id='f568ae61-6d98-4479-809d-1ff7210ac051' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An interfacing stage that raises an external line-level audio signal, whose nominal amplitude is on the order of one volt and referenced to -10 dBV or +4 dBu, up to the much hotter modular or Eurorack operating level of roughly 10 volts peak-to-peak. Beyond gain, such a stage commonly provides suitable input impedance and buffering so that mixers, instruments and interfaces can be processed by modular circuits at an appropriate signal level.' WHERE id='99a6159e-c4b5-4420-aa9c-a3534a88dc2b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A hardware unit that converts MIDI messages into analog control voltages, gates and clock so that MIDI instruments and sequencers can drive analog and modular synthesisers. It maps note numbers to a pitch control voltage, normally at one volt per octave, derives a gate from note-on and note-off, and translates MIDI clock into analog timing pulses, and it may also output velocity, modulation and other controllers as further voltages.' WHERE id='5af6871c-cb9e-4891-966f-531d0d45a8cf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A flexible cable used to route a single signal from one module jack to another in a modular system, thereby defining the signal flow of a patch. In the Eurorack format it is terminated with 3.5 mm mono tip-sleeve phone plugs and carries one unbalanced signal, which may be audio, a control voltage, a gate, a trigger or a clock, since all of these share the same physical connection and voltage range.' WHERE id='be7c8ce2-2eec-4c52-92d6-94d68a436301' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A module that directs or switches signals among several paths within a patch, such as a sequential switch, a matrix mixer or a multiple, sending one input to a selectable output or choosing among several inputs. Routing may be set manually or driven by control voltages, clocks or gates, allowing the topology of a patch to change over time, for instance stepping one source around several destinations under clock control.' WHERE id='a995a5c2-3edd-4624-b4c9-246e217e069e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An interfacing stage that attenuates the hot modular audio level, on the order of 10 volts peak-to-peak, down to standard line level referenced to -10 dBV or +4 dBu, while providing a suitable output impedance to drive mixers, recorders and interfaces. Such an output stage is commonly AC-coupled to remove any residual direct-current offset, protecting downstream equipment and matching the modular signal to conventional studio gear.' WHERE id='d3f93be4-64f8-46d4-a780-4493647265da' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A control voltage applied specifically to vary a parameter such as pitch, filter cutoff or amplitude over time, as distinct from a static setting. Modulation voltages are often bipolar, swinging above and below 0 V, as with a low-frequency oscillator whose output might span roughly -2.5 V to +2.5 V, and are usually passed through an attenuator or voltage-controlled amplifier so that the depth of the effect can be set at the destination.' WHERE id='3315fc1b-494e-4b00-95d3-7f161d4f72ab' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The control voltage that sets an oscillator''s pitch, conventionally scaled at one volt per octave in most modular systems, which corresponds to one twelfth of a volt, about 0.0833 V, per equal-tempered semitone. It is commonly summed with modulation and glide voltages before reaching the oscillator, so that a keyboard, sequencer or MIDI-to-CV converter can set the base note while vibrato and portamento are added on top.' WHERE id='e26cf81e-17c2-488f-b798-b4687bcbfb14' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A process that analyses the fundamental frequency of an incoming audio signal and outputs a proportional control voltage, typically at one volt per octave, usually together with a gate marking when a note is present. It lets a monophonic acoustic or electric source, such as a guitar or the voice, drive a synthesiser''s pitch; accuracy is limited by the pitch tracker''s ability to follow the input cleanly and quickly.' WHERE id='2377b205-0221-4a2f-be8b-fcd14295b9d5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The smoothly interpolated pitch control voltage produced when portamento, or glide, is active, in which the voltage ramps continuously from the previous note''s value to the new one instead of stepping instantly between them. Generated by a slew or lag circuit inserted in the pitch path, the rate of the ramp sets the glide time, and because the path follows one volt per octave the audible result is a continuous slide in pitch between the two notes.' WHERE id='e09ce7a2-de68-45d7-a010-c79a6a05834c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A gate that uses a positive voltage to indicate note-on, also known as a V-trigger. Its output rests at 0 V and rises to a positive level, commonly +5 V and sometimes up to +10 V or +12 V, for as long as the note is held, returning to 0 V at release. This is the standard in Eurorack and Roland-style equipment, and is the opposite convention to the Moog S-trigger, which signals note-on by shorting its output to ground.' WHERE id='aecf9224-507e-4fde-a21b-3c5d05317f82' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A trigger pulse sent to a sequencer, clock divider or counter that returns it to its first step or initial state, so that a pattern restarts from the beginning and stays aligned with the rest of a patch. It is conventionally a short rising-edge pulse of the same 0/+5 V family as clocks and gates, and is used to keep multiple sequences phase-locked or to force a downbeat.' WHERE id='33c89077-0871-40b1-9231-ffb0ccbbef66' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The output voltage of a sample-and-hold circuit, which on each clock or trigger captures the instantaneous value present at its input and holds that value steady until the next clock. When its input is a noise source the result is a sequence of random stepped voltages, a classic modular effect, while sampling a slow waveform produces a quantised, staircase version of it; the held voltage is commonly used to drive pitch or timbre.' WHERE id='d138be76-d98e-443a-ba99-7ca98be49a42' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A circuit that limits the rate of change, or slew rate, of a signal so that its output cannot move faster than a set number of volts per second, converting abrupt steps into ramps. Applied to a pitch control voltage it produces portamento by gliding between notes, and applied to a gate or square wave it rounds the edges into attack and decay slopes, effectively acting as a controllable lag; many designs provide independent rise and fall times.' WHERE id='451a30c1-8c6d-49ea-9d4a-4d6ee35b43d4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A transport-control signal that commands a clock, sequencer or drum machine to begin or halt playback. In the DIN sync, or Sync24, scheme a dedicated run/stop line, held high while running, accompanies the 24-pulse-per-quarter-note clock on a five-pin DIN connector, while in MIDI the equivalent function is carried by the Start and Stop system real-time messages, with status bytes 0xFA and 0xFC, alongside Continue at 0xFB. It carries transport state rather than musical pitch.' WHERE id='e44ea7f5-66a9-4862-9628-70a986d1cce5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A sequencer that outputs patterns of trigger pulses across a set of steps, and often several parallel tracks, rather than pitch control voltages, so that each active step emits a short trigger at the clock rate. It is used to fire drum modules, envelope generators or other event-driven modules in rhythmic patterns, and is the modular counterpart of a step drum programmer, advancing one step per incoming clock pulse.' WHERE id='768e4c0b-ad11-4d7d-b9ab-cdcbf154c56e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A brief, momentary pulse that marks a single instant in time in order to initiate an event, such as starting an envelope, advancing a sequencer or clocking a sample-and-hold. In a modular system it is typically a short rising-edge pulse in the 0/+5 V range, and unlike a gate it conveys only the moment of an event and not its duration, so a destination responds to the edge rather than to how long the signal stays high.' WHERE id='6f52446d-877f-40d0-bb4f-e708ff6acea6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The predominant pitch control-voltage standard, in which pitch is related exponentially to voltage such that each additional one volt raises the pitch by exactly one octave, corresponding to one twelfth of a volt, about 0.0833 V, per equal-tempered semitone. Because voltage is linear in pitch but exponential in frequency, an oscillator must contain an exponential converter to obey it; the scheme is used by Moog, Roland, Sequential and the Eurorack format, in contrast to the Hertz-per-volt convention.' WHERE id='cdd5796e-bee4-405e-915e-b0a9c4e523ab' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Dynamic-range compression applied to the console channel carrying a performer''s personal (radio or head-worn) microphone, in which gain is automatically reduced once the signal exceeds a set threshold. In theatrical vocal reinforcement it narrows the wide level range between whispered and projected delivery, holds a consistent presence against orchestra or band, and increases gain before feedback by preventing brief peaks from forcing a lower overall fader setting.' WHERE id='7bc673c1-bb55-4c21-b9ee-471d2e2d91e9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Frequency-selective compression applied to a performer''s microphone channel that acts only on the sibilant band, typically around five to ten kilohertz, attenuating the harsh ''s'', ''sh'' and ''t'' sounds emphasised by close-worn lavalier and head-worn capsules. It tames sibilance that proximity and high-frequency emphasis exaggerate, keeping amplified speech and sung text intelligible without dulling the overall tone.' WHERE id='d72a946f-4b56-4825-860f-0811a952e68d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The deliberate insertion of electronic delay so that a performer''s reinforced voice reaches the audience just after, and therefore appears to originate from, the performer''s actual position on stage, exploiting the precedence (Haas) effect to preserve natural localisation. It also covers time-aligning the arrivals of the loudspeakers covering a seating area so that the reinforced signal and nearby sources sum coherently rather than smearing intelligibility.' WHERE id='ceef8f39-3e2f-4e2f-a7d7-b0fa1e746ec0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An auxiliary send taken from a performer''s microphone channel and routed to an outboard or internal effects processor, such as reverb or delay, allowing a controllable proportion of that voice to be treated while the dry signal continues through the main mix. The returned, processed signal is blended back at the console to place or colour the voice without altering its direct path.' WHERE id='137495d4-705a-42e4-bc85-e711bb971f7f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Frequency-dependent gain adjustment applied to a performer''s microphone channel to correct tonal balance, compensate for the response and placement of a lavalier or head-worn capsule and for proximity effect, restore intelligibility lost to costume muffling, and notch problem frequencies that promote feedback. It shapes the timbre of the amplified voice so it sits naturally within the reinforcement system.' WHERE id='65acf12d-302d-40fd-91b8-ff5b7d6bb99e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Foldback sent to performers on stage: a monitor feed of reinforcement, music, click or cue audio returned to the acting area through stage loudspeakers or in-ear monitors so that performers can hear themselves and the accompaniment. It gives singers and actors the reference they need to stay in tune and in time when the main system is aimed at the audience rather than the stage.' WHERE id='c1d259e8-7941-494a-a061-68bddb17b016' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The setting of input (preamplifier) gain on the console channel assigned to a performer''s microphone, establishing the correct signal level and headroom from that source before downstream processing and fader control. Proper trim matches the transmitter and capsule output to the console''s optimum operating level, maximising signal-to-noise ratio while leaving margin against transient peaks.' WHERE id='7cca4846-db51-4262-b5ab-820158cca070' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A high-pass (low-cut) filter inserted on a performer''s microphone channel that attenuates energy below a chosen corner frequency, removing stage rumble, footfall, air-handling noise, wind and clothing-borne thumps as well as excess proximity-effect bass from a close-worn capsule. It cleans the low end of the amplified voice and reduces the accumulation of inaudible low-frequency energy across many open microphones.' WHERE id='5d48ec9a-1bc1-427a-ad43-3c4cf9bf8727' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The console input strip assigned to an individual performer''s microphone, providing its preamplifier gain, filtering, equalisation, dynamics, routing and fader for that source. In theatrical mixing each performer''s radio or head-worn microphone typically occupies its own input channel so it can be levelled, processed and cued independently.' WHERE id='94fad14b-5717-427f-a6be-3d2e2ce65e88' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The storing and cue-by-cue recall of a performer channel''s fader level, often via a VCA or DCA and snapshot scenes, so that the mix moves automatically to preset values at each point in the show rather than being ridden entirely by hand. On theatre consoles it lets the operator concentrate on live balance while programmed level changes follow the script.' WHERE id='02c1b43c-fbc0-41ce-bc6a-e497c4894f4a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A personal microphone worn by a performer for voice reinforcement, most commonly a miniature omnidirectional lavalier positioned on the hairline or forehead or a head-worn boom capsule, connected to a body-worn wireless transmitter. Its placement close to the mouth maximises gain before feedback and level over an orchestra while remaining concealed from the audience.' WHERE id='2361aa06-44a7-4b71-a60f-cfdeaa1b0ad1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The programmed opening and closing of individual performer microphone channels by cue, so that a microphone is unmuted only while its wearer is speaking or singing and muted otherwise. Reducing the number of simultaneously open microphones lowers accumulated noise and comb filtering and raises overall gain before feedback.' WHERE id='9caa99b1-cac5-40d1-897d-938ff1ed217e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An auxiliary send from a performer''s microphone channel feeding a reverberation processor, whose output is returned to the mix to place the amplified voice in an artificial acoustic space or to match the scenic environment. The send level governs how wet the voice appears while its direct signal remains present in the main system.' WHERE id='296113fa-5067-439e-8178-b659ae78bd3a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A member of the production''s sound team who works under the sound designer to develop, document and realise the design, drafting system and signal-flow paperwork, supervising installation and programming, and often overseeing the design in the designer''s absence and during the run. It is a recognised theatrical design credit distinct from the production sound engineer or the operator.' WHERE id='e6572f49-206c-4a1f-8394-062918732568' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A defined sound event, whether music, effect, ambience or a reinforcement change, scheduled to occur at a specific moment in a performance, identified and numbered in the prompt book and executed on the operator''s or stage manager''s ''go''. Each cue specifies content, level, routing and timing and is fired manually or by a playback system in the sequence set by the sound designer.' WHERE id='23570728-d317-45d1-b065-6710c4602ab9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The software or hardware system that stores a production''s sound files and reproduces them as cues on command during a performance, handling triggering, level, routing, fades and timing. In contemporary practice it is typically a dedicated cue-playback application running on a computer with a multichannel interface, having replaced tape and disc playback.' WHERE id='cd8d72bd-2457-411f-a0f0-f2398567cc71' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A cue set so that triggering it automatically triggers the following cue in the list without a further ''go'', after any programmed wait, allowing a designer to chain several actions to a single operator command. It is one of the standard continue modes in cue-playback software, distinct from an auto-follow, which waits for the cue''s own action to complete before advancing.' WHERE id='a44445ab-7f97-4408-8b5b-913ad659c685' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A distributed feed of the stage program, the show relay, and often paging and cue calls, sent to backstage areas, wings and crew positions so that performers and technicians can follow the performance and hear calls when they cannot hear the stage directly. It is derived from front-of-house or dedicated relay microphones and routed to backstage loudspeakers.' WHERE id='5f551807-f08a-4ca7-aa08-2ef69d330843' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A second, mirrored cue-playback machine kept in parallel with the primary so that, should the primary fail, playback can continue from the same point with minimal interruption. Redundant playback pairs are standard practice for shows that depend on computer-based sound, and the backup typically follows the primary''s cue position over a network link.' WHERE id='1cba444c-33ed-4fed-9d98-552e5ac26f43' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An audio subgroup, or group bus, to which the individual band or orchestra channels are routed so the ensemble can be controlled and processed collectively on a single pair of faders. It lets the operator adjust the whole band''s level relative to the vocals with one move and apply shared processing or metering to the instrumental mix.' WHERE id='553d34da-c51f-4d1b-a74d-ccf602ead732' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The mapping of a specific wireless bodypack transmitter, together with its RF frequency and receiver, to a console input and to the performer or character who will wear it. Clear assignment underpins RF coordination and microphone tracking, ensuring each transmitter feeds the correct channel and that spare packs can be substituted predictably.' WHERE id='6f16f6b9-58d3-42a7-9f5a-8a8b1856c1e6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dedicated output mix sent from the theatre''s console to an external broadcaster, recording facility or archival system, typically a clean program feed independent of the house reinforcement so that it carries the intended balance without room-specific processing. It may be a full mix or a mix-minus tailored to the receiving destination.' WHERE id='5f47ea05-5a48-4605-a4e7-01dcdabe83ee' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A pre-loaded audio cue held ready for instant, on-demand playback, named after the broadcast tape cartridge (the ''cart'') once used for spot effects and station inserts. In theatrical playback the term denotes a self-contained sound such as an effect, sting, doorbell, telephone ring, gunshot, or short music insert that is assigned to a dedicated button, key, or grid slot and fired manually by the operator on an actor''s action or line, rather than run as a timed step within a linear cue sequence. Modern show-control software preserves the concept as a hot-key grid or cart wall of instantly available cues that can be triggered in any order.' WHERE id='64c7fa93-0820-48cd-b14a-d255b4f14db6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The restoration of a console channel''s stored parameters, including gain, filtering, equalisation, dynamics, routing and fader, from a saved scene or snapshot, returning that channel to a known state at a given point in the show. On digital consoles it is the mechanism by which a programmed mix is reproduced cue to cue.' WHERE id='1729fea1-fd8f-47e0-9e08-e0eca0913674' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A console function, also called recall safe, that isolates a channel or selected parameters within it from scene or snapshot recall, so that its current settings are retained when other stored states are loaded. It is used to keep a live-adjusted channel from being overwritten as the show''s cues advance.' WHERE id='80b8d9a6-5683-4fbb-8fb6-58f05dc756d1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='On a theatre console, an input treated as a character or role rather than a fixed physical input: the microphone of whichever performer is currently playing that role is routed into the character channel so that the channel''s name, processing, level automation and cues remain tied to the character across understudy and cast changes. It underlies the workflow that lets one stored mix serve interchangeable performers.' WHERE id='24f8c5f6-217d-431e-ae19-674ace8f7003' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The recall of a stored set of channel settings and assignments associated with a particular character on a theatre console, restoring that role''s processing, routing and microphone mapping. It works with the character-channel workflow so that swapping the performer in a role reinstates the correct, pre-programmed treatment for that character.' WHERE id='6bf44f0f-99ea-4bc3-83df-b12809e18337' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The console workflow of keeping a production''s mix tied to characters and following which performer, whether principal, understudy or alternate, is playing each role, so that microphone assignments, channel names and stored settings move with the role rather than the physical input. It allows a single programmed show to run correctly regardless of cast changes.' WHERE id='3805dcf0-8438-4b68-9ffc-0c6b488856ef' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Reproduction of a click track, a metronomic timing reference, fed privately to the musical director, band and sometimes performers, usually over in-ear monitors, to lock tempo and keep live playing aligned with pre-recorded tracks, sequenced music or timed effects. The click is routed only to those who need it and kept out of the audience mix.' WHERE id='f8bd8b7c-0393-497f-9584-178650b08138' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dedicated monitor feed associated with the musical director''s position, carrying program, vocals, click or talkback to the conductor and, conversely, the conductor''s cues to the pit and stage, so that the ensemble stays coordinated when sightlines are limited. It forms part of the communications and foldback provision for musical theatre.' WHERE id='b285f08b-98c1-4218-8b8a-444b222ce255' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A control signal generated by momentarily connecting two electrical contacts, closing a relay, switch or general-purpose input, used to fire a cue or start a device without any data protocol. Contact closures are a long-standing, hardware-simple method of interfacing dissimilar show systems, for example letting a switch, a stage-machinery limit or another controller start a sound cue.' WHERE id='2fe6e69c-0a40-471e-b573-d8ecc93e0e08' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The pre-listening or previewing of a cue in isolation, away from the main output, so the operator or designer can confirm its content, level and edit points before it is fired in performance. Auditioning routes the cue to a monitoring path rather than the house system, allowing checks during rehearsal or setup without disturbing the show mix.' WHERE id='620c8a09-536e-4b77-af0b-a447d850ec06' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The programmed interval between the trigger, the ''go'', and the moment a cue''s action actually begins, used to time an event precisely to stage action without a separate operator command; in cue-playback software this is the cue''s pre-wait. It lets a single ''go'' place a sound at an exact later beat.' WHERE id='f7b30320-b902-44cd-9e83-97e697891195' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An operating state of a cueing system in which cue content and parameters can be created and changed offline, as distinct from the running or ''show'' state in which controls are locked and ''go'' advances the stack. Separating the edit and run states protects a programmed show from accidental alteration during performance.' WHERE id='2f0b33c5-2066-43f3-ad97-13c7085b0632' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The duration over which a cue changes a level or parameter, fading a sound in or out or crossfading between cues, programmed so the transition happens smoothly over a set number of seconds rather than instantly. It is a fundamental timing property of playback and crossfade cues.' WHERE id='89d22320-3e0f-4934-8fac-bccc2228933c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The control governing a cue''s playback output level, set when the cue is programmed and adjustable live, determining how loud the cue reproduces and allowing it to be balanced against the stage sound and other cues. It is the cue''s own volume, distinct from the master and output-routing levels.' WHERE id='a8542571-4ee7-4707-8ac9-dc8a58858a11' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A marked position, or pair of positions, within a cue''s media defining a section that repeats continuously so the audio sustains, looping an ambience or bed, until the cue is stopped or advanced. Playback software implements it through loop or slice markers and play counts that return playback to the loop''s start.' WHERE id='d5c07a99-5c68-43aa-86d2-349691c51237' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The routing of a cue''s audio to chosen output channels and loudspeakers, the cue''s output patch and level matrix, so it plays from the intended part of the system, for example a specific onstage speaker or surround zone. Assigning outputs per cue is how spatialised and localised playback is achieved.' WHERE id='0a70ba58-f768-44aa-8e01-91c7b009be3e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The control of a cue''s position across the loudspeaker system by distributing its level between outputs, placing the reproduced sound at a point in the stereo or multichannel field or moving it during playback. In matrix-based playback it is realised through the relative crosspoint levels feeding each output.' WHERE id='8cf22412-3a83-4b7d-9c8f-4303f0485169' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The system component that executes a production''s cue list, firing and running cues on ''go'' with their programmed content, timing, fades and routing. It is the core of a computer-based sound-cue system, managing the ordered sequence of events and their live control during performance.' WHERE id='fff22ea1-6be0-425d-a082-54ae3705e189' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Loading a cue''s media into memory and cueing it to its start point in advance so that, when triggered, it begins instantly and without a gap. Preloading is used where a cue must start precisely on an action, avoiding the small delay that opening and seeking a file at trigger time would introduce.' WHERE id='b5f8c7e8-5b59-49bb-9e0b-22347765cfe1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The retrieval of a stored cue so its saved parameters and readiness are loaded for review or firing; on a console, recalling a cue applies its snapshot of channel and routing settings, while in playback software it selects and stands by the cue for ''go''. It is the mechanism by which a programmed cue is reinstated.' WHERE id='480e97f7-d2cf-41f8-94f9-beb69971707b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The position in a cue''s media at which playback begins, the start time or in-point, set to skip a lead-in or to begin at a chosen moment. Trimming the start point tightens a cue so it fires on exactly the intended sound.' WHERE id='918d2220-a995-4dcc-9173-218b5a6dddcf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The position at which a cue''s playback ends, the end time or out-point, set to trim an unwanted tail or to finish at a chosen moment rather than at the file''s end. It bounds the played portion of the media together with the start point.' WHERE id='d6e62869-d7b5-4697-864a-5066db883682' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The locking of cue playback to an external timing reference, commonly SMPTE or MIDI timecode, so that cues fire and run in step with other systems such as video, music playback or another department''s controller. Synchronising to timecode keeps interdependent events frame-accurate across a performance.' WHERE id='74d8fc30-ec81-4091-a55f-834b9a4e83b6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A theatrical mixing method in which the console advances through a stored sequence of cues, or snapshots, tied to the script, recalling preset fader, mute, routing and processing states at each point rather than being balanced entirely by ear. It makes a complex, repeatable show mix executable cue by cue on a single ''go''.' WHERE id='3b90c24a-2cd8-40e0-a9d3-47227d31a52b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The capture of performers'' spoken text by microphones, and the selection and placement of those microphones, whether personal lavalier or head-worn capsules, stage-floor boundary mics or overhead shotguns, to obtain intelligible speech while minimising noise, spill and feedback. Effective dialogue pickup is the foundation of speech reinforcement in the theatre.' WHERE id='c258af16-cd01-40d5-97e6-3611c2a6f8ce' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The management of a production performed by two casts, or with alternating principals, so that microphone assignments, character channels and stored settings remain correct for whichever cast or performer is on. Built on the character-tracking workflow, it lets one programmed show serve interchangeable casts without re-mixing.' WHERE id='d1672fbb-c1ab-472b-8b21-abbf883bef62' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A show-relay feed of the stage program, and usually the calls and paging, distributed to the dressing rooms so performers and staff can follow the performance and respond to cues to come to the stage. It is derived from relay microphones or the program mix and reproduced on dressing-room loudspeakers.' WHERE id='ff92926e-d3de-4f7f-91ad-3e4e3c5ef404' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An audio subgroup that collects the sound-effects channels or effects returns onto a single group bus for collective level control and processing, keeping the effects manageable as one element against vocals and music. It allows the whole effects layer to be ridden or muted with one control.' WHERE id='9f55bccd-7aee-4756-b384-74a9c027286a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dedicated input path on a mixing console used to bring the output of an effects or reverb processor back into the mix, rather than to carry a primary microphone or line source. Signal is sent to the processor from the channels'' auxiliary (effect) sends, and the processed, wet signal is returned on this channel, or on a purpose-built return input, where its level, equalisation, and bus assignment are controlled independently of the dry source channels. Because the effect is fed from the sends and blended back through the return, the balance of processed to unprocessed sound can be adjusted without altering the routing of the original inputs.' WHERE id='eba1ea55-e1ab-440c-8634-476e4aa8cbf6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A body-worn radio microphone (or, less often, a shared area or float microphone) assigned to a member of the ensemble or chorus rather than to a named principal. Because ensemble singers are numerous and their individual lines are usually less exposed than a lead''s, these channels are commonly pooled, reassigned between performers across the run, or grouped under a single control so the operator can raise the whole chorus as a block during full-company numbers. The distinction from a principal microphone drives mic-plot planning, transmitter and frequency allocation, and overall mixing strategy.' WHERE id='a8e2e620-6acc-42e2-8ce1-3318d57a0461' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A group bus, whether an audio subgroup or a VCA/DCA control group, onto which the console channels carrying ensemble or chorus microphones are assigned so that the collective chorus level can be ridden with a single fader. Grouping the ensemble in this way lets the operator balance the massed voices against principals and the pit as one entity, push the whole chorus up for full-company numbers and pull it down under dialogue, and apply shared muting or processing without touching individual channels. On VCA-equipped desks the grouping is a control-group assignment rather than a summed audio bus, so channel routing to other destinations is unaffected.' WHERE id='15922124-6a0b-436b-a9b1-a36fb2a6f591' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In show-control and cue-playback software, a cue whose action is to change one or more parameters of a target cue, such as an audio cue''s output levels or an effect setting, smoothly over a specified duration following a chosen fade shape, rather than to start playback of new media itself. A fade cue must reference an existing target cue and can raise, lower, or cross-fade levels; in an absolute mode it drives parameters to fixed values, while in a relative mode it offsets them from their current values. Such cues are the standard mechanism for scripted level changes, cross-fades, and fade-outs within a cue sequence.' WHERE id='7f230a49-0f4c-4fbe-a417-bb17d279326c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An audio signal derived from the console and sent back toward the stage so that performers can hear themselves and other sources; foldback is the British term for stage monitoring. The feed is normally taken from dedicated auxiliary buses and mixed independently of the front-of-house balance, then reproduced through stage-edge wedge loudspeakers, sidefills, or in-ear monitors, so that the monitor mix can differ from what the audience hears. Each foldback feed carries a discrete mix tailored to a particular position or performer, allowing, for example, a vocalist''s feed to emphasise their own voice.' WHERE id='1861da6d-81eb-4d94-a31c-54aa0fc6e90a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A cue configured to start automatically upon completion of the preceding cue, rather than waiting for a manual go from the operator. By linking a cue to the one before it, the follow relationship lets a chain of actions run as a sequence keyed to when each cue actually finishes, which distinguishes it from a fixed post-wait continuation that fires after a set interval regardless of whether the previous cue is still running. Follow relationships are used to build automated cue sequences that unfold hands-free once the first cue is triggered.' WHERE id='a2ad1677-eb44-4c5f-8940-a1012bde136e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A control action initiated through a General Purpose Interface (GPI), a set of simple electrical input and output connections on audio consoles and show-control equipment used to exchange low-level control signals with external devices. A GPI input responds to a voltage change or contact closure by executing an assigned function, such as recalling a scene, muting a channel, or starting a cue, so that an external switch, relay, or system can trigger the device; conversely a GPI output can signal external equipment. Because it depends only on a contact closure or logic level, GPI provides a device-independent way to interlock otherwise incompatible systems.' WHERE id='fff78d73-b82d-4bbb-b1ce-b45b5763538c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dedicated audio signal supplied to an assistive listening system so that patrons who are deaf or hard of hearing receive a clear, direct feed of the performance that is largely independent of the room''s acoustics and distance from the stage. The feed is generally a clean programme mix routed to a transmitter, such as an audio-frequency induction (hearing) loop, or an FM or infrared system, that delivers the audio to personal receivers or, in a loop system, directly to a hearing aid''s telecoil. Provision of such a system is required in many assembly venues under accessibility regulations, and the induction-loop variant is governed by an international performance standard.' WHERE id='cfa44897-5f31-459f-aad2-5e1589ffb1cd' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An audio cue configured to repeat continuously, either seamlessly or by returning to its start point, until it is stopped, faded, or superseded by another cue. It is used for sustained material of indefinite length such as room tone, weather and crowd ambience, machinery drones, or an underscore bed that must hold beneath open-ended stage action, so the operator ends it on a later cue rather than at a fixed runtime. Playback software implements looping as a per-cue property, optionally with defined loop points or a crossfade at the wrap to avoid an audible seam.' WHERE id='7476366b-fcec-4309-a612-514af9f4b06e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A production document that maps each wireless microphone channel and transmitter to the performer wearing it, cross-referenced by character, radio frequency, transmitter and receiver number, and often headset element or colour. Also called a mic plot, it is the master reference the sound crew uses to fit and track bodypacks, manage swaps between performers, allocate frequencies without conflict, and confirm which console channel corresponds to which actor scene by scene. It is maintained alongside the channel plot and updated as casting, understudies, swings, and quick-changes require.' WHERE id='c615d425-a4ff-47d6-a172-5bbe9efd5952' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The use of a MIDI message to start, stop, or advance a cue, allowing one device to command another over a MIDI connection. A received note, control-change, or program-change message, or in the standardised MIDI Show Control protocol a system-exclusive command addressed to a specific controlled device, is mapped to a cue action so that a lighting console, sequencer, or timecode source can drive audio playback, or an audio system can trigger other equipment. This gives a common, manufacturer-independent means of synchronising and remotely firing cues across the separate systems of a production.' WHERE id='b4d2b1b9-aaa0-437b-a7ea-01476494cc32' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dedicated foldback or monitor feed supplied to the musical director or conductor, delivering a tailored mix of the elements they must hear to lead the performance, typically stage vocals together with any click or timecode reference and selected pit instruments, via a wedge, in-ear monitors, or a small local mixer at the podium. Because the musical director conducts to what the singers are actually doing on stage, this feed prioritises vocal intelligibility and synchronisation references over front-of-house balance and is mixed independently of the house system.' WHERE id='60f9c73e-aeee-4106-aa61-635676a68ff1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In theatrical sound reinforcement, the capture and reinforcement of a performer''s singing or spoken voice while that performer is out of the audience''s view — in the wings, a backstage vocal booth, a crossover, or another offstage position. It is typically accomplished with a dedicated fixed, handheld, or body-worn radio microphone feeding an assigned console input and routed to the main system so the offstage voice integrates with the onstage action. Offstage pickups serve off-stage solos, choral or announcement voices, and characters who sing or speak before entering; the operator manages level, timing, and spatial placement so the sound reads as coming from the intended part of the stage picture.' WHERE id='e6638a2c-b256-4b3a-afee-2d74088f1171' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The capture and reinforcement of a performer''s voice while that performer is in view on stage, most often via a body-worn radio microphone (lavalier or headset) assigned to that performer, or via area, boundary, or float microphones covering a region of the stage. Each onstage pickup is routed to its own console input so the operator can raise the appropriate microphone as the performer speaks or sings and manage gain-before-feedback, level, and equalization for the acoustics of the house. Onstage pickup is the core mechanism of musical-theatre vocal reinforcement, distinct from offstage pickup, orchestra inputs, and effects playback.' WHERE id='0f6d7026-df8c-4598-b591-1fbf952ec337' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The use of an Open Sound Control (OSC) message to initiate an action such as starting a cue or changing a parameter. OSC is an open, transport-independent, message-based protocol for communication among computers, synthesizers, and other multimedia devices, in which each message carries an address pattern beginning with a slash, a type-tag string describing its arguments, and the arguments themselves, and messages may be grouped into time-tagged bundles. In a show-control context a device sends or receives these messages, commonly over a UDP/IP network, so that an incoming OSC message mapped to a cue or control acts as its trigger, providing a flexible networked alternative to MIDI or contact-closure control.' WHERE id='d04a259b-719a-433c-89ed-092d2b8de62f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dedicated communication and cueing arrangement that lets the musical director and pit orchestra receive the visual and audio cues needed to stay synchronized with stage action and the show-control system. In practice it combines the conductor''s video monitors (stage-manager or camera feeds), tally or cue lights, a talkback or intercom path, and click-track or count-in feeds distributed to the players, so that entrances, tempo changes, and stops align with performers, automation, and any pre-recorded playback. The sound department typically provides and maintains the audio portions — click, talkback, and guide feeds — as part of the pit''s monitoring infrastructure.' WHERE id='317bb87c-110f-44a5-b0db-cc9b128ba029' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The monitor (foldback) feed provided to the pit orchestra so that musicians and the conductor hear the elements they need in balance — typically the reinforced stage vocals, other sections of the orchestra, click or guide tracks, and talkback. Derived as one or more auxiliary or matrix outputs from the mixing console, orchestra foldback is distributed to loudspeakers or in-ear feeds within the pit, with content and level tailored to the players and constrained to limit spill into open stage and audience microphones. It is the pit-orchestra counterpart of stage-monitor foldback and a core part of theatrical monitoring design.' WHERE id='e33340d3-90c9-47ca-b256-5ed81f80138b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A mixing-console input path assigned to a source originating in the pit orchestra — an individual instrument microphone or direct-injection (DI) feed, a section submix, or a keyboard or electronic-instrument output. Each orchestra input carries the usual channel processing (gain, filtering, equalization, dynamics) and is routed to orchestra subgroups, the main mix, and monitor sends so the operator can balance the pit against reinforced vocals. On large musicals the orchestra may occupy dozens of such inputs, often organized by section for grouped control and snapshot recall.' WHERE id='76bb16f6-72d7-4779-8e59-a079f6f8ac9d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The reproduction during a performance of pre-recorded orchestral material delivered as separate multitrack stems — for example strings, brass, percussion, or synthesizer parts — rather than as a single mixed track. Played from a show-playback system feeding a multichannel interface, the individual stems arrive on discrete console inputs so the mixer can balance, equalize, and route them like live sources: supplementing a reduced live pit, replacing absent instruments, or providing a fully virtual orchestra. Locking the playback to a click and to show control keeps the recorded material synchronized with the live conductor and stage action while preserving mix flexibility.' WHERE id='1f1475f6-1b87-4862-a654-d9c1c4ec7580' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A console subgroup (group bus) to which the individual pit-orchestra input channels are routed so the whole orchestra, or a section of it, can be controlled, processed, and balanced with a single fader. Subgrouping the orchestra lets the operator ride the overall pit level against the vocals, apply common processing or effects to the ensemble, and mute or recall the group cleanly, while retaining the individual channels for detailed balance. It is a standard element of musical-theatre mix architecture, typically paired with vocal and playback subgroups feeding the main output or an output matrix.' WHERE id='824fa70f-30b2-4acd-9c1d-bb0eae755e4a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The provision of monitor (foldback) audio within the orchestra pit so the conductor and musicians can hear the stage vocals, other instrumental sections, click or guide tracks, and talkback needed to stay together and in time with the performance. Implemented with pit loudspeakers, personal monitor mixers, or in-ear systems fed from auxiliary or matrix sends on the mixing console, pit monitoring must deliver adequate intelligibility and balance to the players while limiting spill into stage and audience microphones. It is the pit counterpart to onstage monitoring and a distinct subsystem within a show''s overall monitor design.' WHERE id='4e2334fe-c555-4468-9383-4a6853ac693b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A console setting, commonly called recall safe, that protects selected parameters and channels from being overwritten when a scene, snapshot, or preset is recalled. With a parameter made safe, its current value, for example a channel fader level, head-amplifier gain, or output patch, is excluded from the recall operation and retained, while all non-safe settings update to the stored scene. This lets an operator hold certain live settings constant across scene changes, such as keeping a manually ridden level or a temporary patch unaffected by an otherwise wholesale recall.' WHERE id='3722f094-bf16-4bbd-8a0f-b4127b1d85e1' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone placed in or over the orchestra pit specifically to capture the natural, blended acoustic sound of the ensemble as a whole — its section balance and room ambience — rather than to close-mic a single instrument. Its signal adds air, blend, and a sense of the acoustic orchestra to the reinforced mix, reinforces a lightly amplified or acoustic pit, or feeds monitors, archival recordings, and assistive-listening or broadcast feeds. Position and level are managed carefully to limit feedback and spill from stage sources while preserving a natural orchestral timbre.' WHERE id='66479485-522b-473c-94b5-48cbccc9b68b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dedicated monitor feed — and often an associated loudspeaker or in-ear system and video display — provided to the musical director in the orchestra pit. Its audio content is a purpose-built foldback mix, typically the reinforced stage vocals plus selected orchestra sections, click, and talkback, sent from an auxiliary or matrix output so the conductor can hear the singers and ensemble clearly enough to lead tempo and cue entrances. Frequently paired with video showing stage or backstage feeds, the conductor monitor is a critical link keeping the pit synchronized with the performers on stage.' WHERE id='f2a2f6e5-bc58-41d9-97c8-e584647bce9c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An acoustically isolated enclosure associated with the orchestra pit, used to separate a loud instrument — commonly drums, percussion, brass, or an amplified instrument — or, conversely, to protect a quiet source from spill. By containing or excluding sound, it reduces bleed into open microphones, improves mix control and gain-before-feedback, and helps limit noise exposure for nearby players. It may be a permanent architectural feature or a temporary structure of gobos and transparent panels, with talkback, monitoring, and sightline provisions so the isolated musician can still perform with the ensemble and see the conductor.' WHERE id='c165419f-b69d-4c29-a4be-534a16dffead' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The complete arrangement of microphones and direct-injection feeds used to capture the pit orchestra for reinforcement, including the choice, model, and placement of close, section, and ambience microphones, DI boxes for electronic and amplified instruments, and their assignment to console inputs and subgroups. A well-designed pit setup balances separation and gain-before-feedback against a natural, blended orchestral sound, works within the physical constraints and sightlines of the pit, and is documented on input and patch lists so it can be reproduced reliably from performance to performance.' WHERE id='a7d42eab-41b4-49df-b2a9-a446d7494167' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The ensemble of instrumental musicians that accompanies a staged musical, opera, or ballet from the orchestra pit, the lowered area between the front of the stage and the audience, so that the players and conductor remain largely out of the audience''s sightline while staying close to the performers. Its size and instrumentation are set by the production''s orchestration and by the pit''s dimensions, and in amplified musical theatre the ensemble is typically close-miked and mixed into the sound system rather than heard purely acoustically. The pit location keeps the live accompaniment acoustically and visually separated from the action onstage.' WHERE id='7667b1bf-adcc-4fd9-a1e5-87df2d4639ff' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A console subgroup (group bus) that combines the input channels of the pit orchestra — or a defined section of it — under a single fader for unified level control, processing, and muting within the mix. Routing the pit to a subgroup lets the operator balance the whole orchestra against reinforced vocals and playback, apply common processing or effects, and recall or mute the group cleanly while retaining individual channels for detailed adjustment. It is a standard building block of musical-theatre mix architecture, typically feeding the main output or an output matrix alongside vocal and playback subgroups.' WHERE id='2e3603b5-73c8-464a-b92b-97404dddabdb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A mixing-console input path assigned to an output of the show-playback system — the computer and interface that reproduces music, sound effects, and pre-recorded tracks. Bringing playback onto its own console input, rather than patching it straight to an output, lets the operator apply level, equalization, and dynamics, route the material to subgroups and the main mix, and fold it into the live balance and any output matrix. Multichannel playback occupies several such inputs, one per discrete track or stem.' WHERE id='59d88d0b-3d0f-43db-a537-bfabe45719ac' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A console subgroup (group bus) to which the input channels carrying show-playback material — music, sound effects, and pre-recorded tracks or stems — are routed for unified level control, processing, and muting. Grouping playback lets the operator balance all reproduced content against live vocals and orchestra with a single fader, apply common processing, and mute or recall it cleanly, while retaining individual playback channels for detailed control. It is a standard element of theatrical mix architecture, feeding the main output or an output matrix alongside vocal and orchestra subgroups.' WHERE id='8c9e76fe-7163-46f1-95d5-bee79c913935' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The microphone assigned to a principal — a lead or featured performer — in a reinforced production, almost always a body-worn radio microphone (a concealed lavalier or a headset/hairline element) with a dedicated receiver and console input reserved for that role. Principal microphones receive the closest attention in fitting, backup provision, and mix operation because these performers carry the most sung and spoken text; they are distinguished from ensemble microphones allocated to chorus members and from the shared, role-based allocations used for swings and understudies. Assigning one input per principal lets the operator ride, equalize, and cue each lead individually throughout the show.' WHERE id='53169767-645f-46b9-9a1b-3220fb72eb5e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In theatre, the sound practitioner responsible for realizing and running a production''s sound — the installation, maintenance, and operation of the system rather than its creative design. Working under the sound designer, this engineer patches and tests the rig, manages radio microphones and their fitting and frequency coordination, maintains the console and the playback and networking equipment, troubleshoots faults, and on many productions mixes or supervises the mixing of performances. The title overlaps in practice with sound engineer, sound operator, and head of sound, and is distinct from the film-industry production sound mixer who records dialogue on set.' WHERE id='0fcb83f8-2042-4aa2-9d0c-4d75ba5a94ef' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A scene-memory function on a digital mixing console that excludes designated channels, parameters, or processing blocks from being overwritten when a stored scene (snapshot) is recalled. Any element placed in recall safe retains its current live settings while the rest of the console updates to the recalled data, allowing an operator to hold, for example, a channel''s fader constant across a run of scene changes. It acts as a global filter applied to every recall, distinct from the per-scene focus or scope filter that limits what an individual scene stores or restores.' WHERE id='cdadb8ba-9d3f-4ba3-823d-3942b70b207c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A scene-memory filter, on some consoles called focus, that defines which channels and parameter groups an individual stored scene will update when it is recalled, in contrast to recall safe, which is applied globally across all scenes. By narrowing a scene''s scope, the programmer can build a cue that changes only selected parameters, such as the fader levels of a group of vocal channels, while leaving all other console settings untouched, which underpins layered theatrical snapshot programming.' WHERE id='fbc9b9cd-2c63-478e-a61c-23381bcf0d14' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The timed, gradual interpolation of console parameters, typically fader levels and sometimes pan and other continuous controls, from the values of the outgoing scene to those of an incoming scene during a scene or snapshot recall, rather than an instantaneous jump. The duration, and on some consoles the response curve, are stored with the scene so that recalling the next cue produces a smooth audible transition; parameters that cannot be ramped, such as routing or discrete switches, change immediately at the recall point.' WHERE id='35407689-8faf-4fbc-9e27-424db78a706c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The programmed duration over which a console executes the crossfade between one stored scene and the next during a recall, governing how quickly fader and other rampable parameters move to their new values. Set per scene, it lets a theatrical operator build cues that change the mix over a defined interval, matching a lighting fade or a musical transition, rather than switching states abruptly; a transition time of zero yields an immediate snap to the recalled settings.' WHERE id='c15bbefe-8454-4c04-b0df-eb0553c8732e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A change in a fader''s position that is planned and written into the sound operator''s marked script or cue sheet to occur at a specific line, lyric, or moment of stage action, rather than being improvised. Scripted moves — bringing a performer''s microphone up before a line and down after it, riding the orchestra under dialogue, or setting a level for a musical number — are the fundamental actions of live theatrical mixing, annotated in the prompt copy so the mix stays consistent from show to show and can be executed or learned by any operator. On automated consoles such moves may instead be stored within scene or snapshot cues, but the scripted-move concept remains the organizing logic of the mix.' WHERE id='d30b08e8-def8-4fd6-adb2-f7a9ca6235ae' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A muting or unmuting of a specific channel or group that is planned and written into the operator''s marked script or cue sheet to occur at a defined line, lyric, or action. Scripted mutes keep microphones live only when their performer is speaking or singing — reducing spill, feedback risk, and extraneous noise — and open or close effect, orchestra, or playback paths at precise moments. Like scripted fader moves they are annotated in the prompt copy for consistent, repeatable operation, and on automated consoles are commonly encoded within scene or snapshot cues.' WHERE id='1a9a30f2-58a4-47ad-9dae-1432627c96f4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A single, numbered instruction in the running order of a performance that directs the sound department to execute a specific action at a defined moment, such as starting or stopping a sound effect or music track, recalling a mix scene, or triggering a device, on the signal of the stage manager or as an automated follow. Cues are recorded in the prompt book and the operator''s cue sheet in performance sequence, giving every show a repeatable, documented list of sound events synchronized with the action on stage.' WHERE id='1a308a00-28e5-483c-b88c-67ef6541e200' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An arrangement of hardware and software that links otherwise independent production subsystems, including sound playback, lighting, video, automation, and stage machinery, so that their cues can be triggered and coordinated from a common point or in a defined sequence. Communication typically uses standard protocols such as MIDI Show Control, which conveys commands like GO, STOP, and RESUME between controllers, allowing a single operator action or a timecode stream to fire synchronized cues across multiple disciplines during a performance.' WHERE id='cf97133b-3e43-45df-beda-a466fa8efa33' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A preserved, catalogued copy of the complete data that defines a production''s sound — typically the mixing console''s show file, with all settings, patches, scenes or snapshots, and cue lists, together with the playback system''s workspace, media, and supporting documentation. Distinct from a routine day-to-day backup, an archive is a stable, dated record kept for the longer term so a production can be restored, remounted, toured, or reproduced by another company, and so a known-good state exists for reference. Good practice keeps archives on multiple media in more than one location and records the software and firmware versions needed to reload them.' WHERE id='2d99c174-1b96-4a22-bf3e-edf93f0caadf' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A duplicate copy of the current console show file and the playback system''s workspace and media, made so the production''s programmed state can be recovered quickly if the primary device fails, a file is corrupted, or a change must be undone. Backups are taken routinely — commonly after each significant programming session or performance and onto removable media or a second device — and are a basic safeguard in theatrical operation, complementing hardware redundancy. They differ from a long-term archive in being frequent working copies aimed at rapid restoration rather than permanent preservation.' WHERE id='db20a3a4-000e-4de0-8dbd-4e03a7d81f32' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The timed change from one stored console snapshot to the next when a snapshot is recalled, during which rampable parameters such as fader levels crossfade over a programmed duration while non-rampable settings switch at the recall instant. The transition time is saved with each snapshot so that a mix can move smoothly between preset states in step with the staged action, a core mechanism of automated theatrical mixing on snapshot-capable digital consoles.' WHERE id='1101b8d5-5d59-4d2b-acee-4f2f38b0e9cb' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The member of the sound department who runs the sound console and playback during rehearsals and performances, executing each cue, including mix-scene recalls, effect and music playback, and live level adjustments, on the stage manager''s signal or as an automated follow. Working from a prepared cue sheet, the operator is responsible for consistent reproduction of the sound designer''s plot at every performance and for responding to changes and faults during a live show.' WHERE id='58704a8a-c362-4631-a3f0-66f48277e657' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A specialist who prepares and enters the data that a digital console and playback system use to run a show, translating the sound designer''s intentions into stored scenes, snapshots, routing, processing settings, and cue lists. During technical rehearsals the programmer builds and edits this cue structure alongside the designer, so that in performance the operator can reproduce complex mix changes reliably by advancing through the programmed sequence.' WHERE id='788dd37b-ebf5-41b1-b621-41e9705b9d58' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A cue that calls for the playback of a specific sound effect, such as an environmental, mechanical, or spot effect like a doorbell, thunder, or a telephone ring, at a defined point in the action. It specifies the source material, its routing to particular loudspeakers, and its level, and is entered in the performance running order so the operator can trigger it on the stage manager''s signal or as an automated follow to another cue.' WHERE id='771de826-5381-4d5d-8dce-012c5921cae6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A dedicated audio send routed to loudspeakers or in-ear monitors placed for the performers and musicians on or beside the stage, as distinct from the front-of-house mix directed at the audience. Derived from auxiliary or matrix outputs of the console, each feed carries a balance of vocals, instruments, and cue material tailored to what a particular position on stage needs to hear for timing, pitch, and coordination.' WHERE id='164df393-f9e0-4a53-b953-aa42e3416452' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A cue whose function is to halt playback of a currently running element rather than to start a new one, targeting a specific sound, a group of sounds, or all active output. In cue-based playback software it references the target to be stopped and may apply a fade rather than an abrupt cut; it is used to end effects or music at a scripted point or to provide a controlled means of silencing the system on the stage manager''s signal.' WHERE id='89ae624c-d850-481f-b0e1-df45727db127' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A pre-recorded audio element played back to reinforce or enrich a live theatrical sound, adding body, detail, or ambience that the live sources alone do not provide, for example recorded crowd, added low end under a live band, or layered vocal texture. Triggered as part of the show''s playback in synchrony with the live performance, it is mixed in at a supporting level so the augmentation is perceived as part of the natural sound rather than as a separate, foregrounded cue.' WHERE id='0109b21a-83d3-4d2c-b734-402ff0573679' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The microphone provision for a swing — a performer who covers several ensemble, and sometimes principal, tracks and goes on for whichever absent cast member they are covering. Because a swing may play different roles at different performances, their sound is handled either by fitting them with a body-worn radio microphone patched to whichever role''s console input is required that night, or by keeping a dedicated swing receiver and channel that the operator assigns and balances to match the covered track. Managing swing microphones is a routine part of running a long-running musical, ensuring a substitute is reinforced identically to the performer they replace.' WHERE id='27fd746b-b655-4ef6-9028-68008fef15fa' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The live mixing carried out during technical rehearsals, when the sound system, radio microphones, orchestra, and playback are integrated with the staging, lighting, and automation for the first time. During tech the operator and sound designer build and refine the show mix — setting channel levels, equalization, and effects, writing fader moves and mutes into the script or programming scenes and snapshots, and checking coverage, gain-before-feedback, and cue timing — so this is not a finished performance mix but a work in progress established and documented section by section. It is the stage at which the operational mix for the run is largely created.' WHERE id='f2be4523-5a69-4a9e-8002-8a48c1c1b86b' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A live mixing console configured for the demands of theatrical sound, characterized by extensive scene or snapshot automation, high input capacity, and flexible group, VCA, and matrix routing for feeding multiple loudspeaker zones. It allows a single operator to store a complete mix state for each moment of a production and recall those states as cues during the performance, supporting the frequent, precisely timed changes that dialogue reinforcement and musical theatre require.' WHERE id='e1aff01b-8c98-4644-9538-d70d0a54fb96' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A sound practitioner who installs, maintains, and operates the sound systems used in live theatre — mixing consoles, loudspeaker systems, radio and wired microphones, playback and show-control computers, and the networking and cabling that connect them. Under the direction of the sound designer, the theatre sound engineer patches and tests the rig, coordinates radio-microphone frequencies, fits and maintains performer microphones, runs or supports the performance mix, and troubleshoots faults during the run. The term is used broadly and overlaps with sound operator, sound technician, and head of sound, with the exact duties determined by the size of the production and the sound crew.' WHERE id='53738933-bd25-4965-b074-39270c7ad868' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The process by which a playback device, console, or cue engine slaves its own playback position to an incoming timecode signal, typically SMPTE/EBU linear timecode or MIDI timecode, continuously matching it frame for frame so that its output stays locked to an external time reference. Chasing lets sound playback run in tight synchronization with video, an automated band, or another controlling system, and relocate automatically if the master timecode jumps to a new position.' WHERE id='b7a155e6-ff20-4343-9f7e-55bfaaf05c33' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A mechanism that fires a cue automatically when incoming timecode reaches a specified frame value, so that playback and mix events execute at exact, repeatable moments defined against a master time reference rather than on a manual GO. Widely used where sound must lock to video or a click-driven musical performance, it maps individual cues to timecode positions so the show advances itself as the timecode stream plays.' WHERE id='3026a955-64c9-4153-b1b6-33527817e9dc' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A cue that introduces music or sustained sound played underneath spoken dialogue or stage action to support its mood, pace, or emotional tone, as opposed to a discrete spot effect or a scene-change transition. Typically brought in and out at a controlled level so it colors the scene without masking the voices, an underscore cue is timed to the performance and often faded or stopped on a later cue as the dramatic need changes.' WHERE id='517eb97a-a84e-482c-a5bc-7a4b70011b3f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The microphone provision for an understudy — a performer, usually already in the company, prepared to take over a principal role when the regular performer is unavailable. When an understudy goes on, they are fitted with a body-worn radio microphone routed to the console input normally used for that role, so the mix, levels, and cues written for the character apply unchanged; alternatively a dedicated understudy receiver and channel is patched in to match the part. Handling understudy microphones is a standard part of running a reinforced production, ensuring the substitute is reinforced identically to the performer they replace.' WHERE id='4d072bd3-0e10-4cf7-b6b2-5c1343e70f24' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A group, or subgroup, bus on a mixing console to which the individual vocal or radio-microphone channels are routed so that their combined level, and often shared processing, can be controlled with a single fader. Grouping the vocals in this way lets the operator raise or lower all voices together relative to the band during a musical number, apply common dynamics or effects to the vocal blend, and manage a large channel count as one coherent element.' WHERE id='18d851c1-a509-4bc7-af6b-0af23200d7e0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A cue or cue attribute that inserts a defined timed delay into an automated sequence, holding for a set interval after the preceding cue before the following cue is allowed to execute. It lets a programmer build self-running strings of events with precise gaps, so that an effect follows another a fixed number of seconds later without a manual GO, and is distinct from a hold that waits for an operator or stage-manager signal.' WHERE id='2fe35da2-376a-4960-828b-3885568da9c0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Inner groove distortion is the rise in tracing and harmonic distortion that occurs toward the end of a disc record''s side, as the modulated groove approaches the centre. On a constant-angular-velocity disc the groove''s linear speed past the stylus falls steadily as the radius decreases, so a given audio frequency is written into an ever-shorter wavelength; when that wavelength becomes comparable to the finite dimensions of the reproducing stylus tip, the tip can no longer follow the groove walls accurately and tracing distortion increases, most audibly on loud high-frequency content such as sibilant vocals and cymbals. Cutting engineers mitigate it by limiting inner-radius level and high-frequency energy and by managing side length, while playback tracing is improved by fine-line stylus profiles and correct alignment.' WHERE id='18336c90-788a-42dd-b4ed-5aec16f76909' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Vinyl sequencing is the arrangement of a release''s tracks across the sides of a disc, and their placement within each side, undertaken as part of preparing a programme for disc cutting rather than as a purely artistic running order. Because a disc''s playing time, achievable level, and distortion all worsen toward the inner grooves and are traded against one another, the mastering engineer balances total time per side against the desired cutting level and tends to place shorter, quieter, or less high-frequency-dense material toward the end of a side while reserving the outer grooves for the loudest, most demanding tracks. Good sequencing therefore reconciles the artistic order with the physical constraints of the format so that every track cuts and plays back cleanly.' WHERE id='b30003c8-3647-499a-83f1-09befbd01367' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The technique of capturing an acoustic piano with one or more microphones for sound reinforcement or recording, with the type, number, and position of the microphones chosen according to the instrument''s radiation pattern, the desired tonal balance, and the acoustics of the room. On a grand piano, microphones are commonly placed inside the raised lid over the bass and treble strings, often as a spaced or coincident pair to preserve the wide frequency range and stereo image, while an upright is typically miked over the open top or through the removed lower front panel. Placement distance trades tonal detail and attack against captured room ambience, and in live reinforcement it is further constrained by available gain before feedback and by leakage from other stage sources, so closer placement raises level and isolation at the cost of a narrower tonal picture.' WHERE id='935c4132-32e4-471d-8397-093b94af6524' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone positioned at or serving the altar in a house of worship to reinforce, and where required broadcast or record, the speech and liturgical action of clergy at that fixed station. It is typically a low-profile condenser—an overhead hanging capsule or a miniature boundary or gooseneck type—chosen and placed for even pickup across the working area while minimising visual intrusion and rejecting loudspeaker energy and other platform sources to preserve gain before feedback. Because the altar is a location served rather than a single close talker, consistent level and coverage across the celebrant''s range of movement govern placement more than close-miking detail.' WHERE id='14981b5a-6b7a-429d-8b56-a79421774df2' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A set of two microphones deployed to capture the diffuse, reflected sound of a space, such as room reverberation, audience response, or the blended field of an ensemble, rather than one specific near source. The two capsules are arranged in an established stereo configuration, for example a spaced pair or a coincident or near-coincident pair such as XY or ORTF, so that their combined output conveys a sense of spaciousness and a left-to-right image on reproduction. Positioned at a distance where reflected energy dominates the direct sound, the pair is usually mixed in at low level beneath the close microphones to add depth and a natural impression of the venue.' WHERE id='dd0cf76a-d732-46ed-9710-7a66dc3ad778' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone mounted at the ambon (ambo), the elevated reading desk from which scripture and the homily are proclaimed, used to reinforce a single talker''s speech at that fixed liturgical station. It is commonly a cardioid or supercardioid gooseneck or podium condenser positioned close to the reader for high gain before feedback and strong rejection of platform loudspeakers and ambient noise, with capsule placement set for consistent level despite variation in talker height and distance from the stand.' WHERE id='6d0ef08c-8b9e-4498-b352-2dd6b2640548' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='An enclosed, acoustically damped box that houses a guitar or bass loudspeaker together with a close microphone so that the amplifier can be driven at its intended level while very little acoustic energy escapes into the room. The sealed, absorbent-lined enclosure attenuates the miked cabinet''s output substantially, letting a valve amplifier reach its desired distortion and tone on a quiet stage and yielding a captured signal essentially free of stage leakage. It is used where open backline volume would compromise monitoring, feedback control, or a broadcast or recording mix, and it differs from a reactive load box in that it still relies on an acoustic loudspeaker and a microphone to produce its sound.' WHERE id='8e26ced5-e372-485d-bcbf-a2cab0920095' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone or microphone system aimed at the audience or congregation to capture applause, singing, spoken responses, and the general acoustic life of the room for broadcast, streaming, or recording, rather than to reinforce sound within the room itself. It supplies the crowd and ambience component of a program mix so that remote listeners perceive the presence and reaction of the live audience, and it is typically a directional or boundary type placed to favour audience energy while rejecting the main loudspeakers to limit feedback and coloration. Its level is ridden by the operator to convey participation without letting house sound or room noise dominate the program.' WHERE id='9d1885ce-3e10-425e-8ee0-20f3d0ac8801' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A signal-processing method in which a dedicated mixer or algorithm continuously and automatically manages the gain of multiple live microphones, attenuating or muting channels that are not carrying wanted speech and passing those that are. By reducing the number of simultaneously open microphones it lowers accumulated noise and reverberation and raises attainable gain before feedback, with each halving of open microphones recovering on the order of three decibels, while gating or gain-sharing logic makes transitions between talkers inaudible. It is widely applied to lecterns, altars, panel positions, and choir microphones, where many sources share a system but only a few are active at any moment.' WHERE id='a3df46b0-d3b6-4a65-887f-0c43f801680c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In live sound, the practice and the physical means of acoustically containing or reducing the direct sound radiated by backline instrument amplifiers and drums on a worship platform so that stage level does not dominate the reinforced mix or spill into open vocal and speech microphones. Techniques include enclosing guitar and bass amplifiers in isolation baffles or cabinets, screening or shielding the drum kit, lowering or angling amplifier radiation, and substituting direct injection or amplifier modelling for miked cabinets, all intended to reduce stage sound-pressure level, improve gain before feedback, and give the mix engineer control of the balance heard by the congregation.' WHERE id='61bd60b1-b5ce-4f06-b326-4cf01257f319' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A tall loudspeaker column containing multiple individually amplified transducers whose signals are shaped by digital signal processing, applying independent delay, level, and filtering to each driver so that the array''s vertical radiation lobe can be tilted, narrowed, or split electronically without physically moving the enclosure. Controlling the relative phase and amplitude across the aperture lets the system direct sound onto the seating plane and away from reflective ceilings and rear walls, improving the direct-to-reverberant ratio and speech intelligibility in acoustically difficult, reverberant rooms such as churches. Because the steering is performed in software, coverage can be optimised and reconfigured for a given room through the array''s tuning parameters.' WHERE id='4b3d58c8-d7ef-46ae-9476-c92c226ba626' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The technical room from which a live or recorded program is directed, switched, and mixed, housing the vision switcher, audio console, monitoring, communications, and record or transmission equipment together with the operators who run them. In a venue or house-of-worship installation it is the space where camera feeds and the audio program for streaming, television, or archive are combined, monitored on calibrated loudspeakers and displays, and quality-controlled before distribution, kept acoustically and visually isolated from the room being covered. It is distinguished from the front-of-house mix position, which serves the live audience, by its focus on the outgoing broadcast or recorded signal.' WHERE id='af8fd4c9-cd7b-4725-b37d-95684f384f5a' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone assigned to the celebrant—the presiding minister or priest—to reinforce that individual''s speech and chant throughout a service. It is frequently a body-worn wireless lavalier or headset condenser that maintains a fixed mouth-to-capsule distance as the celebrant moves between altar, chair, and other stations, giving stable level and intelligibility; where movement is restricted a fixed altar or ambon microphone may serve instead. Directional pattern, placement, and gain structure prioritise consistent speech pickup, hands-free operation, and rejection of loudspeaker energy to preserve gain before feedback.' WHERE id='981cd013-9e28-4718-9693-c1d46b4a050d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone positioned to cover the choir area—the section occupied by the ensemble—rather than an individual singer, capturing the massed choral sound for reinforcement, broadcast, or recording. It is usually a condenser of cardioid or hypercardioid pattern hung overhead or placed on tall stands in front of and above the singers, with the number and spacing of units set to give even coverage of the section. Height and aiming trade blend and reach against pickup of accompanying instruments and the congregation and against the limited gain before feedback typical of a distant, distributed vocal source.' WHERE id='3b5d84ad-145e-4d92-a31e-f949d528c28d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A monitor (foldback) feed provided to a choir so its members can hear a chosen balance of themselves, the accompaniment, and the worship leader while singing. It is delivered through stage wedges, distributed low-level loudspeakers, or in-ear monitors from a dedicated mix, and is set to give the ensemble timing and pitch reference without generating feedback into the open choir-area microphones. Because a choir occupies a wide area, even coverage and controlled level across the section are the primary design constraints.' WHERE id='277a73c4-97ae-4005-bf1a-6b0441b6c537' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The maximum acoustic gain a sound-reinforcement system can provide to a choir''s microphones before the onset of sustained feedback, set by the loop gain around the microphone-to-loudspeaker-to-microphone path. It is characteristically low for choir reinforcement because the microphones sit relatively far from many distributed, moving singers, which lowers the source level at each capsule, while several microphones are open at once and each doubling of open microphones costs on the order of three decibels of available gain. Practical gain before feedback for a choir is therefore improved by fewer and closer microphones, directional polar patterns, loudspeaker placement and aiming that avoid the microphones, and equalization of the dominant ringing modes.' WHERE id='2f44f9a7-1c84-49d6-9e00-a28210b10f34' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone optimised for capturing a group of singers as a blended whole, most commonly a small condenser suspended above and in front of the choir on a thin cable or carried on a tall stand and angled down into the ensemble. Such microphones typically use a cardioid or supercardioid pattern and are placed so that one capsule spans a defined width of singers, with adjacent coverage overlapped, giving even pickup while keeping the number of open microphones low to preserve gain before feedback. The aim is a natural, homogeneous choral image rather than isolation of individual voices, trading maximum level for blend and low visual intrusion.' WHERE id='ec6dd4d4-47fb-486a-bd1e-d55fb4afe490' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The sound-reinforcement of a choir: raising and distributing the level of a massed vocal ensemble through a loudspeaker system so the congregation hears it clearly over the room and the accompaniment. It combines area or overhead choir microphones with careful gain structure and equalisation to lift a relatively diffuse, distributed source that is prone to low gain before feedback and to leakage from organ and instruments, with microphone count, placement, and processing chosen to preserve choral blend while achieving intelligibility and adequate level.' WHERE id='c3522ed5-d97c-41bc-882e-a1154b7d212f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A technique for reinforcing or recording a choir by dividing it into contiguous zones, by voice part or by physical block of singers, and assigning a microphone to each rather than relying on a single distant capsule. Each microphone covers a manageable arc of the ensemble, placed and angled so that adjacent coverage areas overlap smoothly, which raises usable level and consistency across a wide or uneven group while the total count is kept as low as practical to conserve gain before feedback. Balancing the sections individually at the console then corrects for weaker parts or an asymmetric layout.' WHERE id='41b9e6d4-2b24-4969-b85f-84a579e48186' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone placed close to a specific portion of a choir, such as a particular voice section, a soloist within the group, or a weak or distant block of singers, to add level, presence, or definition to that area over and above the general choir pickup. Analogous to spot or accent microphones in orchestral work, it supplements the main choir microphones rather than replacing them and is mixed in at modest level to reinforce detail while preserving the overall blend. Because it sits near its target it can improve intelligibility and gain for that section, but its contribution must be weighed against the added open-microphone count and possible phase interaction with the main pickup.' WHERE id='564521ba-bcfe-4158-be9b-12ae50953c4c' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='Unwanted mechanical noise, including rustle, scratching, thumps, and high-frequency swishes, introduced when a lavalier or miniature microphone contacts or is disturbed by clothing, jewellery, or skin. It arises from fabric rubbing against the capsule or cable, from the microphone moving relative to the wearer, and from structure-borne vibration transmitted through the mounting, and it is aggravated by stiff or synthetic fabrics and by concealment under garments. It is mitigated by secure, decoupled mounting that isolates the capsule, placement away from moving fabric, protective mounts and clips, and careful choice of position on the wearer, and its control is a central constraint in presenter and liturgical miking.' WHERE id='cfe3436a-448a-438d-b8fc-c4e4eab11fa7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone placed to capture the sound of the assembled congregation—principally its singing and spoken responses—typically for broadcast, streaming, or recording feeds rather than for reinforcement back into the room. It is usually a condenser of cardioid or omnidirectional pattern hung overhead or aimed toward the seating from the platform, positioned to gather a representative blend of the assembly while limiting pickup of the main loudspeakers so as to avoid feedback and hollow, echoing colouration in the transmitted mix.' WHERE id='a0d0fa67-3f1d-4f6c-8344-e03ee9165d30' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone serving pickup of the congregation, a variant designation for a microphone aimed at the assembly to capture its collective singing and spoken responses, most often for broadcast, streaming, or recording rather than for reinforcement into the room itself. It is commonly an overhead-hung or platform-mounted condenser positioned for a representative blend of the seated assembly, with pattern and aiming chosen to minimise pickup of the main loudspeakers and preserve a clean, feedback-free feed.' WHERE id='6a2af25b-82e7-40d3-8763-bfdc188f9341' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The sound-reinforcement of congregational singing and spoken response, in which the assembly''s own sound is captured and reproduced—commonly through distributed or delayed loudspeakers, or into broadcast and recording feeds—so that participants and remote listeners perceive a fuller, more unified corporate sound. It depends on congregation-area microphones and careful gain and delay management, because the source is diffuse, low in level per person, and readily coupled to the main loudspeakers, making feedback control and natural timing the governing constraints.' WHERE id='014fa292-efe5-4f26-a103-88db8f1c1d78' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A method of connecting an electric or electronic instrument directly to a mixing console or recorder without a microphone or loudspeaker, usually by way of a direct injection box that converts the instrument''s high-impedance, unbalanced signal to a low-impedance, balanced, microphone-level feed suitable for long cable runs. The box presents an appropriate load to a pickup or preamplifier output, minimises noise and hum, and commonly provides a ground-lift switch and a parallel through output so the instrument can also feed a stage amplifier. It is used for basses, keyboards, acoustic-electric guitars, and electronic sources to obtain a clean, isolated signal free of the stage level and leakage that miking would introduce.' WHERE id='fb000e11-ee33-4b99-b2b8-216549b9c2e5' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A partial or full barrier placed around a drum kit, typically clear acrylic panels forming a shield and sometimes extended with a ceiling and absorptive treatment, used to reduce the acoustic level of the kit reaching the room, other stage microphones, and the congregation. A simple shield reflects and blocks direct high-frequency energy on the audience or vocal side, while a more complete enclosure with a lid and internal absorption controls a wider range and reduces leakage into open microphones, improving mix control and lowering overall stage volume. Because an enclosure traps sound around the player, it is usually combined with internal absorption and dedicated monitoring to manage the drummer''s own acoustic environment.' WHERE id='8515f880-c84a-4f19-8d99-e407cd938bb7' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A small, acoustically isolated room or fully enclosed structure in which a drum kit is played so that its sound is substantially prevented from entering the surrounding space and from leaking into other microphones. Unlike an open drum shield, a booth provides separation on all sides through walls, ceiling, door, and a treated interior, achieving high transmission loss so the kit can be captured in isolation for recording, broadcast, or a low-stage-volume live mix. The enclosed volume is acoustically treated and requires dedicated monitoring, ventilation, and sightlines for the player to remain usable in performance.' WHERE id='c01dea6b-32df-4156-9f66-e5741bc67613' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A very small microphone worn on the ear, in which a slim boom or capsule is supported by a loop or hook over the ear and positioned near the corner of the mouth to capture the wearer''s voice hands-free. Lighter and less visually intrusive than a full headworn microphone, it uses a miniature omnidirectional or directional capsule and is typically fed to a bodypack wireless transmitter, holding a fairly constant source-to-capsule distance as the head moves and so delivering consistent level and good gain before feedback. It is favoured for presenters, clergy, and performers who need discreet, stable pickup while keeping both hands free.' WHERE id='b33fb375-a07c-4348-9e67-da3dbeb00343' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A percussion instrument in which acoustic drums and cymbals are replaced by trigger pads and cymbal controllers whose sensors detect strikes and send signals to a sound module, or brain, that generates or plays back sampled or synthesized drum sounds. Striking a pad produces a control signal encoding which pad was hit and how hard, from which the module renders the corresponding voice and typically outputs audio through line outputs or headphones and control data over MIDI. Because its level is set electronically rather than acoustically, it is widely used where low stage volume, consistent tone, and a direct connection to the console are required.' WHERE id='6b50574d-a807-4599-b62b-1d7ff24a1f8f' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A keyboard instrument that produces organ tones by electronic means rather than by wind blowing through pipes, generating and combining waveforms, historically with tonewheels or analog oscillators and now commonly through digital synthesis or sampling, under the control of drawbars or stops, one or more manuals, and often a pedalboard. Classic tonewheel designs such as the Hammond form each note by summing electrically generated harmonics selected with drawbars, frequently coloured by vibrato or chorus, percussion, and an external rotary loudspeaker. The instrument outputs an electrical audio signal that is amplified and reproduced through loudspeakers, providing organ voicing without the space and installation of a pipe organ.' WHERE id='919f88d5-7cc7-48b0-afb5-435df237d754' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone designed to be held in the hand for singing or speech, built with an internal shock mount, an integral windscreen and grille, and usually a cardioid or supercardioid pattern to reduce handling noise, popping, and feedback. Vocal handhelds are commonly rugged dynamic or condenser types voiced with a presence rise and tailored low-frequency response suited to close use, and the tight polar pattern combined with a close working distance maximises direct sound and gain before feedback while rejecting off-axis stage sound. They are available wired or as the head of a wireless system and are the standard tool for lead vocalists, worship leaders, and speakers who move about.' WHERE id='8958bd20-b954-4d6f-a258-52c01d4ea983' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone, usually a small cardioid or supercardioid condenser, suspended from a ceiling, truss, or wire on its own thin cable so that it hangs above and in front of a choir or ensemble and is angled down into the group. Suspension positions the capsule at an effective height and distance for even coverage of a block of singers while keeping cabling and hardware nearly invisible, addressing sightline and aesthetic constraints in worship spaces. Because it is fixed and relatively distant, it is used in numbers matched to the choir''s width, with coverage overlapped and the count minimised to preserve gain before feedback.' WHERE id='4dc7f43e-41bd-43d6-8bfd-6ef540965377' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone mounted on a lightweight frame worn on the head, with a boom that positions a miniature capsule a fixed short distance from the mouth so that the source-to-capsule geometry stays constant as the wearer moves. By maintaining close, consistent placement it delivers stable level and tone and high gain before feedback while leaving the hands free, and it typically uses an omnidirectional or directional miniature capsule feeding a bodypack transmitter. It is used by presenters, clergy, theatrical performers, and worship leaders, and offers more secure positioning than an ear-worn earset at the cost of slightly greater visibility.' WHERE id='bad79473-4faf-4f14-8636-9e3914e0d87d' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone provided at a lectern or podium for speech, most commonly a gooseneck design in which a small directional capsule is carried on a flexible or semi-rigid stem mounted to the lectern surface and bent to sit near the talker''s mouth. Directional patterns and close placement favour the speaker''s voice while rejecting room reflections and loudspeaker sound to preserve gain before feedback and intelligibility, and a shock mount with internal pop protection limits thumps and plosives. A fixed lectern microphone suits a talker who remains at the stand and is often integrated with automatic mixing when several positions share a system.' WHERE id='bc4ad556-58a2-465e-b943-b245c249eaa6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A loudspeaker system that produces its characteristic swirling timbre by mechanically rotating its sound-radiating elements, classically a treble horn, one flare of which acts as a counterweight, together with a drum-ported bass rotor driven by separate motors, so that listeners hear cyclic Doppler pitch modulation, amplitude tremolo, and shifting reflections. The rotors can be switched between a slow chorale speed and a fast tremolo speed, and the audible ramp as they spin up and down is part of the effect. Originally built to enliven electronic organ tone, the rotary speaker and its emulations remain a defining element of organ and vintage keyboard sound.' WHERE id='391f8a94-c766-4f42-9651-17c8e302f042' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The closeness of a microphone to its intended sound source and the acoustic consequences that follow from that distance. Reducing the source-to-capsule distance raises the direct-sound level relative to room reverberation and to other sources, improving the direct-to-reverberant ratio, isolation, and gain before feedback in accordance with the inverse-square falloff of direct sound, and with directional pressure-gradient microphones close placement also produces the proximity effect, a rise in low-frequency response as distance decreases. Choosing the working distance therefore trades tonal balance, spill, and feedback margin against a more distant, natural perspective, and it is a primary variable in placing both speech and music microphones.' WHERE id='e6f9b07c-6ec2-4645-9934-6199b996f7a6' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In church architecture, the main longitudinal body of the building where the congregation gathers, extending from the entrance at the west end toward the chancel and sanctuary and often flanked by aisles separated from it by rows of columns or piers. As the largest volume occupied by worshippers, the nave chiefly determines a worship room''s acoustics, its length, height, and hard masonry surfaces producing the long reverberation typical of churches, so it governs loudspeaker coverage, intelligibility, and feedback conditions for sound reinforcement. It is distinguished from the chancel and sanctuary, the areas around the altar reserved for clergy and liturgical action.' WHERE id='fe939629-0a62-4516-9f3b-e8485fb1c490' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The count of microphones simultaneously active, unmuted and contributing, in a sound-reinforcement system at a given moment, abbreviated NOM. Because each additional open microphone adds to the total picked-up noise, reverberation, and feedback-loop energy, increasing the number of open microphones reduces available gain before feedback and degrades clarity, with each doubling of open microphones lowering potential acoustic gain by approximately three decibels. Minimising the number of open microphones, whether manually or through automatic microphone mixing, is therefore a core strategy for maximising intelligibility and headroom in multi-microphone speech and worship systems.' WHERE id='002dd0ad-7b8b-4f8a-ab11-80308e63b51e' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone used to capture a pipe or electronic organ for reinforcement, broadcast, or recording, positioned according to the instrument''s spatially dispersed sound sources and the room acoustics. For a pipe organ the microphones—usually condensers—are placed at a distance that integrates the output of widely separated ranks and includes the reverberant field that forms part of the instrument''s character, whereas a console-based or speaker-fed electronic organ may be taken by close microphones or a direct line output. Placement balances tonal fullness and blend against excessive room reverberation and, in a live setting, against leakage and feedback.' WHERE id='a19e1771-a8dc-4129-9ad1-071d0bc22dde' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The sound-reinforcement of an organ: augmenting or distributing its level through a loudspeaker system, most often to support congregational singing or to convey the instrument evenly in spaces where its acoustic output is unbalanced. Because a pipe organ is a spatially distributed, wide-bandwidth source strongly coupled to room reverberation, reinforcement emphasises distant or multiple microphones, substantial low-frequency capability, and careful equalisation and gain structure to add level and evenness without coloration or feedback; electronic organs are more often reinforced from a direct output.' WHERE id='d60cf860-9684-421f-a7ce-85df81b3ff87' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A tall, narrow loudspeaker in which several drivers are mounted in a vertical line and wired through a fixed internal passive crossover network, presenting a single amplifier load with no per-driver electronic processing. The vertical stacking narrows dispersion in the vertical plane while retaining wide horizontal coverage, projecting sound along the audience plane and reducing energy radiated at the floor and ceiling, which improves clarity in reverberant rooms, and its coverage pattern is fixed by physical geometry and crossover design rather than being electronically steerable. It contrasts with digitally beam-steered columns, offering a simpler, lower-cost approach where a fixed vertical pattern is acceptable.' WHERE id='a9d29642-b5c4-4097-8c23-5788c03c2a64' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A monitor (foldback) feed dedicated to the pastor or preaching minister, providing a controlled balance—typically of the pastor''s own voice together with music and programme cues—so the speaker can hear themselves and follow the service while addressing the congregation. It is delivered by a stage wedge, a small distributed loudspeaker, or an in-ear monitor from a dedicated mix, set for a clear speech reference at a level low enough to avoid feeding back into the pastor''s open microphone.' WHERE id='39408d73-7e12-430a-923c-7f841c812188' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone assigned to the pastor or preaching minister to reinforce speech during the message. It is commonly a body-worn wireless headset or lavalier condenser that keeps a constant mouth-to-capsule distance for stable level and intelligibility as the speaker moves about the platform, although a pulpit gooseneck or handheld may be used instead. Directional pattern, placement, and gain structure are chosen for maximum gain before feedback and rejection of loudspeaker and ambient sound so the spoken word remains clear throughout the space.' WHERE id='62981ab2-72ed-439a-9f10-b470835922ea' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A large keyboard wind instrument that produces sound by driving pressurised air from a blower and wind chest through ranks of pipes, each pipe sounding a single pitch and timbre, with the player selecting which ranks speak by drawing stops and playing one or more manuals and a pedalboard. Its pipes are of flue or reed type and are organised into ranks and divisions controlled from the console, giving a wide range of tone colours and dynamic power, and because the instrument is built into and voiced for a specific room its radiated sound, especially the very low pedal registers, strongly excites the building''s acoustics. Its scale, installation, and coupling to the architecture distinguish it from electronic organs that imitate its sound electrically.' WHERE id='4d1bf28c-d530-421b-ba8c-343d73e524d9' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In a house of worship, the raised stage or dais at the front of the room from which worship is led, comprising the area occupied by clergy, musicians, and their microphones, monitors, instruments, and backline. As an audio-system location it defines the zone where source microphones, foldback loudspeakers, and instrument amplifiers sit relative to the main loudspeakers and the congregation, so its size, height, and surface finishes influence stage sound-pressure level, monitor coverage, sightlines, and the gain before feedback achievable in the space.' WHERE id='07bf4205-2405-417e-ae95-19ef6e6ac859' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A loudspeaker placed on the platform, or stage, and aimed back at performers or speakers so they can hear a dedicated foldback mix of themselves and the program, as distinct from the front-of-house loudspeakers that serve the congregation. Commonly a wedge-shaped floor monitor angled up toward the user, it reproduces a monitor mix so that vocalists and musicians can stay in tune and in time, and because it radiates on stage toward open microphones its level, position, and equalization directly affect gain before feedback and stage spill. Platform monitors may be supplemented or replaced by in-ear monitoring to lower stage volume.' WHERE id='5006ec84-7cd6-4c05-9432-5a46a26bf1ca' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The group of vocalists, and in many settings a small instrumental core, that leads contemporary congregational worship song, typically comprising a lead singer with several backing vocalists. As a subject of sound reinforcement it is the ensemble whose members each require input channels—vocal microphones plus any instrument inputs—and individual or shared foldback, and whose collective balance the mix engineer blends for the congregation; the team''s size and make-up drive vocal channel count, stage layout, and monitoring requirements.' WHERE id='7e495ad2-4fa6-42c4-95d1-0ef7220f9610' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A raised, often enclosed preaching stand in a church from which sermons, readings, and announcements are delivered, traditionally set to one side at the front of the nave and elevated so that the speaker is seen and heard by the congregation. Historically some pulpits incorporated a sounding board, or tester, overhead to project the unamplified voice toward the worshippers. As a fixed speaking position it is a primary location for a directional or gooseneck speech microphone, and its height and hard surfaces influence microphone placement, reflections, and feedback control in the reinforcement system.' WHERE id='bc5c38eb-e327-400e-9b6d-5db9249a7fa0' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone at the pulpit—the elevated stand from which the sermon is delivered—used to reinforce a single speaker''s voice at that fixed station. It is most often a cardioid or supercardioid gooseneck or podium condenser mounted to the pulpit and positioned close to the speaker for high gain before feedback and strong rejection of platform loudspeakers and ambient noise, with capsule placement set for consistent level despite differences in speaker height and distance from the pulpit.' WHERE id='86386a6c-def1-44dc-85a0-ee082c36b0a3' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In ecclesiastical architecture, the most sacred part of a church, the area immediately around the main altar, usually at the east end within or beyond the chancel, reserved for the clergy and the central liturgical action. In common North American Protestant usage the same word is often applied more broadly to the entire main worship room or auditorium in which the congregation assembles, so in worship-sound work the intended sense must be taken from context. Acoustically the term frequently denotes the principal room whose volume and finishes set the reverberation and reinforcement conditions.' WHERE id='cb668f94-36bd-4ea4-8b66-19b61c75cde4' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The reproduction, during a live service or performance, of pre-produced multitrack backing material organised as stems, that is, submixed groupings of a production such as drums, bass, keys, click, and guide vocals, played from a computer, sampler, or multitrack player so that individual groups can be routed and balanced separately at the console. Keeping the backing as separate stems rather than a single stereo file lets the operator control, mute, or reinforce each element and align it with the live musicians, commonly with a dedicated click and guide track sent only to the players'' monitors. It underlies click-tracked worship and theatrical productions in which recorded and live sources must combine seamlessly.' WHERE id='68093c0a-8fb2-4fff-82b2-870adf0bc048' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A microphone placement guideline for multi-microphone setups stating that, to keep audible comb filtering from leakage acceptably low, the distance between any two microphones should be at least three times the distance from each microphone to its intended source. Meeting this ratio makes the spill from a neighbouring source arrive at each microphone roughly nine or more decibels below the wanted close signal, so that when the channels are summed the phase-cancellation interference between them is largely inaudible. It applies whenever several microphones covering different sources are mixed together, as with vocal groups, choirs, lecterns, and drum kits, and it complements rather than replaces control of the number of open microphones.' WHERE id='da664dbc-18ca-480a-a15c-265063132b38' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='In a cruciform church, the transverse arm that crosses the nave at right angles near the junction of the nave and chancel, giving the plan its cross shape and forming, with the crossing, projecting north and south arms. It creates additional coupled volumes and reflective surfaces off the main axis, which complicate loudspeaker coverage and can lengthen or colour the reverberant field for sound reinforcement, and it may seat additional congregation or house a choir or organ. It is defined architecturally by its position at the crossing rather than by any particular liturgical function.' WHERE id='21332a80-51a9-41de-8a70-7ff574ced451' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The group of singers that leads or supports congregational song, distinct from the instrumentalists, typically a lead worship vocalist with several backing singers. For reinforcement each member is an individual vocal input—usually a handheld or headset cardioid microphone—whose levels the engineer blends and to whom foldback is provided so the singers hear themselves and the band; the team''s size sets the vocal channel count and the complexity of the monitor mixes.' WHERE id='c36711e6-630d-4a4e-b83a-bbf1fee9de87' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The instrumental and vocal ensemble that provides contemporary worship music in a service, generally built around a rhythm section of drums, bass, guitars, and keyboards with lead and backing vocals. In sound-reinforcement terms it is the multi-source group requiring the largest share of input channels, backline, and monitoring on the platform, and its instrumentation and stage volume drive decisions about miking, direct injection, backline isolation, and the choice between wedge and in-ear foldback.' WHERE id='757a7c7d-e972-4706-be9b-3debf09f6690' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The main room or auditorium of a house of worship in which the congregation assembles for services—the primary worship space, as distinct from ancillary chapels, foyers, or education areas. As the principal acoustic and audiovisual environment it houses the main loudspeaker system, the platform, and the congregational seating, and its dimensions, geometry, reverberation time, and interior finishes govern the sound-system design, coverage, speech intelligibility, and gain before feedback achievable in the space.' WHERE id='49d3904e-35e2-4569-90fb-793076916396' AND definition='(pending)';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='The person who directs a congregation''s sung and spoken worship, cueing songs, setting keys and tempo, and guiding the flow of the service, usually while singing or playing an instrument. From an audio standpoint the worship leader is the primary vocal and communication channel whose microphone and monitor mix take priority, and whose spoken cues the sound and media operators follow, so the front-of-house and monitor mixes are anchored around this single lead voice.' WHERE id='d1a48d4b-4c74-4b75-bd33-38eb731abab0' AND definition='(pending)';
COMMIT;
